<?php return array (
  'H' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'H',
    'name' => 'Hydrogen',
    'atomic' => 1,
    'category' => 'diatomic nonmetal',
    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 1 (H)',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">Hydrogen</a></b> is the universe\'s ultimate building block. Sitting at the very beginning of the periodic table with atomic number 1, it is the lightest, simplest, and most abundant element in existence. In fact, it makes up about 75% of all normal matter! Most of the <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> in our universe was forged just minutes after the Big Bang, meaning the <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> atoms in your body are nearly 13.8 billion years old. On Earth, hydrogen is almost never found floating freely as a gas. Instead, it loves to team up with other elements, most famously bonding with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> to create the water (H<sub>2</sub>O) that covers our blue planet. In its pure form, hydrogen is a colorless, odorless, non-toxic, and highly flammable gas. Across cultures, its life-giving nature is recognized: in several Indian languages, it is called "Jala-janaka" or "Udajani," which beautifully mirrors its Greek name by also meaning "the creator of water."</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/Hydrogenglow.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 1.008,
    'density' => 0.08988,
    'melt' => 13.99,
    'boil' => 20.271,
    'phase' => 'Gas',
    'discovered_by' => 'Henry Cavendish',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s1',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 2.2,
    'electron_affinity' => 72.769,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 1312,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Isotopes' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">Hydrogen</a> is unique among the elements because its three naturally occurring isotopes have their own special names! The most common is <b>Protium</b> (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">Hydrogen</a>-1), which accounts for over 99.98% of all <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a>. Its nucleus is incredibly simple: just a single proton and no neutrons. The second isotope is <b>Deuterium</b> (Hydrogen-2). It has one proton and one neutron. Most of the universe\'s deuterium was created during the Big Bang. When deuterium replaces protium in water molecules, it forms what scientists call "heavy water," which is commonly used in modern nuclear reactors—like those operating in India\'s Kalpakkam facility. The third isotope, <b>Tritium</b> (Hydrogen-3), has one proton and two neutrons. Tritium is radioactive and glows in the dark! While rare, it is used in self-powered lighting devices like the glowing dials on high-end watches and in cutting-edge nuclear fusion research.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>The discovery of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> is a fascinating tale spanning both ancient philosophy and modern chemistry. Long before modern science, ancient Indian philosophers like Kanada developed sophisticated theories of <i>paramanu</i> (indivisible atoms). Some modern interpretations of ancient Indian texts, like the <i>Agastya Samhita</i>, even point to descriptions of early battery-like setups using <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> that could theoretically split water into <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> gases! In Western science, the Swiss-German alchemist Paracelsus noticed a highly flammable gas bubbling from metals in acids during the 1500s. Fast forward to 1766, and British scientist <b>Henry Cavendish</b> officially isolated the gas by mixing <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> with hydrochloric acid, calling it "flammable air" and proving it created water when burned. In 1788, the French chemist Antoine Lavoisier gave it the modern name, <b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">Hydrogen</a></b>, combining the Greek words <i>hydro</i> (water) and <i>genes</i> (creator).</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>In the vastness of space, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> is the undisputed king. It fuels the stars, including our Sun, where it constantly undergoes nuclear fusion to create <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a>, radiating the light and heat that makes life on Earth possible. It also forms the massive swirling clouds of gas deep in space and makes up the bulk of giant planets like Jupiter and Saturn. But on Earth, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> tells a different story. Because it is so wonderfully light, any unattached <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> gas floated away into space billions of years ago. Today, almost all the hydrogen on our planet is locked away in chemical partnerships. It is a vital part of water, fossil fuels, and every living organism. Interestingly, geologists are now discovering rare pockets of "white hydrogen" trapped deep underground, naturally created when water reacts with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>-rich rocks or radioactive minerals!</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">Hydrogen</a> is rapidly becoming a superstar in the global quest for clean, green energy. While traditionally used to refine petroleum and manufacture fertilizers, its future lies in saving the planet. Because <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> only produces water when burned or used in a fuel cell, it is the ultimate zero-emission energy carrier. Today, <b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> fuel cells</b> are powering buses, long-haul trucks, and even trains—in fact, Indian Railways is actively testing hydrogen-powered passenger coaches! India is aggressively pursuing this frontier through the <b>National Green Hydrogen Mission</b>, aiming for energy independence by 2070. Breakthroughs are happening fast: institutions like the Indian Institute of Science (IISc) are turning biomass into green hydrogen, while IIT Guwahati and the Centre for Nano and Soft Matter Sciences (CeNS) are perfecting ways to split water directly using just sunlight. From powering NASA rocket engines to flying pollution-free airplanes of the future, hydrogen is truly the fuel of tomorrow.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'He' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'He',
    'name' => 'Helium',
    'atomic' => 2,
    'category' => 'noble gas',
    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 2 (He)',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">Helium</a></b> (symbol <b>He</b>, atomic number 2) is a fascinating, colorless, and odorless gas in the noble gas family. It is the second-lightest and second-most abundant element in the universe (after <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a>), making up about 24% of all elemental mass. Though it is constantly created in stars like our Sun through nuclear fusion, it is surprisingly rare on Earth, where it mostly forms from the slow radioactive decay of heavy elements deep underground. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">Helium</a> is famously known for its extreme resistance to cold: it has the lowest boiling point of any known element and is the only element that refuses to freeze into a solid under normal atmospheric pressure, no matter how chilly it gets!</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/00/Helium-glow.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 4.0026022,
    'density' => 0.1786,
    'melt' => 0.95,
    'boil' => 4.222,
    'phase' => 'Gas',
    'discovered_by' => 'Pierre Janssen',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => NULL,
    'electron_affinity' => -48,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 2372.3,
      1 => 5250.5,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'History' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">Helium</a> has a truly out-of-this-world origin story! It is famously the first element to be discovered in space before it was found on Earth. This historic breakthrough happened in India during the total solar eclipse of August 18, 1868. French astronomer Pierre Janssen was observing the eclipse from Guntur, Andhra Pradesh, when he spotted a mysterious bright yellow line in the Sun\'s light spectrum. Soon after, English astronomer Joseph Norman Lockyer independently confirmed it and named the unknown element after <i>Helios</i>, the Greek god of the sun. It wasn\'t until nearly thirty years later, in 1895, that Scottish chemist Sir William Ramsay finally isolated <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a> on Earth by treating a <a href="/school1/ptable/element/U" class="wiki-link" title="Uranium (U)">uranium</a>-bearing mineral with acid. Later in 1905, massive amounts of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a> were unexpectedly found in natural gas fields in Kansas, USA, which is still the primary way we harvest it today.</p>',
      'Characteristics' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">Helium</a> is a true rebel in the world of physics and chemistry. It is the only element that cannot be solidified just by dropping the temperature at standard pressure; it remains a liquid all the way down to absolute zero and requires extreme pressure to freeze! When cooled near absolute zero, liquid <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a> transforms into a bizarre state of matter called a \'superfluid.\' In this state, it has absolutely zero friction or viscosity, allowing it to practically defy gravity as it spontaneously creeps up and over the walls of its container! As an inert noble gas, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a> almost never reacts or bonds with other elements, which makes it incredibly stable, non-toxic, and perfectly safe to handle.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">Helium</a> exists mainly as two stable forms, or isotopes: <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a>-4 and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a>-3. Helium-4 is incredibly stable and by far the most common. The vast majority of the universe\'s helium-4 was forged during the fiery beginnings of the Big Bang, while on Earth, it bubbles up from the underground radioactive decay of heavy elements like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/U" class="wiki-link" title="Uranium (U)">uranium</a>. Helium-3, on the other hand, is extremely rare on our planet but quite abundant in space and solar winds. Because our Moon has been bombarded by these solar winds for billions of years, its surface is rich in helium-3. Scientists are incredibly excited about the prospect of mining the Moon for helium-3 one day, as it could serve as the perfect, clean fuel for future nuclear fusion reactors!</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>While you might first think of party balloons and floating blimps, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a> is a critical resource that powers modern technology. Because of its unmatched cooling abilities, its biggest use is in the medical field, where liquid <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a> supercools the powerful superconducting magnets inside MRI scanners. Without it, modern life-saving medical imaging would be nearly impossible! It is also vital for high-tech manufacturing, like making semiconductor chips for electronics and optical fibers for high-speed internet. In space exploration, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a> is used to pressurize fuel tanks in massive rockets, and deep-sea divers breathe a mixture of helium and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> to avoid \'the bends.\' Today, India continues its historic connection with helium by making significant strides in modern research and technology. Institutions like the Raja Ramanna Centre for Advanced Technology (RRCAT) have developed indigenous helium liquefiers, and a major Liquid Helium Cryogenic facility was recently inaugurated at IIT Bombay to support deep-tech research in quantum sensing. Indian astronomers at the Indian Institute of Astrophysics (IIA) are pioneering new methods to estimate helium abundance in the Sun, while researchers at NIT Durgapur are actively exploring ways to extract domestic helium from India\'s geothermal hot springs!</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Li' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Li',
    'name' => 'Lithium',
    'atomic' => 3,
    'category' => 'alkali metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><strong><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">Lithium</a></strong> (from the Greek word <em>lithos</em>, meaning &quot;stone&quot;) is a fascinating chemical element with the symbol <strong>Li</strong> and atomic number 3. As a soft, silvery-white metal belonging to the alkali metal group, it holds the remarkable title of being the lightest metal and the least dense solid element on Earth. Because it is highly reactive, you will never find it freely in nature; instead, it is always locked away in minerals and salts. Today, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a> is a critical resource for the modern world, powering everything from smartphones to electric vehicles and driving global efforts toward clean energy and technological self-reliance.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e2/0.5_grams_lithium_under_argon.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 6.94,
    'density' => 0.534,
    'melt' => 453.65,
    'boil' => 1603,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Johan August Arfwedson',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s1',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 0.98,
    'electron_affinity' => 59.6326,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 520.2,
      1 => 7298.1,
      2 => 11815,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Isotopes' => '<p>In nature, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a> comes in two stable forms, or isotopes: <sup>6</sup>Li and <sup>7</sup>Li. The vast majority (about 95.15%) is <sup>7</sup>Li. Interestingly, these natural isotopes have unusually low nuclear binding energy compared to their neighbors, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">beryllium</a>. In fact, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a> is the only light element that can produce net energy through nuclear fission! Because of this unique trait, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a> is surprisingly less common in our Solar System than 28 of the first 34 chemical elements, despite being incredibly light.</p><p>Scientists have also discovered seven radioactive isotopes of lithium, though they are highly unstable. The longest-lived is <sup>8</sup>Li, with a half-life of just 838 milliseconds, while the shortest-lived vanishes in a tiny fraction of a second. Lithium\'s isotopes behave differently during natural processes, meaning they naturally separate over time. This separation is also done artificially; for example, the lighter <sup>6</sup>Li isotope has been heavily used in nuclear physics and weapons manufacturing. This has slightly altered the natural ratio of lithium isotopes on Earth, making its standard atomic weight a bit tricky to pin down!</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Because <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a> is highly reactive, it never exists as a pure metal in nature. Instead, it is found hidden in mineral-rich rocks and brines. Historically, the primary sources have been pegmatite minerals, such as spodumene, and massive salt flats like the &quot;<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">Lithium</a> Triangle&quot; spanning the Andes mountains in Argentina, Bolivia, and Chile, where <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a>-rich brine is evaporated in large solar ponds.</p><p>Recently, India made a massive leap onto the global lithium map. In February 2023, the Geological Survey of India (GSI) announced the landmark discovery of 5.9 million tonnes of inferred lithium resources in the Salal-Haimana area of the Reasi district in Jammu &amp; Kashmir. This high-quality deposit—grading at 500 parts per million or higher—marks the country\'s first major lithium find. It strategically positions India as a potential powerhouse in global lithium resources, crucial for its &quot;Aatmanirbhar Bharat&quot; (self-reliant India) initiative to reduce dependence on imports for electric vehicle manufacturing and green energy.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>The story of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a> begins in 1800 when Brazilian scientist José Bonifácio de Andrada e Silva discovered the mineral petalite in a Swedish mine. But it wasn\'t until 1817 that Johan August Arfwedson, working in the lab of famous chemist Jöns Jakob Berzelius, realized this ore contained an entirely new element. Berzelius named the new substance &quot;lithion&quot; (from <em>lithos</em>, Greek for &quot;stone&quot;) because it was discovered in a rock, unlike <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a> which were found in plants and animal blood. The element was officially named <strong><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a></strong>.</p><p>Although early scientists knew <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a> was there (noticing its bright red flame), they couldn\'t easily separate the pure metal. It was finally isolated in 1821 by British chemist William Thomas Brande using a process called electrolysis. Throughout the 20th century, lithium found unexpected uses, from a revolutionary psychiatric treatment for bipolar disorder (discovered by John Cade in 1949) to the production of tritium for nuclear weapons during the Cold War. Today, its history is being written by the clean energy revolution.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">Lithium</a> is often called the &quot;white <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)">gold</a>&quot; of the modern world because it is absolutely essential to today\'s technology and the global energy transition. Its most famous application is in <strong><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a>-ion batteries</strong>. Because <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a> is extremely light and holds a massive electrical charge, these batteries are perfect for powering smartphones, laptops, and electric vehicles (EVs), as well as storing energy from renewable sources like solar and wind.</p><p>India is rapidly advancing its own indigenous lithium technologies to support this transition. For instance, the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO) has developed high-reliability, space-grade lithium-ion cells—including innovative <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">Silicon</a>-Graphite anode-based high-energy density cells—and transferred this technology to Indian industries for commercial production. Similarly, the Council of Scientific &amp; Industrial Research\'s Central Electrochemical Research Institute (CSIR-CECRI) has established India\'s first pilot plant for manufacturing 18650-type Li-ion cells, driving local innovation.</p><p>Beyond batteries, lithium has several other critical industrial uses. It is added to glass and ceramics to make them resistant to sudden temperature changes. In aviation, it is mixed with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a> to create super-strong, lightweight alloys for aircraft. Finally, lithium carbonate continues to be a life-changing pharmaceutical, remaining one of the most effective treatments for bipolar disorder.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Be' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Be',
    'name' => 'Beryllium',
    'atomic' => 4,
    'category' => 'alkaline earth metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">Beryllium</a></a> (symbol <strong>Be</strong>, atomic number 4) is a stiff, lightweight, and fascinating metal. Born from the collision of cosmic rays in deep space, it is relatively rare in the universe. On Earth, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">beryllium</a> is too reactive to exist on its own. Instead, it hides inside beautiful and colorful minerals, such as beryl and emerald, which have been prized for thousands of years.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e2/Beryllium_%28Be%29.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 9.01218315,
    'density' => 1.85,
    'melt' => 1560,
    'boil' => 2742,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Louis Nicolas Vauquelin',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.57,
    'electron_affinity' => -48,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 899.5,
      1 => 1757.1,
      2 => 14848.7,
      3 => 21006.6,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">Beryllium</a> possesses remarkable physical and chemical traits that make it stand out among metals, combining extreme lightness with surprising strength and heat resistance.</p>',
      'Physical properties' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">Beryllium</a> is a hard, steel-gray metal that is brittle at room temperature. It boasts a close-packed hexagonal crystal structure and an exceptional stiffness—making it about 35% stiffer than steel! Despite this incredible rigidity, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">beryllium</a> is remarkably lightweight. This unique combination of stiffness and low density allows sound to travel through it at a staggering speed of 12.9 kilometers per second under normal conditions.</p><p>When it comes to handling heat, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">beryllium</a> is unmatched. It dissipates the most heat per unit weight of any metal, thanks to its high specific heat and excellent thermal conductivity. Furthermore, it barely expands or contracts when heated or cooled. This means that even under extreme temperature changes, beryllium won\'t easily warp or change shape, making it incredibly stable.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">Beryllium</a> is hidden within more than 100 different minerals, though most are quite rare. The most famous is beryl, which includes beloved gemstones like aquamarine and the stunning green emerald. The signature green of an emerald is actually caused by a tiny dash of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cr" class="wiki-link" title="Chromium (Cr)">chromium</a> mixed into the beryl crystal.</p><p>Historically and today, India has been a significant source of beryl. In ancient Indian texts like the <em>Arthashastra</em> (4th century BCE) and the <em>Puranas</em>, beryl was known as <em>vaidurya</em> and emerald as <em>marakata</em>. These stones were highly prized in the <em>Navaratna</em> (nine gems) tradition, symbolizing the planet <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">Mercury</a> and believed to bring protection and fortune.</p><p>Today, the primary ores mined for <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">beryllium</a> are beryl and bertrandite, found in countries like India, the United States, Brazil, and Madagascar. Across the cosmos, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">beryllium</a> is quite rare; the Sun contains only tiny traces of it. On Earth, it is the 47th most abundant element in the crust, typically concentrated in soils and almost entirely absent from ocean waters.</p>',
      'Chemical properties' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">Beryllium</a> atoms are incredibly small and hold onto their outer electrons very tightly. Because of this, it doesn\'t form simple ions easily; instead, it forms strong chemical bonds and links together into long chain-like structures. Interestingly, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">beryllium</a> behaves very similarly to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a>, sharing what chemists call a "diagonal relationship" on the periodic table.</p><p>In everyday conditions, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">beryllium</a> protects itself from the air by instantly forming a microscopic, invisible shield of beryllium oxide on its surface. This armor prevents it from reacting further. However, if heated to extreme temperatures, beryllium will burst into a brilliant flame. Like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a>, it dissolves easily in certain acids and alkaline solutions, but it shrugs off water thanks to its protective oxide shield.</p><p>Beryllium combines with other elements to form a wide variety of fascinating compounds, from heat-resistant white solids like beryllium oxide to striking brick-red compounds like beryllium carbide, which reacts with water to produce methane gas.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>Humans have prized <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">beryllium</a>-containing minerals for thousands of years. As far back as ancient Egypt and ancient India, people treasured beryl and emeralds. Ancient Indian alchemical and gemological texts detailed sophisticated methods for testing and valuing these stones. For a long time, early scientists believed these gems were simply forms of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a> silicate.</p><p>In 1798, French chemist Louis-Nicolas Vauquelin discovered a brand new element hidden inside emeralds. At first, it was named "glucine" because some of its compounds tasted sweet—though we now know <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">beryllium</a> is highly toxic! The name <em><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">beryllium</a></em> was introduced in 1828, but both names were used interchangeably until 1949.</p><p>The pure metal was finally isolated in 1828 by two scientists, Friedrich Wöhler and Antoine Bussy, working entirely independently. One of beryllium\'s most famous historical moments occurred in 1932: when scientist James Chadwick bombarded a sample of beryllium with alpha particles, he observed a strange new radiation. This led directly to his Nobel Prize-winning discovery of the neutron!</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Although you rarely see pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">beryllium</a> in everyday life, it is a critical "behind-the-scenes" material in modern technology. It is most commonly mixed with metals like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> to create specialized alloys. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">Beryllium</a>-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> is highly conductive, incredibly strong, and uniquely "non-sparking"—making it essential for heavy-duty industrial tools used in explosive environments like oil rigs.</p><p>Because it is so stiff, lightweight, and stable across wild temperature changes, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">beryllium</a> is a superstar in the aerospace and defense sectors. It is heavily used in supersonic aircraft, military sensors, and satellites. Most famously, the breathtaking golden mirrors of the <strong>James Webb Space Telescope</strong> are carved from solid beryllium, allowing them to remain perfectly rigid and functional in the freezing vacuum of deep space.</p><p>In India, modern research into beryllium is driven by the nation\'s strategic space, defense, and nuclear sectors. The Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) has developed specialized processes to extract beryllium from domestic Indian beryl ores, producing high-tech alloys for satellites and nuclear reactors. Furthermore, because beryllium is nearly transparent to X-rays, it is the perfect window material for medical imaging equipment, allowing rays to pass through clearly.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'B' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'B',
    'name' => 'Boron',
    'atomic' => 5,
    'category' => 'metalloid',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">Boron</a></a></b> (symbol <b>B</b>, atomic number 5) is an incredibly versatile and fascinating metalloid. Unlike most elements, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a> was not forged in the fiery cores of stars. Instead, it was born in the extreme environment of deep space, created when high-energy cosmic rays smashed into heavier matter. Because of this unique origin, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a> is relatively rare in the universe and the Earth\'s crust. However, because its most common forms—minerals called borates—dissolve easily in water, it can be found in highly concentrated deposits here on Earth. Often described as a "schizophrenic" element, it confusingly blends the properties of both metals and non-metals. Today, boron is essential in everything from building ultra-strong aerospace parts to, possibly, the very origin of life.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a2/Boron.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 10.81,
    'density' => 2.08,
    'melt' => 2349,
    'boil' => 4200,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Joseph Louis Gay-Lussac',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p1',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 2.04,
    'electron_affinity' => 26.989,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 800.6,
      1 => 2427.1,
      2 => 3659.7,
      3 => 25025.8,
      4 => 32826.7,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'History' => '<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/775a874aadfdc99e73b765138414711a.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="250" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1632" data-file-height="1632" /><figcaption>Amorphous <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a></a> powder</figcaption></figure>
<p>The name <i><a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a></i> stems from <i>borax</i>, the mineral from which it was first extracted, and its ending reflects its chemical similarity to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a></a>. Long before it was known as a unique element, early civilizations prized borate minerals. Over 4,000 years ago, ancient societies used them for metalworking. In ancient India, naturally occurring borax was well-known and called <i>Tankana</i> or <i>tincal</i>—a word that became the root for many modern names for the mineral. Harvested from the Himalayas and Tibet, it was widely used in traditional Ayurvedic medicine as <i>Tankana Bhasma</i> for treating ailments, and served as a crucial flux for purifying <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)">gold</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)">silver</a>. Around 300 AD, China began using borax for specialized ceramic glazes, which Marco Polo later introduced to Italy.</p>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/f04a847929d002552be76a1b1bd7b74e.jpg" decoding="async" width="190" height="181" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" data-file-width="535" data-file-height="510" /><figcaption>Sassolite</figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">Boron</a> was not officially recognized as a distinct element until 1808. It was discovered independently by French chemists Joseph Louis Gay-Lussac and Louis Jacques Thénard, and British chemist Sir Humphry Davy. Davy, who playfully named the substance <i>boracium</i>, extracted it by applying electric currents and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a></a> to boric acid. However, completely pure boron proved extremely stubborn to isolate. It took until 1909 for American chemist Ezekiel Weintraub to finally produce the first genuinely pure sample of the element.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'Isotopes' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: Isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a></a></div>
<p>In nature, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a> exists mainly as two stable varieties, or isotopes: <sup>11</sup>B (which makes up about 80.1% of natural <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a>) and <sup>10</sup>B (the remaining 19.9%). Scientists have created 11 other isotopes in laboratories, but they are all incredibly unstable. For instance, <sup>7</sup>B exists for only 3.5×10<sup>−22</sup> seconds before falling apart!</p><p>The two stable isotopes are incredibly useful to researchers. When dissolved in water, <sup>10</sup>B and <sup>11</sup>B behave slightly differently. Because ocean water holds more <sup>11</sup>B compared to rocks on land, geologists can use this unique "isotopic signature" to track the history of ancient environments. In medicine, <sup>10</sup>B is a lifesaver. It is the key ingredient in Boron Neutron Capture Therapy (BNCT), a cutting-edge cancer treatment that uses targeted neutron beams to destroy brain tumors from the inside out, without harming healthy tissue.</p><p>Physicists are also captivated by a highly unstable isotope, <sup>17</sup>B. It possesses a bizarre "nuclear halo," meaning the atom is noticeably larger and more spread out than traditional rules of physics say it should be.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading4">',
      'Occurrence' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: Borate minerals</div>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/86ee14d71e196a043c9b9bb83312e342.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="195" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1024" data-file-height="800" /><figcaption>Borax crystals</figcaption></figure>
<p>On a cosmic scale, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a> is quite rare. Very little of it was made during the Big Bang. Unlike heavier elements like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a> is actually destroyed by the intense heat inside stars rather than created there. Instead, the universe makes <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a> through a dramatic process called "spallation," where high-speed cosmic rays crash into heavier atoms in deep space, shattering them into lighter pieces like boron.</p><p>Here on Earth, you will never find pure elemental boron out in nature. It is highly reactive with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a>, meaning it prefers to form borate compounds. (However, scientists were amazed to find tiny flecks of pure boron in dirt brought back from the Moon!) Even though boron makes up a minuscule 0.001% of the Earth\'s crust, it is easy to mine because many borate minerals—like borax and boric acid—dissolve in water. Over millions of years, water has pooled these minerals together into large, rich deposits, especially in ancient, dried-up lakes and volcanic hot springs.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'Applications' => '<p>Though rarely discussed, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a> is an unsung hero of the modern world. Most of the <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a> mined today is turned into boric acid or borax (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a> tetraborate). In the United States, roughly 70% of this supply is used to make specialized glass and ceramics. If you have ever baked with a Pyrex dish, peered through a telescope lens, or used laboratory beakers, you have relied on borosilicate glass. This special glass uses <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a> to endure sudden, extreme temperature changes without shattering.</p><p>Worldwide, boron\'s biggest job is in the production of fiberglass. Adding boron makes glass fibers tougher and easier to shape, which is vital for building home insulation and sturdy construction materials. Beyond glass, boron helps create incredibly tough ceramics and advanced materials. Boron fibers are exceptionally lightweight yet incredibly strong, making them ideal for high-tech aerospace parts and even bulletproof vests. You can also find boron silently working in automotive fluids to boost efficiency, in household detergents and bleaches, and in the manufacturing of modern computer semiconductors.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Biological role' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">See also: <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">Boron</a></a> deficiency (plant disorder)</div>
<p>For the plant kingdom, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a> is non-negotiable. It acts as a critical micronutrient that builds and strengthens plant cell walls, ensuring healthy crops and abundant harvests. However, it walks a fine line: while plants need <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a> to thrive, soils with too much of it (anything above 1.8 parts per million) become toxic, causing leaves to wither and stunt growth.</p><p>In the microscopic world, nature uses boron to create unique antibiotics. Scientists discovered the first of these, boromycin, in the 1960s, followed by tartrolons in the 1990s. In modern laboratories, synthetic boron compounds called "BODIPYs" are incredibly popular. These brightly glowing fluorescent tags help scientists track and visualize hidden processes inside living cells.</p><p>Perhaps most mind-blowing of all is boron\'s potential link to our origins. Prominent scientists, such as chemist Steve Benner, suspect that ancient borate minerals played a vital role in the origin of life by stabilizing ribose, the fragile sugar backbone of RNA. In 2013, Benner even suggested that ancient Mars had better conditions for this boron-assisted RNA formation than Earth. This fascinating theory hints that primitive life might have actually started on Mars before meteorites carried it to our planet!</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
    ),
  ),
  'C' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'C',
    'name' => 'Carbon',
    'atomic' => 6,
    'category' => 'polyatomic nonmetal',
    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 6 (C)',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">Carbon</a></a></b> (symbol <b>C</b>, atomic number 6) is a versatile polyatomic nonmetal in group 14 of the periodic table. Known since antiquity in forms like charcoal and diamond, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> is the undisputed chemical backbone of all known life on Earth. Because each <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> atom has four outer electrons, it is "tetravalent"—capable of forming four strong covalent bonds with other atoms, including long chains and rings of carbon itself. Carbon makes up about 0.025 percent of Earth\'s crust. It occurs naturally in three isotopes: stable <sup>12</sup>C (98.93%) and <sup>13</sup>C (1.07%), alongside the radioactive isotope <sup>14</sup>C, which decays with a half-life of about 5,700 years and allows scientists to date ancient organic artifacts.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/68/Pure_Carbon.png',
    'atomic_mass' => 12.011,
    'density' => 1.821,
    'melt' => NULL,
    'boil' => NULL,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Ancient Egypt',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p2',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 2.55,
    'electron_affinity' => 121.7763,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 1086.5,
      1 => 2352.6,
      2 => 4620.5,
      3 => 6222.7,
      4 => 37831,
      5 => 47277,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/9a6aeb6acca784b59e2b574971aee376.png" decoding="async" width="330" height="282" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.3" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" data-file-width="2397" data-file-height="2049" /><figcaption>Phase diagram showing <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a>\'s extreme sublimation point and liquid phase boundaries</figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">Carbon</a> is famous for taking on astonishingly different physical personalities—called allotropes—depending on how its atoms are arranged. In graphite, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> atoms bond in flat, hexagonal sheets that easily slide past one another, making it soft, black, and slippery enough to write with. In diamond, carbon atoms form a rigid, three-dimensional lattice where every atom is locked in a tight pyramid structure, creating the hardest naturally occurring substance known. Modern science has unveiled even more exotic carbon structures, such as buckyballs (fullerenes), carbon nanotubes, and one-atom-thick graphene. Renowned Indian scientists, such as Bharat Ratna Prof. C.N.R. Rao, have led pioneering global research into synthesising and understanding these remarkable carbon nanomaterials.</p><p>Chemically, carbon possesses an extraordinary property known as catenation—the ability to form stable, endless chains and rings by bonding with itself and other elements. Because of this, carbon is part of roughly 200 million distinct chemical compounds documented in scientific literature, far surpassing all other elements combined! Carbon also holds the highest sublimation temperature of all elements: at normal sea-level atmospheric pressure, solid carbon does not melt into a liquid when heated. Instead, it turns directly into a gas (sublimes) at an incredible 3,900 K (3,630 °C or 6,560 °F). Liquid carbon is extremely difficult to study because it can only exist under extreme pressures exceeding 100 atmospheres (10.8 megapascals) and temperatures above 4,000 K (3,730 °C). In its gaseous vapor state, excited diatomic carbon molecules (C<sub>2</sub>) produce a striking green glow.</p><p>At the atomic level, carbon is element number 6 with a ground-state electron configuration of 1s<sup>2</sup> 2s<sup>2</sup> 2p<sup>2</sup>, featuring 4 valence electrons in its outer shell. Its first four ionization energies are 1086.5, 2352.6, 4620.5, and 6222.7 kJ/mol—significantly higher than heavier elements in group 14. Carbon has an electronegativity of 2.55 on the Pauling scale, balancing well with neighboring nonmetals. Its covalent bonding radius shrinks as bond strength increases, measuring 77.2 pm for single bonds (C−C), 66.7 pm for double bonds (C=C), and 60.3 pm for triple bonds (C≡C).</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Isotopes' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: Isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a></a></div>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">Carbon</a> nuclei always contain 6 protons, but their neutron count can range from 2 to 16 across 15 known isotopes. Earth\'s natural <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> consists almost entirely of two stable isotopes: carbon-12 (<sup>12</sup>C), which accounts for 98.93%, and carbon-13 (<sup>13</sup>C), which makes up the remaining 1.07%. Biological systems naturally prefer <sup>12</sup>C during photosynthesis and metabolism, leaving living organisms slightly enriched in <sup>12</sup>C. In 1961, the International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC) designated carbon-12 as the official universal standard for defining atomic weights. Meanwhile, carbon-13 plays a vital role in chemistry and medicine as the active nucleus used in carbon-13 Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR) spectroscopy to map organic molecular structures.</p><p>Carbon-14 (<sup>14</sup>C) is a famous natural radioactive isotope produced continuously in Earth\'s upper atmosphere (between 9 and 15 km altitude). High-energy cosmic rays strike atmospheric atoms, creating thermal neutrons that collide with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a>-14 nuclei to yield carbon-14 and a proton. Carbon-14 exists in trace amounts—about 1 part per trillion (1.5 × 10<sup>−10</sup> of atmospheric carbon dioxide). It decays by releasing a 0.158 MeV beta particle (β<sup>−</sup>) with a half-life of 5,700 ± 30 years. Because living organisms absorb carbon dioxide throughout their lives, their carbon-14 ratio matches the atmosphere; after death, <sup>14</sup>C decays predictably without being replenished. Invented in 1949, radiocarbon dating measures this decay to accurately date ancient organic materials, wood, and archaeological relics up to 40,000 years old.</p><p>Among carbon\'s synthetic isotopes, carbon-8 is the shortest-lived, decaying via proton emission with a fleeting half-life of just 3.5 × 10<sup>−21</sup> seconds. On the exotic extreme, carbon-19 exhibits a loose "nuclear halo," where its outer neutron orbits far outside the central core, giving the atom an unusually large nuclear radius.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'Occurrence' => '<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/176f8bf3f36d7ce0366de1ebe2fd0ba4.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="188" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="600" data-file-height="451" /><figcaption>Natural graphite ore</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/d06ef40b45f5946fe7cf3582d5ac905e.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="188" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="360" data-file-height="271" /><figcaption>Raw octahedral diamond crystal</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/fa3ee3e851d8076d00f381063dc87600.png" decoding="async" width="250" height="168" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1687" data-file-height="1133" /><figcaption>Global distribution of dissolved inorganic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> in ocean waters</figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">Carbon</a> is the fourth most abundant element in the observable universe by mass, trailing only <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a>. It is abundant in the Sun, distant stars, comets, and planetary atmospheres. Over 20% of all cosmic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> is bound in complex ring structures called Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbons (PAHs). According to the PAH world hypothesis, these space-borne carbon molecules formed roughly two billion years after the Big Bang and may have helped seed the chemical origins of life. Microscopic diamonds also form naturally inside meteorites and at cosmic impact craters, while carbon-rich asteroids in the outer asteroid belt are eyed as potential targets for futuristic space mining.</p><p>Across planet Earth (mass 5.972 × 10<sup>24</sup> kg), carbon averages about 730 parts per million (ppm), with roughly 2,000 ppm locked in Earth\'s metallic core and 120 ppm spread through the mantle and crust—totalling an estimated 4,360 million gigatonnes (Gt) of carbon. Earth\'s atmosphere contains around 900 Gt of carbon as carbon dioxide (where 1 ppm equals 2.13 Gt). Since 1750, human burning of fossil fuels has added between 347 and 879 Gt of carbon to the atmosphere, contributing to a total of nearly 2,000 Gt absorbed across the atmosphere, oceans, and land peat bogs. World oceans hold approximately 36,000 Gt of dissolved carbon, while the living biosphere accounts for roughly 550 Gt. Vast icy deposits of methane hydrates beneath polar seabed sediments contain another 500 to 3,000 Gt of carbon.</p><p>Geologically, carbon makes up about 12% of massive carbonate rock formations such as limestone, dolomite, and marble. Fossil fuels represent massive mineralized carbon deposits: global coal reserves contain about 900 Gt of carbon (out of 18,000 Gt of total resources), with high-grade anthracite coal consisting of 92% to 98% pure carbon. Proven crude oil reserves hold about 150 Gt, while natural gas reserves hold 105 Gt (175 × 10<sup>12</sup> m<sup>3</sup>), with an estimated 540 Gt (900 × 10<sup>12</sup> m<sup>3</sup>) trapped in shale gas formations.</p><p>Natural graphite is mined globally in major deposits across China, Russia, Mexico, Canada, and India (with prominent Indian reserves in states like Odisha, Jharkhand, Tamil Nadu, and Arunachal Pradesh). Natural diamonds originate deep within Earth\'s mantle and reach the surface through ancient volcanic kimberlite pipes found in South Africa, Botswana, Namibia, the DRC, Angola, Brazil, Australia, Russia, Canada, and Arkansas, USA. Historically, India\'s legendary Golconda mines (in modern Telangana and Andhra Pradesh) were the world\'s primary source of extraordinary diamonds—including the famous Koh-i-Noor and Hope Diamond. Ancient Indian gemological treatises (<i>Ratnaśāstra</i>) and medieval alchemical texts like the <i>Rasa Ratna Samuccaya</i> by Vāgbhaṭa accurately documented the diamond\'s unmatched hardness (<i>Vajra</i>) and chemical invulnerability. In modern Indian science, Birbal Sahni (1891–1949), the pioneer of Indian paleobotany, conducted foundational studies on fossilized plant carbon and Indian coal beds, illuminating how ancient plant matter transformed into carbon rich geological formations over millions of years.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
