<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">Carbon</a> is the chemical basis for all known life, forming the structural foundation of proteins, DNA, carbohydrates, and fats. Beyond food and wood, carbon\'s largest economic role is in energy and materials. Hydrocarbons like natural gas (methane) and crude oil (petroleum) are refined in petrochemical plants to produce gasoline, diesel, jet fuel, and synthetic polymers (plastics). In nature, plants produce cellulose—a natural carbon polymer forming wood, cotton, linen, and hemp—while animals yield natural carbon fibers like wool, silk, and cashmere.</p><p>Carbon is vital across metallurgy and heavy industry. When added to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>, carbon forms steel—the world\'s premier structural alloy. Historically, ancient Indian metallurgists used charcoal (pure carbon) to smelt <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> and forge famous high-carbon Wootz steel (the original Damascus steel), renowned for its strength and wavy patterns. Today, coke (processed coal) acts as a reducing agent in blast furnaces to extract pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> from ore. Carbides formed with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/W" class="wiki-link" title="Tungsten (W)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/W" class="wiki-link" title="Tungsten (W)">tungsten</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a></a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a></a> are among the hardest materials on Earth, used in industrial cutting, grinding, and metalworking tools.</p><p>Graphite mixed with clay forms pencil leads, while pure graphite serves as a high-temperature lubricant, battery electrode material, motor brush, and neutron moderator in nuclear reactors. Charcoal is widely used in sketching, barbecue grilling, and water filtration. Activated charcoal features a porous surface that traps impurities, making it essential in gas masks, air purifiers, water treatment, and emergency medicine to absorb ingested toxins from the stomach.</p><p>Carbon black—a fine powder of elemental carbon—serves as the dark pigment in printing inks, India ink, oil paints, laser printer toners, and automotive finishes, as well as a reinforcing filler in rubber tires. High-performance carbon fiber, produced by heating synthetic polyacrylonitrile (PAN) or polyester fibers until only carbon atoms remain, creates ultra-strong, lightweight composite materials with a higher tensile strength than steel. Carbon fiber composite structures are critical in aerospace, high-end automotive engineering, and space technology—such as payload fairings and rocket casings engineered by the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO) for PSLV and GSLV rockets.</p>
<div style="clear:both;" class=""></div>
