    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 7 (N)',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">Nitrogen</a></a></b> (symbol <b>N</b>, atomic number 7) is the invisible titan of Earth\'s air. As the lightest member of Group 15 on the periodic table (the pnictogens), <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a> is a nonmetal that ranks as the seventh most abundant element in both the Milky Way galaxy and our Solar System. Under normal conditions, two <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a> atoms lock together in a nearly unbreakable chemical bond to form dinitrogen (N<sub>2</sub>)—a colorless, odorless, and harmless gas that makes up roughly 78% of the air we breathe. Despite completely dominating our atmosphere, nitrogen is surprisingly rare in solid rocks and Earth\'s crust because its compounds vaporize and dissolve easily. Yet, without nitrogen, life could not exist: it forms the fundamental backbone of proteins, DNA, and the fertilizers that feed billions of people across the globe.</p>',
