    'sections' => 
    array (
      'History' => '<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/584551aab23bdc952ef7ba9d56495f38.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="293" class="mw-file-element"  data-file-width="416" data-file-height="487" /><figcaption>Daniel Rutherford, discoverer of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a></a></figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">Nitrogen</a></a> compounds have a long and rich history. As far back as antiquity, ammonium chloride was recorded by the Greek historian Herodotus. By the Middle Ages, alchemists routinely worked with powerful <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a> chemicals. They referred to nitric acid as <i>aqua fortis</i> ("strong water") and created <i>aqua regia</i> ("royal water")—a fierce mixture of nitric and hydrochloric acids famous for its unique ability to dissolve <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)">gold</a></a>, the king of metals.</p>
<p>In ancient and medieval India, nitrogen compounds played a central role in metallurgy, medicine, and warfare. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">Potassium</a> nitrate was known in Sanskrit texts as <i>Suryakshar</i> or <i>Saurkshar</i> (and <i>Suvarchika</i>), and in regional dialects as <i>Shora</i> or <i>Shora Qalmi</i>. Seminal alchemical and medicinal treatises such as the 13th-century CE <i>Rasaratnasamuccaya</i> (authored by Vāgbhaṭa) and the <i>Rasarnava</i> document the extraction and purification of <i>Shora</i> from nitrate-rich soils for use in <i>Rasaśāstra</i> (Ayurvedic mineral alchemy). By the medieval era, the fertile plains of Bihar became the undisputed global hub for saltpetre production. Specialized communities known as the <i>Nuniahs</i> (or <i>Luniahs</i>) scraped, leached, and crystallized this "white <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)">gold</a>." Because saltpetre was the indispensable oxidizer in gunpowder, control over Bihar\'s saltpetre trade sparked intense commercial and military conflicts between the Dutch, French, and British East India Companies throughout the 17th to 19th centuries.</p>
<p>The formal discovery of elemental nitrogen took place in 1772 when Scottish physician Daniel Rutherford isolated it and called it "noxious air." Rutherford recognized that this portion of air would not support combustion or animal life, though he distinguished it from Joseph Black\'s "fixed air" (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a></a> dioxide). Around the same time, scientists Carl Wilhelm Scheele, Henry Cavendish, and Joseph Priestley independently studied the gas, calling it "burnt air" or "phlogisticated air." French chemist Antoine Lavoisier termed it "mephitic air" or <i>azote</i> (from the Greek <i>azotikos</i>, meaning "no life"), reflecting how animals suffocated in pure nitrogen. While English speakers preferred the name nitrogen, Lavoisier\'s root lives on in many languages—such as French, Italian, Russian, German (<i>Stickstoff</i>, meaning "suffocating matter"), and Dutch (<i>stikstof</i>)—as well as in chemical terms like hydrazine, azides, and the group name "pnictogens" (from Greek <i>pnigein</i>, "to choke").</p>
<p>The modern English word <i>nitrogen</i> (1794) entered the language from the French <i>nitrogène</i>, coined in 1790 by French chemist Jean-Antoine Chaptal from <i>nitre</i> (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a> nitrate) and the suffix <i>-gène</i> ("producing"), meaning "nitric acid producer." (Historically, nitre was sometimes confused with Egyptian <i>natron</i>, a <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a></a> carbonate mineral containing no nitrogen). In 1910, Lord Rayleigh discovered that passing an electrical discharge through nitrogen gas created "active nitrogen"—a reactive single-atom allotrope that produced a brilliant yellow cloud of light and reacted with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">mercury</a></a> to yield explosive <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">mercury</a> nitride.</p>
<p>Modern Indian chemistry made groundbreaking contributions to nitrogen science through Acharya Prafulla Chandra Ray (1861–1944), widely celebrated as the "Father of Indian Chemistry" and dubbed the "Master of Nitrites." In 1895–1896, Ray synthesized mercurous nitrite (Hg<sub>2</sub>(NO<sub>2</sub>)<sub>2</sub>), opening a whole new chapter in inorganic chemistry. In 1912, Ray achieved another major scientific milestone by isolating pure ammonium nitrite (NH<sub>4</sub>NO<sub>2</sub>), proving for the first time that it could be sublimed at 60°C without decomposing and disproving long-standing misconceptions in global chemistry. Ray also authored the landmark treatise <i>A History of Hindu Chemistry</i> (1902) and established Bengal Chemicals & Pharmaceuticals, India\'s first pharmaceutical company.</p>
<p>For centuries, human access to nitrogen compounds was strictly limited to natural manure and saltpetre deposits. This changed dramatically in the early 20th century with industrial nitrogen fixation techniques like the Frank–Caro process (1895–1899) and the Haber–Bosch process (1908–1913). Today, synthetic nitrogen fertilizers produced via the Haber process sustain roughly half of global food production. Concurrently, the Ostwald process (1902) allowed large-scale conversion of synthetic ammonia into nitric acid, providing the essential raw material for industrial explosives during the World Wars of the 20th century.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'Isotopes' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: Isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a></a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/d4da30989f947cf5db8f2348f03da869.png" decoding="async" width="580" height="121" style="--mw-file-upright: 2.3" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright"  data-file-width="1552" data-file-height="324" /><figcaption>Table of nuclides (Segrè chart) from <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a></a> to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/F" class="wiki-link" title="Fluorine (F)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/F" class="wiki-link" title="Fluorine (F)">fluorine</a></a> (including <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a></a>). Orange indicates proton emission (nuclides outside the proton drip line); pink for positron emission (inverse beta decay); black for stable nuclides; blue for electron emission (beta decay); and violet for neutron emission (nuclides outside the neutron drip line). Proton number increases going up the vertical axis and neutron number going to the right on the horizontal axis.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Natural <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a> is composed of two stable isotopes: nitrogen-14 (<sup>14</sup>N) and nitrogen-15 (<sup>15</sup>N). The lighter <sup>14</sup>N isotope is overwhelmingly dominant, making up 99.634% of naturally occurring nitrogen, while the slightly heavier <sup>15</sup>N makes up the remaining 0.366%, resulting in an average atomic weight of approximately 14.007 u. Both isotopes are forged inside stars during the CNO (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">Carbon</a>-Nitrogen-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">Oxygen</a>) fusion cycle, but <sup>14</sup>N is far more abundant because its formation step involves slow proton capture, causing it to accumulate in greater quantities. Notably, <sup>14</sup>N is one of only five stable "odd–odd" atomic nuclei in nature (containing an odd number of both protons and neutrons), alongside deuterium (<sup>2</sup>H), <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a>-6 (<sup>6</sup>Li), <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a>-10 (<sup>10</sup>B), and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ta" class="wiki-link" title="Tantalum (Ta)">tantalum</a>-180m (<sup>180m</sup>Ta).</p>
<p>While the ratio of <sup>14</sup>N to <sup>15</sup>N remains remarkably constant in the open air, it varies in soils, rocks, and water due to isotopic fractionation caused by biological processes (such as nitrogen uptake, nitrification, and denitrification) and chemical evaporation. Biological reactions naturally favor the lighter <sup>14</sup>N isotope for chemical reactions, leaving the remaining soil or water substrate enriched in <sup>15</sup>N.</p>
<p>In modern scientific research, isotope ratio analysis—expressed as <i>δ</i><sup>15</sup>N—is a powerful tool across ecology, geology, and climate science. Indian researchers at premier institutions like the Indian Institute of Science (IISc), the Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR), and national oceanographic centers extensively analyze <i>δ</i><sup>15</sup>N signatures in monsoonal ocean sediment cores, Himalayan ice proxies, and agricultural watersheds. These isotopic ratio studies help reconstruct ancient Indian monsoonal climate variations, trace agricultural nitrogen runoff in major river basins, and evaluate soil health across rural landscapes.</p>
<p>The heavy isotope <sup>15</sup>N was discovered in 1929 by South African physicist S. M. Naudé. Because <sup>15</sup>N has a fractional nuclear spin of one-half, it produces sharp, narrow spectral lines in Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR) spectroscopy, making it exceptionally valuable for determining the intricate structures of proteins and complex organic molecules. In contrast, <sup>14</sup>N has an integer nuclear spin of one, creating an electric quadrupole moment that causes broader, less defined NMR spectra. Although <sup>15</sup>N NMR faces sensitivity challenges due to its low natural abundance (0.366%) and low gyromagnetic ratio (only 10.14% that of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a></a>-1, making <sup>1</sup>H signal-to-noise about 300 times stronger), scientists overcome this by enriching samples with <sup>15</sup>N via chemical exchange or fractional distillation. Crucially, <sup>15</sup>N-labeled compounds do not easily swap their nitrogen atoms with the surrounding air, keeping labeled biological samples stable during experiments.</p>
<p>Beyond its two stable forms, scientists have artificially created 13 radioactive isotopes of nitrogen, ranging from <sup>9</sup>N to <sup>23</sup>N. Most of these radioisotopes decay in a fraction of a second, but nitrogen-13 (<sup>13</sup>N) has a half-life of about 10 minutes. This makes <sup>13</sup>N indispensable in medicine for Positron Emission Tomography (PET) scanning, where it is generated on-site using medical cyclotrons (by bombarding <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a>-16 with protons) to map blood flow and metabolic activity in real time.</p>
<p>In nuclear power engineering, nitrogen-16 (<sup>16</sup>N) plays a critical safety role. Formed inside pressurized and boiling water nuclear reactors when fast neutrons hit <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a>-16 in cooling water via an (n,p) reaction, <sup>16</sup>N has a brief half-life of 7.1 seconds. However, as it decays back to <sup>16</sup>O, it emits intensely powerful gamma rays (5 to 7 MeV). This requires heavy radiation shielding around primary cooling pipes during reactor operation and acts as an ultra-sensitive, real-time indicator for detecting tiny leaks between primary coolant pipes and secondary steam cycles.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Occurrence' => '<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/8ff0c78907028408a980966c2edd18ad.png" decoding="async" width="450" height="338" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.8" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright"  data-file-width="800" data-file-height="600" /><figcaption>Schematic representation of the flow of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a></a> compounds through a land environment</figcaption></figure>
<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">See also: <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">Nitrogen</a></a> cycle</div>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">Nitrogen</a></a> is the most abundant uncombined element on Earth, making up 78.1% of our atmosphere by volume (75.5% by mass)—a colossal reservoir weighing roughly 3.89 million gigatonnes (3.89×10<sup>18</sup> kg). In contrast, nitrogen is relatively sparse in Earth\'s solid crust, averaging just 19 parts per million (similar in abundance to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Nb" class="wiki-link" title="Niobium (Nb)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Nb" class="wiki-link" title="Niobium (Nb)">niobium</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a></a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a></a>). Natural nitrate minerals are rare, existing primarily as nitre (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a> nitrate, saltpetre) and soda nitre (Chilean saltpetre, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a> nitrate), though mining these minerals was largely rendered obsolete by synthetic chemical processes in the 1920s.</p>
<p>Earth\'s biosphere relies on a continuous global nitrogen cycle. Although nitrogen gas is all around us, plants cannot absorb atmospheric N<sub>2</sub> directly because its double-atom triple bond is extremely tough to break. To become biologically active, nitrogen must be "fixed" into reactive compounds like ammonia. Nature accomplishes this through high-energy lightning strikes—which combine nitrogen and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> into nitrogen oxides—and primarily through specialized diazotrophic soil bacteria that use enzymes called nitrogenases.</p>
<p>In traditional Indian farming practices, sustainable nitrogen fixation has been practiced for millennia through legume crop rotation (such as growing pulses like chickpea, pigeon pea/arhar, and lentils). These crops host symbiotic <i>Rhizobium</i> bacteria in their root nodules, naturally enriching the soil with bio-available nitrogen. Today, the Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) promotes bio-fertilizers to harness these natural microbial allies.</p>
<p>Once fixed, plants absorb ammonia and nitrogen compounds to synthesize essential proteins. Animals eat these plants to construct their own proteins and eventually excrete nitrogenous waste (such as urea and uric acid). When organisms die, decomposers break down their biological matter, releasing nitrogen back into the environment where denitrifying bacteria complete the cycle by converting nitrates back into N<sub>2</sub> gas.</p>
<p>However, human industrial nitrogen fixation (via synthetic urea fertilizers) now rivals natural biological fixation. Overuse of chemical nitrogen fertilizers can cause excessive agricultural runoff into rivers and lakes, triggering eutrophication and severe aquatic "dead zones" where <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> depletion suffocates fish. Furthermore, denitrification releases nitrous oxide (N<sub>2</sub>O)—a greenhouse gas nearly 300 times more potent than <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> dioxide that also degrades the atmospheric ozone layer. To solve this ecological challenge in Indian agriculture, scientists at IFFCO developed revolutionary <b>Liquid Nano Urea</b>. By delivering nanoscale nitrogen particles directly onto crop leaves via foliar spray, Nano Urea boosts nutrient uptake efficiency, drastically reducing chemical leaching into groundwater and major river systems like the Ganga and Yamuna.</p>
<p>Nitrogen chemistry also governs fascinating animal adaptations. Deep-sea saltwater fish produce large amounts of trimethylamine oxide to balance osmotic pressure under intense ocean depth; its conversion into dimethylamine creates the characteristic scent of aging seafood. In mammals, tiny amounts of free-radical nitric oxide (NO) serve as a crucial chemical messenger that relaxes blood vessels and regulates blood circulation. As proteins decay after death, biological enzymes break down amino acids (like ornithine and lysine) into long-chain nitrogen-containing amines called putrescine and cadaverine, which produce the characteristic odor of biological decay.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'Applications' => '<p>Industrial applications of elemental <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a></a> are vast and essential to modern manufacturing and technology. Globally, about two-thirds (2/3) of manufactured <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a> gas is sold in gaseous form, while the remaining one-third (1/3) is distributed as a super-chilled liquid.</p>
<p>As a gas, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a>\'s unreactive, inert nature makes it the ultimate shield against fire, explosion, and decay. Industrial facilities use nitrogen gas to purge fuel tanks, blanket sensitive chemical reactions, and protect delicate microchips during semiconductor fabrication. In the food industry, modified atmosphere packaging (MAP) replaces reactive <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> gas with pure nitrogen in snack bags and food containers, preventing food spoilage, oil rancidity, and stale textures without artificial preservatives.</p>
<p>In its liquid state—boiling at a frigid −196°C (77.36 K)—liquid nitrogen (LN<sub>2</sub>) is a powerhouse of cryogenics. It is widely used for flash-freezing foods, medical cryosurgery (removing skin lesions), and cryo-preserving biological materials such as blood, stem cells, reproductive cells, and genetic banks. Liquid nitrogen also cools high-field superconducting electromagnets used in magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) body scanners and particle physics laboratories.</p>
<p>In India\'s high-tech and space sectors, nitrogen plays an indispensable role. The Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO) uses massive liquid nitrogen systems at facilities like the UR Rao Satellite Centre (URSC) in Bengaluru and the Satish Dhawan Space Centre (SDSC) in Sriharikota. ISRO employs liquid nitrogen inside giant thermal vacuum chambers to simulate the brutal cryogenic cold of outer space, rigorously testing Indian satellites and rocket engine systems before blastoff. On the agricultural and industrial front, massive fertilizer complexes across India (such as IFFCO, KRIBHCO, and National Fertilizers Limited) process atmospheric nitrogen into millions of tonnes of urea to guarantee food security for 1.4 billion people. Looking to the future, under the National Green <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">Hydrogen</a> Mission, India is pioneering <b>Green Ammonia</b> production—using solar and wind energy to harvest atmospheric nitrogen and convert it into zero-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> fertilizers and green maritime fuels.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
