    'name' => 'Oxygen',
    'atomic' => 8,
    'category' => 'diatomic nonmetal',
    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 8 (O)',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">Oxygen</a></b> (symbol <b>O</b>, atomic number 8) is the breath of life itself! Sitting in Group 16 (the chalcogens) of the periodic table, this non-metallic gas is famously reactive, eagerly pairing up with almost every other element to form compounds called oxides. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">Oxygen</a> is the most abundant element in Earth\'s crust, making up nearly half (49.2%) of its total weight in the form of water, silicates, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> dioxide, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> ores. Across the cosmos, it reigns as the third most abundant element in the universe, right after <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a>. On Earth, it makes up about 20.8% of the air we breathe. In ancient Indian philosophy, this invisible, life-giving gas was described as <i>Pranavayu</i> (प्राणवायु)—the vital breath that kindles our internal metabolic fire and sustains every living soul.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a0/Liquid_oxygen_in_a_beaker_%28cropped_and_retouched%29.jpg',
