    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p>As an alkaline earth metal in Group 2 of the periodic table, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a></a> has an atomic number of 12 and a ground-state electron configuration of 1s<sup>2</sup> 2s<sup>2</sup> 2p<sup>6</sup> 3s<sup>2</sup>. It possesses two valence electrons in its outermost shell, which it easily gives up to achieve a stable, noble-gas electronic structure. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">Magnesium</a> is a shiny, silvery-gray solid that bears a striking physical resemblance to its five group siblings—<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">beryllium</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sr" class="wiki-link" title="Strontium (Sr)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sr" class="wiki-link" title="Strontium (Sr)">strontium</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ba" class="wiki-link" title="Barium (Ba)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ba" class="wiki-link" title="Barium (Ba)">barium</a></a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ra" class="wiki-link" title="Radium (Ra)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ra" class="wiki-link" title="Radium (Ra)">radium</a></a>. Because of its high chemical reactivity, pure metallic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a> never exists naturally in its uncombined elemental state on Earth; instead, it is found locked inside rock minerals, ocean brines, and living organisms.</p><p>Magnesium is famous for being remarkably lightweight—with a density of only 1.738 g/cm³, it is just two-thirds the density of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminium (Al)">aluminium</a>. Despite its light weight, magnesium forms exceptionally strong, durable structural alloys when combined with small amounts of other metals, making it a cornerstone element for lightweight engineering in modern transportation, consumer electronics, and space exploration.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Physical properties' => '<p>Elemental <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a></a> is a gray-white metal with an excellent strength-to-weight ratio. It boasts the lowest melting point (923 K or 650 °C) and the lowest boiling point (1,363 K or 1,090 °C) among all the alkaline earth metals. Its low melting point makes it relatively easy to cast and shape into intricate mechanical parts.</p><p>In its pure polycrystalline form, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a> is somewhat brittle and tends to fracture easily along mechanical shear bands under stress. However, its malleability and ductility can be dramatically improved in two ways: by alloying it with tiny amounts of other elements (such as just 1% <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminium (Al)">aluminium</a>) or by refining its microscopic crystal grain size down to about 1 micrometre (μm) or less. These grain-refinement techniques allow engineers to transform brittle raw <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a> into flexible, high-toughness sheets and structural components.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Chemical properties' => '<p>The chemical behavior of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a></a> is defined by its willingness to donate its two 3s outer electrons, forming stable divalent cations (Mg<sup>2+</sup>). It has a Pauling electronegativity of 1.31, with a first ionization energy of 737.7 kJ/mol and a second ionization energy of 1450.7 kJ/mol. Exposed to air at room temperature, solid <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a> tarnishes slightly as it forms a thin, protective surface film of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a> oxide (MgO) that passivates the metal and slows down further atmospheric corrosion.</p><p>When heated in air, magnesium ignites readily and burns with an intensely dazzling, brilliant white flame that reaches temperatures around 3,100 °C (5,610 °F). This intense combustion converts the metal into white magnesium oxide powder (MgO) alongside a small amount of magnesium nitride (Mg<sub>3</sub>N<sub>2</sub>). Magnesium burns so aggressively with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> that it can even continue burning inside an atmosphere of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>), stripping <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> atoms from CO<sub>2</sub> molecules to leave behind solid <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> soot and magnesium oxide! Because of this property, conventional water or CO<sub>2</sub> fire extinguishers cannot extinguish a magnesium fire; specialized Class D dry powder extinguishers or dry sand must be used instead.</p><p>Magnesium reacts slowly with cold water, but reacts rapidly with boiling water or steam to release bubbles of flammable <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a></a> gas and form magnesium hydroxide: Mg + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O → Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>. It also dissolves vigorously in dilute aqueous acids (such as hydrochloric acid), rapidly producing magnesium salts and abundant <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> gas. Magnesium hydroxide, Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub> (famously known as Milk of Magnesia), is poorly soluble in water and acts as a mild, non-toxic alkaline base.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Isotopes' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: Isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a></a></div>
<p>Natural <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a></a> is composed of three stable isotopes: <sup>24</sup>Mg, <sup>25</sup>Mg, and <sup>26</sup>Mg. All three are present in substantial quantities in nature. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">Magnesium</a>-24 is the most abundant by far, making up about 78.99% (roughly 79%) of natural magnesium, while <sup>25</sup>Mg accounts for 10.00% and <sup>26</sup>Mg makes up 11.01%. Among synthetic radioisotopes, <sup>28</sup>Mg is noteworthy; produced in nuclear reactors from the 1950s through the 1970s for biological and physical experiments, its short half-life of 21 hours meant its scientific utility was severely restricted by rapid shipping decay.</p><p>The stable isotope <sup>26</sup>Mg plays a pivotal role in planetary science and isotopic geology. It is the radiogenic daughter product of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a>-26 (<sup>26</sup>Al), a extinct radioactive isotope that decays with a half-life of 717,000 years. Geochemists have discovered excessive, anomalous amounts of stable <sup>26</sup>Mg inside <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">Calcium</a>-Aluminium-rich inclusions (CAIs) found within carbonaceous chondrite meteorites. Scientists concluded that these meteorites condensed in the early solar nebula before the parent <sup>26</sup>Al had time to decay. Because CAIs represent the oldest solid objects in our Solar System (~4.567 billion years old), tracking their <sup>26</sup>Mg abundance offers an invaluable cosmic clock to reconstruct the earliest high-temperature events in solar system history.</p><p>In isotopic geology, researchers plot the ratio of <sup>26</sup>Mg/<sup>24</sup>Mg against the <sup>27</sup>Al/<sup>24</sup>Mg ratio on an isochron plot. While the slope of this isochron line does not directly measure elapsed time, it precisely reveals the initial ratio of <sup>26</sup>Al/<sup>27</sup>Al present in the original solar reservoir when the meteorite system sealed itself off.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Occurrence' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable selfref">See also: Category:<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">Magnesium</a></a> minerals</div>
<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">See also: Boninite</div>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">Magnesium</a></a> is the ninth most abundant element in the observable universe by mass, created during fusion reactions in massive stars. On Earth, it is the eighth most abundant element in the crust by mass (constituting about 2.1% of the crust) and is tied in seventh place with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a> in terms of molar abundance. Although <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a> is present in over 60 distinct minerals, only six are of major commercial importance: magnesite (MgCO<sub>3</sub>), dolomite (CaMg(CO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>), brucite (Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub>), carnallite (KMgCl<sub>3</sub>·6H<sub>2</sub>O), talc (Mg<sub>3</sub>Si<sub>4</sub>O<sub>10</sub>(OH)<sub>2</sub>), and olivine ((Mg,Fe)<sub>2</sub>SiO<sub>4</sub>). It is also found in boninite igneous rocks and natural mineral springs.</p><p>Dissolved magnesium ions (Mg<sup>2+</sup>) represent the second most abundant positive ion in Earth\'s oceans (after <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a></a>), with a concentration of about 1.3 g/kg—roughly one-eighth the mass of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a> ions in seawater. This vast oceanic reservoir, alongside natural salt lakes and subterranean brine wells, makes seawater one of the most attractive and sustainable commercial sources for extracting pure magnesium metal through electrolysis.</p><p>Geographically, India possesses world-renowned reserves of high-grade magnesite and dolomite. The most famous Indian deposit is located in the **Salem district of Tamil Nadu** (the famous "Chalk Hills"), earning Salem the historical distinction of being India\'s **"Steel and Magnesite City."** Commercial magnesite mining began here in 1890 under Henry Turner, establishing the foundation for India\'s high-temperature refractory brick industry. Significant magnesite belts also stretch across the Himalayan terrain of **Uttarakhand** (in Almora and Pithoragarh districts), Karnataka (Mysore), and Rajasthan, while vast dolomite formations across Chhattisgarh, Odisha, and Madhya Pradesh supply essential fluxes to India\'s major steel plants.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'History' => '<p>The name <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a></a> originates from the ancient Greek region of Magnesia (either in Thessaly or Magnesia ad Sipylum, now in modern Turkey), named after the Magnetes tribe. The region yielded three distinct minerals that took centuries to differentiate: magnetite (magnetic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> ore), pyrolusite (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a></a> dioxide), and magnesia (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a> oxide).</p><p>In 1618, a farmer named Henry Wicker at Epsom in Surrey, England, attempted to water his cows from a local well during a drought. The cows refused to drink the bitter-tasting water, but Wicker observed that the mineral water remarkably accelerated the healing of cuts, rashes, and scratches on skin. Upon evaporating the spring water, he obtained white mineral crystals known worldwide as "Epsom salts"—eventually identified as hydrated <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a> sulfate, MgSO<sub>4</sub>·7H<sub>2</sub>O. Epsom salts quickly gained international fame as a medicinal laxative and soothing bath salt.</p><p>Pure metallic magnesium was first isolated in 1808 by Sir Humphry Davy in London. Davy performed electrolysis on a moist paste of magnesia (MgO) and mercuric oxide (HgO) using a powerful battery, forming a magnesium-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">mercury</a> amalgam from which he distilled away the <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">mercury</a>. Davy originally suggested the name <i>magnium</i>, but <i>magnesium</i> was ultimately adopted. In 1831, French chemist Antoine Bussy prepared the metal in coherent metallic form by heating anhydrous magnesium chloride with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a> vapor. Later, the father of physical chemistry in Imperial Russia, Nikolai Beketov (1827–1911), proved that magnesium and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a></a> displace other metals from their salt solutions at high temperatures, establishing early metal reactivity concepts.</p><p>In ancient Indian history and classical Ayurvedic texts on <i>Rasashastra</i> (iatrochemistry), magnesium minerals were recognized and utilized for their therapeutic properties centuries before Western chemistry isolated the pure metal. Magnesite (magnesium carbonate) was known in South Indian tradition as <b><i>Garudapacha</i></b> (or <i>Palgarudapacha</i>) and was formulated to treat bone loss (<i>asthishosha</i>), bodily weakness, and eye afflictions. Hydrous magnesium silicate (talc) was studied as <b><i>Nagapashana</i></b>; through traditional <i>Shodhana</i> (purification) and <i>Marana</i> (calcination), ancient practitioners transformed these minerals into bioavailable <i>Bhasmas</i> used as cardiac tonics and tissue restorers. Furthermore, natural bitter mineral waters (*Khar*) were prescribed in Ayurvedic medicine for their gentle digestive and laxative benefits.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Uses' => '<p>As the lightest structural metal available, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a></a> is vital across modern metallurgy, aerospace, consumer electronics, and green energy. When alloyed with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminium (Al)">aluminium</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a></a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a> creates ultra-light, high-strength alloys used in aircraft structures, automotive engine blocks, transmission cases, steering wheels, laptop casings, and high-performance power tools. In heavy industry, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a> acts as a desulfurizer and deoxidizer in steelmaking, and serves as a reducing agent in the Kroll process to extract pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zr" class="wiki-link" title="Zirconium (Zr)">zirconium</a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hf" class="wiki-link" title="Hafnium (Hf)">hafnium</a> from their chloride ores. Attached to underground steel pipelines, water heaters, and ship hulls, magnesium blocks act as sacrificial anodes, corroding preferentially to protect steel structures from rust.</p><p>Because magnesium burns with an intensely brilliant white light, powdered magnesium is used in emergency signal flares, military tracer ammunition, fireworks, theatrical pyrotechnics, and vintage camera flashbulbs. In refractory applications, calcined magnesite (MgO) is crafted into heat-resistant bricks that line steel-smelting furnaces capable of enduring temperatures above 2,000 °C.</p><p>Biologically, magnesium is indispensable to life on Earth. It sits at the exact center of the <b>chlorophyll</b> molecule—the green pigment in plants that absorbs solar energy to power photosynthesis, producing the <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a> we breathe and the food we eat. In humans, the Mg<sup>2+</sup> ion is essential for over 300 enzymatic reactions, regulating ATP energy transfer, DNA synthesis, nerve impulse transmission, and muscle contraction. Milk of Magnesia, Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub>, is widely used as a gentle antacid and laxative, while Epsom salt baths soothe sore muscles.</p><p>In modern Indian science and technology, magnesium plays a crucial role across space, medicine, and clean energy. The <b>Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO)</b>, working alongside <b>CSIR-NIIST</b> (Thiruvananthapuram) and <b>CSIR-CECRI</b> (Karaikudi), has pioneered lightweight Aluminium-Magnesium-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)">Scandium</a> (Al-Mg-Sc) and specialized magnesium alloy castings. These advanced materials reduce structural weight in satellite frames, launch vehicles (PSLV and GSLV), and liquid propulsion engine systems (LPSC). At <b>IIT Madras</b>, researchers have engineered cutting-edge bioresorbable magnesium alloy orthopedic implants coated with nano-hydroxyapatite. These implants provide structural support for broken bones and then dissolve harmlessly inside the body as natural bone heals, eliminating the need for painful secondary removal surgeries. Furthermore, scientists at <b>IIT Madras</b> and <b>IISc Bangalore</b> are developing solid-state <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> storage systems using <b>magnesium hydride (MgH<sub>2</sub>)</b>, designing specialized heat-exchanger reactors to safely store green <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> for future clean energy grids. Finally, in the world-famous tea plantations of <b>Assam and the Nilgiris</b>, agricultural application of Epsom salt (MgSO<sub>4</sub>·7H<sub>2</sub>O) corrects soil magnesium deficiency, boosting chlorophyll production and enhancing the yield and flavor profile of Indian tea.</p>
<div style="clear:both;" class=""></div>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
    ),
  ),
  'Al' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Al',
    'name' => 'Aluminum',
    'atomic' => 13,
    'category' => 'post-transition metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">Aluminum</a></a></b> (symbol <b>Al</b>, atomic number 13) is the lightweight champion of the metal world. Sitting in Group 13 of the periodic table, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a> is a silvery-white, soft, non-magnetic, and remarkably flexible metal. It holds the proud title of being the third most abundant chemical element in Earth\'s crust—surpassed only by <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a></a>—and the single most abundant metal on our planet, making up roughly 8.1% of Earth\'s solid surface. Weighing only one-third as much as steel, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a> instantly forms a microscopic, self-healing shield of oxide when exposed to air, making it virtually immune to rust. From ancient Indian Ayurvedic remedies and vibrant textile dyes to high-speed trains, beverage cans, and ISRO space rockets, aluminum is an indispensable pillar of modern civilization.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/Aluminium.jpg',
