    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 14 (Si)',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">Silicon</a></a></b> (symbol <b>Si</b>, atomic number 14) is a shiny, dark blue-grey metalloid and semiconductor that forms the bedrock of both modern electronics and Earth\'s geology. Positioned in group 14 of the periodic table directly below <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a></a> and above <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ge" class="wiki-link" title="Germanium (Ge)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ge" class="wiki-link" title="Germanium (Ge)">germanium</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sn" class="wiki-link" title="Tin (Sn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sn" class="wiki-link" title="Tin (Sn)">tin</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">lead</a></a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fl" class="wiki-link" title="Flerovium (Fl)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fl" class="wiki-link" title="Flerovium (Fl)">flerovium</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a> is a tetravalent element that readily shares electrons to construct strong covalent networks. It is the second most abundant element in Earth\'s crust (27.2% by mass), surpassed only by <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a>. From ancient Harappan silica beads and sacred Indian <i>Sphatika</i> quartz crystals to structural plant cell walls and microprocessors powering global supercomputers, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a> spans the bridge between natural history and futuristic human technology.</p>',
