    'sections' => 
    array (
      'History' => '<p>Because <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a></a> is so abundant across the Earth\'s surface, natural <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a> compounds like quartz, sand, and silicate minerals have been shaped by human hands for thousands of years. Early civilizations recognized the durability and beauty of silica rock crystals. Predynastic Egyptians fashioned ornamental beads and small vases from quartz, while ancient Chinese artisans carved delicate vessels out of rock crystal. By around 1500 BC, Egyptian and Phoenician glassmakers had learned to fuse silica sand with alkali plant ash to manufacture translucent glass containers, while early builders combined natural silicate clays with lime to create durable construction mortars.</p>
<p>In ancient India, silica-based minerals formed the backbone of a sophisticated lapidary and metallurgical tradition. During the Indus Valley Civilization (2600–1900 BC), master craftsmen at sites such as Lothal, Chanhu-daro, and Rakhigarhi excelled in working silica stones including quartz, agate, jasper, and chalcedony. Harappan bead-makers developed an advanced heat-treatment technique—baking <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>-rich agates in specialized clay kilns to transform them into deep orange-red carnelian beads that were traded across Ancient Mesopotamia and Egypt. The Harappans also synthesized <i>faience</i>, an early artificial ceramic made by crushing quartz sand, mixing it with an alkali binder, and firing it at high temperatures to yield turquoise-glazed ornaments.</p>
<p>Pure, transparent quartz crystal holds a profound place in classical Indian literature, where it is known as <i>Sphatika</i> (स्फटिक). Mentioned in foundational texts such as the <i>Rigveda</i>, Varahamihira\'s <i>Brihat Samhita</i>, the <i>Garuda Purana</i>, and various Saiva Agamas, <i>Sphatika</i> was revered as a material of absolute clarity and divine energy. Associated with Lord Shiva in the form of carved <i>Sphatika Lingams</i> and with Goddess Lakshmi, quartz rosaries (<i>Sphatika malas</i>) were prized in traditional Indian medicine and spiritual practice for their soothing, cooling (<i>sheeta</i>) physical and mental properties. The town of Khambhat (Cambay) in Gujarat has maintained an unbroken four-thousand-year heritage as a world capital for lapidary agate and quartz craft.</p>
<p>In Western scientific history, 18th-century chemists suspected that silica (flint) was the oxide of an undiscovered element. Antoine Lavoisier listed silica among the elementary earths in 1789. In 1811, French chemists Joseph Louis Gay-Lussac and Louis Jacques Thénard produced crude, impure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a> by heating silicon tetrafluoride with metallic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a></a>. However, it was Swedish chemist Jöns Jacob Berzelius who isolated pure elemental silicon in 1824 by heating <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a> fluorosilicate with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a> metal and thoroughly washing away the residual fluorides. English chemist Thomas Thomson coined the name <i>silicon</i> in 1817, combining the Latin word <i>silex</i> (meaning \'flint\') with the suffix <i>-on</i> to highlight its chemical structural kinship with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a></a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a></a>.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Characteristics' => '<p>In its elemental form, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a></a> is a hard, brittle crystalline solid featuring a distinct dark blue-grey metallic lustre. It crystallizes in a face-centered diamond cubic lattice structure—identical to that of diamond—where each <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a> atom is bonded covalently to four neighboring atoms in a rigid tetrahedral symmetry. Pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a> has a density of 2.329 g/cm³, a high melting point of 1687 K (1414 °C), and a boiling point of 3538 K (3265 °C). Notably, silicon exhibits an unusual physical density anomaly shared with water: it expands by approximately 9% as it transitions from a liquid state into a solid crystal lattice upon freezing.</p>
<p>Electrically, silicon is a classic semiconductor with an indirect energy bandgap of 1.12 electron-volts (eV) at room temperature. At absolute zero temperature, pure silicon acts as a complete electrical insulator because all of its valence electrons are locked tight within covalent bonds. As the temperature rises, ambient thermal energy excites a small fraction of electrons across the bandgap into the conduction band, allowing electrical current to flow. The electrical conductivity of silicon can be precisely engineered over several orders of magnitude through a process called <i>doping</i>—deliberately introducing microscopic trace amounts of electron-rich elements like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a></a> (to create n-type semiconductors) or electron-deficient elements like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a></a> (to create p-type semiconductors).</p>
<p>Chemically, silicon is relatively unreactive at ambient room temperatures. When exposed to air, its surface immediately reacts with atmospheric <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a> to construct a transparent, microscopically thin layer of silicon dioxide (SiO₂). This passive oxide skin shields the underlying bulk crystal from further corrosion or chemical attack. Silicon does not react with water or most acids under normal conditions, though it dissolves readily in hot concentrated aqueous alkalis (such as <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a> hydroxide) to produce silicates and release <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a></a> gas. At elevated temperatures (> 900 °C), silicon reacts vigorously with halogens (forming volatile tetrahalides like SiF₄ and SiCl₄), burns in <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> to yield silica, and reacts with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> to form high-strength ceramics like silicon nitride (Si₃N₄) and silicon carbide (SiC).</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Isotopes' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: Isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a></a></div>
<p>Naturally occurring <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a></a> consists of three stable isotopes: <sup>28</sup>Si (making up 92.24% of natural abundance), <sup>29</sup>Si (4.67%), and <sup>30</sup>Si (3.07%). Among these stable forms, <sup>28</sup>Si is the most dominant because it is created directly during stellar fusion through the alpha process (sequential addition of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a> nuclei). The odd-mass isotope <sup>29</sup>Si possesses a nuclear spin of I = 1/2, making it the only natural <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a> isotope usable in Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR) and Electron Paramagnetic Resonance (EPR) spectroscopy to analyze molecular structures in chemistry and material science.</p>
<p>In cosmic astrophysics, <sup>28</sup>Si plays a central role in stellar evolution through the <i>silicon-burning process</i>. During the final, violent stages of nuclear fusion inside massive stars (greater than 8 to 11 times the mass of our Sun), extreme temperatures exceed 3 billion Kelvin. Under these conditions, photodisintegration breaks down nuclei while alpha particles fuse with <sup>28</sup>Si to synthesize heavier elements such as <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a></a>, and ultimately <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a></a>-56 (which decays into <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a>-56). The silicon-burning phase represents the ultimate nuclear fuel stage of a star before its core collapses, resulting in a cataclysmic Type II supernova explosion.</p>
<p>Scientists have characterized 22 radioactive isotopes of silicon, spanning mass numbers from 22 to 45 (with ultrashort-lived <sup>46</sup>Si observed in laboratory collisions of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a>-70 with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Be" class="wiki-link" title="Beryllium (Be)">beryllium</a> at particle accelerator facilities). The two longest-lived radioisotopes are <sup>32</sup>Si, with a half-life of roughly 157 years, and <sup>31</sup>Si, with a half-life of 2.62 hours. All other radioisotopes have half-lives shorter than seven seconds, with the vast majority decaying in less than one-tenth of a second. <sup>32</sup>Si undergoes low-energy beta decay into <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a></a>-32 and eventually stable <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a></a>-32. <sup>31</sup>Si is easily generated by exposing natural silicon to neutron bombardment in nuclear reactors, making it a valuable tracer in quantitative activation analysis as it decays via beta emission carrying up to 1.48 MeV of energy to stable <sup>31</sup>P.</p>
<p>Silicon isotopes serve as valuable tools in oceanography and climate science. Dissolved silicon enters the oceans through river transport, continental rock weathering, aeolian windblown dust, and deep hydrothermal vents. Because river inputs and deep groundwater flows have distinct <sup>30</sup>Si to <sup>28</sup>Si isotopic signatures, oceanographers trace variations in marine <sup>30</sup>Si levels to reconstruct ancient ocean circulation currents and biological productivity. A distinct deep-water <sup>30</sup>Si gradient of over 0.3 parts per thousand exists between the Atlantic and Pacific ocean basins, reflecting the long-term biological removal of silicon by marine plankton.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Occurrence' => '<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/39de794f99d69d9ba4e2e332d2457efd.jpg" decoding="async" width="190" height="232" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright"  data-file-width="327" data-file-height="400" /><figcaption>Olivine</figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">Silicon</a></a> is the eighth most abundant element in the cosmos by mass, behind <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ne" class="wiki-link" title="Neon (Ne)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ne" class="wiki-link" title="Neon (Ne)">neon</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a></a>. On Earth, however, planetary differentiation concentrated <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a> heavily in the outer rocky layers. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">Silicon</a> makes up 27.2% of Earth\'s crust by weight, ranking second only to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> (45.5%), with which it is almost universally bound as silica (SiO₂) or complex silicate minerals. Earth\'s metallic core (comprising ~31.5% of the planet\'s mass) is composed mostly of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a> alloys (Fe₂₅Ni₂Co₀.₁S₃), whereas the thick mantle (68.1% of Earth\'s mass) is rich in dense <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a>-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> silicates such as olivine, (Mg,Fe)₂SiO₄. Lighter siliceous minerals floated to the surface to create the crust (0.4% of Earth\'s mass).</p>
<p>As molten magma cools and crystallizes within the crust, silicate minerals form in a predictable sequence known as Bowen\'s reaction series. Dense olivine crystallizes first, followed progressively by pyroxene, amphibole, biotite mica, orthoclase feldspar, muscovite mica, and finally quartz. This sequence reflects a trend toward increasingly complex, interconnected silicon-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> tetrahedra. Over geological timescales, rain and atmosphere weather these igneous rocks, producing sedimentary deposits of clay, shale, and sandstone, while regional metamorphism under heat and pressure creates an immense array of crystalline metamorphic minerals.</p>
<p>Silicon continuously cycles between land and sea through four major oceanic fluxes: continental weathering, riverine transport, dissolution of windborne terrigenous silicates, and hydrothermal reactions along submarine mid-ocean basalts. Every year, windstorms lift between 300 and 900 million tonnes of aeolian dust into Earth\'s atmosphere, delivering 80 to 240 million tonnes of particulate silicon into the oceans. Massive dust plumes from the Sahara and Gobi deserts supply critical silicon inputs into the North Atlantic and Pacific Oceans respectively, fueling marine biological growth.</p>
<p>India is endowed with vast high-grade silica sand and quartz reserves distributed across major geological formations. The state of <b>Rajasthan</b> (with extensive mining belts in Ajmer, Bhilwara, Udaipur, and Alwar) is a leading domestic producer of high-purity quartz and quartzite. <b>Gujarat</b> holds immense silica sand deposits in Kutch and Bharuch, supplying raw materials for chemical and glass manufacturing. High-purity silica sand is also mined extensively in Prakasam and Nellore in Andhra Pradesh, while <b>Sankargarh</b> near Prayagraj (Uttar Pradesh) stands as one of India\'s historically premier hubs for glass-grade silica sand, powering the glass, ceramic, and construction industries of South Asia.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Applications' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">Silicon</a></a> is the indispensable foundation of modern microelectronics and digital computing. Ultra-pure monocrystalline <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a>—refined to an astounding \'nine-nines\' purity (99.9999999%) using the Czochralski crystal growth process—is sliced into paper-thin semiconductor wafers. These wafers are etched to create billions of microscopic transistors, microprocessors, memory chips, integrated circuits, and light-emitting diodes that power smartphones, computers, industrial robotics, and internet infrastructure. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">Silicon</a> solar photovoltaic (PV) cells convert sunlight directly into clean electricity, driving global renewable energy transformation.</p>
<p>Beyond electronics, silicon is essential in heavy metallurgy and chemical manufacturing. Elemental silicon is alloyed with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a> to produce ferrosilicon, which acts as a powerful deoxidizer and strengthening agent in steelmaking. Silicon-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a> alloys (such as Silumin) exhibit exceptional fluid castability and wear resistance, making them ideal for automotive engine blocks, cylinder heads, and aerospace structures. In organic chemistry, synthetic silicon-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> polymers called <i>silicones</i> yield versatile, heat-resistant sealants, lubricants, medical implants, flexible cookware, and cosmetics.</p>
<p>Silica sand and silicates remain fundamental to global infrastructure. Pure quartz sand is melted to make container glass, flat window glass, and ultra-transparent optical fiber cables for high-speed telecommunications. Silicate minerals are essential raw materials for clay pottery, fine porcelain, bricks, and Portland cement—the primary binder in concrete. Industrial synthetic silicon compounds like silicon carbide (carborundum, SiC) serve as super-hard abrasives, cutting tools, bulletproof ceramic armor, and high-power high-temperature semiconductor components.</p>
<p>India is currently advancing its technological leadership through the national <b>India Semiconductor Mission (ISM)</b>, an ambitious government initiative designed to establish commercial silicon wafer fabrication facilities, compound semiconductor foundries, and advanced chip packaging (OSAT) hubs nationwide. India\'s premier scientific institutions contribute significantly to space-grade and defense microelectronics: the <b>Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO)</b>, through its Semiconductor Laboratory (SCL Mohali), fabricates indigenous space-hardened microprocessors like the 32-bit <i>Vikram</i> processor and collaborates with IIT Madras to develop the <i>IRIS</i> RISC-V controller chip for satellite navigation and launch vehicles. Meanwhile, researchers at the <b>Indian Institute of Science (IISc, Bengaluru)</b> <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">lead</a> world-class innovation in silicon photonics, silicon-on-insulator (SOI) microchips, and on-chip electrostatic discharge (ESD) protection for next-generation global microelectronics.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Biological role' => '<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/249d6d32cf723b1a83d4ac06e47996c2.jpg" decoding="async" width="190" height="190" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright"  data-file-width="1600" data-file-height="1600" /><figcaption>A diatom, enclosed in a silica cell wall</figcaption></figure>
<p>Although <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a></a> is abundant in rocks and sand, very few organisms can metabolize it directly. Marine organisms such as diatoms, radiolarians, and siliceous glass sponges extract soluble orthosilicic acid (H₄SiO₄) from seawater to construct intricate, jewel-like microscopic cell walls and skeletons composed of biogenic amorphous silica (SiO₂·nH₂O). Diatoms alone generate over 20% of Earth\'s <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> and form the biological foundation of marine food webs; when they die, their silica frustules sink to the ocean seabed, creating thick deposits of diatomaceous earth used industrially in filtration, agriculture, and thermal insulation.</p>
<p>In terrestrial biology, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a> is a vital beneficial nutrient for many plants, particularly monocots like grasses, sugarcane, bamboo, and rice. Roots absorb orthosilicic acid from soil water and transport it through xylem vessels. Within plant tissues, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a> precipitates into rigid cell wall complexes and microscopic opal deposits known as <i>phytoliths</i>. This structural reinforcement stiffens plant stems, keeping leaves positioned to capture sunlight and preventing crop lodging (falling over during storms). Silicon also functions as an active biochemical defense: high silica content wears down the mouthparts of herbivorous insects, creates a physical barrier against fungal pathogens like rice blast disease (<i>Magnaporthe oryzae</i>), and triggers plant defense signaling pathways that produce protective phytoalexins and volatile organic compounds.</p>
<p>In Indian agriculture, silicon plays an indispensable role in maintaining paddy (rice) crop productivity. India is the world\'s second-largest producer of rice, a crop that absorbs more silicon than almost any other cultivated plant. Agricultural research by the Indian Agricultural Research Institute (IARI) and ICAR has demonstrated that applying silicon fertilizers to Indian paddy soils dramatically increases grain yield, enhances climate resilience against severe monsoon winds, reduces dependence on chemical fungicides, and neutralizes soil toxicities caused by excess heavy metals like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminium (Al)">aluminium</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/As" class="wiki-link" title="Arsenic (As)">arsenic</a>.</p>
<p>In human health, trace amounts of silicon are necessary for optimal bone mineralization, cartilage synthesis, and maintaining structural elasticity in skin, hair, nails, and blood vessel walls. In astrobiology, silicon is widely studied as the most plausible alternative to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a></a> for hypothetical extraterrestrial life. Because silicon sits directly below <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> in group 14, it shares the ability to form four covalent bonds and create complex chains and rings (such as silanes and silicones), offering a theoretical chemical framework for life under extreme extra-terrestrial environments.</p>',
    ),
  ),
