    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">Phosphorus</a></a></b> (symbol <b>P</b>, atomic number 15) is a highly reactive polyatomic nonmetal essential to all living things. Named from the Greek word for "light-bearer," <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a> glows faintly in the dark when exposed to air due to slow oxidation. Because it reacts so readily with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> and other elements, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a> is never found as a free element on Earth; instead, it is mined from abundant phosphate rocks. Phosphorus exists in several distinct forms (allotropes), including hyper-reactive white phosphorus, stable red phosphorus used in safety matches, and semiconducting black phosphorus. From forming the structural backbone of DNA and powering cellular energy to nourishing agricultural crops worldwide, phosphorus is a fundamental building block of life.</p>',
