    array (
      'History' => '<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/abc53f207a48982969e0a6955319a7e9.jpg" decoding="async" width="190" height="246" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" data-file-width="4724" data-file-height="6126" /><figcaption><i>The Alchemist in Search of the Philosophers Stone</i> (1771), by Joseph Wright, depicting Hennig Brand discovering <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a></a>.</figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">Phosphorus</a></a> holds a special place in chemical history as the very first element discovered whose existence was unknown to ancient civilizations. Its discovery is credited to the Hamburg alchemist Hennig Brand in 1669. Brand was attempting to create the legendary Philosopher\'s Stone—a mythical substance believed to turn base metals into <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)">gold</a>. Reasoning that human urine contained secret life-giving properties, he collected large quantities, allowed it to rot, boiled it down to a thick paste, and distilled it at intense heat. When he cooled the resulting vapors under water, he obtained a white, waxy substance that glowed mysteriously in the dark and burst into brilliant white flames when exposed to air. He named it <i><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a> mirabilis</i>, Latin for \'miraculous bearer of light\'. The word itself traces back to Ancient Greek <i>Phōsphoros</i> (\'light-bearer\'), which originally referred to the god of the morning star, the planet Venus.</p>
<p>Brand kept his recipe secret at first, but later sold it for 200 thalers to Johann Daniel Kraft of Dresden. Kraft toured Europe demonstrating the glowing miracle, eventually visiting London where he met the pioneering natural philosopher Robert Boyle. News of the mysterious source leaked out, enabling German chemist Johann Kunckel to reproduce phosphorus in Sweden in 1678. By 1680, Robert Boyle independently perfected its manufacture, using sand in the reaction to react with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a> metaphosphate and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a>: 4 NaPO<sub>3</sub> + 2 SiO<sub>2</sub> + 10 C → 2 Na<sub>2</sub>SiO<sub>3</sub> + 10 CO + P<sub>4</sub>. Boyle used phosphorus to ignite <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a></a>-tipped wooden splints—the direct predecessors of modern matches. His assistant, Ambrose Godfrey-Hanckwitz, turned phosphorus production into a profitable commercial business.</p>
<p>In 1769, Swedish chemists Johan Gottlieb Gahn and Carl Wilhelm Scheele discovered that bones are rich in <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a></a> phosphate. By treating bone ash with sulfuric acid, dehydrating the product, and distilling it with charcoal, they produced elemental white phosphorus on a larger scale. In 1777, Antoine Lavoisier officially recognized phosphorus as a distinct chemical element. Bone ash remained the world\'s main industrial source of phosphorus until the mid-19th century.</p>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/032507c794ce8faa1bd8550962815850.jpg" decoding="async" width="190" height="249" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" data-file-width="558" data-file-height="730" /><figcaption>Guano mining in the Central Chincha Islands, c. 1860</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/86214456f0feaae5939ea9b41eb0307f.JPG" decoding="async" width="250" height="167" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="5472" data-file-height="3648" /><figcaption>Matches from 1828. The <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a></a>-tipped match is dipped into liquid containing white phosphorus, and ignites as it is pulled out of the bottle.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/f2528328539af0f023a5ff1363c4ec59.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="194" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="8310" data-file-height="6436" /><figcaption>A worker tends an electric phosphate smelting furnace in Muscle Shoals, Alabama, 1942</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/133086dfa508ea4c29f769f08fc8c414.gif" decoding="async" width="250" height="188" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="2956" data-file-height="2224" /><figcaption>White phosphorus shell explosion in France during the First World War (1918)</figcaption></figure>
<p>Agriculture took a giant leap forward through phosphorus history. In 1609, Inca Garcilaso de la Vega described how indigenous South American civilizations harvested guano (accumulated seabird droppings) as an extraordinarily rich crop fertilizer. In the early 1800s, explorer Alexander von Humboldt introduced guano to Europe, kicking off a global fertilizer boom. By the late 19th century, guano reserves were heavily depleted, paving the way for mineral phosphate rock mining and superphosphate chemical fertilizers.</p>
<p>Early friction matches, invented by Charles Sauria in 1830, contained white phosphorus. However, these matches were notoriously dangerous. Match factory workers suffered from severe industrial poisoning known as "phossy jaw"—a painful necrosis of the jawbone caused by inhaling white phosphorus fumes. Public outrage over these conditions led to famous labor protests, including the 1888 London Matchgirls\' strike. The discovery of non-toxic red phosphorus allowed safety matches to be developed. In 1900, French chemists Henri Sévène and Emile David Cahen introduced phosphorus sesquisulfide (P<sub>4</sub>S<sub>3</sub>) for safe strike-anywhere matches. By 1906, international treaties under the Berne Convention banned white phosphorus in matches worldwide.</p>
<p>In 1888, James Burgess Readman patented the submerged-arc electric furnace, enabling large-scale industrial production of white phosphorus directly from phosphate rock. Sadly, this high volume also made phosphorus a terrifying weapon of war. In World War I, white phosphorus was used in incendiary bullets to shoot down <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a></a>-filled Zeppelins, as well as in smoke screens and tracer rounds. In World War II, white phosphorus hand grenades (M15, M34, No 77) and incendiary bombs were deployed on a massive scale, including the destructive bombing of Hamburg—ironically the very city where Brand had discovered phosphorus three centuries earlier.</p>
<p>Ancient Indian literature poetically captured humanity\'s wonder with luminescent materials. Treatises on gemology (<i>Ratna Shastra</i>) and texts like the <i>Garuda Purana</i> described self-illuminating gems called <i>Jyotirmani</i> that glowed in the dark. In modern Indian history, the matchstick industry established deep roots in the 1920s with Wimco in Maharashtra and transformed into a thriving national industry clustered around Kovilpatti and Sivakasi in Tamil Nadu, which pioneered safe red-phosphorus match manufacturing for millions of homes across India.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'Characteristics' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">Phosphorus</a></a> is a nonmetal located in Group 15 (the pnictogens) and Period 3 of the periodic table, with an atomic number of 15 and ground-state electron configuration 1s<sup>2</sup> 2s<sup>2</sup> 2p<sup>6</sup> 3s<sup>2</sup> 3p<sup>3</sup>. Having five valence electrons, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a> forms diverse chemical bonds and exhibits oxidation states ranging from −3 in phosphine gas (PH<sub>3</sub>) to +5 in phosphoric acid (H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4</sub>) and inorganic phosphate rocks.</p>
<p>Because <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a> reacts so vigorously with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a>, it never occurs as a free element in nature. Instead, pure phosphorus exists in several dramatically different physical forms, called allotropes:</p>
<p><b>White Phosphorus (P<sub>4</sub>):</b> Composed of tetrahedral molecules where four phosphorus atoms form a tight, strained pyramid. It is a soft, translucent, waxy solid that is extremely toxic and volatile. White phosphorus spontaneously catches fire in air at room temperature (around 30 °C), making it pyrophoric. Because of this high reactivity, it must be stored under water. In the dark, white phosphorus glows with an eerie greenish light due to chemiluminescence—a faint light produced as it slowly reacts with atmospheric <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a>.</p>
<p><b>Red Phosphorus:</b> Formed by heating white phosphorus to 250 °C in the absence of air. Red phosphorus forms a complex polymeric chain structure. It is non-toxic, far less reactive, stable in air, and does not catch fire spontaneously. It is the safe red material used on the striking strips of modern matchboxes.</p>
<p><b>Black Phosphorus & Phosphorene:</b> Created by heating white phosphorus under high pressure, black phosphorus is the thermodynamically most stable allotrope. It features a puckered, honeycomb-like layered lattice resembling graphite and conducts electricity as a semiconductor. When isolated into single-atom-thick two-dimensional sheets, it forms <i>phosphorene</i>. Unlike graphene, which lacks an intrinsic bandgap, phosphorene possesses a direct, tunable bandgap and high charge carrier mobility, making it a superstar candidate for futuristic nanoelectronics and flexible light sensors.</p>
<p><b>Violet (Hittorf\'s) Phosphorus:</b> A complex monoclinic crystal structure produced by dissolving red phosphorus in molten <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">lead</a></a> and cooling it slowly.</p>
<p>In modern Indian science, premier research institutions such as the Indian Institute of Science (IISc, Bengaluru), IIT Delhi, and CSIR laboratories (like CSIR-CECRI in Karaikudi) are leading advanced studies on phosphorene nanomaterials. Indian researchers are developing protective polymer coatings to prevent phosphorene from degrading in ambient air and engineering phosphorus-based nanocomposites as high-capacity anodes for next-generation <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a>-ion and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a>-ion batteries.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Isotopes' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: Isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a></a></div>
<p>Scientists have identified 22 isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a></a>, ranging from <sup>26</sup>P to <sup>47</sup>P. However, naturally occurring <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a> consists entirely of a single stable isotope: <b>phosphorus-31 (<sup>31</sup>P)</b>, giving it a 100% natural abundance. Because <sup>31</sup>P has a nuclear spin of 1/2 and high abundance, <sup>31</sup>P Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR) spectroscopy is a powerful analytical technique used by chemists worldwide to determine the structure of phosphorus-bearing organic and inorganic compounds.</p>
<p>Among its synthetic radioactive isotopes, two hold immense importance in biological research and medical diagnostics:</p>
<p><b>Phosphorus-32 (<sup>32</sup>P):</b> A strong beta-particle emitter (1.71 MeV energy) with a half-life of 14.3 days. In life science laboratories, <sup>32</sup>P is routinely incorporated into nucleic acids to create radiolabeled DNA and RNA probes for Northern and Southern blot analyses. Because its high-energy beta rays can penetrate skin and eyes, laboratory workers must wear protective gear and work behind low-density plastic or acrylic shields. Dense shielding materials like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">lead</a></a> are avoided because high-energy beta particles striking heavy atoms produce secondary X-rays known as Bremsstrahlung ("braking radiation").</p>
<p><b>Phosphorus-33 (<sup>33</sup>P):</b> A gentler beta emitter (0.25 MeV energy) with a half-life of 25.4 days. Its lower energy emissions make it ideal for delicate applications such as high-resolution DNA sequencing.</p>
<p>In India, nuclear and agricultural scientists at the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) in Mumbai work alongside researchers at the Indian Agricultural Research Institute (ICAR-IARI, New Delhi) using <sup>32</sup>P radiotracers. By injecting <sup>32</sup>P-labeled fertilizers into experimental soils, Indian scientists precisely track how plant roots absorb phosphorus, enabling farmers across India to maximize Phosphorus Use Efficiency (PUE) and boost agricultural productivity.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Biological role' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">Phosphorus</a></a> is an absolute necessity for life on Earth. An adult human body contains roughly 700 grams of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a>, making it one of the most abundant minerals in human biology. It plays critical structural, energy, and genetic roles across every living cell:</p>
<p><b>DNA and RNA Backbone:</b> Phosphate groups (PO<sub>4</sub><sup>3−</sup>) form the rigid sugar-phosphate side-chains that link genetic building blocks together, creating the famous double-helix structure of DNA and RNA that stores and executes genetic instructions.</p>
<p><b>Cellular Energy Currency:</b> Adenosine Triphosphate (ATP) is the universal energy molecule used by all living organisms. When cells break one of ATP\'s high-energy phosphate bonds to form ADP (adenosine diphosphate), energy is released to power muscle contractions, nerve signals, protein synthesis, and cellular transport.</p>
<p><b>Cell Membranes:</b> Phospholipids—molecules with a water-loving phosphate head and oil-loving fatty acid tail—assemble into double-layered membranes (bilayers) that wrap around every cell, regulating what enters and leaves.</p>
<p><b>Bones and Teeth:</b> Approximately 85% of body <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a> combines with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a></a> to form <i>hydroxyapatite</i> [Ca<sub>10</sub>(PO<sub>4</sub>)<sub>6</sub>(OH)<sub>2</sub>], the hard mineral matrix that gives structural rigidity to skeletons and teeth enamel.</p>
<p><b>Plant Growth & Agriculture:</b> In plants, phosphorus is one of the three primary macronutrients (N-P-K), essential for vigorous root development, photosynthesis, early flowering, seed production, and crop maturation.</p>
<p>In Indian agriculture, soil health is a primary national focus. Many Indian soils, particularly acidic red soils and alkaline calcareous soils, tend to bind added phosphorus into insoluble compounds of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a>, or <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a>, making it difficult for crops to absorb. Under India\'s national Soil Health Card initiative, agricultural scientists at ICAR recommend using Phosphate Solubilizing Bacteria (PSB) such as <i>Pseudomonas</i> and <i>Bacillus</i> species alongside bio-fertilizers. These friendly microbes release natural organic acids that dissolve fixed soil phosphorus, restoring soil fertility for Indian farmers growing staple crops like paddy, wheat, and pulses.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Applications' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">This section is about the applications of elemental <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a></a>. For the applications of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a></a> compounds, see <a class="mw-selflink-fragment" href="#Compounds"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">Phosphorus</a></a> § Compounds</a>.</div>
<p>Elemental <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a> and its chemical compounds are vital to modern industry, agriculture, and high technology:</p>
<p><b>Agricultural Fertilizers:</b> Over 90% of all mined phosphate rock is converted into agricultural fertilizers. Primary phosphatic fertilizers include Single Superphosphate (SSP), Di-ammonium Phosphate (DAP), and Mono-ammonium Phosphate (MAP), which replenish soil phosphorus and feed billions of people across the globe.</p>
<p><b>Matches and Pyrotechnics:</b> Red phosphorus is a vital ingredient on the friction striking surface of safety matchboxes, reacting with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a></a> chlorate on match heads to ignite safely. Phosphorus sesquisulfide (P<sub>4</sub>S<sub>3</sub>) is used in strike-anywhere matches, while elemental phosphorus powers military smoke screens and signaling flares.</p>
<p><b>Food & Industrial Chemistry:</b> Phosphoric acid (H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4</sub>) gives cola soft drinks their characteristic sharp, tangy flavor and acts as a rust converter on steel. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">Sodium</a> tripolyphosphate (STPP) is used in water softening and detergent formulations, while <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a> phosphates act as leavening agents in baking powder and polishing agents in toothpaste.</p>
<p><b>Metallurgy & Semiconductors:</b> Phosphorus serves as a deoxidizer in <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> and bronze alloys, producing durable <i>phosphor bronze</i> used in heavy-duty springs, bearings, and musical instruments. In electronics, tiny amounts of high-purity phosphorus are added as an n-type dopant to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a> wafers to create microprocessors and solar cells.</p>
<p><b>Geographical & Industrial Significance in India:</b></p>
<p>India plays a major role in the global phosphorus economy. The <b>Jhamarkotra Rock Phosphate Mine</b>, located near Udaipur in Rajasthan and operated by Rajasthan State Mines and Minerals Limited (RSMML), is India\'s largest indigenous commercial deposit of rock phosphate. It yields high-grade stromatolitic phosphorite (+30% to 34% P<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub>) crucial for domestic fertilizer production.</p>
<p>To support India\'s Green Revolution and ensure national food security, domestic fertilizer giants like <b>IFFCO</b> (Indian Farmers Fertiliser Cooperative), <b>Coromandel International</b>, and <b>GSFC</b> process millions of tonnes of phosphate rock into DAP and SSP fertilizers every year. Meanwhile, the industrial cluster around <b>Sivakasi</b> and <b>Kovilpatti</b> in Tamil Nadu forms India\'s matchstick and safety pyrotechnics capital, producing safety matches using red phosphorus formulations for consumers across South Asia.</p>
<div style="clear:both;" class=""></div>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
    ),
  ),
  'S' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'S',
    'name' => 'Sulfur',
    'atomic' => 16,
    'category' => 'polyatomic nonmetal',
    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 16 (S)',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">Sulfur</a></a></b> (also spelled <b>sulphur</b>) is a nonmetallic chemical element with symbol <b>S</b> and atomic number 16. Recognized by its brilliant lemon-yellow color, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> is one of nature\'s most abundant and versatile elements. At room temperature, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> atoms group together into crown-shaped octatomic rings with the chemical formula S<sub>8</sub>. Known since antiquity as <i>brimstone</i> (or <i>Gandhak</i> in ancient India), sulfur plays a crucial role in modern life—from fertilizing crops and manufacturing rubber to powering advanced batteries. In August 2023, India\'s historic <b>Chandrayaan-3</b> mission made international headlines by confirming the unambiguous presence of sulfur on the surface of the Moon\'s South Pole.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/23/Native_sulfur_%28Vodinskoe_Deposit%3B_quarry_near_Samara%2C_Russia%29_9.jpg',
