    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p>Located in Group 16 of the periodic table directly below <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a></a> is a polyatomic nonmetal prized for its remarkable chemical versatility. In its pure elemental form at room temperature, it exists as a soft, crystalline solid with a vivid lemon-yellow hue. Pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> is completely odorless; the pungent \'rotten egg\' odor often associated with it comes from <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> sulfide gas (H<sub>2</sub>S) or organic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> compounds rather than the element itself.</p>
<p>Sulfur is multivalent, meaning its atoms can share or gain different numbers of electrons depending on what they react with. This allows sulfur to form a vast array of chemical compounds, ranging from volcanic gases and mineral sulfides to essential biological proteins. Its ability to bond with nearly every other element on the periodic table makes it an indispensable pillar of both geology and biochemistry.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Physical properties' => '<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/fbeef40435b8c6e1d603791874012321.png" decoding="async" width="190" height="228" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" data-file-width="440" data-file-height="528" /><figcaption>As a solid, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a></a> is a characteristic lemon yellow; when burned, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a></a> melts into a blood-red liquid and emits a blue flame.</figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">Sulfur</a></a> is famous for taking on several distinct physical forms, known as allotropes. The most famous and stable allotrope at room temperature is octasulfur (cyclo-S<sub>8</sub>), where eight sulfur atoms interlock to form a puckered, crown-like ring. This ring structure possesses high symmetry (point group D<sub>4d</sub>) and zero electric dipole moment (0 D). Solid octasulfur is soft, bright yellow, odorless, and has a density of roughly 2 g/cm³. All stable solid allotropes of sulfur are excellent electrical insulators.</p>
<p>When heated, sulfur undergoes fascinating phase changes. At room temperature, it exists as &alpha;-octasulfur. When warmed to 95.2 &deg;C (203.4 &deg;F)—well below its melting point—it slowly transforms into &beta;-octasulfur. This phase change alters how the S<sub>8</sub> rings pack together without breaking the rings themselves. Pure sulfur melts at 115.21 &deg;C (239.38 &deg;F) into a mobile, pale-yellow liquid consisting mostly of &beta;-S<sub>8</sub> molecules. When cooled, molten sulfur freezes at 119.6 &deg;C (247.3 &deg;F).</p>
<p>As liquid sulfur is heated further between its melting point and boiling point (444.6 &deg;C / 832.3 &deg;F), it transforms into &gamma;-sulfur. Uniquely, as the temperature rises past 160 &deg;C, the liquid grows thicker and more viscous instead of becoming runnier. This occurs because the S<sub>8</sub> rings snap open and link together into long, tangled polymer chains. Above 200 &deg;C (392 &deg;F), the liquid turns a dark blood-red color, and at even higher temperatures, extreme heat breaks these long chains apart (depolymerization), causing the liquid to become thin and fluid once again. When ignited in air, sulfur melts into a dark red liquid and burns with a striking blue flame, releasing sulfur dioxide gas.</p>
<p>Sulfur also undergoes noticeable sublimation (evaporating directly from a solid to a gas) at temperatures between 20 &deg;C (68 &deg;F) and 50 &deg;C (122 &deg;F), and sublimates rapidly in boiling water at 100 &deg;C (212 &deg;F). Elemental sulfur is completely insoluble in water, but it dissolves readily in nonpolar solvents such as <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> disulfide (CS<sub>2</sub>), benzene, toluene, and supercritical <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>).</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Chemical properties' => '<p>Under normal conditions, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a></a> reacts very slowly with water through a process called hydrolysis. Water protons adsorb onto S<sub>8</sub> molecular clusters, causing the <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> to undergo disproportionation (simultaneously oxidizing and reducing) into <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> sulfide (H<sub>2</sub>S) gas and sulfuric acid (H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>):</p>
<div class="block-indent"><span class="frac"><span class="num">1</span>&#8260;<span class="den">2</span></span> S<sub>8</sub> + 4 H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; 3 H<sub>2</sub>S + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub></div>
<div class="thumb tmulti floatleft"><div class="thumbinner multiimageinner" style="width:100px;max-width:100px"><div class="trow"><div class="tsingle" style="width:90px;max-width:90px"><div class="thumbimage" style="height:167px;overflow:hidden"><span typeof="mw:File"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/73838df46d65b602ab6e53372fcdc274.png" decoding="async" width="88" height="167" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="426" data-file-height="810" /></span></div></div></div><div class="trow"><div class="tsingle" style="width:90px;max-width:90px"><div class="thumbimage" style="height:124px;overflow:hidden"><span typeof="mw:File"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/ecb9ac1c35c77f87ea9dbed7a17397e7.jpg" decoding="async" width="88" height="124" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="660" data-file-height="932" /></span></div></div></div><div class="trow" style="display:flex"><div class="thumbcaption">Top: Liquid <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a></a> sulfide inside a test tube. Bottom: A bottle of sulfuric acid.</div></div></div></div>
<p>The chemical behavior and oxidation states of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> depend heavily on temperature, energy input, and catalysts. Its second, fourth, and sixth ionization energies are 2,252 kJ/mol, 4,556 kJ/mol, and 8,495.8 kJ/mol, respectively. When sulfur burns in air under normal conditions, it reacts with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a> to form sulfur dioxide (SO<sub>2</sub>), where sulfur has an oxidation state of +4. To reach its maximum oxidation state of +6 and form sulfur trioxide (SO<sub>3</sub>), temperatures must be raised to 400–600 &deg;C (750–1,100 &deg;F) in the presence of a catalyst like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/V" class="wiki-link" title="Vanadium (V)">vanadium</a> pentoxide.</p>
<p>When sulfur reacts with elements less electronegative than itself—such as metals or <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a></a>—it acts as an oxidizing agent, gaining electrons to adopt an oxidation state of −2 and forming sulfides. Sulfur is extremely reactive overall, forming compounds with almost every element on the periodic table except noble gases. It even reacts at elevated temperatures with notoriously unreactive noble metals like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ir" class="wiki-link" title="Iridium (Ir)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ir" class="wiki-link" title="Iridium (Ir)">iridium</a></a> to yield <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ir" class="wiki-link" title="Iridium (Ir)">iridium</a> disulfide (IrS<sub>2</sub>).</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Isotopes' => '<div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: Isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a></a></div>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">Sulfur</a></a> has 23 known isotopes, four of which are stable: <sup>32</sup>S (94.99% &plusmn; 0.26%), <sup>33</sup>S (0.75% &plusmn; 0.02%), <sup>34</sup>S (4.25% &plusmn; 0.24%), and <sup>36</sup>S (0.01% &plusmn; 0.01%). The overwhelming dominance of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a>-32 (<sup>32</sup>S) is due to cosmic nucleosynthesis: it is forged in massive exploding stars (supernovae) during alpha-process fusion reactions. Other stable isotopes like <sup>33</sup>S and <sup>34</sup>S are formed in secondary stellar bypass processes, with novae explosions contributing a higher proportion of <sup>33</sup>S than supernovae.</p>
<p>The ratio between the two most abundant stable isotopes—sulfur-32 and sulfur-34 (expressed as &delta;<sup>34</sup>S)—varies noticeably across different geological and environmental samples. Measuring this isotope ratio provides scientists with a powerful chemical clock and environmental tracer. It allows geologists to age-date mineral ores, estimate temperatures of ancient ore-water reactions, determine pH and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> fugacity during ore formation, and trace sulfate-reducing bacteria in marine sediments. Microbes in ocean sediments take in sulfate and process lighter <sup>32</sup>S faster than <sup>34</sup>S, fractionating sulfur isotopes by up to 66 permil (66&permil;).</p>
<p>In ecology, sulfur isotope signatures help track environmental pollution and water cycles. In forest ecosystems, atmospheric rainfall and rock weathering contribute distinct sulfur signatures. For instance, isotope measurements in Rocky Mountain lakes have enabled researchers to distinguish atmospheric pollution sources from natural watershed runoff.</p>
<p>Among radioactive isotopes, sulfur-35 (<sup>35</sup>S) is the most significant, with a half-life of 87.37 days. All other radioisotopes of sulfur decay in under 3 hours. Natural <sup>35</sup>S is formed in trace amounts when high-energy cosmic rays strike atmospheric <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a></a> (<sup>40</sup>Ar). In laboratories, <sup>35</sup>S is produced by neutron bombardment of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a></a> chloride (<sup>35</sup>Cl + n &rarr; <sup>35</sup>S + p). Because <sup>35</sup>S emits low-energy beta radiation, it served as a crucial radioactive tracer in molecular biology—most notably in the famous 1952 Hershey-Chase experiment that definitively proved DNA, not protein, carries genetic information. Its low radiation energy makes compounds containing <sup>35</sup>S relatively safe to handle with standard laboratory precautions.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'History' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">Sulfur</a></a> is one of the few chemical elements that has been known and utilized since prehistoric times. Because it naturally occurs as bright yellow mineral deposits around active volcanoes and hot springs, ancient civilizations across the globe gathered and experimented with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> long before modern chemistry emerged. In ancient Germanic and English traditions, it was named <i>brimstone</i> (literally "burning stone"). The ancient Egyptian Ebers Papyrus (c. 1500 BC) described <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> ointments for treating eyelid inflammation, while the ancient Greek poet Homer documented its use as a medicinal fumigant.</p>
<p>In ancient India, sulfur held a sacred and medical status, known in Sanskrit as <b>Gandhak</b> (गन्धक, meaning "the odorous one"). In the classical Indian alchemical tradition of <b>Rasa Shastra</b> (iatrochemistry), sulfur was classified as a paramount <i>Uparasa</i> (vital mineral secondary to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">mercury</a>). Ancient Indian alchemists discovered that combining purified sulfur with liquid <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">mercury</a></a> (<i>Parada</i>) created <i>Kajjali</i> (black <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">mercury</a> sulfide)—a fundamental matrix used to formulate celebrated Ayurvedic medicines such as <i>Gandhak Rasayan</i>, <i>Rasa-Sindoor</i>, and <i>Makardhwaj</i>. To ensure safety and medicinal efficacy, ancient texts mandated a rigorous purification ritual called <i>Shodhana</i>, where raw sulfur was melted and repeatedly poured into warm cow\'s milk or herbal juices (such as <i>Bhringraj</i>) to eliminate impurities before compounding.</p>
<p>In ancient China (3rd century BC), sulfur was called <i>Liuhuang</i>. By the 9th century AD, Chinese alchemists combined sulfur, charcoal (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a></a>), and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a> nitrate (saltpeter) to invent gunpowder (<i>huoyao</i>), a breakthrough that permanently altered human history and warfare. In 1777, French chemist Antoine Lavoisier performed rigorous combustion experiments that proved sulfur was an elemental substance rather than a compound containing phlogiston. Industrial sulfur mining exploded in 1894 when German-American engineer Herman Frasch invented the Frasch process, which injected superheated water underground to melt sulfur deposits and pump pure liquid sulfur to the surface.</p>
<p>In August 2023, sulfur added a monumental new chapter to space exploration. India\'s <b>Chandrayaan-3</b> mission landed on the Moon, and its <i>Pragyan</i> rover used Laser-Induced Breakdown Spectroscopy (LIBS) and Alpha Particle X-ray Spectroscope (APXS) instruments to achieve the world\'s first in-situ confirmation of sulfur at the lunar South Pole. This landmark discovery provides deep clues about lunar volcanic history and offers critical raw materials for building future human bases on the Moon.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Applications' => '<p>Elemental <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a></a> is one of the most vital raw materials driving global industry. Over 85% of all harvested <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> is converted into sulfuric acid (H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>)—often nicknamed the "king of chemicals." Sulfuric acid is the single most produced industrial chemical worldwide, serving as the essential catalyst and ingredient for manufacturing superphosphate and ammonium sulfate fertilizers, which nourish agricultural crops that feed billions of people across the globe.</p>
<p>Another major industrial application of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> is the vulcanization of rubber, discovered by Charles Goodyear in 1839. By heating raw sticky rubber with sulfur, sulfur atoms form durable chemical cross-links between polymer chains. This turns soft rubber into tough, elastic, heat-resistant vulcanized rubber, which is used to produce vehicle tires, conveyor belts, shoe soles, and industrial hoses.</p>
<p>In energy storage and green technology, sulfur is spearheading next-generation battery development. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">Lithium</a>-Sulfur (Li-S) batteries offer theoretical energy densities up to five times higher than current <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a>-ion batteries at a fraction of the cost and weight. Premier Indian research institutions like CSIR, IISc, and IITs, alongside ISRO space scientists, are actively pioneering advanced Li-S battery technologies to power electric vehicles, renewable energy grids, and space exploration missions.</p>
<p>Sulfur also plays prominent roles in agriculture, medicine, and consumer products. Powdered sulfur is applied directly to crops as an organic fungicide and pesticide to combat mildew, as well as a soil amendment to lower the pH of alkaline soils. In medicine, sulfur\'s antimicrobial and antifungal properties make it a popular active ingredient in acne treatments, medicated soaps, and anti-dandruff shampoos. It is also used in manufacturing matches, fireworks, black powder, synthetic detergents, pulp and paper bleaching, and pharmaceuticals.</p>
<p>Following ISRO\'s Chandrayaan-3 discovery of sulfur at the lunar south pole, space scientists are designing methods to use lunar sulfur as a localized building block. By mixing sulfur with lunar regolith (soil), astronaut engineers can produce "sulfur concrete"—a water-free, durable construction material ideally suited for building radiation-shielded habitats and landing pads on the Moon without transporting heavy materials from Earth.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Biological role' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">Sulfur</a></a> is an essential element required by every living organism on Earth. In the human body, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> ranks as the eighth most abundant element by mass. An average 70 kg (150 lb) adult body contains about 140 grams (4.9 oz) of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a>—roughly equal to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a></a> and slightly more than <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a></a> or <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cl" class="wiki-link" title="Chlorine (Cl)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cl" class="wiki-link" title="Chlorine (Cl)">chlorine</a></a>.</p>
<p>Humans obtain sulfur primarily through dietary proteins containing sulfur-bearing amino acids: <b>methionine</b> and <b>cysteine</b>. Methionine is an essential amino acid that initiates protein synthesis, while cysteine contains reactive thiol (-SH) groups that form strong disulfide bridges between protein chains. These disulfide bonds act as structural staples, providing strength and elasticity to tough structural proteins like keratin in human hair, skin, and nails, as well as maintaining the 3D folded shapes of enzymes and antibodies.</p>
<p>Sulfur is also an essential component of vital vitamins and coenzymes, including thiamine (vitamin B<sub>1</sub>), biotin (vitamin B<sub>7</sub>), and Coenzyme A. Furthermore, sulfur forms part of <b>glutathione</b>—the body\'s master antioxidant—which neutralizes toxic free radicals and protects cellular DNA from oxidative damage. In plants, sulfur is indispensable for synthesizing chlorophyll and plant proteins; sulfur-deficient plants develop yellowing leaves and stunted growth, underscoring its crucial role in agricultural food chains.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
