    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/48c81740be01773b2c506fd01ba9ed98.jpg" decoding="async" width="190" height="170" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright"  data-file-width="1829" data-file-height="1632" /><figcaption>A small piece of rapidly melting solid <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a></a></figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">Argon</a></a> is completely colorless, odorless, nonflammable, and non-toxic whether it exists as a solid, liquid, or gas. In water, it dissolves about as easily as <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a> gas and is roughly 2.5 times more soluble than <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a></a>. Under normal everyday conditions, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a> is chemically inert, meaning its atoms have a full outer shell of electrons and refuse to bond with other elements to form stable chemical compounds at room temperature.</p><p>However, under extreme laboratory conditions, scientists have nudged argon into making rare, temporary chemical bonds. For example, argon fluorohydride (HArF)—a compound made of argon, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/F" class="wiki-link" title="Fluorine (F)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/F" class="wiki-link" title="Fluorine (F)">fluorine</a></a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a></a>—can exist, but only at ultracold temperatures below 17 K (−256.1 °C; −429.1 °F). Argon can also form physical traps called clathrates, where individual argon atoms become locked inside frozen water-ice cages without forming chemical bonds. Researchers have also created short-lived charged ions such as <span class="chemf nowrap">ArH<span class="nowrap"><span style="display:inline-block;margin-bottom:-0.3em;vertical-align:0.8em;line-height:1em;font-size:80%;text-align:left"><sup style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline">+</sup><br /><sub style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline"></sub></span></span></span> (argononium) and high-energy excimer molecules like ArF. Theoretical supercomputer calculations predict that several other stable argon compounds might be possible, though chemists have not yet synthesized them in the lab.</p><p>Because argon ions can be energized and focused easily, modern laboratories—such as the Inter-University Accelerator Centre (IUAC) in New Delhi and the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) in Mumbai—frequently use ionized argon streams for high-precision ion-beam etching, surface modification, and advanced materials analysis.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'History' => '<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/04c9a83c83b980a29499cb4f0b024f32.png" decoding="async" width="150" height="338" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.6" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright"  data-file-width="357" data-file-height="804" /><figcaption>A: test-tube, B: dilute alkali, C: U-shaped glass tube, D: <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pt" class="wiki-link" title="Platinum (Pt)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pt" class="wiki-link" title="Platinum (Pt)">platinum</a></a> electrode</figcaption></figure>
<p>The name <i><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a></a></i> comes from the Ancient Greek word <span title="Ancient Greek (to 1453)-language text"><span lang="grc">ἀργόν</span></span> (the neuter form of <span title="Ancient Greek (to 1453)-language text"><span lang="grc">ἀργός</span></span>), which literally means "lazy" or "inactive." Its discoverers chose this name because the gas stubbornly refused to undergo chemical reactions with any other element. Long before its actual discovery, English scientist Henry Cavendish suspected in 1785 that an unreactive gas was hiding inside air, but he was unable to isolate it.</p><p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">Argon</a> was finally isolated from air in 1894 by Scottish chemist Sir William Ramsay and English physicist Lord Rayleigh at University College London. They noticed a tantalizing mystery: <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a> gas extracted from chemical compounds was 0.5% lighter than <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a> isolated directly from atmospheric air. This tiny discrepancy led them to suspect that air contained an undiscovered, heavier gas. To capture it, Rayleigh and Ramsay replicated Cavendish\'s classic experiment. They sealed air and extra <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a> inside an inverted test tube (A) over a solution of dilute alkali (B, originally <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a></a> hydroxide). Using U-shaped glass tubes (CC) to insulate <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pt" class="wiki-link" title="Platinum (Pt)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pt" class="wiki-link" title="Platinum (Pt)">platinum</a></a> electrodes (DD), they powered a high-voltage electrical arc with Grove batteries and a Ruhmkorff induction coil. The electric spark forced <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> to react into nitrogen oxides, which were absorbed by the alkali along with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a></a> dioxide. After sparking until the gas volume stopped shrinking and nitrogen spectral lines vanished, they soaked up remaining <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> using alkaline pyrogallate. What remained was a dense, inert gas that they named <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a>.</p>
<figure typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/77827d9e81d7c304cf0aca426132cb79.jpg" decoding="async" width="160" height="258" class="mw-file-element"  data-file-width="2046" data-file-height="3293" /><figcaption>Captioned "Argon", caricature of Lord Rayleigh in <i>Vanity Fair</i>, 1899</figcaption></figure>
<p>Independently in 1882, scientists H. F. Newall and W. N. Hartley had caught glimpses of argon when analyzing the light spectrum of air, noting unexplained glowing lines that didn\'t belong to any known element.</p><p>For decades after its discovery, argon was assigned the chemical symbol "A". In 1957, the International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC) officially changed its standard symbol to "Ar".</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'Occurrence' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">Argon</a></a> is surprisingly abundant in our atmosphere, constituting 0.934% of air by volume and 1.288% by total mass. By comparison, Earth\'s solid crust contains about 1.2 parts per million (ppm) of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a>, while seawater contains roughly 0.45 ppm. Because air is by far the richest source on Earth, virtually all commercial <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a> is collected directly from the atmosphere.</p><p>Industrially, argon is captured using cryogenic air separation—a process where clean air is chilled to extreme sub-zero temperatures until it turns liquid, then distilled fractionally. This process separates argon alongside pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ne" class="wiki-link" title="Neon (Ne)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ne" class="wiki-link" title="Neon (Ne)">neon</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Kr" class="wiki-link" title="Krypton (Kr)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Kr" class="wiki-link" title="Krypton (Kr)">krypton</a></a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Xe" class="wiki-link" title="Xenon (Xe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Xe" class="wiki-link" title="Xenon (Xe)">xenon</a></a>. Major industrial gas production facilities across India—such as those run by Inox Air Products and Linde India—harvest thousands of tons of argon from atmospheric air each year to supply Indian steelmakers, electronics plants, and fabrication workshops.</p><p>Beyond Earth, argon has been detected in planetary atmospheres across the Solar System. The thin atmospheres of Mars, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">Mercury</a></a>, and Titan (Saturn\'s largest moon) all contain measurable amounts of argon, dominated by its radiogenic isotope, argon-40.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'Isotopes' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: Isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a></a></div>
<p>On Earth, natural <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a></a> consists of three stable isotopes: <span class="chemf nowrap"><span class="nowrap"><span style="display:inline-block;margin-bottom:-0.3em;vertical-align:0.5em;line-height:1em;font-size:80%;text-align:left"><sup style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline">40</sup><br /><sub style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline"></sub></span></span>Ar</span> (making up 99.6% of terrestrial <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a>), <span class="chemf nowrap"><span class="nowrap"><span style="display:inline-block;margin-bottom:-0.3em;vertical-align:0.5em;line-height:1em;font-size:80%;text-align:left"><sup style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline">36</sup><br /><sub style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline"></sub></span></span>Ar</span> (0.34%), and <span class="chemf nowrap"><span class="nowrap"><span style="display:inline-block;margin-bottom:-0.3em;vertical-align:0.5em;line-height:1em;font-size:80%;text-align:left"><sup style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline">38</sup><br /><sub style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline"></sub></span></span>Ar</span> (0.06%). Almost all of Earth\'s argon-40 comes from the radioactive decay of <span class="chemf nowrap"><span class="nowrap"><span style="display:inline-block;margin-bottom:-0.3em;vertical-align:0.5em;line-height:1em;font-size:80%;text-align:left"><sup style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline">40</sup><br /><sub style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline"></sub></span></span>K</span> (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a>-40), a naturally occurring radioisotope with a half-life of 1.25 billion years. Over geologic time, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a>-40 slowly decays: about 11.2% transforms into stable <span class="chemf nowrap"><span class="nowrap"><span style="display:inline-block;margin-bottom:-0.3em;vertical-align:0.5em;line-height:1em;font-size:80%;text-align:left"><sup style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline">40</sup><br /><sub style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline"></sub></span></span>Ar</span> via electron capture or positron emission, while 88.8% decays into stable <span class="chemf nowrap"><span class="nowrap"><span style="display:inline-block;margin-bottom:-0.3em;vertical-align:0.5em;line-height:1em;font-size:80%;text-align:left"><sup style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline">40</sup><br /><sub style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline"></sub></span></span>Ca</span> (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a>-40) via beta decay. Geologists rely on this decay ratio for <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">Potassium</a>–Argon (K–Ar) dating to determine the exact age of ancient rocks and volcanic formations.</p><p>Radioactive argon isotopes also exist in smaller amounts. In the upper atmosphere, cosmic rays striking <span class="chemf nowrap"><span class="nowrap"><span style="display:inline-block;margin-bottom:-0.3em;vertical-align:0.5em;line-height:1em;font-size:80%;text-align:left"><sup style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline">40</sup><br /><sub style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline"></sub></span></span>Ar</span> create radioactive <span class="chemf nowrap"><span class="nowrap"><span style="display:inline-block;margin-bottom:-0.3em;vertical-align:0.5em;line-height:1em;font-size:80%;text-align:left"><sup style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline">39</sup><br /><sub style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline"></sub></span></span>Ar</span>, which is also produced underground when neutrons hit potassium. Another isotope, <span class="chemf nowrap"><span class="nowrap"><span style="display:inline-block;margin-bottom:-0.3em;vertical-align:0.5em;line-height:1em;font-size:80%;text-align:left"><sup style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline">37</sup><br /><sub style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline"></sub></span></span>Ar</span> (half-life of 35 days), is created underground when neutrons strike <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a> during subsurface nuclear detonations.</p><p>Across the cosmos, argon\'s isotopic recipe is dramatically different from Earth\'s. In burning stars, stellar nucleosynthesis produces mostly primordial <span class="chemf nowrap"><span class="nowrap"><span style="display:inline-block;margin-bottom:-0.3em;vertical-align:0.5em;line-height:1em;font-size:80%;text-align:left"><sup style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline">36</sup><br /><sub style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline"></sub></span></span>Ar</span> through the alpha-process. Measurements of solar wind show that solar argon is 84.6% <span class="chemf nowrap"><span class="nowrap"><span style="display:inline-block;margin-bottom:-0.3em;vertical-align:0.5em;line-height:1em;font-size:80%;text-align:left"><sup style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline">36</sup><br /><sub style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline"></sub></span></span>Ar</span>, and the outer gas giant planets have a <sup>36</sup>Ar : <sup>38</sup>Ar : <sup>40</sup>Ar ratio of 8400 : 1600 : 1. On Earth, primordial <span class="chemf nowrap"><span class="nowrap"><span style="display:inline-block;margin-bottom:-0.3em;vertical-align:0.5em;line-height:1em;font-size:80%;text-align:left"><sup style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline">36</sup><br /><sub style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline"></sub></span></span>Ar</span> makes up only 31.5 ppmv (= 9,340 ppmv × 0.337%) of the atmosphere—similar to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ne" class="wiki-link" title="Neon (Ne)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ne" class="wiki-link" title="Neon (Ne)">neon</a></a> (18.18 ppmv).</p><p>This heavy concentration of radiogenic argon-40 on Earth created a famous puzzle in chemistry. Standard Earth argon has an atomic mass of 39.948—making it heavier than the next element on the table, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a> (39.098). When Dmitri Mendeleev first built the periodic table by atomic weight, putting heavier argon before lighter potassium seemed wrong. Physicist Henry Moseley eventually solved the riddle by proving that the periodic table is actually arranged by atomic number (argon has 18 protons, potassium has 19), confirming argon\'s proper spot.</p><p>In space exploration, argon isotopes are a vital key to understanding planetary geology. Notably, the Indian Space Research Organisation\'s (ISRO) <b>Chandrayaan-2</b> lunar orbiter carried the <b>CHACE-2</b> payload (Chandra\'s Atmospheric Composition Explorer-2), which successfully detected and mapped <span class="chemf nowrap"><span class="nowrap"><span style="display:inline-block;margin-bottom:-0.3em;vertical-align:0.5em;line-height:1em;font-size:80%;text-align:left"><sup style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline">40</sup><br /><sub style="font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;vertical-align:baseline"></sub></span></span>Ar</span> in the Moon\'s tenuous exosphere. By tracking how argon-40 seeps out from radioactive potassium decay deep inside the lunar crust, Indian scientists gained groundbreaking insights into lunar interior dynamics and volcanic degassing processes.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'Applications' => '<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/e67e83cba340b4c2218f602e0a37cb3b.jpg" decoding="async" width="200" height="300" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.8" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright"  data-file-width="512" data-file-height="768" /><figcaption>Cylinders containing <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a></a> gas for use in extinguishing fire without damaging server equipment</figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">Argon</a></a> is prized in industry and technology because of several unique features:</p>
<ul><li>It is chemically completely inert and non-reactive.</li><li>It is the most affordable alternative when <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a></a> is not inert enough for high-temperature chemical reactions.</li><li>It has exceptionally low thermal conductivity, making it an excellent heat insulator.</li><li>It has unique electronic properties, including distinct ionization energy and light emission spectrum.</li></ul>
<p>While other noble gases could theoretically do many of these jobs, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a> is by far the cheapest because it is harvested on a massive scale as a byproduct during liquid <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a> and liquid <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a></a> production in cryogenic air separation plants. Except for <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a></a>, argon is the most plentiful noble gas by far.</p><p>Its inert nature makes argon essential for arc welding (such as TIG and MIG welding), where it creates a protective gas shield around white-hot metal joints to stop <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> and moisture from weakening the weld. In metallurgy, argon is blown through molten steel in the Argon <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">Oxygen</a> Decarburization (AOD) process to refine high-purity stainless steel—a vital technique used heavily in Indian steel mills. It is also used as a protective atmosphere when growing ultra-pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a> crystals for solar panels and microchips, and when refining reactive metals like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a></a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zr" class="wiki-link" title="Zirconium (Zr)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zr" class="wiki-link" title="Zirconium (Zr)">zirconium</a></a>.</p><p>Thanks to its low thermal conductivity, argon is injected between the double glass panes of modern insulated energy-efficient windows to block heat transfer. In lighting, it fills incandescent lightbulbs to preserve <a href="/school1/ptable/element/W" class="wiki-link" title="Tungsten (W)">tungsten</a> filaments from burning up, and glows with a distinct blue-violet light in <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ne" class="wiki-link" title="Neon (Ne)">neon</a>-style discharge tubes. Argon gas is also installed in automatic fire suppression systems in server rooms and data centers, snuffing out blazes without leaving water or chemical residues that could ruin sensitive computers.</p><p>In advanced research, Argon Fluoride (ArF) excimer lasers emit ultra-short ultraviolet light used in semiconductor photolithography to print nanometer-scale circuits on microchips. In India\'s nuclear and physical science centers—including the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) and the Inter-University Accelerator Centre (IUAC)—argon serves as a cover gas for nuclear reactors, a plasma medium for material etching, and an ion source in particle accelerators for cutting-edge materials research.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
    ),
