    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p>As a group 2 alkaline earth metal, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a></a> is highly reactive and never found in its pure metallic form in nature. Instead, it readily binds with other elements to form rich minerals like limestone, marble, chalk, and gypsum. In pure form, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a> is light and silvery, but it quickly tarnishes in air, forming a dark protective layer of oxide and nitride.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Physical properties' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">Calcium</a></a> melts at 842 °C (1,115 K) and boils at 1,494 °C (1,757 K)—significantly higher temperatures than its group neighbors <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a></a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sr" class="wiki-link" title="Strontium (Sr)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sr" class="wiki-link" title="Strontium (Sr)">strontium</a></a>. With a density of only 1.526 g/cm³ (or 1.55 g/cm³ in bulk), <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a> is the lightest metal in its group. Although it is harder than <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">lead</a></a>, it is soft enough to be cut with a sharp knife with some effort.</p><p>At room temperature, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a> atoms crystallize in a face-centered cubic structure, but when heated above 443 °C (716 K), its crystal arrangement shifts to body-centered cubic. While <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a> are better electrical conductors than calcium by volume, calcium is actually a superior conductor by mass because of its remarkably low density. Because calcium reacts quickly with air on Earth, it is impractical for terrestrial wiring, but scientists have considered using it as an ultra-lightweight electrical conductor in outer space.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Chemical properties' => '<p>The chemical behavior of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a></a> is typical for heavy alkaline earth metals. It reacts spontaneously with water—faster than <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a></a> but slower than <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sr" class="wiki-link" title="Strontium (Sr)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sr" class="wiki-link" title="Strontium (Sr)">strontium</a></a>—producing <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a> hydroxide solution and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> gas bubbles. Exposed to room air, it combines with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a></a> to form <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a> oxide (CaO) and calcium nitride (Ca3N2). Fine calcium dust burns spontaneously in air to yield nitride. However, bulk chunks of calcium form a protective hydration film in moist air, and can be stored indefinitely at room temperature if humidity remains below 30%.</p><p>Calcium forms several oxides, including quicklime (CaO), calcium peroxide (CaO2) under high <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> pressure, and a yellow superoxide Ca(O2)2. Its hydroxide, Ca(OH)2 (slaked lime), is a strong base. Calcium forms all four standard dihalides and produces exceptionally abundant natural minerals such as calcium carbonate (CaCO3) and calcium sulfate (CaSO4). Like alkali metals and heavy alkaline earth metals (as well as <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Eu" class="wiki-link" title="Europium (Eu)">europium</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Yb" class="wiki-link" title="Ytterbium (Yb)">ytterbium</a>), pure calcium metal dissolves directly in liquid ammonia to form a striking, deep-blue conducting solution.</p><p>Because the calcium ion (Ca<sup>2+</sup>) is relatively large, it can surround itself with many neighboring atoms, achieving high coordination numbers up to 24 in intermetallic compounds like CaZn13. In analytical chemistry and water treatment, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a>-based chelating agents like EDTA and polyphosphates readily bind calcium ions, effectively softening hard water by removing excess calcium.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>Natural <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a></a> consists of a mixture of five stable isotopes (<sup>40</sup>Ca, <sup>42</sup>Ca, <sup>43</sup>Ca, <sup>44</sup>Ca, and <sup>46</sup>Ca) plus one extremely long-lived radioactive isotope, <sup>48</sup>Ca. With a half-life of 4.3 × 10<sup>19</sup> years (trillions of times longer than the age of the universe), <sup>48</sup>Ca is considered stable for all practical purposes. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">Calcium</a> is the lightest element in the periodic table to possess six naturally occurring isotopes.</p><p>The overwhelming majority of natural <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a>—96.941%—is <sup>40</sup>Ca. Created inside massive stars during the <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a>-burning phase through alpha particle fusion, <sup>40</sup>Ca is the heaviest stable atomic nucleus with equal numbers of protons and neutrons (20 of each). Its abundance on Earth is also continuously supplemented by the extremely slow decay of primordial <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a>-40 (<sup>40</sup>K). The second most common isotope, <sup>44</sup>Ca (2.806%), forms when unstable <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a>-44 (<sup>44</sup>Ti) decays via double electron capture. The remaining natural isotopes (<sup>42</sup>Ca, <sup>43</sup>Ca, <sup>46</sup>Ca, and <sup>48</sup>Ca) are rare, each making up less than 1% of natural calcium. <sup>46</sup>Ca is produced in hot stellar s-processes, while <sup>48</sup>Ca is formed by r-process neutron capture in Type Ia supernovae.</p><p>Both <sup>40</sup>Ca and <sup>48</sup>Ca are notable because they are \'doubly magic\' nuclei—having completely filled proton and neutron energy shells (20 protons with 20 or 28 neutrons). Calcium is the only chemical element with two primordial doubly magic isotopes. Because <sup>48</sup>Ca is doubly magic, its beta decay is blocked by a severe nuclear spin mismatch, forcing it to undergo double beta decay into <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a>-48—making it the lightest known nucleus to undergo double beta decay. Synthetic radioisotopes of calcium range from <sup>35</sup>Ca to <sup>60</sup>Ca. Among these, <sup>41</sup>Ca (half-life of ~100,000 years) is a cosmogenic nuclide created by cosmic ray neutron activation of <sup>40</sup>Ca, serving as an important cosmic clock.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'History' => '<p>Humans have utilized <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a></a> compounds for thousands of years. Lime plaster was used as early as 7000 BC to craft statues in the ancient Near East (\'Ain Ghazal), and the earliest recorded lime kiln dates back to 2500 BC in Khafajah, Mesopotamia. Ancient Egyptians used dehydrated gypsum plaster (CaSO4·2H2O) in constructing the Great Pyramid of Giza and royal tombs like Tutankhamun\'s, while ancient Romans heated limestone (CaCO3) to produce lime mortars for iconic aqueducts and monuments. The word <i><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a></i> comes from the Latin <i>calx</i>, meaning \'lime\'.</p><p>In ancient India, builders mastered <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a> chemistry to create exceptionally durable architectural monuments. Known as traditional lime mortar (*Chunam*), builders blended quicklime/slaked lime with rich organic additives such as jaggery (unrefined palm sugar), curd (casein binder), fenugreek, and fruit extracts like *haritaki* (chebulic myrobalan) and bael. This yielded an extraordinarily strong, flexible, and self-healing plaster that endured centuries of monsoons across ancient rock-cut temples, South Indian monuments like Thanjavur Palace, and Rajasthan\'s mirror-smooth *Araish* wall plaster. Simultaneously, ancient Ayurvedic texts on *Rasashastra* (iatrochemistry) developed a dedicated calcium mineral branch known as *Sudha Varga*. Ayurvedic physicians processed marine calcium sources like red coral (*Praval*) and conch shells (*Shankha*) through rigorous purification (*Shodhana*) and high-temperature calcination (*Marana*) to create bioavailable remedies like *Praval Bhasma* and *Shankha Bhasma*—fine calcium micro/nanoparticles used to strengthen bones, treat osteoporosis, and soothe digestive acidity.</p><p>In European scientific history, Roman writer Vitruvius noted that burned lime became lighter than the original limestone. In 1755, Scottish chemist Joseph Black proved that this weight loss was caused by the liberation of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> dioxide gas. In 1789, Antoine Lavoisier listed lime (*chaux*) among five un-reducible \'salifiable earths\', suspecting that all earths were actually metallic oxides. Finally, in 1808, Sir Humphry Davy isolated pure calcium metal in London by electrolyzing a mixture of lime and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">mercury</a> oxide on a <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pt" class="wiki-link" title="Platinum (Pt)">platinum</a> electrode, building on pioneering work by Jöns Jakob Berzelius and Magnus Martin of Pontin.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Applications' => '<p>Metallic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a></a> plays a critical role in heavy metallurgy. Its largest single industrial application is in steelmaking, where <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a> acts as a powerful deoxidizer and desulfurizer. Added to liquid steel, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a> forms floating liquid lime aluminates and sulfide inclusions that float to the top or disperse into microscopic spheres, dramatically improving the steel\'s castability, cleanliness, and mechanical strength. Small amounts of calcium (0.1%) are alloyed with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">lead</a> to create maintenance-free automotive batteries that reduce water loss and self-discharge compared to traditional <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sb" class="wiki-link" title="Antimony (Sb)">antimony</a>-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">lead</a> batteries. Calcium is also used to strengthen <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a> bearing alloys, control graphitic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> in cast <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>, remove <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Bi" class="wiki-link" title="Bismuth (Bi)">bismuth</a> impurities from <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">lead</a>, and serve as a reducing agent in isolating pure metals like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cr" class="wiki-link" title="Chromium (Cr)">chromium</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zr" class="wiki-link" title="Zirconium (Zr)">zirconium</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Th" class="wiki-link" title="Thorium (Th)">thorium</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/V" class="wiki-link" title="Vanadium (V)">vanadium</a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/U" class="wiki-link" title="Uranium (U)">uranium</a>. Household drain cleaners also use calcium metal to generate intense heat and strong calcium hydroxide base to saponify oils and liquefy hair clogs. Furthermore, calcium reacts with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> to form solid calcium hydride (CaH2), providing a safe, portable medium for <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> gas storage.</p><p>In modern medicine and oceanography, calcium isotope ratios offer groundbreaking analytical tools. Because lighter calcium isotopes (<sup>40</sup>Ca) precipitate slightly faster than heavier ones (<sup>44</sup>Ca) when building mineral skeletons, tracking the <sup>44</sup>Ca/<sup>40</sup>Ca ratio in human blood or urine reveals the real-time balance between bone formation and bone resorption, enabling early diagnosis of metabolic bone diseases like osteoporosis. In oceanography, changes in marine <sup>44</sup>Ca/<sup>40</sup>Ca ratios over geological time demonstrate that ocean calcium concentrations fluctuate rather than remaining constant, shedding light on ancient oceanic climate changes and global <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> cycles.</p><p>Calcium compounds are vital across the food, medical, and pharmaceutical sectors. Calcium lactate, calcium diphosphate, and tricalcium phosphate are widely used to fortify foods (calcium supplements are featured on the World Health Organization\'s List of Essential Medicines). Tricalcium phosphate also serves as a polishing agent in toothpaste and antacids, while calcium lactobionate acts as a pharmaceutical suspending agent, and calcium phosphate serves as a baking leavening agent. Industrial applications include calcium sulfite as a paper bleach and disinfectant, calcium silicate as a rubber reinforcer, and calcium acetate in metallic soaps and synthetic resins.</p><p>Geographically, India possesses vast natural deposits of limestone (calcium carbonate) estimated in tens of billions of tonnes across key mineral-producing states such as Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh, Rajasthan, Gujarat, Chhattisgarh, and Madhya Pradesh. These massive resources feed India\'s position as the world\'s second-largest cement industry, driving modern global infrastructure development.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
    ),
  ),
