    array (
      'Isotopes' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: Isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)">scandium</a></a></div>
<p>In nature, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)">scandium</a></a> is mono-isotopic, existing exclusively as a single stable isotope: <b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)">scandium</a>-45 (<sup>45</sup>Sc)</b>, which has a nuclear spin of 7/2. All natural scandium atoms on Earth consist of 21 protons, 21 electrons, and 24 neutrons.</p>
<p>Nuclear scientists have synthesized 26 radioactive isotopes of scandium, ranging from <sup>37</sup>Sc to <sup>63</sup>Sc. Among these synthetic radioisotopes, the most stable and useful are:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b><sup>46</sup>Sc</b> (half-life of 83.76 days): Used as an industrial radioactive tracer in underground pipeline flow monitoring and oil well tracking.</li>
  <li><b><sup>47</sup>Sc</b> (half-life of 3.35 days): A promising medical radioisotope undergoing intense research for targeted cancer therapy and diagnostic nuclear medicine.</li>
  <li><b><sup>48</sup>Sc</b> (half-life of 43.67 hours), <b><sup>44</sup>Sc</b> (half-life of 4.04 hours), and <b><sup>43</sup>Sc</b> (half-life of 3.89 hours).</li>
</ul>
<p>All remaining radioisotopes have half-lives under an hour, with the majority decaying in less than 15 seconds. Scandium also forms several nuclear isomers (excited nuclear states), the most stable of which is <b><sup>44m3</sup>Sc</b> with a half-life of 58.6 hours. Remarkably, <sup>44m3</sup>Sc holds the record as the lightest atomic nucleus to possess a long-lived nuclear isomer.</p>
<p>Lightweight scandium isotopes with masses below <sup>45</sup>Sc (such as <sup>37</sup>Sc to <sup>44</sup>Sc) decay primarily by electron capture or positron emission into isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a></a> (with the ultra-light <sup>37</sup>Sc–<sup>39</sup>Sc decaying by proton emission, and initial detections characterizing their mass excess). Heavier isotopes above <sup>45</sup>Sc decay via beta emission into isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a></a>. In Indian nuclear research institutions like BARC (Bhabha Atomic Research Centre), radioisotopes like <sup>46</sup>Sc are produced in research reactors for agricultural tracer studies and industrial hydrology applications.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Contrary to its historical label as a "rare earth," <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)">scandium</a></a> is actually relatively abundant in Earth\'s crust—averaging 18 to 25 parts per million (ppm), which is comparable to the abundance of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a></a> (20–30 ppm). <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)">Scandium</a> ranks as the 50th most abundant element overall on Earth (and 35th in the crust). Beyond Earth, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)">scandium</a> is much more widespread: it is the 23rd most common element in the Sun and the 26th most abundant element in distant stars.</p>
<p>In cosmic origins, stable scandium is forged deep inside massive exploding stars (supernovae) through rapid neutron capture (the <i>r-process</i>), such as reactions combining <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a></a>-28 with neutrons (<sup>28</sup>Si + 17n → <sup>45</sup>Sc). Cosmic rays speeding through space also create scandium by smashing into heavier <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a>-group atomic nuclei in interstellar space—a process known as cosmic ray spallation (<sup>56</sup>Fe + p → <sup>45</sup>Sc + <sup>11</sup>C + n).</p>
<p>Despite its presence across Earth\'s crust, scandium rarely concentrates into rich ore deposits because its ionic radius closely resembles that of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a></a>, and aluminium, causing it to disperse in tiny trace amounts inside over 800 common rock-forming minerals. Only a handful of ultra-rare Scandinavian and Malagasy minerals—such as <i>thortveitite</i>, <i>euxenite</i>, and <i>gadolinite</i>—contain high concentrations. Thortveitite is the richest mineral source, containing up to 45% scandium oxide (Sc<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>).</p>
<p>In India, scandium occurs naturally in trace amounts within the heavy mineral beach sands of Kerala, Tamil Nadu, and Odisha, where state enterprise IREL (India) Limited processes monazite and ilmenite deposits. Additionally, India\'s vast reserves of bauxite ore mined for aluminium production contain valuable hidden concentrations of scandium. When bauxite is refined into alumina, scandium accumulates in the byproduct known as "red mud" (bauxite residue). To turn this industrial byproduct into strategic national wealth, Indian research bodies like the Jawaharlal Nehru Aluminium Research Development & Design Centre (JNARDDC) in Nagpur, CSIR-IMMT in Bhubaneswar, and NITI Aayog are developing pilot plants with major producers like Hindalco, NALCO, and Vedanta to sustainably extract pure scandium oxide from Indian red mud.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'History' => '<p>The story of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)">scandium</a></a> begins in 1869 when the famous Russian chemist Dmitri Mendeleev, creator of the periodic table, noticed a gap in his table. He mathematically predicted that an unknown element—which he named <i>ekaboron</i>—must exist with an atomic weight between 40 and 48. Ten years later, in 1879, Swedish chemist Lars Fredrik Nilson and his team isolated a new oxide while analyzing rare minerals (<i>euxenite</i> and <i>gadolinite</i>) found in Scandinavia. Nilson isolated about two grams of highly pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)">scandium</a> oxide and named the element <i><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)">scandium</a></i> after the Latin word <i>Scandia</i> (Scandinavia). Although Nilson did not initially connect his discovery to Mendeleev\'s prediction, Swedish chemist Per Teodor Cleve recognized that scandium was indeed Mendeleev\'s missing <i>ekaboron</i>.</p>
<p>Isolating pure metallic scandium proved extremely difficult because of its strong affinity for <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a>. It was not until 1937 that scientists produced tiny amounts of pure scandium metal through the high-temperature electrolysis of a molten salt mixture of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a></a>, and scandium chlorides at 700–800 °C. The world\'s first full pound (0.45 kg) of 99% pure scandium metal was only manufactured in 1960.</p>
<p>Industrial interest skyrocketed in 1971 when researchers in the United States and the USSR independently patented <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminium (Al)">aluminium</a>-scandium alloys, discovering that trace amounts of scandium made aluminium immensely stronger and crack-resistant during welding. During the Cold War in the 1980s and 1990s, laser crystals made of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Gd" class="wiki-link" title="Gadolinium (Gd)">gadolinium</a>-scandium-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a> garnet (GSGG) were developed for cutting-edge military lasers under the U.S. Strategic Defense Initiative (SDI).</p>
<p>In India\'s scientific and historical narrative, while scandium was identified during modern European mineral exploration, India\'s deep traditional expertise in extraction chemistry (<i>Rasashastra</i>) has evolved into 21st-century strategic metallurgy. Recognizing scandium as a vital critical mineral for national sovereignty and high-technology growth, leading Indian scientific institutions—including CSIR laboratories, DRDO, and JNARDDC—are actively writing the next chapter of scandium history by pioneering self-reliant green extraction technologies.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'Applications' => '<p>Although world production of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)">scandium</a></a> is small—only a few tonnes annually—it is considered a super-element because of its transformative impact when added in tiny quantities to other materials.</p>
<p><b>1. Super-Alloys for Aerospace and Sports:</b> The single largest application of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)">scandium</a> is in high-performance <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminium (Al)">aluminium</a>-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)">scandium</a> (Al-Sc) alloys. Adding just 0.1% to 0.5% scandium by weight to aluminium creates micro-precipitates of Al<sub>3</sub>Sc. These tiny structures prevent recrystallization and grain growth when the metal is heated, dramatically boosting tensile strength by up to 50%, improving heat resistance, and eliminating hot-cracking during welding. This allows aircraft and rocket components to be welded rather than riveted, saving 4–5% in overall structural weight.</p>
<p>In Indian science and space technology, CSIR-NIIST (National Institute for Interdisciplinary Science and Technology, Thiruvananthapuram) and DRDO\'s Defence Metallurgical Research Laboratory (DMRL) are actively developing indigenous Al-Mg-Sc alloy sheets, plates, and extrusions. These lightweight, high-strength scandium alloys are being evaluated for rocket motor casings and propulsion structures at ISRO\'s Liquid Propulsion Systems Centre (LPSC) and aircraft nacelle cowlings at CSIR-NAL, driving India\'s self-reliance (<i>Atmanirbhar Bharat</i>) in strategic aerospace materials.</p>
<p>In consumer products, Al-Sc alloys are used to manufacture ultra-lightweight, durable sports equipment including premium bicycle frames, baseball bats, lacrosse sticks, tent poles, and high-end firearm frames.</p>
<p><b>2. High-Intensity Lighting:</b> When scandium iodide (ScI<sub>3</sub>) is added to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">mercury</a> vapor lamps, it produces a brilliant white light that closely mimics natural sunlight. These high-intensity discharge lamps feature exceptional color rendering and are widely used in major sports stadiums, arena illumination, and movie set lighting.</p>
<p><b>3. Clean Energy and Solid Oxide Fuel Cells (SOFCs):</b> Scandium oxide (scandia, Sc<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>) is a critical material in solid oxide fuel cells. Adding scandium to zirconia creates scandia-stabilized zirconia (SSZ), a high-performance ceramic electrolyte. SSZ conducts <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> ions efficiently at lower operating temperatures than traditional materials, significantly enhancing the efficiency, durability, and lifespan of clean fuel cells for green energy generation.</p>
<p><b>4. Advanced Lasers and Defense Technologies:</b> Crystals composed of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Gd" class="wiki-link" title="Gadolinium (Gd)">gadolinium</a>-scandium-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a> garnet (GSGG) are twice as efficient as standard Nd:YAG laser crystals. They are utilized in high-power industrial cutting lasers, rangefinders, and strategic defense technologies.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
    ),
