    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Physical properties' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">Titanium</a> is celebrated worldwide for its exceptional <b>strength-to-weight ratio</b>. It is a shiny, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)">silver</a>-white metal that possesses the strength of common structural steel while being nearly 45% lighter. Compared to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminium (Al)">aluminium</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a> is 60% denser but more than twice as strong as standard aerospace aluminium alloys (such as 6061-T6). Advanced <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a> alloys (like Beta C) can reach extraordinary tensile strengths exceeding 1,400 megapascals (MPa), making them tough enough to withstand severe structural stresses.</p>
<p>Despite its strength, titanium has a relatively high melting point of 1,668 °C (3,034 °F). While sometimes mistaken for a refractory metal, it is technically categorized as a transition metal. Pure titanium is quite ductile—meaning it can be drawn into thin wires or hammered into sheets without snapping—especially when kept free of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a> contamination. It is paramagnetic (only weakly attracted to magnetic fields), non-magnetic, and is a relatively poor conductor of heat and electricity compared to metals like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a></a> or <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)">silver</a></a>. When cooled to ultra-low temperatures near absolute zero (below its critical temperature of 0.49 K), titanium becomes a superconductor, carrying electricity with zero electrical resistance.</p>
<p>While remarkably strong, titanium is not as hard as specialized heat-treated tool steel, and it tends to lose its mechanical strength when heated above 430 °C (806 °F). Machining and shaping titanium requires specialized techniques and liquid coolants, because the metal tends to stick or "gall" against cutting tools. However, like high-grade steel, titanium structures possess a natural <b>fatigue limit</b>, meaning they can endure billions of repeating stress cycles without structural failure, guaranteeing decades of reliable service in aircraft and engines.</p>
<p>At the microscopic atomic level, titanium exhibits two different crystalline structures (allotropes). At room temperature, it exists in a hexagonal close-packed pattern known as the <b>alpha (α) form</b>. When heated above 882 °C (1,620 °F), its atoms rearrange into a body-centered cubic lattice known as the <b>beta (β) form</b>. The metal\'s heat capacity spikes dramatically right at this transition temperature before leveling off in the beta phase.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Chemical properties' => '<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img alt="Graph showing the Pourbaix diagram of titanium" src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/b18691045050a72c44658072535d14bf.png" decoding="async" width="250" height="257" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="497" data-file-height="511" /><figcaption>Pourbaix diagram for <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a></a> in pure water, perchloric acid, or <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a></a> hydroxide</figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">Titanium</a>\'s most legendary feature is its extreme resistance to corrosion, caused by a clever natural phenomenon called <b>passivation</b>. The instant fresh <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a> metal is exposed to air or moisture, it reacts with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a> to form an invisible, non-porous protective skin of titanium dioxide (TiO<sub>2</sub>). Although this self-healing shield starts out only 1 to 2 nanometers thick, it thickens over several years to about 25 nanometers, completely sealing the metal underneath. Because of this oxide armor, titanium refuses to rust or corrode in ocean seawater, salt spray, harsh organic acids, and corrosive chemicals like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cl" class="wiki-link" title="Chlorine (Cl)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cl" class="wiki-link" title="Chlorine (Cl)">chlorine</a></a> or aqua regia (a powerful mixture of nitric and hydrochloric acid that dissolves <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)">gold</a>).</p>
<p>At room temperature, titanium easily resists dilute hydrochloric and sulfuric acids as well as chloride solutions. However, it can be attacked and dissolved by concentrated hot acids, or by hydrofluoric acid (which destroys the oxide film). While chemically calm under normal conditions, titanium becomes fiercely reactive at elevated temperatures. If heated in open air, titanium will catch fire and burn brightly at temperatures below its melting point. For this reason, titanium melting and welding must always be conducted in a vacuum or under an inert gas shield such as <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a>.</p>
<p>When heated to 550 °C (1,022 °F), titanium reacts vigorously with halogens like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cl" class="wiki-link" title="Chlorine (Cl)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cl" class="wiki-link" title="Chlorine (Cl)">chlorine</a></a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Br" class="wiki-link" title="Bromine (Br)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Br" class="wiki-link" title="Bromine (Br)">bromine</a></a> to form titanium tetrachloride (TiCl<sub>4</sub>) and titanium tetrabromide (TiBr<sub>4</sub>). At 1,200 °C in air (or 610 °C in pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a>), it burns to produce pure titanium dioxide. Interestingly, titanium is one of the very few chemical elements that can burn in an atmosphere of pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a></a> gas; at 800 °C, it reacts with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a> to form golden titanium nitride (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sn" class="wiki-link" title="Tin (Sn)">TiN</a>), a glass-hard compound that causes the metal to become brittle if uncontrolled, but serves as a super-tough protective coating on drill bits when applied intentionally.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Occurrence' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">Titanium</a></a> is surprisingly plentiful on Earth. It is the <b>ninth-most abundant element</b> in Earth\'s crust (making up about 0.63% of the crust by mass) and the seventh-most abundant structural metal. It is present in almost all igneous rocks—the U.S. Geological Survey found <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a> in 784 out of 801 rock types analyzed—as well as in soils (0.5% to 1.5%), living plants, animals, and natural bodies of water. The ocean contains dissolved <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a> at a tiny concentration of about 4 picomolar.</p>
<p>In nature, titanium is never found lying around as a free shiny metal because it binds tightly with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a>. Instead, it occurs in minerals such as <b>ilmenite</b> (FeTiO<sub>3</sub>), <b>rutile</b> (TiO<sub>2</sub>), anatase, brookite, perovskite, titanite (sphene), and the ultra-rare mineral akaogiite. Among these, ilmenite and rutile are the primary commercial ores mined worldwide, with total global mineral reserves exceeding 2 billion tonnes.</p>
<p><b>Indian Mineral Wealth & Coastline Geography:</b> India holds some of the world\'s richest and highest-grade reserves of heavy mineral beach sands containing ilmenite and rutile. These prized black sand deposits stretch across five major coastal zones: the famous <b>Chavara coastal belt in Kollam, Kerala</b>, the beaches of <b>Manavalakurichi in Tamil Nadu</b>, <b>Chatrapur (Ganjam)</b> and <b>Brahmagiri in Odisha</b>, and <b>Bhavanapadu in Andhra Pradesh</b>. The government-owned <i>Kerala Minerals and Metals Limited (KMML)</i> operates an integrated mining and processing facility at Chavara, extracting ilmenite directly from Kerala\'s golden coast. Additionally, inland geological formations such as the vanadiferous titaniferous magnetite deposits in <b>Mayurbhanj (Odisha)</b> contain significant amounts of titanium dioxide alongside <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/V" class="wiki-link" title="Vanadium (V)">vanadium</a>.</p>
<p>Beyond Earth, titanium has a rich cosmic footprint. It is present in meteorites, in the fiery atmosphere of the Sun, and in cool M-type stars (surfacing at 3,200 °C). Moon rocks retrieved during NASA\'s Apollo 17 lunar mission revealed an astonishing 12.1% titanium dioxide (TiO<sub>2</sub>) content. While native metallic titanium requires extreme deep-Earth mantle pressures (2.8 to 4.0 gigapascals) to form naturally on Earth, lunar soil samples have revealed tiny microscopic nanocrystals of pure native titanium created by meteoroid impacts in the vacuum of space.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Isotopes' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: Isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a></a></div>
<p>Natural <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a> found across Earth is a mixture of <b>five stable isotopes</b>: <sup>46</sup>Ti, <sup>47</sup>Ti, <sup>48</sup>Ti, <sup>49</sup>Ti, and <sup>50</sup>Ti. By far the most common of these is <b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a>-48 (<sup>48</sup>Ti)</b>, which makes up about 73.8% of all titanium atoms in existence.</p>
<p>Nuclear scientists have also created 23 synthetic radioactive isotopes of titanium, ranging from light <sup>39</sup>Ti to ultra-heavy <sup>66</sup>Ti. Most of these radioisotopes are extremely fleeting, decaying in less than half a second. However, a few have measurable lives: <b>titanium-44 (<sup>44</sup>Ti)</b> has a half-life of 63 years and is used by astrophysicists to trace stellar explosions (supernovae), while <sup>45</sup>Ti lasts 184.8 minutes, <sup>51</sup>Ti lasts 5.76 minutes, and <sup>52</sup>Ti lasts 1.7 minutes.</p>
<p>Titanium isotopes lighter than <sup>46</sup>Ti decay primarily through positron emission (or electron capture in <sup>44</sup>Ti) to transform into isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)">scandium</a></a>. Conversely, isotopes heavier than <sup>50</sup>Ti decay by emitting beta particles to become isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/V" class="wiki-link" title="Vanadium (V)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/V" class="wiki-link" title="Vanadium (V)">vanadium</a></a>. When bombarded with high-energy deuteron beams in particle accelerators, titanium becomes radioactive, releasing energetic positrons and hard gamma rays.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'History' => '<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img alt="Engraved profile image of a mid-age male with high forehead. The person is wearing a coat and a neckerchief." src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/90fa4620e90102e415814b1cf7c8af2a.jpg" decoding="async" width="190" height="248" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" data-file-width="848" data-file-height="1108" /><figcaption>Martin Heinrich Klaproth named <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a></a> for the Titans of Greek mythology.</figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">Titanium</a> was first discovered in <b>1791</b> by an English clergyman and amateur geologist named <b>William Gregor</b> in Cornwall, England. While walking near a stream in the parish of Manaccan, Gregor noticed unusual black magnetic sand (which we now know as ilmenite). Analyzing the sand, he separated <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> oxide from an unknown white metallic oxide, which he realized contained a completely new element. He published his discovery in scientific journals and named the new oxide <i>manaccanite</i>.</p>
<p>Four years later, in 1795, Prussian chemist <b>Martin Heinrich Klaproth</b> independently rediscovered the same element in a mineral called rutile from Hungary (modern Slovakia). Unaware of Gregor\'s earlier work, Klaproth named the element <b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">Titanium</a></b> after the Titans—the legendary giants of Greek mythology known for their immense power. When Klaproth later learned of Gregor\'s work, he tested a sample of manaccanite and graciously confirmed that Gregor had indeed discovered titanium first.</p>
<p>Unlocking pure titanium metal from its ore proved to be a daunting challenge that stumped scientists for over a century. Unlike <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>, titanium cannot be smelted by heating its ore with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a></a>, because titanium greedily reacts with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> to form brittle titanium carbide. In 1887, Swedish chemists Lars Fredrik Nilson and Otto Pettersson produced 95% pure metal by reacting titanium oxide with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cl" class="wiki-link" title="Chlorine (Cl)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cl" class="wiki-link" title="Chlorine (Cl)">chlorine</a></a> gas and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a></a>. In 1910, American scientist Matthew A. Hunter developed the <b>Hunter process</b> at Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute, producing 99.9% pure metal by heating titanium tetrachloride (TiCl<sub>4</sub>) with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a> under extreme pressure.</p>
<p>The true commercial breakthrough arrived in the 1930s when Luxembourgish metallurgist <b>William Justin Kroll</b> reduced TiCl<sub>4</sub> using <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a></a>, and later refined the technique in 1940 using <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a></a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a>. Known as the <b>Kroll process</b>, this multi-step chemical reaction produces porous chunks of pure metal called <b>titanium sponge</b> and remains the dominant industrial manufacturing method today. Highly pure laboratory titanium was also achieved in 1925 by Anton Eduard van Arkel and Jan Hendrik de Boer using the iodide vapor process.</p>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img alt="Rough pieces of shiny metal" src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/59886db42b86e4860718d9c720d6d638.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="188" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="600" data-file-height="451" /><figcaption>Titanium "sponge", made by the Kroll process</figcaption></figure>
<p>During the Cold War in the 1950s and 1960s, titanium became a crucial strategic defense material. The Soviet Union built titanium-hulled military submarines (such as the Alfa, Mike, and Sierra II classes) capable of diving to extreme depths, while the United States used massive quantities of titanium to build record-breaking supersonic aircraft like the SR-71 Blackbird, Lockheed A-12, and F-100 Super Sabre. The U.S. maintained strategic stockpiles of titanium sponge (over 15,000 metric tons allocated annually).</p>
<p><b>Modern Indian Scientific Leadership:</b> For decades, aerospace-grade titanium sponge technology was tightly guarded by a handful of nations. To achieve complete self-reliance (<i>Aatmanirbhar Bharat</i>) in high-tech space and defense applications, India\'s <b>Defence Metallurgical Research Laboratory (DMRL)</b> under DRDO spent over two decades pioneering indigenous technology to manufacture aerospace-grade titanium sponge using the Kroll process. Sponsored and funded by the <b>Vikram Sarabhai Space Centre (VSSC)</b> of the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO), a state-of-the-art commercial <b>Titanium Sponge Plant (TSP)</b> was established at KMML in Chavara, Kerala, and fully commissioned in 2015. This landmark achievement made India one of only a select few nations in the world capable of producing high-purity aerospace titanium from raw coastal sand under one roof, providing critical titanium alloy components for ISRO\'s rocket engines, satellite propellant tanks (including those on <b>Chandrayaan-3</b>), and naval defense platforms.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'Applications' => '<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img alt="A hand holding a cylinder of reflective silvery metal" src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/7f7f0a2f6fdef6c39103614bdbdd4711.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="167" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="2160" data-file-height="1440" /><figcaption>A <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a></a> cylinder</figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">Titanium</a></a> plays an irreplaceable role across aerospace, medicine, heavy industry, and everyday consumer products:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Aerospace & Space Exploration:</b> Because of its immense strength and low weight, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a> is widely alloyed with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminium (Al)">aluminium</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/V" class="wiki-link" title="Vanadium (V)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/V" class="wiki-link" title="Vanadium (V)">vanadium</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mo" class="wiki-link" title="Molybdenum (Mo)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mo" class="wiki-link" title="Molybdenum (Mo)">molybdenum</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a></a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a> (such as the famous Ti-6Al-4V alloy). It is used for jet engine fan blades, airframe structural parts, rocket motor casings, and high-pressure propellant tanks for spacecraft—including ISRO\'s PSLV, GSLV, and Moon missions. Mill products include sheet, plate, bar, wire, forgings, and castings.</li>
<li><b>Medical Implants & Prosthetics:</b> Titanium is uniquely <i>biocompatible</i>—the human body does not reject it, and bone tissue actually grows directly onto titanium surfaces in a process called <i>osseointegration</i>. It is the material of choice for artificial hip and knee joints, dental implants, skull plates, pacemakers, and bone fixation screws.</li>
<li><b>Industrial & Chemical Processing:</b> Because seawater and harsh chemicals cannot corrode titanium, it is heavily used in coastal power stations, desalinization plants (converting sea water into drinking water), naval warships, submarine hulls, chemical reactors, heat exchangers, and oil drilling rigs.</li>
<li><b>Metallurgy & Steel Production:</b> Added to steel as ferro-titanium, titanium refines grain size, acts as a deoxidizer, and prevents carbide precipitation in stainless steel, preserving strength and rust resistance.</li>
<li><b>Everyday White Pigment:</b> Over 95% of all mined titanium ore is turned into <b>titanium dioxide (TiO<sub>2</sub>)</b>, an intensely white, non-toxic pigment with an ultra-high refractive index. It gives supreme brightness and opacity to white paint, sunscreen, paper, plastics, toothpaste, food coloring, and cosmetics.</li>
<li><b>Pyrotechnics & Recreation:</b> Finely powdered titanium metal burns with blinding white sparks, adding dramatic effects to fireworks and stage pyrotechnics. In sporting equipment, titanium produces lightweight premium golf clubs, bicycle frames, tennis rackets, and lightweight camping gear.</li>
</ul>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
