    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">Manganese</a></b> (symbol <b>Mn</b>, atomic number 25) is a hard, brittle, silvery-gray transition metal that closely resembles <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>. Named from the ancient region of Magnesia, it is never found as a pure metal in nature but occurs abundantly in minerals like pyrolusite (MnO<sub>2</sub>). <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">Manganese</a> is the essential powerhouse of modern metallurgy—adding strength, toughness, and rust-resistance to steel—and plays a vital role in household batteries, agricultural nutrients, and chemical oxidizers. India has deep historical and scientific ties to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a>, spanning ancient <i>Wootz steel</i> crucible metallurgy, <i>Rasa Shastra</i> texts (where it was recognized as <i>Vaikrant</i>), Asia\'s deepest underground manganese mine at Balaghat, deep-sea mining in the Indian Ocean, and cutting-edge battery recycling research.</p>',
