    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">Manganese</a></b> is a versatile transition metal located in Group 7 and Period 4 of the periodic table. Its electron configuration is [Ar] 3d<sup>5</sup> 4s<sup>2</sup>. With five unpaired electrons in its 3d subshell, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a> exhibits rich chemical reactivity and forms a wide array of colourful compounds.</p>

<h3>Chemical Reactivity and Oxidation States</h3>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">Manganese</a> is chemically active and reacts readily with air, water, and acids. When exposed to moist air, it slowly tarnishes, forming a protective oxide layer. In water containing dissolved <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a>, it oxidizes ("rusts") much like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>. In its pure elemental form, manganese is far too brittle to be used alone, but it excels when alloyed with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a>, and other metals.</p>

<p>Manganese displays a wide range of oxidation states from -3 to +7, making it a chameleon of transition metal chemistry. Its key oxidation states include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>+2 (Manganese II):</b> Pale pink in color, found in manganese sulfate (MnSO<sub>4</sub>) and rhodochrosite. This is the most chemically stable ion in aqueous solutions and plays a vital role as a plant biological cofactor.</li>
  <li><b>+3 (Manganese III):</b> Found in braunite and intermediate biological catalytic complexes.</li>
  <li><b>+4 (Manganese IV):</b> Found in dark brown or black pyrolusite (MnO<sub>2</sub>), a strong oxidizing agent used in glassmaking, chemical synthesis, and dry-cell batteries.</li>
  <li><b>+7 (Manganese VII):</b> Famous for intense purple <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a> permanganate (KMnO<sub>4</sub>), a powerful oxidizer used in water purification, laboratory titrations, and medical disinfectants.</li>
</ul>

<h3><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">Sulfur</a>-Fixing and Deoxidizing Power</h3>
<p>In chemical and metallurgical processing, manganese has a powerful chemical affinity for <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a>. During steelmaking, manganese acts as a scavenger: it binds with unwanted <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> to form manganese sulfide (MnS), preventing brittle <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> sulfide from causing cracks when hot steel is shaped. It also reacts with dissolved <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> to form manganese oxides, purifying the molten metal.</p>

<h3>Magnetic & Green Chemistry Behavior</h3>
<p>Because of its unpaired d-electrons, manganese metal and its common ions are <b>paramagnetic</b>—meaning they are weakly attracted to external magnetic fields, although elemental manganese is not permanently magnetic. In modern green chemistry, non-toxic manganese complexes are actively studied as eco-friendly catalysts to replace expensive, scarce noble metals in industrial organic synthesis.</p>',
      'Physical properties' => '<p>In its pure metallic state, <b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a></b> is a hard, brittle, silvery-gray metal with a distinct luster, visually resembling <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>. However, unlike <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>, pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a> is extremely brittle and cannot be easily hammered, bent, or machined without fracturing.</p>

<h3>Key Physical Constants</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Density:</b> 7.21 g/cm³ (slightly lighter than <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>).</li>
  <li><b>Melting Point:</b> 1,519 K (1,246 °C or 2,275 °F).</li>
  <li><b>Boiling Point:</b> 2,334 K (2,061 °C or 3,742 °F).</li>
  <li><b>Phase:</b> Solid at room temperature.</li>
  <li><b>Hardness & Texture:</b> Hard and brittle; shatters easily under high impact when unalloyed.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Thermal and Oxidation Behavior</h3>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">Manganese</a> has a high melting point and requires substantial thermal energy to melt. At room temperature, manganese tarnishes slowly in air, acquiring a dull oxide film. In water containing dissolved <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a>, it gradually oxidizes or "rusts", breaking down in a process similar to iron corrosion. When heated to high temperatures in air, it burns to form manganese tetroxide (Mn<sub>3</sub>O<sub>4</sub>).</p>

<h3>Magnetic Behavior and Allotropes</h3>
<p>Neither elemental manganese metal nor its common ores are ferromagnetic (permanently magnetic like lodestone). Instead, manganese metal and its simple ions exhibit <b>paramagnetism</b>, responding weakly to external magnetic fields. Solid manganese exists in four distinct crystal structures (allotropes—alpha, beta, gamma, and delta) depending on temperature, with the complex cubic alpha-phase being stable at room temperature.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>Naturally occurring <b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a></b> consists of a single stable isotope, <b><sup>55</sup>Mn</b> (100% natural abundance). Because it has only one stable isotope, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a> is classified as a monoisotopic element.</p>

<h3>Radioisotopes and Decay Modes</h3>
<p>Synthetic nuclear research has isolated 25 radioisotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a>, ranging from <sup>46</sup>Mn to <sup>72</sup>Mn, along with 3 nuclear isomers (meta states). The most stable radioactive isotopes are:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b><sup>53</sup>Mn:</b> Half-life of 3.7 million years.</li>
  <li><b><sup>54</sup>Mn:</b> Half-life of 312.08 days.</li>
  <li><b><sup>52</sup>Mn:</b> Half-life of 5.591 days.</li>
</ul>
<p>All other radioisotopes have half-lives shorter than 3 hours, and the majority decay in less than 1 minute. For isotopes lighter than stable <sup>55</sup>Mn, the primary decay mode is electron capture. For heavier isotopes, the primary decay mode is beta decay.</p>

<h3>Stellar Origin and Supernova Nucleosynthesis</h3>
<p>Manganese is part of the "<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> group" of cosmic elements. Astrophysicists believe these elements were synthesized in massive stars shortly before catastrophic supernova explosions. Minute quantities of radioactive <sup>53</sup>Mn are continually formed on meteorites and Earth through cosmic ray impacts (spallation) on <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> atoms.</p>

<h3>Isotope Geology and Solar System Dating</h3>
<p>Because radioactive <sup>53</sup>Mn decays into <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cr" class="wiki-link" title="Chromium (Cr)">chromium</a>-53 (<sup>53</sup>Cr) with a 3.7-million-year half-life, the ratio of manganese to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cr" class="wiki-link" title="Chromium (Cr)">chromium</a> (Mn/Cr) acts as a cosmic clock in isotope geology. By measuring variations in <sup>53</sup>Cr/<sup>52</sup>Cr and Mn/Cr ratios in ancient meteorites, scientists confirmed a non-zero initial <sup>53</sup>Mn/<sup>55</sup>Mn ratio in early solar matter. Along with isotopic evidence from <sup>26</sup>Al and <sup>107</sup>Pd, this proves that <sup>53</sup>Mn decayed <i>in situ</i> inside early planetary bodies, confirming nucleosynthetic processes right before the coalescence of the Solar System over 4.5 billion years ago.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>The history of <b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a></b> is a fascinating journey spanning tens of thousands of years, linking cave art pigments, alchemical glassmaking, ancient Indian metallurgy, and modern battery technology.</p>

<h3>Etymology: The Tale of Two Magnesias</h3>
<p>The name <i><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a></i> comes from the ancient region of Magnesia (located in modern Greece or Turkey). In antiquity, two distinct black minerals were discovered there, both named <i>magnes</i>:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Male magnes:</b> Magnetic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> ore (magnetite or lodestone), which attracted <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> and gave us the word <i>magnet</i>.</li>
  <li><b>Female magnes:</b> Non-magnetic black mineral (pyrolusite or <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a> dioxide, MnO<sub>2</sub>), which did not attract <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> but decolorized green glass.</li>
</ul>
<p>By the 16th century, glassmakers and alchemists referred to manganese dioxide as <i>manganesum</i> to distinguish black <i>magnesia nigra</i> from white <i>magnesia alba</i> (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a> oxide). Italian physician Michele Mercati called the black mineral <i>manganesa</i>, which evolved into <i>manganese</i> (and German <i>Mangan</i>), while white magnesia eventually yielded the name <i><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a></i>.</p>

<h3>Prehistoric Pigments and Ancient Glass</h3>
<p>Natural manganese dioxide is abundant and was used as an ancient pigment. Black drawings in France\'s Gargas and Lascaux caves, created 24,000 to 30,000 years ago, were made using mineral MnO<sub>2</sub>. Ancient Egyptian, Roman, and 14th-century Venetian glassmakers added manganese compounds to glass as "glassmaker\'s soap" to clarify green iron impurities or tint glass purple.</p>

<h3>Ancient Indian Context: Rasa Shastra & Wootz Steel Metallurgy</h3>
<p>India possesses a rich ancient heritage in manganese science. In <b>Rasa Shastra</b> (the Ayurvedic alchemy of metals and minerals), manganese ores were identified as <b>Vaikrant</b> and classified among the prestigious <i>Maharasas</i> (eight primary minerals) in classic texts such as the <i>Rasa Ratna Samuchaya</i>. Traditional scholars performed meticulous <i>Shodhana</i> (purification) and <i>Marana</i> (calcination) processes to make therapeutic mineral ashes (<i>Bhasma</i>).</p>

<p>In metallurgy, ancient Indian artisans as early as 300 BCE forged world-renowned <b>Wootz steel</b>—a high-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> crucible steel made at ancient production sites like Kodumanal (Tamil Nadu) and exported worldwide. Indian metalsmiths empirically selected manganese-rich iron ores and pyrolusite fluxes to deoxidize molten steel, remove <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a>, and boost toughness. Archaeological studies of the 1,600-year-old <b>Delhi Iron Pillar</b> reveal trace manganese (~0.05%), contributing to its legendary corrosion resistance.</p>

<h3>Alchemical Experiments and Chemical Isolation</h3>
<p>In the 17th and 18th centuries, chemists utilized manganese dioxide in pioneering experiments. Johann Glauber (17th c.) and Ignatius Gottfried Kaim (1770) discovered that heating MnO<sub>2</sub> with alkalis yielded purple permanganate salts. In the mid-18th century, Swedish chemist Carl Wilhelm Scheele reacted MnO<sub>2</sub> with acids to discover <b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cl" class="wiki-link" title="Chlorine (Cl)">chlorine</a> gas</b> (later commercialized via the Leblanc and Weldon processes).</p>
<p>Scheele recognized that pyrolusite contained an unknown metal element. In <b>1774</b>, Swedish chemist <b>Johan Gottlieb Gahn</b> officially isolated an impure sample of manganese metal by reducing manganese dioxide with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> in a crucible. (Kaim may also have isolated a metal sample around 1770).</p>

<h3>Steel Revolutions, Manganism Warning, and Batteries</h3>
<p>Historians speculated that Spartan sword steel gained extraordinary hardness from manganese-bearing Greek iron ores. In 1816, manganese was proven to harden iron without increasing brittleness. In 1856, British metallurgist Robert Forester Mushet introduced <i>spiegeleisen</i> (a 15% manganese iron alloy) into Bessemer steelmaking, enabling mass steel production.</p>
<p>In 1837, British scholar James Couper first documented that heavy occupational exposure among manganese miners triggered a severe Parkinson\'s-like movement disorder, now known as <i>manganism</i>. In 1866, Georges Leclanché invented the Leclanché dry cell battery using MnO<sub>2</sub> as a cathode depolarizer, establishing manganese\'s role in 20th-century <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a>-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> and alkaline dry batteries. In 1912, manganese phosphate conversion coatings (Parkerizing) were patented to rust-proof firearms.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">Manganese</a></b> is the 12th most abundant element in the Earth\'s crust, constituting approximately 1,000 parts per million (0.1%) of crustal material. It is widely distributed across land, soil, atmosphere, and oceans.</p>

<h3>Environmental Distribution and Key Ores</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Soil:</b> Contains 7 to 9,000 ppm of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a>, with a global average of 440 ppm.</li>
  <li><b>Atmosphere:</b> Present in trace concentrations averaging 0.01 µg/m³.</li>
  <li><b>Primary Minerals:</b> <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">Manganese</a> never occurs naturally as a pure native metal. Its chief economic minerals are <b>pyrolusite</b> (MnO<sub>2</sub>), <b>braunite</b> (Mn<sup>2+</sup>Mn<sup>3+</sup><sub>6</sub>SiO<sub>12</sub>), <b>psilomelane</b> ((Ba,H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>2</sub>Mn<sub>5</sub>O<sub>10</sub>), and <b>rhodochrosite</b> (manganese carbonate, MnCO<sub>3</sub>). Manganese ores usually occur in close proximity to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> ores like sphalerite.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Global Reserves and Mining Leaders</h3>
<p>Land-based manganese resources are massive but unevenly distributed. About <b>80% of known global reserves</b> are located in South Africa, mainly in the Kalahari manganese fields near Hotazel (estimated at 15 billion tons). In 2011, South Africa led world production with 3.4 million tons. Other major manganese mining nations include Australia, China, Gabon, Brazil, India, Kazakhstan, Ghana, Ukraine, and Malaysia.</p>

<h3>Indian Geography: MOIL and Asia\'s Deepest Mine</h3>
<p>India is one of the world\'s leading producers of manganese ore. State-run central public enterprise <b>MOIL Limited</b> (formerly Manganese Ore India Limited) operates 11 major mines in Central India. Notably, the <b>Balaghat Mine</b> in Madhya Pradesh is recognized as the largest and deepest underground manganese mine in Asia. Other vital Indian mines operate in Nagpur and Bhandara districts (Maharashtra) and Keonjhar and Sundargarh districts (Odisha).</p>

<h3>Indian Ocean Deep-Sea Manganese Nodules</h3>
<p>In addition to terrestrial mines, huge deposits of manganese cover the abyssal ocean floor in the form of <b>polymetallic nodules</b> (manganese nodules). Under India\'s national <i>Deep Ocean Mission</i>, the National Institute of Ocean Technology (NIOT) and the Ministry of Earth Sciences are developing deep-sea mining technology. India holds exclusive rights granted by the International Seabed Authority (ISA) to explore 75,000 square kilometers in the Central Indian Ocean Basin, harvesting manganese-rich seabed nodules using submersibles like <i>MATSYA 6000</i>.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>From strengthening skyscraper steel and powering consumer electronics to supporting crop growth and advancing clean energy, <b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a></b> is an indispensable element in modern industry and daily life.</p>

<h3>1. Steelmaking and Metallurgy (85–90% of Global Use)</h3>
<p>The overwhelming majority of global <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a> production is consumed by the <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> and steel industry. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">Manganese</a> serves three vital roles: deoxidizing molten <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>, removing <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> impurities, and forming high-strength alloys.</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Ferro-Manganese & Silico-Manganese:</b> Essential master alloys added to molten <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> during steel production.</li>
  <li><b>High-Strength Steel:</b> Manganese enhances hardness, tensile strength, toughness, workability, and wear resistance. Tough Hadfield steel (containing 12–14% Mn) is used in rail tracks, rock crushers, excavator buckets, and bank vaults.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Household Dry Batteries</h3>
<p>Manganese dioxide (MnO<sub>2</sub>) has served as the cathode depolarizer in standard disposable dry batteries—such as <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a>-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> and long-lasting alkaline batteries—for over a century, providing reliable portable power.</p>

<h3>3. Rust Protection & Chemical Oxidizers</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Rust-Proof Coatings:</b> Electrochemical manganese phosphate coatings (Parkerizing) protect steel firearms, tools, and auto parts against rust and wear.</li>
  <li><b>Water Purification:</b> <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">Potassium</a> permanganate (KMnO<sub>4</sub>) is an intense oxidizer used to purify drinking water, disinfect municipal wastewater, and act as a medical antiseptic.</li>
  <li><b>Glass, Ceramics & Rubber:</b> MnO<sub>2</sub> clarifies green glass, pigments ceramics purple/brown, and accelerates rubber processing.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Agriculture and Nutrition</h3>
<p>Manganese sulfate (MnSO<sub>4</sub>) is added to agricultural fertilizers and animal feeds as an essential micronutrient, supporting plant photosynthesis and enzyme activity while functioning as a crop fungicide.</p>

<h3>5. Modern Indian Scientific Research: Green Energy & Recycling</h3>
<p>Indian scientific institutions are driving innovation in manganese-based green chemistry and clean energy:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Battery Recycling (CSIR-NML):</b> The CSIR-National Metallurgical Laboratory in Jamshedpur has commissioned pilot hydrometallurgical recycling facilities to extract high-purity manganese, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a> from spent electric vehicle and electronic batteries, fostering a circular economy.</li>
  <li><b>Advanced Energy Storage (CSIR-CECRI & IITs):</b> Researchers at CSIR-CECRI (Karaikudi) and IITs (such as IIT Madras and IIT Bombay) are developing eco-friendly manganese-based <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a>-ion and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a>-manganese oxide (LMO) battery cathodes, as well as manganese catalysts for sustainable green chemistry.</li>
</ul>',
    ),
