    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 26 (Fe)',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">Iron</a></a></b> (symbol <b>Fe</b>, from Latin <i>ferrum</i>, atomic number 26) is a vital transition metal located in group 8 and period 4 of the periodic table. It is the most abundant chemical element on Earth by total mass, forming much of our planet\'s liquid outer core and solid inner core, and ranking as the fourth most abundant element in Earth\'s crust (about 5%). From the <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>-rich core of distant supergiant stars to the red hemoglobin carrying <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> in our blood, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> is the master element of cosmic architecture and human civilization. In ancient India, iron metallurgy reached legendary heights with the invention of high-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> <i>Wootz</i> crucible steel and rust-resistant monuments like the famous 4th-century Iron Pillar of Delhi. Today, iron alloyed with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a></a> forms steel—the indispensable foundation of modern skyscrapers, bridges, railways, and space rockets.</p>',
