    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">Iron</a> is a lustrous, silvery-white transition metal with a density of 7.874 g/cm³. Under normal atmospheric conditions, pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> melts at 1,811 K (1,538 °C or 2,800 °F) and boils at 3,134 K (2,861 °C or 5,182 °F). At the atomic level, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> has an atomic number of 26 and a ground-state electron configuration of 1s<sup>2</sup> 2s<sup>2</sup> 2p<sup>6</sup> 3s<sup>2</sup> 3p<sup>6</sup> 4s<sup>2</sup> 3d<sup>6</sup> (or [Ar] 3d<sup>6</sup> 4s<sup>2</sup>). Its valence electrons allow it to adopt multiple oxidation states, most commonly +2 (ferrous, Fe<sup>2+</sup>) and +3 (ferric, Fe<sup>3+</sup>). Iron has a Pauling electronegativity of 1.83, an electron affinity of 14.785 kJ/mol, and a first ionization energy of 762.5 kJ/mol followed by higher ionization steps (1,561.9, 2,957, 5,290, 7,240, 9,560, and up to 895,161 kJ/mol for its 26th electron).</p><p>Iron is famous for its ferromagnetism—it is strongly attracted to magnets and can be permanently magnetized at room temperature. However, when heated above its Curie temperature of 1,043 K (770 °C), iron loses its permanent magnetism and becomes paramagnetic. Pure elemental iron is relatively soft and malleable, but its mechanical properties transform dramatically when combined with minute amounts of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a></a> and other metals. For instance, while pure single-crystal iron has a modest tensile strength of only 10 MPa and a Brinell hardness of 3, cold-worked iron reaches 690 MPa (hardness 200), pearlitic steel achieves 1,200 MPa (hardness 350), martensitic steel reaches 2,070 MPa (hardness 600), ausformed hardened steel reaches an impressive 2,930 MPa (hardness 850–1,200), and microscopic iron "whiskers" exhibit a mind-boggling tensile strength of 11,000 MPa!</p><p>Unlike metals such as <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a></a> or <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cr" class="wiki-link" title="Chromium (Cr)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cr" class="wiki-link" title="Chromium (Cr)">chromium</a></a>, which form a protective oxide shield when exposed to air, ordinary iron rusts continuously in the presence of moisture and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a>. Rust (hydrated iron oxide) is flaky and porous, flaking off to continuously expose fresh underlying metal to further oxidation. However, under special metallurgical conditions—such as the high-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a> forge-welding techniques developed in ancient India—iron can form a non-porous passive layer that halts corrosion completely.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: Isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a></div><p>Natural <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> on Earth is composed of four stable isotopes: <sup>56</sup>Fe (the most abundant at 91.754%), <sup>54</sup>Fe (5.845%), <sup>57</sup>Fe (2.119%), and <sup>58</sup>Fe (0.282%). Scientists have also synthesized 24 artificial radioactive isotopes in nuclear laboratories. Among the stable isotopes, <sup>57</sup>Fe is uniquely important in physics and chemistry because it is the only stable <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> isotope with a nuclear spin (−1/2). This nuclear spin makes <sup>57</sup>Fe the primary core isotope used in Mössbauer spectroscopy, an ultra-precise analytical technique used to study electronic structures, magnetic properties, and chemical bonding in materials and biological proteins. Theoretically, <sup>54</sup>Fe can undergo double electron capture to form <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cr" class="wiki-link" title="Chromium (Cr)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cr" class="wiki-link" title="Chromium (Cr)">chromium</a></a>-54 (<sup>54</sup>Cr), but this extremely rare event has never been observed in experiment, establishing a lower half-life limit of 4.4 × 10<sup>20</sup> years (trillions of times longer than the age of the universe!).</p><p>Iron-60 (<sup>60</sup>Fe) is an extinct radioactive isotope with a half-life of 2.6 million years. Although no original <sup>60</sup>Fe remains from Earth\'s formation, its decay product—its granddaughter nuclide stable <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a></a>-60 (<sup>60</sup>Ni)—serves as a cosmic clock. By measuring <sup>60</sup>Ni concentrations in primitive meteorites such as <i>Semarkona</i> and <i>Chervony Kut</i>, planetary scientists proved that <sup>60</sup>Fe was active during the early formation of the Solar System 4.6 billion years ago. The heat released by decaying <sup>60</sup>Fe, alongside <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a></a>-26 (<sup>26</sup>Al), helped melt and differentiate early asteroids into metallic cores and rocky mantles.</p><p>In stellar astrophysics, <sup>56</sup>Fe holds a monumental place as the ultimate endpoint of nuclear fusion inside massive stars. During the <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a>-burning phase inside supergiant stars, lighter nuclei fuse into radioactive <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a></a>-56 (<sup>56</sup>Ni, made of 14 alpha particles). Inside stars, intense gamma radiation causes photodisintegration, preventing fusion from efficiently building heavier elements beyond <sup>56</sup>Ni. With a half-life of about 6 days, <sup>56</sup>Ni rapidly decays into radioactive <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a></a>-56 (<sup>56</sup>Co), which then decays into stable <sup>56</sup>Fe. Although <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a></a>-62 (<sup>62</sup>Ni) has a marginally higher binding energy per nucleon than <sup>56</sup>Fe, stellar conditions strongly favor producing <sup>56</sup>Fe because <sup>56</sup>Fe has a lower mass per nucleon due to its higher fraction of lighter protons. Elements heavier than iron cannot form via normal fusion; they require the extreme neutron-burst environment of a supernova via rapid neutron capture (the r-process).</p><p>Theoretical cosmologists speculate that in the extremely distant future of the universe—trillions upon trillions of years from now, assuming protons do not decay—cold quantum tunnelling will allow light atomic nuclei to fuse into <sup>56</sup>Fe, while heavy elements decay into iron. In this hypothetical epoch, all stellar-mass objects across the cosmos will eventually condense into cold, dead spheres of pure iron.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">Iron</a> is the sixth most abundant element in the universe and the single most abundant refractory element in space. On Earth, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> accounts for roughly 32.1% of our planet\'s total mass, making it Earth\'s most abundant element by mass. The vast majority of Earth\'s <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> resides deep below our feet, concentrated in the liquid outer core and solid inner core (alloyed with about 5% to 10% <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a></a>). In the Earth\'s crust, iron ranks as the fourth most abundant element (about 5% by mass), trailing only <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a></a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a></a>. Native metallic iron is rare on Earth\'s surface except when delivered by iron-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a> meteorites.</p><p>In nature, iron occurs primarily in mineral ores as oxides, carbonates, and sulfides. The most important commercial iron ores are hematite (Fe<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>, rich red ore), magnetite (Fe<sub>3</sub>O<sub>4</sub>, magnetic black ore), goethite, limonite, and siderite (FeCO<sub>3</sub>), as well as iron pyrite (FeS<sub>2</sub>, known as "fool\'s <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)">gold</a>").</p><p>India is blessed with some of the world\'s largest and highest-grade iron ore deposits, primarily located in ancient Precambrian rock formations across peninsular India. The country\'s iron ore geography is centered around major mineral belts:</p><ul><li><b>Odisha–Jharkhand Belt:</b> India\'s richest iron ore region, producing high-grade hematite. Major mining centers include Keonjhar, Sundargarh, Mayurbhanj, and Jajpur in Odisha, alongside the Singhbhum district in Jharkhand. The historic Noamundi and Joda mines in this belt have supplied Tata Steel for over a century.</li><li><b>Durg–Bastar–Chandrapur Belt:</b> Spanning Chhattisgarh and Maharashtra, this belt contains the famous <b>Bailadila</b> iron ore range in Dantewada district—renowned for producing some of the highest-purity hematite ore in the world—and the Dalli-Rajhara mines that supply the Bhilai Steel Plant.</li><li><b>Bellary–Chitradurga–Chikkamagaluru–Tumakuru Belt:</b> Located in Karnataka, this region is a major hub for high-grade hematite and magnetite ores (such as Hospet-Bellary and the Kudremukh magnetite deposits).</li><li><b>Maharashtra–Goa Belt:</b> Coastal iron ore reserves that support major domestic steel mills and international export shipments.</li></ul>',
      'History' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: History of ferrous metallurgy</div><p>Human interaction with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> began thousands of years ago with meteorites—ancient Sumerians and Egyptians called <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> "metal from heaven" (<i>an-bar</i> in Sumerian, <i>bij-n-pt</i> in Egyptian). The transition from bronze to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> tools around 1200 BCE marked the dawn of the Iron Age across the ancient world.</p><p>In ancient India, iron technology developed independently as early as the 2nd millennium BCE. Early literary references appear in the <i>Rigveda</i>, which uses the term <i>ayas</i> for metal, while later Vedic texts (the <i>Yajurveda</i> and <i>Atharvaveda</i>) explicitly distinguish <i>krishna-ayas</i> or <i>syama-ayas</i> ("black metal," meaning iron) from <i>loha-ayas</i> (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a>). Classic Indian medical treatises such as the <i>Sushruta Samhita</i> (c. 600 BCE) detailed the fabrication of over 100 specialized surgical instruments (<i>sastra</i>) made of tempered iron-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> alloys, emphasizing controlled quenching in water, oil, or milk to obtain razor-sharp surgical blades.</p><p>By the 6th century BCE, South Indian metallurgists (in present-day Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, Telangana, and Andhra Pradesh) pioneered one of the greatest innovations in world metallurgical history: <b>Wootz Steel</b> (known locally as <i>Ukku</i> or <i>Urukku</i>, meaning "melted steel"). Artisans placed high-purity iron ore together with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a>-rich organic plant matter (such as <i>Cassia auriculata</i> leaves and wood) inside sealed clay crucibles, heating them in high-temperature bloomery furnaces. This process produced ultra-high-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> crucible steel containing microscopic cementite nanostructures. Exported across ancient maritime trade routes to the Middle East, Wootz steel was forged into legendary Damascus blades, famed worldwide for their razor sharpness and flowing water-like surface patterns.</p><p>Ancient India also mastered forge-welding massive iron structures with exceptional corrosion resistance. The world-famous <b>Iron Pillar of Delhi</b>, erected in the 4th–5th century CE during the reign of King Chandragupta II Vikramaditya (currently at the Qutb complex), stands 7.2 meters tall and weighs over 6 tonnes. Despite exposure to Delhi\'s monsoon rains and seasonal sun for over 1,600 years, the pillar remains virtually rust-free! Landmark research led by Indian metallurgist <b>Prof. R. Balasubramaniam</b> of IIT Kanpur revealed the scientific secret: ancient Indian blacksmiths used charcoal smelting, leaving a high concentration of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a> (~0.25%) in the wrought iron. The <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a> acts as a catalyst, inducing the formation of an ultra-thin protective passive layer composed of crystalline iron <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> phosphate hydrate (FePO<sub>4</sub>·H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4</sub>·4H<sub>2</sub>O), magnetite (Fe<sub>3</sub>O<sub>4</sub>), and amorphous misawite (δ-FeOOH). This self-healing passive film seals the metal surface and prevents further oxidation. Similar rust-resistant iron structures include the massive iron beams of the Konark Sun Temple in Odisha and the Dhar Iron Pillar in Madhya Pradesh.</p><p>In the alchemical tradition of <i>Rasa Shastra</i> (recorded in classical texts like the <i>Rasaratnasamuchchaya</i>), Indian scholar-chemists classified iron into categories such as <i>munda</i> (wrought/cast iron), <i>tiksna</i> (hard crucible steel), and <i>kanta</i> (magnetic iron). They prepared <i>Lauha Bhasma</i>—micro-calcined nano-iron ash—used in Ayurvedic medicine to treat <i>pandu roga</i> (anemia) and boost cellular vitality.</p><p>In modern times, India\'s industrial iron revolution was spearheaded by visionary industrialist <b>Jamsetji Nusserwanji Tata</b>, who laid the foundation for Tata Iron and Steel Company (Tata Steel) in 1907 at Sakchi (now Jamshedpur, Jharkhand), creating Asia\'s first integrated steel city. Today, modern scientific advancements are driven by premier research institutes such as the <b>CSIR-National Metallurgical Laboratory (NML)</b> in Jamshedpur, which pioneers eco-friendly mineral beneficiation and iron ore pelletization, and the <b>Steel Authority of India Limited (SAIL)</b>, keeping India among the top global producers of steel.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<table class="wikitable floatright"><caption>Characteristic values of tensile strength (TS) and Brinell hardness (BH) of various forms of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a>.</caption><tbody><tr><th>Material</th><th>TS <br />(MPa)</th><th>BH <br />(Brinell)</th></tr><tr><td><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">Iron</a></a> whiskers</td><td>11000</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Ausformed (hardened) <br />steel</td><td>2930</td><td>850–1200</td></tr><tr><td>Martensitic steel</td><td>2070</td><td>600</td></tr><tr><td>Bainitic steel</td><td>1380</td><td>400</td></tr><tr><td>Pearlitic steel</td><td>1200</td><td>350</td></tr><tr><td>Cold-worked <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a></td><td>690</td><td>200</td></tr><tr><td>Small-grain iron</td><td>340</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">Carbon</a></a>-containing iron</td><td>140</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td>Pure, single-crystal iron</td><td>10</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Iron is by far the most widely used of all metals, accounting for over 90% of global metal production. Its immense strength, abundance, and low cost make it the undisputed backbone of modern engineering and construction. Because pure iron is relatively soft, almost all industrial iron is converted into alloys—primarily steel. Steel is produced by combining iron with small amounts of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> (0.002% to 2.1%) along with alloying elements like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mn" class="wiki-link" title="Manganese (Mn)">manganese</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cr" class="wiki-link" title="Chromium (Cr)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cr" class="wiki-link" title="Chromium (Cr)">chromium</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mo" class="wiki-link" title="Molybdenum (Mo)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mo" class="wiki-link" title="Molybdenum (Mo)">molybdenum</a></a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/V" class="wiki-link" title="Vanadium (V)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/V" class="wiki-link" title="Vanadium (V)">vanadium</a></a>.</p><p>Major applications of iron and steel include:</p><ul><li><b>Structural Construction:</b> Reinforcing steel bars (rebar), structural I-beams, girders, bridges, skyscrapers, concrete foundations, and roofing sheets.</li><li><b>Transportation & Heavy Industry:</b> Automotive chassis, engine blocks, railway tracks, train locomotives, cargo ships, container vessels, heavy mining machinery, and agricultural tractors.</li><li><b>Specialty Alloys & High-Tech Defense:</b> Stainless steel (containing at least 10.5% <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cr" class="wiki-link" title="Chromium (Cr)">chromium</a>) resists rust and is used in kitchenware, cutlery, surgical instruments, and food processing plants. Ultra-high-strength maraging steels and iron-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a>-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a> superalloys are engineered by organizations like <b>ISRO</b> (Indian Space Research Organisation) and <b>DRDO</b> for rocket motor casings, missile bodies, and aerospace turbines.</li><li><b>Magnetism & Electrical Machinery:</b> <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">Silicon</a>-steel laminations are used in electric transformer cores, electric motors, power generators, and electromagnets due to their high magnetic permeability and low hysteresis loss.</li><li><b>Chemical Catalysis & Pigments:</b> Iron compounds act as critical industrial catalysts, most notably in the Haber-Bosch process to synthesize ammonia fertilizer from <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a>. Iron oxide pigments provide durable red, yellow, and black colors in paints, brick masonry, and ceramics.</li><li><b>Biological Necessity & Medicine:</b> Iron is an essential trace nutrient for almost all living organisms. In human physiology, iron forms the active core of <b>hemoglobin</b> in red blood cells, which binds and carries <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> from our lungs to every tissue in the body, and <b>myoglobin</b> in muscle tissue. Iron deficiency leads to anemia, causing fatigue and weakness. In traditional Indian medicine (Ayurveda), micro-calcined iron preparations known as <i>Lauha Bhasma</i> have been used for centuries to treat anemia and restore vitality.</li></ul>',
    ),
  ),
  'Co' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Co',
    'name' => 'Cobalt',
    'atomic' => 27,
    'category' => 'transition metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">Cobalt</a></a></b> is a hard, lustrous, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)">silver</a>-gray transition metal with the symbol <b>Co</b> and atomic number <b>27</b>. Named after legendary German mountain goblins called <i>kobolds</i>, pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a> is ferromagnetic—meaning it is strongly attracted to magnets—and remains magnetic up to an extraordinarily high temperature. In the Earth\'s crust, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a> is rarely found as a free metal; it occurs almost exclusively bound inside mineral compounds alongside <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a></a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a></a>, with rare uncombined natural metal found only inside ancient meteoric <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a> alloys. Famous across human history for giving a breathtaking deep blue color to glass and ceramics—such as India\'s world-renowned Jaipur Blue Pottery—cobalt has evolved into a vital modern material that powers electric vehicle batteries, aerospace superalloys, and life-saving nuclear cancer therapy developed by Indian scientists at BARC.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/62/Cobalt_ore_2.jpg',
