    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img alt="a sample of pure cobalt" src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/909af75d84e779f8fd4e4cb9f07b2661.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="227" class="mw-file-element"  data-file-width="649" data-file-height="589" /><figcaption>A block of electrolytically refined <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a></a> (99.9% purity) cut from a large plate</figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">Cobalt</a></a> is a tough, ferromagnetic metal with a density (specific gravity) of 8.9 grams per cubic centimeter. Among all ferromagnetic elements, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a> holds its magnetism up to the highest temperature known: its Curie temperature is a scorching 1,115 °C (2,039 °F), above which it loses its permanent magnetic properties. Its intrinsic magnetic moment measures between 1.6 and 1.7 Bohr magnetons per atom, and its ability to channel magnetic fields (relative permeability) is about two-thirds that of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a>. Metallic cobalt can freeze into two different crystal patterns: hexagonal close-packed (hcp) at lower temperatures and face-centered cubic (fcc) at higher temperatures. The theoretical transition temperature between these two crystal structures is 450 °C (842 °F), but because the energy difference between them is tiny, pure cobalt samples often feature a random mixing or intergrowth of both structures.</p>
<p>Chemically, cobalt is a weakly reducing metal that resists everyday corrosion because it naturally forms a thin, protective oxide skin (passivation film) on its surface. However, it reacts readily when heated with halogens and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a></a>. Burning cobalt in <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a> forms cobalt(II,III) oxide (Co<sub>3</sub>O<sub>4</sub>), which sheds <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a> at 900 °C (1,650 °F) to yield cobalt monoxide (CoO). Cobalt reacts directly with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/F" class="wiki-link" title="Fluorine (F)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/F" class="wiki-link" title="Fluorine (F)">fluorine</a></a> gas (F<sub>2</sub>) at 520 K (247 °C) to form cobalt trifluoride (CoF<sub>3</sub>), and bonds with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cl" class="wiki-link" title="Chlorine (Cl)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cl" class="wiki-link" title="Chlorine (Cl)">chlorine</a></a> (Cl<sub>2</sub>), <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Br" class="wiki-link" title="Bromine (Br)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Br" class="wiki-link" title="Bromine (Br)">bromine</a></a> (Br<sub>2</sub>), and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/I" class="wiki-link" title="Iodine (I)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/I" class="wiki-link" title="Iodine (I)">iodine</a></a> (I<sub>2</sub>) to produce binary halides. While cobalt refuses to react with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a></a> gas (H<sub>2</sub>) or <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a></a> gas (N<sub>2</sub>) even when strongly heated, it combines with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/P" class="wiki-link" title="Phosphorus (P)">phosphorus</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/As" class="wiki-link" title="Arsenic (As)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/As" class="wiki-link" title="Arsenic (As)">arsenic</a></a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a></a>. At room temperature, it dissolves slowly in mineral acids and oxidizes very gradually in moist air, but remains untarnished in completely dry air.</p>
<p>These remarkable thermal and magnetic properties make cobalt alloys essential for high-temperature engineering. In India, research institutes such as the Defence Metallurgical Research Laboratory (DMRL) in Hyderabad utilize cobalt-base alloys to fabricate high-strength, heat-resistant components capable of enduring extreme operational stresses.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'Isotopes' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: Isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a></a></div>
<p>Natural <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a> on Earth consists entirely of a single stable isotope: <b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a>-59 (<sup>59</sup>Co)</b>. Beyond this stable form, scientists have created and characterized 22 radioactive isotopes ranging from <sup>50</sup>Co to <sup>78</sup>Co, along with 4 nuclear isomers (meta states) that all decay in under 15 minutes. Among the radioisotopes, the most stable and famous is <b>cobalt-60 (<sup>60</sup>Co)</b>, which has a half-life of 5.2714 years. Other notable radioisotopes include <sup>57</sup>Co (half-life of 271.81 days), <sup>56</sup>Co (77.24 days), and <sup>58</sup>Co (70.84 days). All other cobalt radioisotopes have half-lives shorter than 18 hours, and most break down in less than a single second.</p>
<p>The decay pathways of cobalt isotopes depend on their mass relative to stable <sup>59</sup>Co. Isotopes lighter than <sup>59</sup>Co primarily decay via electron capture into isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a> (element 26). Conversely, heavier isotopes decay via beta emission into isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a></a> (element 28). The stable <sup>59</sup>Co nucleus possesses a magnetic quadrupole moment and can be detected using Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR) spectroscopy. In fact, <sup>59</sup>Co features the broadest chemical shift range of any NMR-active nucleus—spanning over 20,000 parts per million (ppm) with signal widths reaching up to 20 kHz! Chemists frequently use <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a> hexacyanocobaltate (0.1M K<sub>3</sub>Co(CN)<sub>6</sub> in D<sub>2</sub>O) as an NMR reference standard because its highly symmetrical structure produces sharp, clear spectral lines.</p>
<p>Synthetic <b>cobalt-60</b> plays a vital, life-saving role in modern medicine and industry in India. India is one of the world\'s leading producers of industrial cobalt-60, creating the isotope by irradiating cobalt-59 target absorber rods inside Pressurised Heavy Water Reactors (PHWRs) at the <b>Rajasthan Atomic Power Station (RAPS)</b> in Rawatbhata. The irradiated sources are processed at the Rajasthan Atomic Power Project Cobalt Facility (RAPPCOF) in Kota and distributed by the Board of Radiation & Isotope Technology (BRIT). Crucially, scientists at the <b>Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC)</b> developed the <b>Bhabhatron</b>—an indigenous, highly affordable Cobalt-60 teletherapy machine. The Bhabhatron uses high-energy gamma rays emitted by <sup>60</sup>Co to destroy cancer tumors, bringing accessible radiation therapy to hospitals across India and being donated to developing nations across Africa and Asia.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'History' => '<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img alt="cobalt blue Chinese porcelain" src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/1b6a8b8e40ffc886e2e3eb9177330acb.jpg" decoding="async" width="190" height="236" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright"  data-file-width="2206" data-file-height="2735" /><figcaption>Early Chinese blue and white porcelain, manufactured <abbr title="circa">c.</abbr><span style="white-space:nowrap;">&#8201;1335</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>For thousands of years, human civilizations have prized <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a></a> compounds for their ability to impart an intense, mesmerizing blue color to glass, glazes, and ceramics. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">Cobalt</a> pigments have been detected in ancient Egyptian statues, Persian jewelry dating to the 3rd millennium BC, Roman glass ruins at Pompeii destroyed in 79 AD, and legendary Chinese porcelain from the Tang (618–907 AD) and Ming (1368–1644 AD) dynasties. Bronze Age artisans cast blue <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a> glass as early as the 14th century BC, evidenced by a glass ingot recovered from the Uluburun shipwreck off the Turkish coast. Ancient Egyptians obtained cobalt colorants from cobalt-bearing alum deposits in their Western Oases to decorate 18th-Dynasty glassware (1550–1292 BC).</p>
<p>In India, cobalt blue glazes established a celebrated artistic lineage. During the Mughal period, Turko-Persian artistic influences brought blue ceramic techniques into the subcontinent, where cobalt glazes adorned architectural tiles and pottery. This craft evolved into <b>Jaipur Blue Pottery</b>, which received grand royal patronage in the 19th century from Maharaja Sawai Ram Singh II of Jaipur. Unlike traditional ceramics, Jaipur Blue Pottery uses no clay; artisans craft a dough from powdered quartz, glass powder, Fuller\'s earth (<i>multani mitti</i>), borax, and gum, painting it with cobalt oxide glaze. Because cobalt oxide maintains its rich color without degrading under intense kiln temperatures, it produces the stunning blue designs famous worldwide today. Historical research shows that Indian painters and artisans sourced cobalt glazes both through vibrant overland trade routes (such as Kashan in Persia) and regional mineral preparations.</p>
<p>The name <i>cobalt</i> originates from the 16th-century German word <i>kobald</i>, referring to troublesome mountain goblins or earth spirits. Medieval miners in Saxony struggled to extract <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a></a> or <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)">silver</a></a> from cobalt ores; instead of metal, smelting produced useless dark powders (cobalt oxide) and released foul, poisonous fumes of volatile <a href="/school1/ptable/element/As" class="wiki-link" title="Arsenic (As)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/As" class="wiki-link" title="Arsenic (As)">arsenic</a></a> oxide. Believing mischievous spirits had cursed the ore, miners dubbed it "cobalt"—a term recorded by Renaissance alchemists Paracelsus, Georgius Agricola, and Basil Valentine.</p>
<p>Swedish chemist <b>Georg Brandt</b> discovered cobalt as a distinct new element around 1735. Brandt demonstrated that cobalt was a previously unknown "semi-metal"—distinct from <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Bi" class="wiki-link" title="Bismuth (Bi)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Bi" class="wiki-link" title="Bismuth (Bi)">bismuth</a></a>—and proved that cobalt compounds were the true source of the blue color in glass (which had previously been misattributed to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Bi" class="wiki-link" title="Bismuth (Bi)">bismuth</a>). Cobalt holds a historic milestone as the very first metal discovered in modern history; all previously known metals (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)">silver</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)">gold</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">mercury</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sn" class="wiki-link" title="Tin (Sn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sn" class="wiki-link" title="Tin (Sn)">tin</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">lead</a></a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Bi" class="wiki-link" title="Bismuth (Bi)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Bi" class="wiki-link" title="Bismuth (Bi)">bismuth</a></a>) had been used since prehistoric times without recorded discoverers.</p>
<p>During the 19th century, large-scale production of cobalt blue pigment (cobalt oxide combined with alumina) and smalt (powdered cobalt glass used for ceramics) flourished at Norway\'s Blaafarveværket, following earlier 16th-century smalt mining across Norway, Sweden, Saxony, and Hungary. Global mining shifted to New Caledonia in 1864, Ontario (Canada) in 1904, and the massive <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a>-cobalt deposits of Katanga Province in the Democratic Republic of the Congo in 1914. When the 1978 Shaba conflict briefly disrupted Katanga\'s mines, global markets adapted quickly through recycling and cobalt-free alternatives.</p>
<p>In physics, John Livingood and Glenn T. Seaborg discovered the radioisotope cobalt-60 in 1938. In 1956, physicist Chien-Shiung Wu used cobalt-60 nuclei at Columbia University in her landmark experiment that disproved the law of conservation of parity in weak nuclear beta decay. Following World War II, growing military demand for cobalt in jet engine superalloys led the United States to open major domestic mines at Blackbird Canyon, Idaho. In the 21st century, cobalt has become a vital strategic resource driving rechargeable <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a>-ion batteries, high-temperature superalloys, and green energy technology.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Stable <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a></a> is forged deep inside dying massive stars and supernova explosions through the r-process of nuclear fusion. It makes up approximately 0.0029% (29 parts per million) of the Earth\'s crust, where it is almost always found in close association with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a></a>. Both elements are signature components of meteoric <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a>, where metallic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a>-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> alloys remain shielded from atmospheric <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> and moisture. In Earth\'s oceans, dissolved <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a> readily reacts with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cl" class="wiki-link" title="Chlorine (Cl)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cl" class="wiki-link" title="Chlorine (Cl)">chlorine</a></a>.</p>
<p>On land, cobalt occurs bound in chemical compounds within <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a></a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a> mineral deposits. Its major natural minerals include the sulfides cobaltite (CoAsS), safflorite (CoAs<sub>2</sub>), glaucodot ((Co,Fe)AsS), and skutterudite (CoAs<sub>3</sub>). In the rich <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> beds of Katanga, the mineral cattierite occurs alongside vaesite. When exposed to air and water, these mineral sulfides weather into striking pink erythrite (also known as "cobalt glance," Co<sub>3</sub>(AsO<sub>4</sub>)<sub>2</sub>·8H<sub>2</sub>O) and spherocobaltite (CoCO<sub>3</sub>). Trace amounts of cobalt compounds exist throughout rocks, soil, plants, and animals worldwide. Pure uncombined natural cobalt metal is exceedingly rare on Earth, but has been reported in rock cores from the Kola Superdeep Borehole and lunar rock samples returned by the Soviet Luna 24 mission, likely formed by high-energy meteorite impacts. Cobalt is also present in tobacco smoke, as tobacco plants absorb heavy metals from soil into their leaves.</p>
<p>In India, cobalt is recognized as a strategic critical mineral essential for clean energy and defense. While primary cobalt deposits are scarce, India possesses significant potential secondary reserves associated with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> ores. The most prominent occurrence is in the <b>Sukinda Valley of Odisha</b>, where cobalt is contained within nickeliferous limonite overburden generated during chromite mining. Cobalt occurrences are also documented in the <b>Khetri Copper Belt of Rajasthan</b> (within minerals like cobaltite and cobalt-bearing pentlandite at the Madan-Kudan mine), the <b>Singhbhum Copper Belt of Jharkhand</b>, and ultramafic ophiolite rocks in <b>Nagaland</b>, which are actively evaluated by the Geological Survey of India (GSI).</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'Applications' => '<p>Global consumption of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a></a> reached 116,000 tonnes in 2016 and continues to expand rapidly. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">Cobalt</a>\'s unique combination of high melting point, strength at elevated temperatures, and magnetic properties makes it indispensable across modern technology:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Rechargeable Batteries:</b> <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">Cobalt</a> is a core component of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a>-ion battery cathodes (such as <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">Lithium</a> Cobalt Oxide, LiCoO<sub>2</sub>, and NMC formulations), providing high energy density and thermal stability for smartphones, laptops, and electric vehicles. In India, under the National Mission on Transformative Mobility and Battery Storage, premier institutes like CSIR-CECRI (Central Electrochemical Research Institute) and the IITs are actively researching advanced cobalt cathode chemistry and battery recycling.</li>
<li><b>Superalloys & Aerospace:</b> Cobalt-base superalloys maintain exceptional mechanical strength, tough corrosion resistance, and structural stability at temperatures above 1,000 °C. They are essential for jet engine turbine blades, industrial gas turbines, and rocket thrusters. In India, institutes like DMRL and ISRO utilize cobalt superalloys for aerospace engines and rocket hardware.</li>
<li><b>Permanent Magnets:</b> Cobalt is alloyed with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a> to make powerful Alnico magnets, and with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sm" class="wiki-link" title="Samarium (Sm)">samarium</a> to produce high-performance <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sm" class="wiki-link" title="Samarium (Sm)">Samarium</a>-Cobalt (SmCo) magnets used in aerospace sensors, electric motors, and defense electronics.</li>
<li><b>Industrial Catalysts & Pigments:</b> Cobalt catalysts are used extensively in petroleum refining (hydrodesulfurization to remove <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> from fuels) and chemical synthesis. Cobalt oxide continues to power brilliant blue pigments (cobalt blue and smalt) for ceramics, glassmaking, and paints.</li>
</ul>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Biological role' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">Cobalt</a></a> is an essential ultratrace element required for the health and metabolism of humans and animals. Its primary biological function is as the central metal atom in <b>cobalamin</b>, universally known as <b>Vitamin B<sub>12</sub></b>. Neither plants nor animals can synthesize Vitamin B<sub>12</sub> on their own; it is produced exclusively by specialized bacteria and archaea. In ruminant animals such as cows, sheep, and buffaloes, microbes residing in the stomach convert dietary <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a> salts into Vitamin B<sub>12</sub>. Maintaining a minimum <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a> level in grazing soils (recommending a daily livestock intake of 0.20 mg/kg) is therefore vital for livestock health.</p>
<p>In cobalamin-based proteins, a ring structure called corrin holds the cobalt atom. Coenzyme B<sub>12</sub> features a rare, highly reactive <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a>-cobalt (C–Co) chemical bond that participates directly in enzymatic reactions. In humans, Vitamin B<sub>12</sub> operates via two main active forms:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Methylcobalamin (MeB<sub>12</sub>):</b> Facilitates methyl (−CH<sub>3</sub>) group transfers essential for DNA synthesis and cellular replication.</li>
<li><b>Adenosylcobalamin:</b> Catalyzes molecular rearrangements involving direct <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> transfers. For example, the enzyme methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (MUT) relies on adenosylcobalamin to convert methylmalonyl-CoA into succinyl-CoA—a crucial metabolic step for extracting energy from fats and proteins.</li>
</ul>
<p>Beyond Vitamin B<sub>12</sub>, biological systems utilize other non-corrin cobaltoproteins where cobalt binds directly to the protein structure. These include <i>methionine aminopeptidase 2</i> (an essential human and mammalian enzyme) and <i>nitrile hydratase</i> (a bacterial enzyme that metabolizes nitriles).</p>
<p>In India, where a large population follows a traditional vegetarian diet, Vitamin B<sub>12</sub> nutrition is a significant focus of public health research supported by ICMR (Indian Council of Medical Research). Because plants do not produce cobalamin, dairy products derived from ruminant livestock—whose gut bacteria process cobalt—serve as a principal dietary source of Vitamin B<sub>12</sub> for millions across the nation.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
