    'category' => 'transition metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">Nickel</a></a></b> (symbol <b>Ni</b>, atomic number 28) is a lustrous, silvery-white transition metal with a subtle, warm golden tinge. Renowned for its exceptional hardness, ductility, and remarkable ability to resist heat and corrosion, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a> is an unsung hero of chemistry and modern technology. Locked deep within Earth\'s metallic core alongside <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a>, it is also a centerpiece of cosmic chemistry and ancient history—from 2nd-century BCE Greco-Bactrian cupronickel coins minted in ancient India\'s northwestern frontier to high-performance superalloys powering ISRO space missions today. Whether protecting household cutlery from rust, energizing electric vehicle batteries, or serving as an essential micro-nutrient for soil microbes, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a> seamlessly bridges Earth\'s deep geological history with futuristic green technology.</p>',
