      'Isotopes' => '<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">

=== Isotopes ===
</div>
<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: Isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a></a></div>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">Nickel</a> isotopes span a wide range of atomic masses, from lightweight <sup>48</sup>Ni all the way up to heavy <sup>82</sup>Ni. In nature, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a> is composed of five stable isotopes: <sup>58</sup>Ni, <sup>60</sup>Ni, <sup>61</sup>Ni, <sup>62</sup>Ni, and <sup>64</sup>Ni. The most abundant of these is <b>nickel-58</b>, which makes up about 68.077% of all natural nickel on Earth.</p><p>Among all atomic nuclei in the universe, <b>nickel-62</b> holds a mind-boggling distinction: it possesses the highest nuclear binding energy per nucleon of any known nuclide (8.7946 MeV/nucleon). Nuclear binding energy is the "cosmic glue" that holds an atom\'s protons and neutrons together, making <sup>62</sup>Ni the most tightly bound nucleus in existence! Although popular science often incorrectly gives this crown to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a>-56 or <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>-58, nickel-62 is the true nuclear champion. Despite this, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> remains far more abundant in the universe because extreme heat and high-energy light inside giant stars break nickel apart (a process called photodisintegration) before it can accumulate in large amounts.</p><p>Nickel also plays a fascinating role in planetary history. <b>Nickel-60</b> is the decay product of the extinct radioactive isotope iron-60 (which had a half-life of 2.6 million years). By measuring tiny variations of <sup>60</sup>Ni in meteorites, scientists can peer back 4.5 billion years to reconstruct the birth of our Solar System. In total, nuclear physicists have characterized 26 radioactive nickel isotopes. The longest-lived are <b>nickel-59</b> (half-life of 76,000 years), <b>nickel-63</b> (100.1 years), and <b>nickel-56</b> (6.1 days). Most other radioisotopes fall apart in less than 60 hours, and many in under 30 seconds. Nickel also exhibits one excited metastable state.</p><p>In astrophysics, radioactive <b>nickel-56</b> is forged during the intense <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a>-burning phase of dying giant stars and released during explosive Type Ia supernovae. As <sup>56</sup>Ni decays into <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a></a>-56 and eventually stable <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>-56 via electron capture, it releases energy that powers the brilliant glowing light curves of supernovae observed across distant galaxies. Meanwhile, long-lived <sup>59</sup>Ni acts as a geological clock: cosmogenic <sup>59</sup>Ni created by space radiation helps scientists date how long meteorites have rested on Earth and measure cosmic dust buried in ancient polar ice sheets and ocean sediments.</p><p>At the extreme frontiers of nuclear physics lie two "doubly magic" isotopes: <b>nickel-48</b> (discovered in 1999) and <b>nickel-78</b> (half-life 110 milliseconds). Nickel-48 contains 28 protons and 20 neutrons, making it the most proton-rich heavy nucleus known, while <sup>78</sup>Ni (28 protons, 50 neutrons) is a vital building block in supernova element creation (r-process). Both isotopes possess filled nuclear shells that make them remarkably stable despite extreme proton-neutron imbalances.</p><p>On Earth, <b>nickel-63</b> is created in nuclear reactors when <sup>62</sup>Ni absorbs neutrons. Because it emits steady streams of high-speed electrons (beta particles), <sup>63</sup>Ni is used in specialized high-speed electronic switches called krytrons and is being researched to build long-lasting, miniaturized betavoltaic nuclear batteries for space probes and medical implants.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">

=== Occurrence ===
</div>
<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">See also: Ore genesis and Category:<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">Nickel</a></a> minerals</div>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/6ffc41fc5bfe79dfd983b271ad437f91.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="188" class="mw-file-element"  data-file-width="3264" data-file-height="2448" /><figcaption>Widmanstätten pattern showing the two forms of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a></a>–<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a>, kamacite and taenite, in an octahedrite meteorite</figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">Nickel</a> is a widespread element on Earth and across the cosmos. Geophysical evidence indicates that the vast majority of Earth\'s nickel is locked deep inside the planet\'s liquid outer core and solid inner core, where it exists as an alloy mixed with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a>. In space, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>-nickel alloys are common in metallic meteorites, existing as two primary mineral forms: <b>kamacite</b> (containing 5% to 10% nickel) and <b>taenite</b> (containing 20% to 65% nickel). When meteorites are polished and etched with acid, these alloys display geometric cross-hatched patterns known as Widmanstätten patterns. Nickel in meteorites was first scientifically identified in 1799 by French chemist Joseph-Louis Proust while analyzing a meteorite sample from Campo del Cielo, Argentina.</p><p>On Earth\'s crust, identified land-based nickel resources containing 1% nickel or greater exceed 130 million tonnes—roughly double the current known economic reserves. Geologists classify nickel deposits into two main types: <b>laterites</b> (oxides) which make up about 60% of land reserves, and <b>sulfide deposits</b> which account for 40%. Oxide ores include nickeliferous limonite, (Fe,Ni)O(OH), and garnierite (a hydrous nickel silicate mixture). Sulfide ores primarily consist of pentlandite and pyrrhotite, (Fe,Ni)<sub>9</sub>S<sub>8</sub> and (Fe,Ni)<sub>7</sub>S<sub>6</sub>, along with millerite and the nickel-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/As" class="wiki-link" title="Arsenic (As)">arsenic</a> mineral nickeline.</p><p>In India, the primary repository of nickel is located in the famous <b>Sukinda Valley</b> in the Jajpur district of Odisha. Sukinda holds over 95% of India\'s nickel resources, primarily stored as nickeliferous laterite and within Chromite Overburden (COB)—waste rock produced during <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cr" class="wiki-link" title="Chromium (Cr)">chromium</a> mining containing 0.4% to 0.9% nickel. Nickel deposits are also found alongside <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a></a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/U" class="wiki-link" title="Uranium (U)">uranium</a> mineralization in the historic Singhbhum Shear Zone around Jaduguda and Ghatsila in Jharkhand, as well as in Malanjkhand in Madhya Pradesh.</p>',
      'History' => '<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">

=== History ===
</div>
<p>The human connection with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a> spans thousands of years, starting long before anyone knew <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a> existed as a distinct element. Unintentional use of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a> dates back to at least 3500 BCE, when ancient bronzes from Syria were crafted containing up to 2% nickel. In ancient China, metalworkers produced a prized silvery alloy known as <i>baitong</i> ("white <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a>") around 1700–1400 BCE. Exported to Europe as <i>paktong</i> in the 17th century, its true nickel content was not scientifically proved until 1822.</p><p>One of the most extraordinary ancient numismatic breakthroughs occurred in the 2nd century BCE in the northwestern region of the Indian subcontinent (ancient Bactria and Gandhara). Greco-Bactrian kings—such as <b>Agathocles</b>, <b>Euthydemus II</b>, and <b>Pantaleon</b>—issued striking coins made from a cupronickel alloy containing approximately 75% <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a></a> and 25% nickel. These ancient didrachms and chalkons featured images of Dionysos and panthers, representing the first intentional use of cupronickel coinage in human history—sharing the exact 75:25 <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a>-to-nickel ratio used in modern U.S. nickel coins two millennia later!</p>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/04795ef517c561d3ef96e6c67b2046bc.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="182" class="mw-file-element"  data-file-width="2452" data-file-height="1789" /><figcaption>Nickeline/niccolite</figcaption></figure>
<p>In medieval Germany, miners working in the Ore Mountains discovered a reddish mineral that looked promisingly like copper ore. However, no matter how hard they tried to smelt it, they could extract no copper at all. Frustrated, the miners blamed a mischievous sprite of German folklore named <i>Nickel</i> (similar to "Old Nick" or the devil) for cursing the ore, calling it <i>Kupfernickel</i> ("copper-demon"). Today, this mineral is known as nickeline or niccolite (nickel arsenide, NiAs).</p><p>In 1751, Swedish chemist and mineralogist <b>Baron Axel Fredrik Cronstedt</b> attempted to extract copper from kupfernickel at a <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a></a> mine in Los, Sweden. Instead of copper, he isolated a brand-new silvery-white metal. Cronstedt named the element <b>nickel</b> after the mythical mine spirit that gave the mineral its name. Initially, nickel was produced in small quantities as a byproduct of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a> blue pigment manufacturing (starting in 1824). Large-scale industrial smelting began in Norway in 1848, and global demand exploded after 1889 when nickel was added to steel. Major mining booms followed the discovery of laterite ores in New Caledonia (1865), and massive sulfide deposits in Canada\'s Sudbury Basin (1883), Russia\'s Norilsk-Talnakh (1920), and South Africa\'s Merensky Reef (1924).</p>',
      'Applications' => '<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">

=== Applications ===
</div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/83fbf5ece5398c701713731eb4350bd2.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="453" class="mw-file-element"  data-file-width="587" data-file-height="1064" /><figcaption><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">Nickel</a></a> foam (top) and its internal structure (bottom)</figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">Nickel</a> is predominantly an alloying metal. Globally, about 68% of all refined <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a> goes into manufacturing <b>stainless steel</b>, 10% into nonferrous alloys, 9% into electroplating, 7% into alloy steel, 3% into foundries, and 4% into other products including rechargeable batteries. Across end-use industries, engineering accounts for 27%, metal goods 20%, transport 14%, tubular products 14%, electronics 11%, building and construction 10%, and miscellaneous uses 5%.</p><p>Nickel adds exceptional toughness, tensile strength, and corrosion resistance to metals. It is essential in everyday goods—from stainless steel cookware, coins, and electric guitar strings to microphone capsules, plumbing fixtures, and specialized magnetic alloys like Permalloy, Invar, and Elinvar. Historically, nickel was used to forge traditional ceremonial weapons across Southeast Asia (such as ornate <i>Kris</i> daggers with patterned <i>pamor</i> blades made from meteoric <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>). Because of its corrosion resistance, nickel was also used as a <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)">silver</a> substitute and cheap coinage metal.</p>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/d8a76ff81251c12a9a4a28cd3c63a5c9.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="255" class="mw-file-element"  data-file-width="470" data-file-height="480" /><figcaption>A "horseshoe magnet" made of alnico nickel alloy</figcaption></figure>
<p>In high technology and energy, nickel plays a massive role. <b>Alnico magnets</b> (made of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a>, nickel, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a>) create strong permanent magnetic fields. Porous nickel foam and mesh serve as gas diffusion electrodes in alkaline fuel cells. Nickel catalysts, especially finely divided <b>Raney nickel</b> (a nickel-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a> alloy), are widely used to hydrogenate unsaturated vegetable oils into margarine. In medicine, studies (such as Forte et al.) have investigated trace nickel levels in human blood (0.89 ng/mL in type 2 diabetics vs 0.77 ng/mL in controls). Nickel is also magnetostrictive—contracting slightly by about 50 parts per million when exposed to a magnetic field—and acts as a tough binder (6% to 12% by weight) in cemented <a href="/school1/ptable/element/W" class="wiki-link" title="Tungsten (W)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/W" class="wiki-link" title="Tungsten (W)">tungsten</a></a> carbide tools.</p><p>In mining, nickel acts as an effective fire assay collector for <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pt" class="wiki-link" title="Platinum (Pt)">Platinum</a> Group Elements (PGE: <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pt" class="wiki-link" title="Platinum (Pt)">platinum</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pd" class="wiki-link" title="Palladium (Pd)">palladium</a>, etc.) and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)">gold</a></a>. In advanced materials, <b>Nitinol</b> (a nickel-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a></a> alloy) exhibits incredible shape memory and superelasticity, springing back to its original shape when heated. In 2025, QuesTek Innovations and Stoke Space developed 3D-printed nickel superalloys engineered for reusable spacecraft engines operating in extreme high-pressure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> environments (such as the Zenith full-flow staged combustion engine).</p><p>India holds a vital position in nickel science and strategic engineering. Institutions like <b>CSIR-NML</b> (National Metallurgical Laboratory, Jamshedpur) and <b>CSIR-IMMT</b> (Bhubaneswar) have pioneered indigenous hydrometallurgical leaching, reduction roasting, and bio-metallurgical processes using fungi like <i>Aspergillus niger</i> to extract nickel and ferro-nickel from Sukinda\'s low-grade chromite overburden, transforming mining waste into strategic wealth. Defense public sector enterprise <b>MIDHANI</b> (Mishra Dhatu Nigam Limited, Hyderabad) manufactures specialized nickel-based superalloys (such as Superni, Inconel, and Nimonic) crucial for DRDO defense systems, jet engines (LCA Tejas), and ISRO rocket engines (GSLV, Chandrayaan, and Gaganyaan space missions). Furthermore, institutions like <b>IGCAR</b> (Kalpakkam) and <b>BARC</b> rely on nickel superalloys for structural integrity in India\'s <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a>-cooled Fast Breeder Reactors.</p>',
      'Biological role' => '<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">

=== Biological role ===
</div>
<p>Although its biological importance was not recognized until the 1970s, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a> is now known to be an essential trace element for plants, bacteria, archaea, and fungi. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">Nickel</a> sits at the active core of several critical enzymes. The enzyme <b>urease</b> uses <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a> to hydrolyze urea into ammonia and carbamate; in pathogenic bacteria, urease acts as a key virulence factor. <b>NiFe hydrogenases</b> use nickel and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> to catalyze the conversion of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> gas (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">H<sub>2</sub></a>) into protons and electrons (and vice versa).</p><p>In methanogenic archaea, a nickel-tetrapyrrole coenzyme known as <b>cofactor F430</b> powers the enzyme methyl coenzyme M reductase, which produces methane gas. Other nickel-containing enzymes include <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> monoxide dehydrogenase (featuring an Fe-Ni-S cluster), a rare bacterial class of superoxide dismutase, and glyoxalase I enzymes found in bacteria and trypanosomal parasites (which use Ni<sup>2+</sup> instead of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a></a>).</p><p>In human health, dietary nickel may act as a prebiotic by nourishing beneficial bacteria in the large intestine. The U.S. Institute of Medicine has not established a Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) for humans, but sets a tolerable upper intake limit of 1 mg/day for soluble nickel salts. Typical dietary intake ranges between 70 and 100 μg/day, of which less than 10% is absorbed and excreted via urine. Small amounts of nickel leach into food cooked in stainless steel cookware—for example, an acidic serving of tomato sauce leaches an average of 88 μg of nickel after 10 cooking cycles.</p><p>On a planetary scale, nickel played a dramatic role in Earth\'s worst mass extinction! Massive volcanic eruptions of the Siberian Traps 252 million years ago flooded the oceans with nickel. This sudden nutrient boom triggered an explosive population growth of methane-producing archaea (<i>Methanosarcina</i>), releasing vast clouds of methane gas into the atmosphere. This led to extreme global warming and ocean acidification, causing the Permian–Triassic extinction event that wiped out over 90% of all species on Earth.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Cu' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Cu',
    'name' => 'Copper',
