    'category' => 'transition metal',
    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 29 (Cu)',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">Copper</a></a></b> is a chemical element with the symbol <b>Cu</b> and atomic number <b>29</b>. Known in ancient Sanskrit texts as <i>Tamra</i> (ताम्र), <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> is a soft, malleable, and ductile transition metal renowned for its warm reddish-orange metallic luster and exceptional ability to conduct heat and electricity. As one of the very few metals that occurs naturally in pure metallic form, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> has been shaped by human hands for over 10,000 years—from the ancient Indus Valley Civilization, where it produced bronze artwork and durable <i>Tamra-patra</i> (copper plate inscriptions), to the modern world, where it powers electrical grids, microelectronics, and renewable energy. Beyond its industrial brilliance, copper is an essential biological micronutrient for all living organisms and possesses natural antimicrobial properties long celebrated in traditional Ayurvedic practices such as <i>Tamra Jal</i>.</p>',
