    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/07e0f771ff81abafdfedf01389073169.JPG" decoding="async" width="180" height="178" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.7" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright"  data-file-width="3616" data-file-height="3584" /><figcaption>A <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a></a> disc (99.95% pure) made by continuous casting; etched to reveal crystallites</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/743b6be6f1f8c340a6fcf46d8f9f9b75.gif" decoding="async" width="180" height="115" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.7" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright"  data-file-width="306" data-file-height="196" /><figcaption><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">Copper</a></a> just above its melting point keeps its pink luster color when enough light outshines the orange incandescence color.</figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">Copper</a></a> is a soft, highly malleable, and remarkably ductile transition metal with a distinctive pinkish-orange metallic sheen when freshly cut or polished. Along with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)">gold</a></a>, it is one of the only two non-grey metallic elements in the periodic table. When copper melts at 1084.62 °C (1357.77 K), it retains its rich pinkish luster provided external light outshines its glowing orange incandescence.</p>
<p>Among non-precious metals, copper boasts unmatched electrical and thermal conductivity, second only to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)">silver</a></a>. This exceptional conductivity arises from its unique electron configuration ([Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹), in which a single electron in the outermost 4s shell can move with extreme ease through the metal\'s crystal lattice. Pure copper is physically soft and weak under mechanical stress, but its strength and hardness can be dramatically enhanced by mixing it into versatile alloys.</p>
<p>While pure copper does not react with pure water, prolonged exposure to moist atmospheric air causes it to slowly react with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a></a> dioxide, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> compounds. This oxidation process forms a protective, pale-green layer of basic copper carbonate known as a patina or verdigris. This green coating acts as a durable shield, protecting the underlying metal from further corrosion for centuries—a feature visible on historic copper domes, architectural monuments, and ancient Indian statues.</p>
<p>Copper forms several essential metal alloys. Combining copper with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a></a> yields brass, while fusing it with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sn" class="wiki-link" title="Tin (Sn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sn" class="wiki-link" title="Tin (Sn)">tin</a></a> creates bronze. Alloying copper with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a></a> produces cupronickel, prized for marine hardware and coinage, as well as constantan, an alloy whose stable electrical resistance across temperature shifts makes it indispensable in thermocouples and strain gauges. Sterling <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)">silver</a> contains 7.5% copper blended with pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)">silver</a> to add structural rigidity for jewelry and tableware.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Isotopes' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: Isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a></a></div>
<p>Scientists have identified 29 distinct isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a></a>, ranging from mass numbers 52 to 80. Natural <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> on Earth is composed of two stable isotopes: <b><sup>63</sup>Cu</b>, which makes up roughly 69.15% of naturally occurring copper, and <b><sup>65</sup>Cu</b>, which accounts for the remaining 30.85%. Both of these stable nuclei possess a nuclear spin of 3/2.</p>
<p>The remaining 27 copper isotopes are radioactive. The most stable radioisotope is <b><sup>67</sup>Cu</b>, with a half-life of 61.83 hours. Researchers have also characterized 10 metastable nuclear isomers, among which <b><sup>68m</sup>Cu</b> is the longest-lived, boasting a half-life of 3.8 minutes. Radioactive copper isotopes display clear decay patterns based on their mass numbers: isotopes lighter than mass 64 decay via positron emission (β⁺) into <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a></a>, whereas isotopes heavier than mass 64 undergo electron emission (β⁻) into <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a></a>. Sitting right in the middle is <b><sup>64</sup>Cu</b> (half-life of 12.7 hours), which is unique because it decays through both β⁺ and β⁻ pathways.</p>
<p>Radioactive copper isotopes play a crucial role in modern nuclear medicine. In particular, <b><sup>62</sup>Cu</b> (used in the radiopharmaceutical <sup>62</sup>Cu-PTSM) and <b><sup>64</sup>Cu</b> serve as essential positron-emitting tracers in Positron Emission Tomography (PET) scanning, enabling doctors to monitor cardiac blood flow, evaluate brain tissue perfusion, and target cancerous tumors with extreme precision.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Occurrence' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">See also: List of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a></a> ores</div>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">Copper</a></a> is forged deep within massive exploding stars (supernovae) and stellar nucleosynthesis. It ranks as the 26th most abundant element in the Earth\'s crust, occurring at an average concentration of about 50 parts per million (ppm)—making it less abundant than <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a></a> (75 ppm) but far more plentiful than <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">lead</a></a> (14 ppm). Typical background concentrations of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> are around 1 ng/m³ in atmospheric air, 150 mg/kg in soil, 30 mg/kg in plant vegetation, 2 μg/L in freshwater, and 0.5 μg/L in ocean seawater.</p>
<p>In nature, copper occurs both as pure native copper metal and within a rich spectrum of minerals. Major copper-bearing minerals include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Sulfides:</b> Chalcopyrite (CuFeS₂, the principal commercial ore), bornite ("peacock ore"), digenite, covellite, and chalcocite.</li>
  <li><b>Sulfosalts:</b> Tetrahedrite-tennantite and enargite.</li>
  <li><b>Carbonates:</b> Azurite (brilliant deep blue) and malachite (vibrant green).</li>
  <li><b>Oxides:</b> Cuprite (copper(I) oxide, Cu₂O) and tenorite (copper(II) oxide, CuO).</li>
</ul>
<p>While native copper usually exists as polycrystalline masses, spectacular individual specimens have been uncovered throughout history. The largest single mass of native elemental copper ever discovered was unearthed in 1857 on the Keweenaw Peninsula in Michigan, USA, weighing an extraordinary 420 tonnes. The largest documented single crystal of native copper measured 4.4 × 3.2 × 3.2 cm.</p>
<p>In India, copper mining has a rich geographical and geological significance. The largest single contiguous copper ore deposit in India is the <b>Malanjkhand Copper Belt</b>, located in the Balaghat district of Madhya Pradesh. Holding nearly 300 million tonnes of ore reserves, Malanjkhand is operated by the public sector enterprise <b>Hindustan Copper Limited (HCL)</b> and accounts for over 70% of India\'s total indigenous copper ore production. Other historic mining centers include the <b>Khetri Copper Belt</b> in Rajasthan and the <b>Singhbhum Copper Belt</b> in Jharkhand. To efficiently extract copper from lean Indian ores, premier research institutions like the <b>CSIR-National Metallurgical Laboratory (CSIR-NML)</b> in Jamshedpur pioneered advanced mineral flotation flowsheets and eco-friendly bioleaching techniques using thermophilic bacteria.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2">',
      'Biological role' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">Copper</a></a> in biology</div>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">Copper</a></a> is an essential trace micronutrient vital for the survival of all living organisms, including plants, animals, fungi, and micro-organisms. In the human body, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> acts as a non-negotiable cofactor for key enzymes that drive critical biological functions:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Cytochrome c oxidase:</b> The key enzyme responsible for cellular energy (ATP) production inside mitochondria.</li>
  <li><b>Superoxide dismutase:</b> A critical antioxidant enzyme that neutralizes toxic free radicals and protects cellular DNA from oxidative damage.</li>
  <li><b>Tyrosinase:</b> Essential for synthesizing melanin, the pigment responsible for skin, hair, and eye coloration.</li>
  <li><b>Ceruloplasmin:</b> Transports over 95% of total copper in human blood plasma and regulates body <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a> metabolism.</li>
</ul>
<p>In nature\'s design, copper plays a breathtaking respiratory role. While humans and mammals rely on <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>-centered hemoglobin to carry <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> in red blood cells, many invertebrates—such as octopuses, snails, spiders, and crustaceans—use copper-based proteins called <b>hemocyanin</b>. When bound to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a>, hemocyanin turns a striking royal blue color, giving these creatures true "blue blood."</p>
<p>Copper is also famous for its potent <b>oligodynamic effect</b>—a natural antimicrobial phenomenon where trace copper ions dissolve into liquids and rapidly destroy bacteria, viruses, and fungi by rupturing their cell membranes and damaging their nucleic acids. Solid copper surfaces kill over 99.9% of harmful bacteria, including <i>E. coli</i>, <i>Salmonella</i>, and superbugs like MRSA, within two hours of contact.</p>
<p>This biological purity was recognized thousands of years ago in India. Ancient Ayurvedic treatises, including the <b>Charaka Samhita</b> and <b>Sushruta Samhita</b>, extolled the virtues of <i>Tamra</i> (copper) for purifying water and maintaining health. The classical practice of storing clean water overnight in a pure copper vessel—known as <b>Tamra Jal</b>—allows trace copper ions to naturally sterilize the water, boost digestive fire (<i>Agni</i>), and balance the body\'s three doshas (Vata, Pitta, and Kapha). Modern scientific studies confirm that this traditional practice effectively neutralizes waterborne microbial pathogens.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'History' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">Copper</a></a> holds a legendary place in human history as one of the earliest metals ever mastered by civilization, alongside <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)">gold</a></a> and meteoritic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a>. Because <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> occurs naturally in pure metallic form, early humans began fashioning <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> tools, beads, and ornaments over 10,000 years ago (c. 8000 BCE). The era following the Neolithic period is known as the Chalcolithic Age (or Copper Age), marking humanity\'s transition from stone tools to metallurgy. Around 3500 BCE, ancient metalsmiths discovered that smelting copper ore with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sn" class="wiki-link" title="Tin (Sn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sn" class="wiki-link" title="Tin (Sn)">tin</a></a> produced bronze—a much harder alloy that ushered in the Bronze Age and transformed tools, armor, and architecture.</p>
<p>The English word <i>copper</i> stems from the Old English <i>coper</i>, which derives from the Latin <i>cuprum</i>—a shortened form of <i>aes cyprium</i> ("metal of Cyprus"). The Romans mined vast quantities of high-grade copper on the Mediterranean island of Cyprus, establishing it as the primary supplier of the ancient Western world.</p>
<p>In India, copper has an ancient metallurgical heritage spanning over five millennia:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Harappan Metallurgy (c. 3300–1300 BCE):</b> Artisans of the Indus Valley (Harappan) Civilization extracted copper ore from the ancient <b>Khetri mines</b> in Rajasthan to craft weapons, household utensils, and artistic masterpieces. They perfected the <i>cire perdue</i> (lost-wax) casting technique, creating world-famous artifacts such as the 4,500-year-old bronze statuette of the <i>Dancing Girl of Mohenjo-daro</i>.</li>
  <li><b>Tamra-patra (Copper Plate Inscriptions):</b> For over two thousand years across ancient and medieval India, kings from the Maurya, Gupta, and Chola empires recorded royal decrees, land grants, and legal contracts on durable copper plates known as <i>Tamra-patra</i> (or <i>Tamra-shasana</i>). Indian rulers chose copper because it resists rusting and atmospheric decay, ensuring that historical records survived intact for centuries (such as the Maurya-era Sohgaura copper plate).</li>
  <li><b>The Sultanganj Buddha:</b> Cast during the Gupta-Pala transitional period (c. 500–700 CE) in Bihar, the magnificent <b>Sultanganj Buddha</b> stands 2.3 meters tall and weighs over 500 kg. It remains the largest complete ancient pure-copper statue in the world, representing an extraordinary pinnacle of ancient Indian metallurgical engineering and lost-wax metal casting.</li>
</ul>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
      'Applications' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">See also: <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">Copper</a></a> in renewable energy</div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/00f8286e62f90b3ab06dac437ce33ea5.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="167" class="mw-file-element"  data-file-width="1200" data-file-height="800" /><figcaption><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">Copper</a></a> fittings for soldered plumbing joints</figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">Copper</a></a> is an indispensable material in modern industry, architecture, and technology. Its global consumption is divided among four main sectors:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Electrical Wire & Electronics (60%):</b> Power distribution grids, household wiring, electric motor windings, transformers, and printed circuit board traces rely heavily on copper\'s supreme electrical conductivity.</li>
  <li><b>Roofing & Plumbing (20%):</b> Corrosion-resistant copper pipes, fittings, flashing, and architectural cladding provide durable, leak-free water supply systems and timeless building exteriors.</li>
  <li><b>Industrial Machinery (15%):</b> Heat exchangers, industrial boilers, hydraulic lines, and radiator cores utilize copper\'s outstanding thermal conductivity.</li>
  <li><b>Alloys & Specialty Uses (5%):</b> Brass, bronze, cupronickel, and coinage metals.</li>
</ul>
<p>For over two centuries, copper-based anti-fouling paints have been applied to marine boat hulls to suppress the growth of algae, barnacles, and shellfish. In agriculture, copper compounds such as copper sulfate (Bordeaux mixture) serve as essential fungicides and soil nutritional supplements. In culinary arts, copper cookware is prized by chefs for its fast, uniform heat distribution (such as whipping egg whites into voluminous peaks), though copper pots are usually lined with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sn" class="wiki-link" title="Tin (Sn)">tin</a> or stainless steel to prevent reacting with acidic foods.</p>
<p>Because pure copper is soft and ductile, machining pure copper can be tricky as copper shavings stick to cutting tools; hence, free-machining brass and copper alloys are preferred for precision machined components.</p>
<p>In modern high-tech and scientific frontiers, copper plays a central role. In the clean energy transition, electric vehicles (EVs) require up to four times more copper than conventional internal combustion cars for electric motors, battery interconnects, and charging infrastructure. In physics and material science, copper oxides form the foundation of high-temperature <b>cuprate superconductors</b>, such as <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Y" class="wiki-link" title="Yttrium (Y)">Yttrium</a> <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ba" class="wiki-link" title="Barium (Ba)">Barium</a> Copper Oxide (YBCO). Premier Indian research organizations—including the <b>Tata Institute of Fundamental Research (TIFR)</b>, <b>Indian Institute of Science (IISc) Bangalore</b>, and <b>Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC)</b>—conduct cutting-edge research on cuprate superconductors that conduct electricity with zero energy loss. Additionally, Indian scientists at IITs and CSIR laboratories are developing eco-friendly copper nanoparticle catalysts for antimicrobial surface coatings and advanced wastewater purification systems.</p>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">',
