  array (
    'symbol' => 'Zn',
    'name' => 'Zinc',
    'atomic' => 30,
    'category' => 'transition metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">Zinc</a></a></b> (symbol <b>Zn</b>, atomic number 30), historically known in commerce as <i>spelter</i>, is a versatile, bluish-white metal located in Group 12 of the periodic table. Chemically, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> shares a close kinship with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a></a>, forming stable ions in the +2 oxidation state with a very similar ionic size. Best known for its remarkable ability to shield steel from rust through galvanization, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> is also a critical biological element essential for all living creatures. India holds a monumental place in zinc\'s story: ancient Indian metallurgists at Zawar in Rajasthan pioneered the world\'s very first industrial-scale zinc smelting and downward distillation process over two thousand years ago, earning India historic recognition as the birthplace of pure metallic zinc extraction.</p>',
