    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/11c864b33d62911a360d5512c7839d82.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="250" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1032" data-file-height="1032" /><figcaption>Piece of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a></a> sheet</figcaption></figure><p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">Zinc</a> is a lustrous, bluish-white metal with diamagnetic magnetic properties. When exposed to moist air, its shiny surface gradually reacts to form a dull, protective layer of basic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> carbonate that prevents deeper corrosion. Zinc is somewhat less dense than <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a> (7.14 g/cm³) and crystallizes in a distorted hexagonal close-packed (hcp) crystal lattice, where each zinc atom has six nearest neighbors in its own plane at 265.9 pm and six others at a distance of 290.6 pm.</p><p>Zinc exhibits fascinating temperature-dependent mechanical properties. At room temperature, it is relatively hard and brittle. However, when warmed to between 100 °C and 150 °C, zinc undergoes a transformation, becoming remarkably malleable and ductile so that it can easily be rolled into thin sheets or shaped into complex forms. If heated further above 210 °C, it becomes extremely brittle once again and can be crushed into a fine powder simply by hammering!</p><p>As an electrical conductor, zinc performs moderately well. Unusually for a metal, zinc has a relatively low melting point of 419.53 °C (692.68 K) and a boiling point of 907 °C (1180 K). Its melting point is lower than any other d-block transition metal except <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">mercury</a></a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cd" class="wiki-link" title="Cadmium (Cd)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cd" class="wiki-link" title="Cadmium (Cd)">cadmium</a></a>. Because of their completely filled d-electron shells (3d<sup>10</sup>) and low melting points, zinc, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cd" class="wiki-link" title="Cadmium (Cd)">cadmium</a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">mercury</a> are frequently set apart from typical transition metals in detailed chemical classifications.</p><p>Zinc readily forms alloys with a vast array of metals, most famously creating brass when combined with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a></a>. It also forms binary alloys with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminium (Al)">aluminium</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sb" class="wiki-link" title="Antimony (Sb)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sb" class="wiki-link" title="Antimony (Sb)">antimony</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Bi" class="wiki-link" title="Bismuth (Bi)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Bi" class="wiki-link" title="Bismuth (Bi)">bismuth</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)">gold</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">lead</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">mercury</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)">silver</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sn" class="wiki-link" title="Tin (Sn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sn" class="wiki-link" title="Tin (Sn)">tin</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Te" class="wiki-link" title="Tellurium (Te)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Te" class="wiki-link" title="Tellurium (Te)">tellurium</a></a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a></a>. Interestingly, while neither zinc nor <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zr" class="wiki-link" title="Zirconium (Zr)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zr" class="wiki-link" title="Zirconium (Zr)">zirconium</a></a> is magnetic on its own, their intermetallic compound <b>ZrZn<sub>2</sub></b> unexpectedly becomes ferromagnetic when cooled below 35 K!</p>',
      'Physical properties' => '<p>In its physical state, elemental <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> is a solid metal with a density of 7.14 g/cm³ at room temperature, making it moderately heavy yet easy to work with in structural applications. Pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> displays a shiny, metallic, bluish-gray surface when freshly cut. It melts at 419.53 °C (692.68 K) and boils at 907 °C (1180 K). Its heat of fusion is 7.32 kJ/mol, and its heat of vaporization is 115 kJ/mol, reflecting the relatively weak metallic bonding caused by its completely filled 3d<sup>10</sup> electron subshell.</p><p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">Zinc</a>\'s electrical conductivity is roughly 27% that of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a>, allowing it to function effectively in electrical groundings, battery anodes, and protective coatings. Sound waves travel through solid zinc at approximately 3,850 m/s. Its thermal expansion coefficient is 30.2 µm/(m·K), meaning it expands predictably under heating. When alloyed, zinc dramatically lowers the melting point of companion metals, which makes zinc alloys like Zamak ideal for precision die-casting of complex shapes with tight tolerances.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img alt="A black shiny lump of solid with uneven surface" src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/cfb2b830fafbcfc1ee570ba132180147.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="231" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="372" data-file-height="343" /><figcaption>Sphalerite (ZnS)</figcaption></figure><p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">Zinc</a> makes up approximately 70 parts per million (0.007%) of Earth\'s continental crust by mass, ranking as the 24th most abundant crustal element. Across the Solar System, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> is the 22nd most abundant element, averaging 312 ppm. Background environmental concentrations of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> typically do not exceed 1 µg/m³ in air, 300 mg/kg in soil, 100 mg/kg in vegetation, 20 µg/L in freshwater, and 5 µg/L in seawater.</p><p>Geochemically, zinc is classified as a <i>chalcophile</i> element—meaning it prefers to pair up with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a></a> and heavy chalcogens rather than <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a></a> or halogens. The principal commercial ore of zinc is <b>sphalerite</b> (zinc sulfide, ZnS), which contains 60% to 62% zinc by mass and crystallized under reducing conditions in Earth\'s early crust. Other important zinc minerals include smithsonite (zinc carbonate, ZnCO<sub>3</sub>), hemimorphite (zinc silicate), wurtzite (a hexagonal crystal form of ZnS), and hydrozincite. Most of these non-sulfide minerals were formed by long-term weathering of primordial sphalerite deposits.</p><p>Globally identified zinc resources total between 1.9 and 2.8 billion tonnes, with major mining reserves located in Australia, China, Canada, the United States, and Iran. Up to 2002, approximately 346 million tonnes of zinc had been mined throughout human history, with an estimated 109 to 305 million tonnes currently in active use. Data from the United States Geological Survey (USGS) shows that even though global zinc production grew by over 80% in recent decades, ongoing exploration continuously discovers new reserves, maintaining a stable supply lifespan for future generations.</p><p><b>India\'s Geographical Relevance:</b> India is home to some of the richest zinc deposits in the world, concentrated overwhelmingly in the state of <b>Rajasthan</b>. The <b>Rampura Agucha mine</b> in Bhilwara district, operated by Hindustan Zinc Limited (HZL, a Vedanta company), is celebrated as the world\'s largest underground zinc-producing mine! Other major operating mines in Rajasthan include Zawar, Sindesar Khurd, Rajpura Dariba, and Kayad. Hindustan Zinc controls roughly 77% of India\'s primary zinc market, making India a global powerhouse in primary zinc production and metallurgy.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>Naturally occurring <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> on Earth consists of five stable isotopes: <sup>64</sup>Zn (49.17% natural abundance), <sup>66</sup>Zn (27.73%), <sup>68</sup>Zn (18.45%), <sup>67</sup>Zn (4.04%), and <sup>70</sup>Zn (0.61%). Among these, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a>-64 is by far the most abundant in nature.</p><p>Nuclear scientists have synthesized several dozen radioisotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a>. The most stable radioactive isotope is <b><sup>65</sup>Zn</b>, which has a half-life of 243.66 days, followed by <sup>72</sup>Zn with a half-life of 46.5 hours. Zinc also features 10 nuclear isomers—metastable excited states of the atomic nucleus—led by <sup>69m</sup>Zn with a half-life of 13.75 hours. The superscript <i>m</i> signifies a metastable state that eventually drops to the ground state by emitting high-energy gamma ray photons.</p><p>The decay pathways of zinc radioisotopes follow clear rules based on atomic mass:</p><p>Zinc radioisotopes lighter than mass 66 decay primarily via <b>electron capture</b>, absorbing an inner shell electron to transform into an isotope of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a></a>:</p><p style="text-align:center;">\\( \\text{}^{n}_{30}\\text{Zn} + e^{-} \\rightarrow \\text{}^{n}_{29}\\text{Cu} + \\nu_e \\)</p><p>Conversely, zinc radioisotopes heavier than mass 66 decay primarily via <b>beta-minus emission (β<sup>−</sup>)</b>, ejecting an electron and an electron antineutrino to form an isotope of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a></a>:</p><p style="text-align:center;">\\( \\text{}^{n}_{30}\\text{Zn} \\rightarrow \\text{}^{n}_{31}\\text{Ga} + e^{-} + \\bar{\\nu}_e \\)</p>',
      'History' => '<p>India holds a preeminent place in the global history of science as the undisputed birthplace of pure metallic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> production. While ancient civilizations in the Mediterranean and China knew how to make brass by smelting <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> ores together with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a>-rich minerals, isolating pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> metal was an extraordinary technical challenge. Because zinc metal boils at 907 °C—a temperature lower than what is required to reduce zinc ore—in conventional top-heated furnaces, zinc vapor instantly rises, reacts with atmospheric <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a>, and escapes as zinc oxide smoke.</p><p><b>The Zawar Distillation Revolution:</b> Ancient Indian metallurgists at <b>Zawar</b>, near Udaipur in Rajasthan, solved this ancient conundrum as early as 750 BCE by inventing a revolutionary <b>downward distillation</b> process (known in Sanskrit alchemical texts as <i>tiryakpatana yantra</i>). They packed brinjal-shaped (eggplant-shaped) clay retorts with zinc ore (sphalerite), charcoal, and organic reagents. These retorts were inverted over open furnace hearths with clay tubes extending downward into water-cooled collection vessels below. When heated from above, the zinc ore reduced to zinc vapor, which traveled downward away from the fire, condensing into droplets of pure metallic zinc!</p><p>By the 12th century CE, Zawar had grown into the world\'s first industrial-scale zinc refinery, operating hundreds of distillation furnaces simultaneously. This pioneering technology flourished in India centuries before Western Europe managed to produce metallic zinc.</p><p><b>Ancient Indian Texts &amp; Terminology:</b> In ancient Sanskrit, zinc was known as <i>Rasaka</i>, <i>Jasada</i>, or <b>Yashada</b>. Famous alchemical and medical treatises documented its production and properties:</p><ul><li><b>Rasa Ratnakara</b>, authored by the legendary sage and chemist <b>Nagarjuna</b> (c. 9th–10th century CE), contains detailed descriptions of extracting metallic zinc (<i>rasaka</i>) and preparing brass (<i>pittala</i>).</li><li><b>Rasa Ratna Samuccaya</b> (13th century) provides intricate engineering details and drawings of the <i>tiryakpatana yantra</i> furnace used for downward zinc distillation.</li><li>Ayurvedic medicine developed <b>Yashada Bhasma</b> (calcined zinc oxide ash), a revered traditional formulation used for centuries to treat eye disorders, promote skin healing, and boost immunity.</li></ul><p><b>Transmission to Europe:</b> In 1738, English metallurgist William Champion patented a downward zinc distillation method in Bristol that was virtually identical to the Zawar technique long practiced in Rajasthan. European chemists like Paracelsus introduced the name <i>zincum</i> in the 16th century (derived from German <i>Zinke</i>, meaning \'jagged teeth\', describing zinc crystals in furnaces), and German chemist Andreas Sigismund Marggraf isolated pure metallic zinc in 1746.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<ol><li><b>Galvanizing (55%):</b> Coating <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> and steel with a thin layer of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> to shield them from atmospheric rust. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">Zinc</a> acts as a sacrificial anode (cathodic protection)—even if the outer surface is scratched, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> corrodes first to preserve the <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> beneath. Galvanized <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> (GI) sheets, guardrails, utility poles, bridges, and automobile bodies rely heavily on zinc.</li><li><b>Brass and Bronze (16%):</b> Brass (an alloy of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> and zinc) is prized for its golden appearance, acoustic resonance, and low friction, making it essential for musical instruments, plumbing valves, decorative artifacts, and door locks.</li><li><b>Die-Casting Alloys (21%):</b> High-purity zinc alloys like <b>Zamak</b> (zinc, aluminium, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a>) are die-cast into precision components for automobiles, electrical housings, toys, and door handles.</li><li><b>Chemicals and Healthcare (8%):</b> Zinc oxide (ZnO) is a vital ingredient in sunscreens (reflecting harmful UV rays), rubber tire manufacturing (vulcanization), calamine lotion, diaper rash ointments, white pigments, and dietary supplements.</li></ol><p><b>Batteries &amp; Indian Energy Innovations:</b> Zinc has long powered dry-cell batteries (zinc-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> and alkaline batteries) due to its favorable electrochemical potential. Today, as the world moves toward renewable energy storage, <b>zinc-air batteries</b> are emerging as a safe, eco-friendly, and cost-effective alternative to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a>-ion batteries. In India, <b>Hindustan Zinc Limited</b> partnered with <b>IIT Madras</b> to successfully build a 1 kWh rechargeable zinc-air battery prototype for grid storage. Meanwhile, Indian researchers at <b>SASTRA Deemed University</b> (supported by DST) developed innovative silica-ZnO nanofluid electrolytes to suppress zinc corrosion, while institutes like <b>CSIR-NCL</b> and <b>IISc Bangalore</b> are actively advancing next-generation zinc-ion energy storage systems.</p>',
      'Biological role' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">Zinc</a> is an indispensable trace element for all forms of life—humans, animals, plants, and microorganisms. It is the second most abundant trace metal in the human body after <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a></a>, and holds the unique distinction of being the <b>only metal present in all six enzyme classes</b>!</p><p>More than 300 enzymes and 1,000 transcription factors depend on <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> ions for structural stability and catalytic activity. Approximately 10% of all human proteins (~3,000 proteins) bind <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a>. It is stored and transported inside cells by proteins called metallothioneins, usually coordinated to the amino acid side chains of cysteine, histidine, aspartic acid, and glutamic acid. Zinc plays crucial roles in DNA synthesis, cell division, immune function, wound healing, and gene expression.</p><p>An adult human body contains roughly 2 to 4 grams of zinc, distributed mainly in skeletal muscle, bone, brain, liver, kidneys, skin, and retina. The prostate gland and seminal fluid contain extraordinarily high zinc concentrations, vital for prostate health and reproductive fertility. Intestinal absorption of dietary zinc is carefully regulated by transporter proteins such as ZIP4 and TRPM7.</p><p>In the brain, zinc is stored within synaptic vesicles of glutamatergic neurons and released during nerve transmission, regulating synaptic plasticity, learning, and memory. The transporter SLC30A3 regulates cerebral zinc levels. Maintaining proper zinc balance is critical: excess synaptic zinc can trigger neurotoxicity and oxidative stress, while zinc deficiency impairs cognitive development.</p><p><b>Agricultural Importance in India:</b> Zinc is equally vital for plant growth, building cell walls and activating growth hormones. However, zinc deficiency is widespread in Indian agricultural soils, affecting <b>35% to 50% of Indian soils</b> (and projected to reach 63%). Soil zinc deficiency reduces crop yields and leads to "hidden hunger" in humans—causing childhood stunting and weakened immunity. To combat this, the <b>Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR)</b> and Indian agricultural universities promote zinc-fortified fertilizers (zinc-coated urea) and biofortified crop varieties like zinc-dense rice (<i>CR Dhan 315</i>) and wheat (<i>WB 02</i>), ensuring nutritional security across the nation.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Ga' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Ga',
    'name' => 'Gallium',
    'atomic' => 31,
    'category' => 'post-transition metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">Gallium</a></a></b> (symbol <b>Ga</b>, atomic number 31) is a remarkable post-transition metal in Group 13 of the periodic table, situated directly below <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminium (Al)">aluminium</a>. Famous for its captivating physical trick, pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a> is a soft, silvery-blue metal with an extraordinarily low melting point of 29.76 °C (85.57 °F)—meaning a solid crystal of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a> will literally melt into a mirror-like liquid puddle right in the warmth of your hand! Elemental gallium does not occur freely in nature; instead, it is hidden as a trace passenger inside <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a></a> ores and bauxite (aluminium ore). Once considered a rare chemical curiosity, gallium has become indispensable to modern technology, driving ultra-fast microchips, 5G cellular networks, high-efficiency solar cells, and energy-saving LED lights. India holds strategic importance in the global gallium landscape, featuring pioneering extraction initiatives at National Aluminium Company (<b>NALCO</b>) in Odisha using indigenous technology developed with the <b>Bhabha Atomic Research Centre</b> (<b>BARC</b>), alongside world-class Gallium Nitride (<b>GaN</b>) semiconductor research spearheaded by the <b>Indian Institute of Science (IISc)</b> and <b>DRDO</b>.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b1/Solid_gallium_%28Ga%29.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 69.7231,
    'density' => 5.91,
    'melt' => 302.9146,
    'boil' => 2673,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Lecoq de Boisbaudran',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p1',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.81,
    'electron_affinity' => 41,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 578.8,
      1 => 1979.3,
      2 => 2963,
      3 => 6180,
