    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">Gallium</a> is a soft, silvery metal with a subtle bluish luster when pure. In its solid state, it is brittle enough to fracture like glass if struck, but soft enough to be cut with a knife. What makes <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a> world-famous is its astonishing thermal behavior: it melts at just 29.76 °C (85.57 °F), slightly above standard room temperature. Unlike <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">mercury</a></a>, which is a liquid metal that poses severe toxic risks, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a> is low in toxicity and safe to hold in an open hand, where body heat quickly turns it into a shimmering liquid pool.</p><p>Gallium possesses one of the widest liquid temperature spans of any element on the periodic table. While it melts at ~30 °C, it does not boil until it reaches an extraordinary 2,673 °C (4,843 °F)! This gives liquid gallium a staggering liquid range of over 2,640 °C, making it ideal for high-temperature thermometers and extreme thermal applications where other liquids would instantly vaporize.</p><p>Another fascinating quirk of gallium is its behavior upon freezing. Like water, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ge" class="wiki-link" title="Germanium (Ge)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ge" class="wiki-link" title="Germanium (Ge)">germanium</a></a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Bi" class="wiki-link" title="Bismuth (Bi)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Bi" class="wiki-link" title="Bismuth (Bi)">bismuth</a></a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sb" class="wiki-link" title="Antimony (Sb)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sb" class="wiki-link" title="Antimony (Sb)">antimony</a></a>, gallium expands by 3.1% when it solidifies from a liquid. Because of this expansion, solidifying gallium can exert immense mechanical pressure. Scientists and jewelers learn early never to store liquid gallium in rigid glass or metal containers, because as it freezes, it will burst the container wide open!</p><p>Liquid gallium easily "wets" surfaces such as glass, porcelain, and human skin, leaving behind a fine dark-gray metallic residue. Furthermore, liquid gallium readily diffuses into the microscopic grain boundaries of other metals—most notably <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminium (Al)">aluminium</a>, aluminium-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a></a> alloys, and structural steel. This causes a dramatic loss of ductility known as <i>liquid metal embrittlement</i>. A single drop of liquid gallium placed on a sturdy aluminium girder slowly seeps into its molecular lattice, rendering the solid metal so brittle that it can be crumbled into dust by hand!</p>',
      'Physical properties' => '<ul style="text-align:left" class="gallery mw-gallery-packed"><li class="gallerybox" style="width: 202px"><div class="thumb" style="width: 200px;"><span typeof="mw:File"><img alt="Crystalline (orthorhomobic) gallium" src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/abd1a895bc3f449408869ef5adfb1b76.jpg" decoding="async" width="200" height="150" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1024" data-file-height="768" /></span></div><div class="gallerytext">Crystalline (orthorhombic) <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a></a></div></li><li class="gallerybox" style="width: 186px"><div class="thumb" style="width: 184px;"><span typeof="mw:File"><img alt="Crystallization of gallium from the liquid" src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/34ca1a35f5191b75800b23801a5ae55d.JPG" decoding="async" width="184" height="150" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="2470" data-file-height="2018" /></span></div><div class="gallerytext">Crystallization of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a></a> from liquid</div></li><li class="gallerybox" style="width: 208.66666666667px"><div class="thumb" style="width: 206.66666666667px;"><span typeof="mw:File"><img alt="Liquid gallium at 86 °F (30 °C)" src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/d1943d0f4fb5edd0e9bb0e41cd94eeb7.png" decoding="async" width="207" height="150" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1443" data-file-height="1049" /></span></div><div class="gallerytext">Liquid <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a></a> at 30 °C</div></li></ul><p>Elemental gallium exhibits a density of 5.91 g/cm³ in its solid state at 20 °C. Unusually for most substances, liquid gallium is denser than solid gallium (liquid density is ~6.095 g/cm³ at its melting point), which is why solid gallium crystals float on liquid gallium just as ice cubes float on liquid water.</p><p>Under ambient conditions, gallium does not adopt simple metallic structures like face-centered cubic or body-centered cubic packing. Instead, its room-temperature stable crystal phase (known as Ga-I) forms a complex orthorhombic lattice containing eight atoms per conventional unit cell. Within this unit cell, each gallium atom has only one nearest neighbor at a close distance of 244 pm, while its remaining six unit-cell neighbors are spaced 27 to 39 pm farther away. The two closest atoms form covalently bonded <b>Ga<sub>2</sub> dimers</b>, which serve as the fundamental structural building blocks of the crystal. Because strong covalent bonds bind the pairs internally while weaker forces hold the dimers together in the lattice, relatively little thermal energy is needed to melt the crystal—explaining why gallium melts at such a remarkably low temperature compared to neighboring elements like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminium (Al)">aluminium</a> (660.32 °C) and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/In" class="wiki-link" title="Indium (In)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/In" class="wiki-link" title="Indium (In)">indium</a></a> (156.6 °C).</p><p>This crystal arrangement is strikingly similar to that of diatomic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/I" class="wiki-link" title="Iodine (I)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/I" class="wiki-link" title="Iodine (I)">iodine</a></a>. It arises because gallium\'s outer 4p electron extends beyond the core nucleus and [Ar]3d<sup>10</sup> electron shell. Because filled 3d<sup>10</sup> orbitals shield the positive nuclear charge poorly, gallium\'s outer electron is held tightly, giving gallium a higher first ionization energy (578.8 kJ/mol) than aluminium (577.5 kJ/mol).</p><p>Gallium\'s physical properties are highly <i>anisotropic</i>—meaning their values vary significantly along its three main crystal axes (<i>a</i>, <i>b</i>, and <i>c</i>):</p><table class="wikitable"><caption>Properties of gallium along different crystal axes</caption><tbody><tr><th>Property</th><th><i>a</i></th><th><i>b</i></th><th><i>c</i></th></tr><tr><td>Linear thermal expansion α (~25 °C, µm/(m·K))</td><td>16</td><td>11</td><td>31</td></tr><tr><td>Electrical resistivity ρ (29.7 °C, nΩ·m)</td><td>543</td><td>174</td><td>81</td></tr><tr><td>Electrical resistivity ρ (0 °C, nΩ·m)</td><td>480</td><td>154</td><td>71.6</td></tr><tr><td>Electrical resistivity ρ (77 K, nΩ·m)</td><td>101</td><td>30.8</td><td>14.3</td></tr><tr><td>Electrical resistivity ρ (4.2 K, pΩ·m)</td><td>13.8</td><td>6.8</td><td>1.6</td></tr></tbody></table><p>As shown above, electrical resistivity along the <i>a</i>-axis is nearly seven times higher than along the <i>c</i>-axis at room temperature. Furthermore, upon melting, gallium\'s coefficient of thermal expansion increases dramatically by several hundred percent.</p>',
      'Chemical properties' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">Gallium</a> belongs to Group 13 (the <a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a> family) and exhibits electron configuration [Ar] 3d<sup>10</sup> 4s<sup>2</sup> 4p<sup>1</sup>. Its chemistry is dominated by the <b>+3 oxidation state</b> (trivalent <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a>), where it sheds its three valence electrons (4s<sup>2</sup> 4p<sup>1</sup>) to form stable Ga<sup>3+</sup> ions.</p><p>Like aluminium, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a> forms <b>amphoteric</b> oxides and hydroxides—meaning they react readily with both acids and bases. Gallium oxide (Ga<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>) and gallium hydroxide (Ga(OH)<sub>3</sub>) dissolve in strong acids to produce trivalent gallium salts such as gallium nitrate [Ga(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>] or gallium chloride [GaCl<sub>3</sub>]:</p><p style="text-align:center;">\\( \\text{Ga(OH)}_3 + 3\\text{HCl} \\rightarrow \\text{GaCl}_3 + 3\\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\)</p><p>When treated with strong alkalis like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a> hydroxide (NaOH), gallium oxide and hydroxide dissolve to form soluble gallate complex ions [Ga(OH)<sub>4</sub>]<sup>−</sup>:</p><p style="text-align:center;">\\( \\text{Ga(OH)}_3 + \\text{NaOH} \\rightarrow \\text{Na}[\\text{Ga(OH)}_4] \\)</p><p>Gallium also exhibits lower oxidation states, though less frequently than heavier group members <a href="/school1/ptable/element/In" class="wiki-link" title="Indium (In)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/In" class="wiki-link" title="Indium (In)">indium</a></a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Tl" class="wiki-link" title="Thallium (Tl)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Tl" class="wiki-link" title="Thallium (Tl)">thallium</a></a>. The +1 oxidation state occurs in specialized compounds: for example, gallium dichloride (GaCl<sub>2</sub>) is actually a mixed-valence compound composed of equal parts gallium(I) and gallium(III), accurately written as Ga<sup>I</sup>[Ga<sup>III</sup>Cl<sub>4</sub>]. Simple monohalides like GaCl are unstable above 0 °C, spontaneously disproportionating into elemental gallium metal and gallium trichloride (GaCl<sub>3</sub>). True +2 oxidation states feature direct Ga–Ga metal-metal bonds, as found in gallium monosulfide (GaS, formulated as Ga<sub>2</sub><sup>4+</sup>(S<sup>2−</sup>)<sub>2</sub>) and the dioxane complex Ga<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>4</sub>(C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>8</sub>O<sub>2</sub>)<sub>2</sub>.</p><p>At ambient temperatures, metallic gallium does not tarnish in dry air because it rapidly coats itself with a microscopic, protective passivating film of gallium oxide. However, when heated to high temperatures, it burns brilliantly in pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a> to yield white Ga<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> powder. Gallium reacts slowly with water at room temperature, but dissolves smoothly in non-oxidizing mineral acids (like HCl or H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>) and alkalis, evolving <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a></a> gas.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">Gallium</a> is not found in its free elemental state anywhere in Earth\'s crust. It has an average crustal abundance of approximately 16.9 parts per million (ppm), ranking as the 34th most abundant element in Earth\'s crust—comparable in overall abundance to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">lead</a></a> (14 ppm), <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a></a> (25 ppm), and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Nb" class="wiki-link" title="Niobium (Nb)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Nb" class="wiki-link" title="Niobium (Nb)">niobium</a></a> (20 ppm).</p><p>However, unlike <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">lead</a> or <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Co" class="wiki-link" title="Cobalt (Co)">cobalt</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a> is geochemically <i>dispersed</i>. Because Ga<sup>3+</sup> (ionic radius 62 pm) closely matches Al<sup>3+</sup> (53 pm) and Zn<sup>2+</sup> (74 pm) in size and charge, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a> replaces aluminium in bauxite minerals and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> in sulfide ores rather than forming concentrated deposits of its own. Rare independent gallium minerals do exist—such as gallite (CuGaS<sub>2</sub>) and germanite—but they are far too scarce to serve as economic sources of the metal. Consequently, virtually all global gallium is produced as a valuable by-product during the industrial refining of bauxite (which typically contains ~50 ppm gallium) and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> sphalerite ores.</p><p>The United States Geological Survey (USGS) estimates that world reserves of bauxite and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> ores contain over 1 million tonnes of gallium. Additional small quantities exist in coal flue dusts (<1% by mass). Global annual refinery production of primary gallium passed 100 tonnes in 2010 and accelerated to approximately 450 tonnes per year in recent years.</p><p><b>India\'s Geographical Relevance & Extraction Initiatives:</b> India holds vast reserves of bauxite across major mining belts in Odisha, Andhra Pradesh, Gujarat, Jharkhand, and Chhattisgarh. The <b>National Aluminium Company Limited (NALCO)</b>, operating one of Asia\'s largest integrated alumina refineries at <b>Damanjodi in Koraput district, Odisha</b>, is at the forefront of securing India\'s domestic gallium supply.</p><p>To reduce dependence on foreign imports of this critical strategic mineral, NALCO collaborated with premier national laboratories—including the <b>Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC)</b> and the <b>Heavy Water Board (HWB)</b>—to develop indigenous solvent extraction technology for recovering high-purity gallium directly from Bayer process alumina liquor and red mud residue. NALCO established a 10-tonne per year pilot extraction facility at Damanjodi. Simultaneously, the <b>Jawaharlal Nehru Aluminium Research Development and Design Centre (JNARDDC)</b> in Nagpur leads nationwide research on recovering strategic metals like gallium and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sc" class="wiki-link" title="Scandium (Sc)">scandium</a> from bauxite tailings. Furthermore, India\'s major zinc mining hubs in Rajasthan (such as the world-class Rampura Agucha and Zawar mines operated by Hindustan Zinc) process sphalerite ores containing trace gallium, reinforcing India\'s potential for mineral self-reliance.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">Gallium</a> possesses 30 known isotopes, spanning atomic mass numbers from 60 to 89. Naturally occurring <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a> on Earth is composed of two stable, non-radioactive isotopes:</p><ul><li><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">Gallium</a>-69 (<sup>69</sup>Ga):</b> Makes up 60.11% of natural gallium.</li><li><b>Gallium-71 (<sup>71</sup>Ga):</b> Makes up the remaining 39.89% of natural gallium.</li></ul><p>All other 28 isotopes are synthetic radioisotopes. Among these, the longest-lived radioisotope is <b>gallium-67 (<sup>67</sup>Ga)</b>, with a half-life of 3.2617 days. Gallium-67 decays by electron capture into stable <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a></a>-67 while emitting diagnostic gamma rays. In nuclear medicine, <sup>67</sup>Ga-citrate injections are widely utilized in <b>gallium scans</b> to locate medical infections, chronic inflammatory foci, and specific cancers like Hodgkin\'s lymphoma.</p><p>Another vital medical radioisotope is <b>gallium-68 (<sup>68</sup>Ga)</b>, which has a short half-life of 67.84 minutes. Produced conveniently on-site in hospital radioisotope generators from parent <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ge" class="wiki-link" title="Germanium (Ge)">germanium</a>-68 (<sup>68</sup>Ge), gallium-68 decays via positron emission (β<sup>+</sup>) to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a>-68. When attached to targeting molecules like DOTA-TATE, gallium-68 is a premier agent for <b>Positron Emission Tomography (PET)</b> scanning, enabling high-resolution imaging of neuroendocrine tumors.</p><p>Gallium radioisotopes display clear decay patterns based on atomic mass:</p><p>Isotopes lighter than stable <sup>69</sup>Ga decay predominantly through positron emission (β<sup>+</sup>) or electron capture, converting into isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a></a>:</p><p style="text-align:center;">\\( \\text{}^{n}_{31}\\text{Ga} + e^{-} \\rightarrow \\text{}^{n}_{30}\\text{Zn} + \\nu_e \\)</p><p>Isotopes heavier than stable <sup>71</sup>Ga decay primarily via beta-minus emission (β<sup>−</sup>), turning into isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ge" class="wiki-link" title="Germanium (Ge)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ge" class="wiki-link" title="Germanium (Ge)">germanium</a></a>:</p><p style="text-align:center;">\\( \\text{}^{n}_{31}\\text{Ga} \\rightarrow \\text{}^{n}_{32}\\text{Ge} + e^{-} + \\bar{\\nu}_e \\)</p><p>Gallium-70 (half-life 21.1 minutes) is a fascinating crossover case that decays through both pathways—either shedding an electron to form <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ge" class="wiki-link" title="Germanium (Ge)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ge" class="wiki-link" title="Germanium (Ge)">germanium</a></a>-70 or capturing an electron to form <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a>-70.</p>',
      'History' => '<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><span><video id="mwe_player_0" poster="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Gallium_drops.ogv/250px--Gallium_drops.ogv.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser" controls="" preload="none" data-mw-tmh="" class="mw-file-element" width="250" height="141" data-durationhint="10" data-mwtitle="Gallium_drops.ogv" data-mwprovider="wikimediacommons" resource="/wiki/File:Gallium_drops.ogv"><source src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/e/e4/Gallium_drops.ogv/Gallium_drops.ogv.480p.vp9.webm" type="video/webm; codecs=&quot;vp9, opus&quot;" data-transcodekey="480p.vp9.webm" data-width="854" data-height="480" /><source src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/e/e4/Gallium_drops.ogv/Gallium_drops.ogv.1080p.vp9.webm" type="video/webm; codecs=&quot;vp9, opus&quot;" data-transcodekey="1080p.vp9.webm" data-width="1920" data-height="1080" /><source src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/Gallium_drops.ogv?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=api&amp;utm_content=original" type="video/ogg; codecs=&quot;theora, vorbis&quot;" data-width="1920" data-height="1080" /><source src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/e/e4/Gallium_drops.ogv/Gallium_drops.ogv.240p.vp9.webm" type="video/webm; codecs=&quot;vp9, opus&quot;" data-transcodekey="240p.vp9.webm" data-width="426" data-height="240" /></video></span><figcaption>Small <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a></a> droplets fusing together</figcaption></figure><p>The history of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a> is one of the most famous scientific triumphs in the development of chemistry. In 1871, Russian chemist <b>Dmitri Mendeleev</b> created his landmark Periodic Table. Spotting an empty gap directly below <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminium (Al)">aluminium</a>, Mendeleev boldly predicted the existence of an undiscovered element he provisionally named <b>eka-aluminium</b> (symbol <i>Ea</i>, meaning \'one step beyond aluminium\' in Sanskrit prefix terminology).</p><p>Mendeleev did not merely guess its existence—he calculated its specific physical and chemical properties using the trend laws of his periodic table. Four years later, in 1875, French chemist <b>Paul-Émile Lecoq de Boisbaudran</b> discovered the element in a sample of sphalerite mineral from the Pyrenees using spectrographic analysis, recognizing its two characteristic violet emission lines. Later that year, Lecoq isolated pure metallic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a> by electrolyzing gallium hydroxide dissolved in <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a> hydroxide (KOH).</p><p>Mendeleev\'s predictions matched the actual properties of gallium with astonishing precision:</p><table class="wikitable"><caption>Comparison between Mendeleev\'s 1871 predictions and actual properties of gallium</caption><tbody><tr><th>Property</th><th>Mendeleev\'s Prediction (1871)</th><th>Actual Properties</th></tr><tr><th>Atomic weight</th><td>~68</td><td>69.723</td></tr><tr><th>Density</th><td>5.9 g/cm³</td><td>5.904 g/cm³</td></tr><tr><th>Melting point</th><td>Low</td><td>29.767 °C</td></tr><tr><th>Formula of oxide</th><td>M<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub></td><td>Ga<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub></td></tr><tr><th>Density of oxide</th><td>5.5 g/cm³</td><td>5.88 g/cm³</td></tr><tr><th>Nature of hydroxide</th><td>Amphoteric</td><td>Amphoteric</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Remarkably, when Lecoq de Boisbaudran first measured the density of his new element as 4.7 g/cm³, Mendeleev wrote him a letter suggesting he re-purify his sample and re-measure it. Upon doing so, de Boisbaudran obtained the correct value of 5.904 g/cm³—exactly as Mendeleev had calculated! This historic validation established the Periodic Law as a true predictive science.</p><p>Lecoq named the element <i>Gallia</i>, after Latin <i>Gallia</i> (meaning \'Gaul\', the historical region of France). Contemporary chemists suspected a clever multilingual pun: <i>Le coq</i> is French for \'the rooster\', and the Latin word for rooster is <i>gallus</i>! Although Lecoq publicly denied this in an 1877 article, the double meaning remains a famous piece of science folklore.</p><p><b>Ancient Indian Metallurgical Connections:</b> While isolated metallic gallium was unknown in antiquity due to its low concentration in ores, its host minerals—sphalerite (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> sulfide) and bauxite clays—were mined and processed in ancient India. In classical Indian alchemical texts like <i>Rasa Ratnakara</i> (authored by chemist Nagarjuna c. 9th–10th century CE) and <i>Rasa Ratna Samuccaya</i> (13th century CE), <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> ores were classified under <i>Rasaka</i> and refined at <b>Zawar in Rajasthan</b> using revolutionary downward distillation furnaces (<i>tiryakpatana yantra</i>). Modern geochemical analyses of Zawar <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> tailings reveal trace concentrations of gallium, showing that gallium compounds passed through ancient Indian furnaces over 2,000 years ago as silent partners in early zinc extraction!</p><p><b>The Semiconductor & LED Revolution:</b> For nearly a century after its discovery, gallium was used primarily in high-temperature thermometers and low-melting alloys. The era of modern electronics transformed gallium in the 1960s with the synthesis of <b>Gallium Arsenide (GaAs)</b>, a compound semiconductor with superior electron speed compared to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a>. In the early 1990s, Japanese researchers Shuji Nakamura, Isamu Akasaki, and Hiroshi Amano developed high-brightness blue Light-Emitting Diodes (LEDs) using <b>Gallium Nitride (GaN)</b> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/In" class="wiki-link" title="Indium (In)">Indium</a> Gallium Nitride (InGaN), earning the <b>2014 Nobel Prize in Physics</b>. Today, blue GaN LEDs form the heart of energy-saving white LED lightbulbs worldwide.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<ol><li><b>Semiconductors & Microchips (98% of world demand):</b> <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">Gallium</a> is dominated by compound semiconductors:<ul><li><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">Gallium</a> Arsenide (GaAs):</b> Features electron mobility up to six times faster than <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a></a>. GaAs integrated circuits power high-frequency radio-frequency (RF) power amplifiers in 5G smartphones, radar systems, satellite communications, and ultra-high-efficiency multi-junction solar panels used in space exploration.</li><li><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">Gallium</a> Nitride (GaN):</b> A wide-bandgap semiconductor capable of handling high power, extreme voltages, and high temperatures. GaN is replacing <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a> in electric vehicle (EV) fast chargers, 5G cellular base stations, high-performance power adapters, and blue/ultraviolet laser diodes (used in Blu-ray players and optical projection).</li></ul></li><li><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Gd" class="wiki-link" title="Gadolinium (Gd)">Gadolinium</a> Gallium Garnet (GGG):</b> Synthetic single-crystal GGG substrates are used in magneto-optical devices, bubble memory research, and specialized laser optics (~2% of market).</li><li><b>Liquid Metal Alloys & Galinstan:</b> Gallium forms non-toxic eutectic alloys with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/In" class="wiki-link" title="Indium (In)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/In" class="wiki-link" title="Indium (In)">indium</a></a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sn" class="wiki-link" title="Tin (Sn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sn" class="wiki-link" title="Tin (Sn)">tin</a></a>. The most famous is <b>Galinstan</b> (68.5% Ga, 21.5% In, 10% Sn), which remains liquid down to −19 °C (−2 °F). Galinstan has successfully replaced toxic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">mercury</a></a> in medical thermometers, high-performance computer liquid cooling systems, flexible electronic circuits, and soft robotics.</li><li><b>Permanent Magnets & Solar Energy:</b> Adding trace amounts of gallium to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Nd" class="wiki-link" title="Neodymium (Nd)">neodymium</a>-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/B" class="wiki-link" title="Boron (B)">boron</a> (NdFeB) permanent magnets enhances their thermal stability and coercivity in electric vehicle motors. Gallium is also a key component in CIGS (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a></a> <a href="/school1/ptable/element/In" class="wiki-link" title="Indium (In)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/In" class="wiki-link" title="Indium (In)">indium</a></a> gallium selenide) thin-film solar panels.</li></ol><p><b>India\'s Strategic Semiconductor Ecosystem (GAETEC & IISc):</b> India has designated gallium as a critical strategic mineral for defense, aerospace, and telecommunications self-reliance (<i>Atmanirbhar Bharat</i>):</p><ul><li><b>GAETEC (Gallium Arsenide Enabling Technology Centre), Hyderabad:</b> Established in 1996 as a specialized compound semiconductor foundry under DRDO/SITAR, GAETEC manufactures indigenous GaAs Monolithic Microwave Integrated Circuits (MMICs) and High Electron Mobility Transistors (HEMTs). These components power India\'s defense AESA radars, electronic warfare platforms, missile seekers, and ISRO satellite missions (such as the radar imaging satellite EOS-04).</li><li><b>IISc CeNSE & GaN Semiconductor Hub:</b> At the <b>Indian Institute of Science (IISc), Bengaluru</b>, researchers at the Centre for Nano Science and Engineering (<b>CeNSE</b>), in collaboration with DRDO\'s Solid State Physics Laboratory (<b>SSPL</b>, Delhi), established India\'s first sovereign <b>GaN-on-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">Silicon</a> and GaN-on-SiC semiconductor process foundry</b>. They developed India\'s first indigenous GaN power transistors (e-mode HEMTs). To bring this technology to market, IISc researchers launched deep-tech startup <b>AGNIT Semiconductors</b>, manufacturing commercial GaN components for 5G telecommunications, green energy electronics, and national defense radar systems.</li></ul>',
      'Biological role' => '<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">Gallium</a> has no known natural biological role in humans, animals, plants, or microorganisms. Because elemental <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a> salts are not readily absorbed by the body, metallic gallium is low in toxicity and considered safe to handle in small quantities, though it leaves gray smudges on skin.</p><p><b>The "Trojan Horse" Mechanism in Medicine:</b> Trivalent gallium ions (Ga<sup>3+</sup>) possess an ionic radius (62 pm) and electrical charge nearly identical to ferric <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> ions (Fe<sup>3+</sup>, 64 pm). Because of this remarkable physical resemblance, biological transport mechanisms inside bacteria and rapidly dividing human cancer cells mistake Ga<sup>3+</sup> for Fe<sup>3+</sup> and actively absorb it.</p><p>However, unlike <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>, gallium cannot be reduced to a +2 oxidation state under physiological conditions. Once inside a cell, Ga<sup>3+</sup> substitutes for Fe<sup>3+</sup> in key metabolic enzymes (such as ribonucleotide reductase) but fails to carry out electron transfer reactions. This effectively shuts down cellular respiration, DNA replication, and protein synthesis—acting like a cellular <b>Trojan Horse</b> that starves disease-causing bacteria and tumor cells of essential <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>!</p><p><b>Medical Therapies & Antimicrobial Research:</b></p><ul><li><b>Gallium Nitrate (Ganite):</b> An FDA-approved intravenous medication used to treat cancer-related hypercalcemia (dangerously elevated blood <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a> levels). It inhibits bone breakdown while stifling tumor growth.</li><li><b>Antimicrobial & Anti-biofilm Treatments:</b> Gallium compounds are actively researched as novel treatments for multidrug-resistant "superbug" infections (such as <i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i> in cystic fibrosis patients), where gallium disrupts bacterial iron uptake and destroys protective bacterial biofilms.</li><li><b>Nuclear Medicine Diagnostics:</b> Radiopharmaceuticals containing <sup>67</sup>Ga and <sup>68</sup>Ga play vital roles in hospital PET scans and diagnostic imaging worldwide.</li></ul>',
    ),
  ),
  'Ge' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Ge',
    'name' => 'Germanium',
    'atomic' => 32,
    'category' => 'metalloid',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ge" class="wiki-link" title="Germanium (Ge)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ge" class="wiki-link" title="Germanium (Ge)">Germanium</a></a></b> (symbol <b>Ge</b>, atomic number <b>32</b>) is a lustrous, hard, grayish-white metalloid that sits in the <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> family of the periodic table, right between <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a></a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sn" class="wiki-link" title="Tin (Sn)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Sn" class="wiki-link" title="Tin (Sn)">tin</a></a>. In its pure form, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ge" class="wiki-link" title="Germanium (Ge)">germanium</a> looks almost identical to shiny crystalline <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a>, behaving as a remarkable semiconductor that paved the way for modern electronics. While it was once the king of early transistors, today <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ge" class="wiki-link" title="Germanium (Ge)">germanium</a> plays a critical role in high-speed optical fiber communications, infrared night-vision technology, and ultra-efficient space solar panels. Though germanium does not naturally exist as a free metal, its history reaches from ancient <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Zn" class="wiki-link" title="Zinc (Zn)">zinc</a> smelting techniques in India to outer space. Today, the Government of India classifies germanium as one of its 30 strategic <b>Critical Minerals</b> vital for high-tech self-reliance (<i>Atmanirbhar Bharat</i>), while Indian astronomers continue to uncover its cosmic origins in distant stars.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Polycrystalline-germanium.jpg',
