    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">Selenium</a></a></b> is a chemical element in Group 16 of the periodic table, placing it in the chalcogen family right between <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a></a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Te" class="wiki-link" title="Tellurium (Te)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Te" class="wiki-link" title="Tellurium (Te)">tellurium</a></a>. Classified as a polyatomic nonmetal (sometimes described as a metalloid due to its intermediate semiconductor properties), its chemical behavior bridges the light, reactive nonmetal <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a> and the heavier, more metallic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Te" class="wiki-link" title="Tellurium (Te)">tellurium</a>.</p>
<p>Chemically, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">selenium</a> has an electron configuration of <code>[Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s² 4p⁴</code> and a Pauling electronegativity of 2.55. It forms compounds in several oxidation states, most commonly -2, +4, and +6. <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">Selenium</a> burns in air with a blue flame to form selenium dioxide (SeO₂), which dissolves in water to produce selenious acid (H₂SeO₃). When reacted with metals, selenium forms selenides containing the Se²⁻ ion, behaving similarly to sulfides. It also reacts with halogens to form halides like selenium tetrafluoride (SeF₄) and selenium dichloride (SeCl₂), and with strong reducing agents to form colorful polyselenide chains (Seₙ²⁻). Unlike its neighbor <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a>, liquid selenium does not exhibit dramatic viscosity increases when heated. It strongly resists oxidation by ordinary air and is not attacked by non-oxidizing acids like hydrochloric acid.</p>',
      'Physical properties' => '<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/95a9307846eda7bc0e55fa8962bb8206.jpg" decoding="async" width="190" height="148" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" data-file-width="704" data-file-height="547" /><figcaption>Structure of hexagonal (gray) <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">selenium</a></a></figcaption></figure>
<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">Selenium</a></a></b> is famous for its structural diversity, existing in several distinct allotropes (different physical forms) that transform depending on temperature and how quickly it is heated or cooled:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Gray (Hexagonal) <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">Selenium</a>:</b> The most stable, dense, and commercially important form of selenium has a density of 4.81 g/cm³. It features a chiral hexagonal crystal lattice (space group 152 or 154 depending on chirality) made up of long, helical polymeric chains. Within each chain, Se–Se covalent bonds measure 237.3 pm with a bond angle of 103.1°, while adjacent chains sit 343.6 pm apart. Gray selenium melts at 221 °C (494 K) and boils at 685 °C (958 K). Unlike all other allotropes, gray selenium is insoluble in <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> disulfide (CS₂) and acts as a semiconductor with notable <b>photoconductivity</b>—meaning its electrical conductivity increases significantly when exposed to light!</li>
  <li><b>Red Amorphous Selenium:</b> When selenium is precipitated during chemical reactions, it usually forms a fine, brick-red powder with a density of about 4.28 g/cm³.</li>
  <li><b>Black Vitreous Selenium:</b> Produced when molten selenium is rapidly quenched, this shiny, black, glass-like solid is brittle and slightly soluble in CS₂. Its structure consists of complex, irregular polymeric rings containing up to 1,000 selenium atoms. Upon heating, black selenium softens at 50 °C and converts into stable gray selenium at 180 °C (a transformation accelerated by trace halogens or amines).</li>
  <li><b>Red Monoclinic Selenium (α, β, and γ forms):</b> When black selenium is dissolved in CS₂ and the solvent is evaporated at controlled rates, three distinct red monoclinic crystalline forms appear (all belonging to space group 14). These forms are built from puckered cyclooctaselenium (Se∯ / Se₈) rings, very similar to S₈ rings in <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a>. In these Se₈ rings, the Se–Se bond length averages 233.5 pm with a bond angle of 105.7°. The α-form represents the most densely packed arrangement, while in the γ-form, half the rings are oriented in one configuration and half in its mirror image. Other minor allotropes can also contain Se₆ or Se₇ rings.</li>
</ul>',
      'Isotopes' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: Isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">selenium</a></a></div>
<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">Selenium</a></a></b> has seven naturally occurring isotopes. Five of these are completely stable: <sup>74</sup>Se, <sup>76</sup>Se, <sup>77</sup>Se, <sup>78</sup>Se, and <sup>80</sup>Se, with <b><sup>80</sup>Se</b> being the most abundant in nature at 49.6%. Naturally occurring <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">selenium</a> also contains a long-lived primordial radioisotope, <b><sup>82</sup>Se</b>, which undergoes double beta decay with an astronomical half-life of 8.76 × 10¹⁹ years (nearly 6 billion times the age of the Universe!). Additionally, minute traces of <b><sup>79</sup>Se</b> (half-life of 327,000 years) occur naturally in <a href="/school1/ptable/element/U" class="wiki-link" title="Uranium (U)">uranium</a> ores as a product of nuclear fission.</p>
<p>Beyond natural forms, scientists have synthesized numerous unstable radioisotopes ranging from <sup>64</sup>Se to <sup>95</sup>Se. The most useful synthetic radioisotope is <b><sup>75</sup>Se</b>, which has a half-life of 119.78 days and emits gamma rays widely used in industrial non-destructive testing (radiography) and nuclear medical imaging. Another long-lived synthetic isotope is <sup>72</sup>Se, with a half-life of 8.4 days. Radioactive isotopes lighter than the stable range primarily decay by beta-plus decay (positron emission) into isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/As" class="wiki-link" title="Arsenic (As)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/As" class="wiki-link" title="Arsenic (As)">arsenic</a></a>, while those heavier than the stable range undergo beta-minus decay into isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Br" class="wiki-link" title="Bromine (Br)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Br" class="wiki-link" title="Bromine (Br)">bromine</a></a>.</p>

<table class="wikitable">
  <caption><b>Key Selenium Isotopes</b></caption>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Isotope</th>
      <th>Natural Abundance / Type</th>
      <th>Half-life</th>
      <th>Decay Mode</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><b><sup>74</sup>Se</b></td><td>0.89% (Primordial)</td><td>Stable</td><td>None</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b><sup>75</sup>Se</b></td><td>Synthetic (Radioisotope)</td><td>119.78 days</td><td>Electron Capture → <sup>75</sup>As</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b><sup>76</sup>Se</b></td><td>9.37% (Primordial)</td><td>Stable</td><td>None</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b><sup>77</sup>Se</b></td><td>7.63% (Primordial)</td><td>Stable</td><td>None</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b><sup>78</sup>Se</b></td><td>23.77% (Primordial)</td><td>Stable</td><td>None</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b><sup>79</sup>Se</b></td><td>Trace (Fission Product)</td><td>327,000 years</td><td>Beta-minus → <sup>79</sup>Br</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b><sup>80</sup>Se</b></td><td>49.61% (Primordial)</td><td>Stable</td><td>None</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b><sup>82</sup>Se</b></td><td>8.73% (Primordial)</td><td>8.76 × 10¹⁹ years</td><td>Double Beta-minus → <sup>82</sup>Kr</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>',
      'History' => '<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/ac3e0d52a7c9d8e3d16d03132a8a711e.jpg" decoding="async" width="190" height="253" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" data-file-width="1200" data-file-height="1600" /><figcaption><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">Selenium</a></a> is named after Selene, the Greek Goddess of the Moon</figcaption></figure>
<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">Selenium</a></a></b> was discovered in 1817 by famous Swedish chemists Jöns Jacob Berzelius and Johan Gottlieb Gahn. While analyzing a reddish-brown sediment left behind in the <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">lead</a> chambers of a sulfuric acid plant in Gripsholm, Sweden, they initially suspected the substance was the known element <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Te" class="wiki-link" title="Tellurium (Te)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Te" class="wiki-link" title="Tellurium (Te)">tellurium</a></a> (named after <i>Tellus</i>, the Roman goddess of the Earth). However, after careful isolation, Berzelius realized he had found a completely new element. To complement <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Te" class="wiki-link" title="Tellurium (Te)">tellurium</a>, he named the new element <i><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">selenium</a></i>, after <b>Selene</b>, the Greek goddess of the Moon.</p>

<p>In 1873, English electrical engineer Willoughby Smith made a groundbreaking discovery: the electrical resistance of gray selenium drops sharply when exposed to light! This phenomenon of <b>photoconductivity</b> sparked an industrial revolution in light sensing. In the mid-1870s, Werner von Siemens built the first commercial selenium photoelectric sensors, and in 1879, Alexander Graham Bell utilized a selenium photodetector to build the <b>photophone</b>—the world\'s first device to transmit spoken voice wirelessly on a beam of light. By the 1930s, selenium rectifiers were developed to convert alternating current (AC) into direct current (DC), becoming the backbone of power electronics until <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a> rectifiers replaced them in the 1970s. Selenium also revolutionized office work as the photoreceptor coating on light-sensitive drums in early xerographic photocopiers.</p>

<p>Selenium\'s biological mystery took longer to solve. For over a century, selenium was known only as a potent toxin that poisoned livestock eating accumulator weeds on Western ranges. In 1954, biochemist Jane Pinsent found the first hints of selenium\'s biological role in microorganisms. In 1957, Klaus Schwarz and Walter Mertz proved that selenium is an indispensable trace element for mammals. During the 1970s, researchers discovered selenium at the active heart of the vital antioxidant enzyme glutathione peroxidase, and in the 1980s, scientists revealed that selenium is incorporated into proteins as <b>selenocysteine</b>—the 21st amino acid of life, uniquely encoded by the UGA codon through a specialized SECIS RNA element.</p>

<p><b>Indian Historical & Alchemical Connections:</b> In classical Indian alchemy and Ayurveda (<i>Rasashastra</i>), ancient metallurgists studied mineral classifications known as <i>Maharasas</i> in texts like <i>Rasa Ratna Samuccaya</i>. Among these minerals, the mysterious substance <b>Chapala</b> has been identified by several historians of Indian science and modern researchers as selenium-containing ores or <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Bi" class="wiki-link" title="Bismuth (Bi)">bismuth</a> compounds. Through traditional multi-step purification (<i>Shodhana</i>) and high-temperature furnace incineration (<i>Bhasmikarana</i>), ancient practitioners converted mineral complexes into bio-compatible <i>Bhasmas</i> (ash preparations)—an early precursor to modern nano-medicine!</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable selfref">See also: Category:Selenide minerals</div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/c46db06e7c0d96e4ffb280f36d1860c8.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="185" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="2236" data-file-height="1658" /><figcaption>Native <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">selenium</a></a> in sandstone, from a <a href="/school1/ptable/element/U" class="wiki-link" title="Uranium (U)">uranium</a> mine near Grants, New Mexico</figcaption></figure>
<p>Native (pure elemental) <b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">selenium</a></a></b> is an extremely rare natural mineral, occasionally forming steep rhombohedra or microscopic, hair-like (acicular) crystals. In minerals, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">selenium</a> occurs in inorganic forms such as selenides, selenates, and selenites, but dedicated selenium ores are exceptionally scarce. <i>(Note: The common decorative mineral named "selenite" is actually a transparent variety of gypsum—<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a> sulfate hydrate—named after the Moon long before selenium was discovered, and contains no selenium at all!)</i></p>

<p>Instead of forming its own rich mines, selenium occurs primarily as an impurity replacing <a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/S" class="wiki-link" title="Sulfur (S)">sulfur</a></a> in sulfide ores of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ni" class="wiki-link" title="Nickel (Ni)">nickel</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">lead</a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>. Consequently, virtually all commercial selenium is recovered as a valuable byproduct during the electrolytic refining of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> anodic slimes. Selenium ranks 44th in crustal abundance at approximately 0.05 to 0.09 mg/kg (ppm). Average concentrations in the atmosphere are under 1 ng/m³, while freshwater and seawater hold around 0.5 µg/L.</p>

<p>In ecology, certain plants known as <i>selenium accumulators</i> or <i>indicator plants</i> thrive in selenium-rich soils by absorbing high concentrations of selenate. Key species include <i>Astragalus</i> (locoweeds), prince\'s plume (<i>Stanleya</i>), woody asters (<i>Xylorhiza</i>), and false goldenweed (<i>Oonopsis</i>). Animals eating these plants can suffer severe selenium poisoning.</p>

<p><b>Indian Geographical & Agricultural Context (The Seleniferous Belt of Punjab):</b> In India, selenium exhibits a dramatic local geographical story. In the northeastern foothill region of Punjab—specifically across pockets in the <b>Nawanshahr (Shahid Bhagat Singh Nagar)</b> and <b>Hoshiarpur</b> districts near the Shivalik mountain range—the soil is naturally enriched with high levels of selenium. Rainwater streams washing down from the Shivalik hills carry selenium-dense sediments into agricultural fields, while intensive groundwater irrigation concentrates soluble selenate in topsoil. In these "seleniferous soils," crops such as wheat and rice absorb excessive selenium, exhibiting characteristic "snow-white" leaf chlorosis and stunting. Consuming fodder and grain from these areas leads to selenium toxicity (selenosis) in local livestock (causing hoof deformation and hair loss) and humans (causing nail cracking and hair thinning). Institutions like <b>Punjab Agricultural University (PAU)</b> and the <b>Indian Council of Medical Research (ICMR)</b> have conducted extensive soil mapping and mitigation research to safeguard Indian agriculture and public health in these pockets.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<div class="mw-heading mw-heading3">
<p>A broad spectrum of technological, industrial, and health applications rely on <b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">selenium</a></a></b> and its chemical compounds:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Glassmaking & Ceramics:</b> <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">Selenium</a> is widely used in glass manufacturing. Adding tiny amounts neutralizes the pale green tint caused by <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a> impurities in clear glass bottles and windows. Larger quantities impart a brilliant ruby-red color to decorative glass, traffic signals, and artistic ceramics.</li>
  <li><b>Electronics, Photovoltaics & Copiers:</b> Due to its photoconductivity, gray <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">selenium</a> was historically used in light meters, photocells, exposure meters, and selenium rectifiers. In modern green energy, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cu" class="wiki-link" title="Copper (Cu)">copper</a> <a href="/school1/ptable/element/In" class="wiki-link" title="Indium (In)">indium</a> <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ga" class="wiki-link" title="Gallium (Ga)">gallium</a> selenide (<b>CIGS</b>) thin-film solar cells deliver high efficiency in flexible photovoltaic panels. Selenium photoconductive drums were also fundamental to xerographic copiers.</li>
  <li><b>Pigments & Paints:</b> <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cd" class="wiki-link" title="Cadmium (Cd)">Cadmium</a> sulfoselenide compounds produce intensely bright, heat-resistant red, orange, and maroon pigments used in plastics, industrial coatings, artists\' paints, and enamels.</li>
  <li><b>Metallurgy:</b> Adding selenium to brass and stainless steel improves machinability while allowing manufacturers to replace toxic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">lead</a> in plumbing fixtures and hardware.</li>
  <li><b>Healthcare & Consumer Products:</b> Selenium disulfide (<b>SeS₂</b>) is the active ingredient in medical anti-dandruff shampoos, killing scalp fungi and slowing skin cell shedding. Selenium supplements and fortified livestock feed prevent deficiency diseases.</li>
  <li><b>Modern Indian Scientific Research & Nuclear Applications:</b>
    <ul>
      <li><b>BARC & Industrial Radiography:</b> Scientists at the <b>Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC)</b> in Mumbai utilize the synthetic radioisotope <b>Selenium-75 (<sup>75</sup>Se)</b> as an effective gamma-ray source for non-destructive testing (NDT). Gamma cameras powered by <sup>75</sup>Se inspect weld integrity in heavy gas pipelines, boilers, and structural steel across India\'s industrial sector.</li>
      <li><b>Nanotechnology & Energy Storage:</b> Research teams across <b>Indian Institutes of Technology (IITs)</b> and CSIR laboratories actively develop selenium quantum dots, biosensors, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a>-selenium / <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Li" class="wiki-link" title="Lithium (Li)">lithium</a>-selenium battery technologies for next-generation energy storage.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ul>',
      'Biological role' => '<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" /><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)"><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Se" class="wiki-link" title="Selenium (Se)">Selenium</a></a> in biology</div>
