    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Physical properties' => '<p>Praseodymium is a soft, silvery metal that can be easily shaped and stretched. It is paramagnetic at room temperature, which means it is weakly attracted to magnets. Unlike some other rare-earth metals that have complicated magnetic behaviors at freezing temperatures, praseodymium keeps things simple and stays paramagnetic unless it gets extremely cold. It is strong and malleable, making it a wonderful ingredient for specialized metal alloys.</p>',
      'Chemical properties' => '<p>When left out in the open air, praseodymium slowly tarnishes. Much like how iron forms rust, praseodymium grows a flaky green oxide crust; a small piece can completely corrode away in about a year! Because of this, it is usually stored in protective oil or sealed glass. It also reacts nicely with hot water to form praseodymium hydroxide, and it readily combines with all halogens (like chlorine and fluorine) to create various interesting salts. Most often, it prefers a \'+3\' oxidation state, giving its compounds a beautiful yellowish-green color that scientists love using to tint specialized glasses.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>In nature, praseodymium is as pure as it gets: it has only one stable, naturally occurring form, known as praseodymium-141. This makes it what scientists call a \'monoisotopic\' element. Its atomic core holds exactly 82 neutrons—a \'magic number\' in physics that grants it extra stability. While researchers have created 38 other artificial, radioactive versions of praseodymium in labs, almost all of them break down in less than a minute. The longest-lasting artificial form is praseodymium-143, which sticks around for about 13 and a half days before decaying.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>The story of praseodymium begins in the mid-1800s when European chemists were trying to untangle a mysterious earth called \'didymium\'. For decades, they thought didymium was a single element. But in 1885, a clever Austrian chemist named Carl Auer von Welsbach managed to split it into two distinct new elements. Because one of them formed bright green salts, he named it \'praseodymium\', combining the Greek words \'prasinos\' (meaning leek-green) and \'didymos\' (meaning twin). Its twin was named neodymium. Historically, ancient Indian alchemists (practitioners of <i>Rasashastra</i>) did not know of this element, as their profound metallurgical expertise focused on base and noble metals. However, the legacy of Indian metallurgy has evolved, and today Indian scientists are at the forefront of unlocking the secrets of rare earths like praseodymium.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Despite being called a \'rare-earth\' metal, praseodymium isn\'t actually that rare! It is more common in the Earth\'s crust than elements like tin or boron. However, it is never found naturally by itself; it is always mingled with its rare-earth siblings like cerium, lanthanum, and neodymium. For India, this element is of massive geographical and strategic importance. India is home to about 6% of the world\'s rare earth reserves. Praseodymium is primarily extracted from a mineral called monazite, which is abundantly found in the coastal beach sands of Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Odisha, and Andhra Pradesh, as well as in inland deposits in Jharkhand and West Bengal. Recognizing its importance, India is developing \'Rare Earth Corridors\' to seamlessly link the mining and processing of these vital sands.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Praseodymium has a colorful resume! In the 1920s, it was first used to give glass and ceramics a vibrant, warm yellow-orange hue known as \'Praseodymium Yellow.\' Today, its uses are much more high-tech. Because of its unique light-filtering and glowing properties, it is essential in creating fiber optic cables, lasers, and even the safety goggles worn by welders and glassblowers. But its biggest claim to fame is its partnership with neodymium. Together, they form incredibly strong, durable permanent magnets (NdPr magnets) used in wind turbines, electric vehicle motors, and modern electronics. Indian scientific contributions are heavily focused on these applications. Modern Indian researchers are developing advanced sensors—such as cobalt-doped praseodymium orthoferrite for CO2 sensing—and supercapacitors for energy storage. Additionally, Indian chemical engineers are pioneering advanced solvent extraction techniques to efficiently separate praseodymium from its twin, neodymium, aiming to power the nation\'s clean energy revolution by 2030.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Nd' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Nd',
    'name' => 'Neodymium',
    'atomic' => 60,
    'category' => 'lanthanide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><strong>Neodymium (Nd)</strong> is a fascinating, silvery-white metal that belongs to a group of elements known as the rare-earth metals. Despite the name "rare," it is actually fairly common in the Earth\'s crust—about as common as familiar metals like copper, cobalt, or nickel! When exposed to air, neodymium quickly gets a colorful tarnish, producing pink, purple, and yellow rust-like compounds.</p>

<p>Most famously, neodymium is the magic ingredient behind the world\'s strongest permanent magnets. You can find these powerful magnets in everything from tiny earbuds and smartphone speakers to massive wind turbines and electric vehicle motors. But its talents don\'t stop there! Neodymium is also used to color glass with a beautiful reddish-purple tint that changes color under different lighting, and it\'s a key part of powerful lasers used in scientific research.</p>

<p>While neodymium was discovered in Austria in 1885, today it has immense global importance, particularly in India. India holds about 6-8% of the world\'s rare earth reserves, with significant neodymium-bearing monazite sands along the coasts of Andhra Pradesh, Odisha, Kerala, and Tamil Nadu. Driven by initiatives like the <em>Atmanirbhar Bharat</em> (Self-Reliant India) and the National Critical Mineral Mission, India is aggressively scaling up its own neodymium production to power the green energy revolution and secure its future in advanced technologies.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c9/Neodymium_%2860_Nd%29.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 144.2423,
    'density' => 7.01,
    'melt' => 1297,
    'boil' => 3347,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Carl Auer von Welsbach',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f4',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.14,
    'electron_affinity' => 184.87,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 533.1,
      1 => 1040,
      2 => 2130,
      3 => 3900,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Physical properties' => '
<h3>Physical Properties</h3>
<p>In its pure form, neodymium is a hard, shiny metal with a bright, silvery luster. Unlike a regular kitchen magnet that sticks to your fridge, pure neodymium is only weakly magnetic at room temperature (a property called paramagnetism). However, when cooled down to extremely low temperatures (below -253 °C), it reveals complex magnetic behaviors.</p>
<p>To create the super-strong magnets we use every day, neodymium is mixed (or alloyed) with iron and boron. This magical combination creates the legendary "NdFeB" magnets, which are incredibly strong for their small size and weight.</p>
',
      'Chemical properties' => '
<h3>Chemical Properties</h3>
<p>Neodymium is a very active element. If you leave a piece of neodymium metal out in the open air, it will quickly tarnish and rust, much like iron. The rust layer it forms doesn\'t protect the metal; instead, it flakes off, allowing the rest of the metal to continue corroding until it completely turns into powder. Because of this, neodymium is usually stored in mineral oil or sealed tightly away from air and moisture.</p>
<p>When it reacts, neodymium typically forms compounds with a +3 charge, which often have beautiful pastel colors like violet, pink, and lilac. It can react slowly with cold water (or quickly with hot water) to produce hydrogen gas, and it eagerly combines with elements like fluorine, chlorine, bromine, and iodine to form colorful salts.</p>
',
      'Isotopes' => '
<h3>Isotopes</h3>
<p>Atoms of the same element can have different weights, called isotopes. In nature, neodymium is a mix of five stable isotopes (meaning they don\'t break down over time) and two very mildly radioactive ones. The most common stable isotope is Neodymium-142.</p>
<p>The two natural radioactive isotopes, Neodymium-144 and Neodymium-150, have half-lives that are unimaginably long—far longer than the age of the universe! Because they decay so incredibly slowly, they are completely safe to be around. Scientists even use the slow decay of other elements into neodymium to figure out the age of ancient rocks and meteorites, a technique known as samarium-neodymium dating.</p>
',
      'History' => '
<h3>History</h3>
<p>The story of neodymium is a tale of mistaken identity. In 1839, a Swedish chemist named Carl Gustaf Mosander extracted what he thought was a new element called "didymium" (meaning "twin" in Greek). For over 40 years, scientists believed didymium was a single, pure element.</p>
<p>However, in 1885, an Austrian chemist named Carl Auer von Welsbach realized that didymium was actually a mixture of two different elements hiding together. He successfully separated them, naming the green-colored one praseodymium ("green twin") and the pink-colored one neodymium, which means "new twin."</p>
<p>For a long time, purifying neodymium was very difficult. It wasn\'t until the 1950s, using new techniques developed during World War II, that scientists were able to easily produce high-purity neodymium on a large scale.</p>
',
      'Occurrence' => '
<h3>Where is it Found?</h3>
<p>Despite being a "rare-earth" metal, neodymium is not actually rare—it\'s widely distributed in the Earth\'s crust. However, it is never found naturally as a pure metal. Instead, it is locked inside minerals like monazite and bastnäsite, mixed up with several other rare-earth elements.</p>
<p>Today, China produces the vast majority of the world\'s commercial neodymium, but <strong>India plays a crucial and growing role in the global supply chain</strong>. India\'s coastal beaches, particularly in Andhra Pradesh (which holds about 30% of the country\'s monazite), Odisha, Kerala, and Tamil Nadu, are incredibly rich in monazite sands. Newer explorations have also found promising inland deposits in states like Arunachal Pradesh and Madhya Pradesh.</p>
<p>Under the guidance of India\'s National Critical Mineral Mission and companies like IREL (India) Limited, the country is rapidly scaling up its mining and processing capabilities. This push aims to increase India\'s domestic production of neodymium to over 500 tonnes by 2027, securing a vital resource for the booming electric vehicle and wind energy markets.</p>
',
      'Applications' => '
<h3>Modern Applications and Scientific Breakthroughs</h3>
<p>Neodymium is the unsung hero of modern technology. Its most famous application is in <strong>neodymium magnets</strong> (NdFeB). These are the strongest permanent magnets commercially available. Because they are so powerful, they allow engineers to make electronic devices smaller and lighter. They are essential for smartphone speakers, hard disk drives, cordless tools, electric vehicle motors, and the massive generators inside wind turbines.</p>
<p>In addition to magnets, neodymium is a fantastic glass additive. It gives glass a mesmerizing purple hue that shifts color depending on the lighting. This special glass is also used in powerful <strong>lasers</strong>, which are utilized in everything from medical eye surgery to cutting steel and even advanced nuclear fusion experiments.</p>
<p><strong>A Modern Indian Scientific Breakthrough:</strong> Neodymium is not just a material for today\'s technology, but also tomorrow\'s. In a groundbreaking 2025 discovery, scientists at the <strong>Jawaharlal Nehru Centre for Advanced Scientific Research (JNCASR) in Bengaluru, India</strong>, unlocked a brand-new form of magnetism in neodymium nitride (NdN) thin films. They discovered that the magnetism in this material is driven by the <em>orbital motion</em> of electrons rather than their traditional "spin." This major leap in the field of <strong>"orbitronics"</strong> paves the way for a future of ultra-fast, highly energy-efficient quantum devices and advanced computer memory, ensuring that neodymium remains at the cutting edge of global scientific innovation!</p>
',
    ),
  ),
  'Pm' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Pm',
    'name' => 'Promethium',
    'atomic' => 61,
    'category' => 'lanthanide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '
    <h2>The Stolen Fire of the Atomic Age</h2>
    <p><strong>Promethium</strong> (atomic number 61) is a rare and highly radioactive metal. In Greek mythology, Prometheus stole fire from the gods to give to humanity. Similarly, promethium offers a kind of "nuclear fire" that provides long-lasting power. In a beautiful cross-cultural parallel, the ancient Indian traditions revere <em>Agni</em>, the god of fire, who represents the spark of life and the transformative energy of the universe. Just as ancient Vedic texts describe Agni as an eternal flame, the steady radioactive glow of promethium serves as an enduring source of energy for modern technology.</p>
    <p>Because it is so radioactive, promethium does not naturally occur on Earth in large amounts—it is constantly glowing and decaying. Instead, it was discovered in the fallout of nuclear reactions in 1945. Today, scientists at institutions like India\'s Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) study its unique properties, while space agencies around the world, including the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO), explore similar radioisotope technologies to keep satellites and space probes powered in the freezing darkness of deep space.</p>
    ',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/Promethium.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 145,
    'density' => 7.26,
    'melt' => 1315,
    'boil' => 3273,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Chien Shiung Wu',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f5',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.13,
    'electron_affinity' => 12.45,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 540,
      1 => 1050,
      2 => 2150,
      3 => 3970,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Physical properties' => '
    <h3>A Glowing Metal</h3>
    <p>If you could safely hold a piece of promethium, you would see a silvery-white metal. But you wouldn\'t want to hold it for long! It is intensely radioactive. The radiation it emits causes the air around it to glow with a pale blue or greenish light in the dark. It is a solid metal at room temperature, but it melts at about 1,042 °C (1,908 °F).</p>
    <p>Like the eternal fires described in ancient Indian <em>Vedas</em>, promethium produces its own steady warmth and light as its atoms decay over time.</p>
    ',
      'Isotopes' => '
    <h3>Unstable Atoms</h3>
    <p>Every element is made of atoms, and atoms have different variations called <em>isotopes</em>. Most elements have at least one stable, unchanging isotope. Promethium is an exception—<strong>none of its isotopes are stable</strong>. Every single atom of promethium will eventually break apart (decay) into other elements, releasing energy.</p>
    <p>Its longest-lived isotope, Promethium-145, lasts for about 17.7 years before half of it disappears. The most useful isotope, Promethium-147, has a half-life of just 2.6 years. This fleeting, temporary nature means promethium is always transforming, echoing the ancient philosophical concepts of impermanence found in early Buddhist and Hindu thought across the Indian subcontinent.</p>
    ',
      'Occurrence' => '
    <h3>Rarer Than Gold</h3>
    <p>You won\'t find promethium easily in nature. Because all of it decays so quickly, any promethium that existed when Earth was formed billions of years ago has long since turned into other elements. Today, the only natural promethium on Earth is created in tiny amounts when uranium ores undergo rare natural nuclear reactions.</p>
    <p>Scientists estimate there are less than 600 grams (about 1.3 pounds) of natural promethium in Earth\'s entire crust at any given moment! Almost all the promethium used in science and industry today is artificially created in nuclear reactors, such as those operated by the Department of Atomic Energy in India and similar organizations globally.</p>
    ',
      'History' => '
    <h3>The Missing Element</h3>
    <p>For decades, scientists knew there had to be an element number 61 to fill a gap in the periodic table, but it was nowhere to be found. Researchers around the world searched for it in minerals and ores, often mistaking other elements for it.</p>
    <p>It wasn\'t until 1945, during the Manhattan Project in the United States, that chemists finally proved its existence by analyzing the radioactive byproducts of a nuclear reactor. Grace Mary Coryell, the wife of one of the discoverers, suggested the name "promethium" after the Greek titan Prometheus, signifying the brave new era of nuclear energy.</p>
    ',
      'Applications' => '
    <h3>Powering the Future</h3>
    <p>Promethium\'s steady release of energy makes it incredibly useful. When placed inside a special material, its radiation creates a reliable light source that doesn\'t need electricity. It is also used to make <strong>atomic batteries</strong>. Unlike the chemical batteries in your phone, atomic batteries can last for years without needing a charge.</p>
    <p>These batteries are perfect for places where it\'s impossible to change a battery, such as pacemakers inside the human body or remote space probes. In India, modern space exploration spearheaded by ISRO relies on the deep understanding of such nuclear materials to design long-lasting power sources for missions reaching out to the Moon, Mars, and beyond, turning the "stolen fire" of promethium into a tool for cosmic discovery.</p>
    ',
    ),
  ),
  'Sm' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Sm',
    'name' => 'Samarium',
    'atomic' => 62,
    'category' => 'lanthanide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p>Samarium is a fascinating silvery-white metal with the symbol Sm and atomic number 62. Though it sounds like it might be from another world, samarium is part of the "rare earth" family (specifically, the lanthanides) and is found right here on Earth. Discovered in 1879 by French chemist Paul-Émile Lecoq de Boisbaudran, it was the very first chemical element to be named after a real person—Vassili Samarsky-Bykhovets, a Russian mine official!</p>
<p>Samarium is famous for making incredibly powerful magnets. When combined with cobalt to make "samarium-cobalt" magnets, it creates a magnetic force that is not only incredibly strong but can survive blistering temperatures—over 700°C (1,292°F)—without losing its power! This makes it a superstar in defense, aerospace, and everyday gadgets.</p>
<p>In the medical world, a radioactive version of samarium (Samarium-153) is a cancer-fighting hero, used in medicines that target and kill cancer cells in bones. Another form (Samarium-149) acts as a safety sponge in nuclear reactors by soaking up stray neutrons.</p>
<p>In India, samarium plays a critical strategic role. Extracted primarily from the rich monazite sands found along the Indian coastline, it is a key focus of India\'s "National Critical Mineral Mission." Facilities like the Rare Earth Permanent Magnet (REPM) plant in Visakhapatnam are turning domestic samarium into high-tech magnets to build a self-reliant future in advanced technology!</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/88/Samarium-2.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 150.362,
    'density' => 7.52,
    'melt' => 1345,
    'boil' => 2173,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Lecoq de Boisbaudran',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f6',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.17,
    'electron_affinity' => 15.63,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 544.5,
      1 => 1070,
      2 => 2260,
      3 => 3990,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Physical properties' => '<p>If you held a piece of samarium in your hand, it would feel similar to zinc in terms of hardness and weight. When freshly cut, it sparkles with a bright, silvery shine. However, if left out in the open air, it slowly tarnishes and loses its luster.</p>
<p>Samarium loves to change its internal shape depending on the temperature and pressure. At room temperature, its atoms are arranged in one pattern, but if you heat it up to over 700°C (1,300°F), the atoms shift into a new arrangement. Heat it even more, and they shift again!</p>
<p>One of the coolest physical quirks of samarium is its magnetic personality. While the metal itself is paramagnetic (weakly attracted to magnets) at room temperature, it becomes uniquely magnetic when chilled to nearly absolute zero. Furthermore, scientists have discovered that mixing a tiny amount of samarium into certain iron-based materials can turn them into "superconductors"—materials that conduct electricity with zero resistance—at relatively warm temperatures for superconductors!</p>',
      'Chemical properties' => '<p>Samarium is quite a reactive metal. If you leave it exposed to air, it slowly rusts at room temperature. If it gets too hot (around 150°C or 302°F), it can even burst into flames! Because it loves reacting with oxygen and moisture, it has to be stored carefully, often sealed tightly under an inert gas like argon or submerged in mineral oil.</p>
<p>When samarium meets water, a chemical dance begins. It reacts slowly with cold water but much more rapidly with hot water, bubbling up as it releases hydrogen gas and forms samarium hydroxide. If you drop it into certain acids, like dilute sulfuric acid, it dissolves easily and creates a beautiful pale-green to yellow liquid!</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>In nature, samarium is a mix of five perfectly stable forms (called isotopes) and two slightly radioactive ones that break down incredibly slowly. The most common form is Samarium-152, making up over a quarter of all natural samarium.</p>
<p>One of its slightly radioactive forms, Samarium-147, decays so slowly—taking over a hundred billion years—that scientists use it as a "geological clock." By measuring how much of it has broken down, geologists can determine the age of ancient rocks and meteorites!</p>
<p>Samarium also has some fascinating artificial forms created in labs. Samarium-149 is famous for its ability to absorb neutrons, making it an essential "safety net" to control the reactions in nuclear power plants. On the medical frontier, Samarium-153, which is highly radioactive but short-lived (with a half-life of less than two days), is used in a drug called Quadramet. When injected into patients, it safely travels to the bones and destroys pain-causing cancer cells.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>The story of samarium is a tale of international discovery. In the late 19th century, scientists across Europe were racing to discover new elements in strange minerals. French chemist Paul-Émile Lecoq de Boisbaudran officially discovered samarium in Paris in 1879. He extracted it from a heavy, dark mineral called "samarskite."</p>
<p>Interestingly, samarskite was named after Vassili Samarsky-Bykhovets, a Russian mine official who had allowed scientists to study the mineral samples from the Ural Mountains. Because Boisbaudran named the element after the mineral, Samarium became the very first element on the periodic table to indirectly honor a real person!</p>
<p>For a long time, samarium didn\'t have many practical uses. But in the 1950s, new technologies allowed scientists to separate it perfectly, and soon they discovered its amazing magnetic properties and nuclear benefits.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Samarium is a true unsung hero of modern technology. Its most famous role is in <strong>Samarium-Cobalt (SmCo) magnets</strong>. Unlike regular magnets, which can lose their power when they get too hot, samarium-cobalt magnets stay incredibly strong even at blistering temperatures up to 700°C (1,292°F). This makes them essential for things that run hot, like jet engines, military radar systems, and precision aerospace instruments.</p>
<p>Beyond magnets, samarium is a lifesaver. The radioactive isotope Samarium-153 is the active ingredient in specialized cancer medicines that target bone tumors. Meanwhile, Samarium-149 is used to build control rods for nuclear reactors because it acts like a sponge, safely absorbing stray neutrons to keep the nuclear reaction stable.</p>
<p><strong>The Indian Context and Modern Research:</strong></p>
<p>In India, samarium holds immense strategic value. The country is home to vast reserves of monazite sands along its coastal regions, which are rich in rare earth elements like samarium. Recognizing its importance for national defense and green technology, the Indian government\'s <strong>National Critical Mineral Mission</strong> focuses on building a self-reliant rare earth supply chain.</p>
<p>A shining example of this is the dedicated Rare Earth Permanent Magnet (REPM) plant in Visakhapatnam, Andhra Pradesh, operated by IREL (India) Limited. This facility takes domestic samarium and turns it into the high-performance SmCo magnets needed for India\'s defense and space programs.</p>
<p>Indian scientists are also at the cutting edge of samarium research. Institutes like the Indian Institute of Space Science and Technology (IIST) and the Institute of Chemical Technology (ICT) in Mumbai are actively exploring new uses for the element. Modern Indian research includes creating samarium-doped glasses for specialized radiation shielding and developing samarium-based nanomaterials that could one day be used for advanced biological imaging and disease diagnosis. From ancient coastal sands to futuristic space and medical technologies, samarium is a vital part of India\'s scientific journey!</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Eu' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Eu',
    'name' => 'Europium',
    'atomic' => 63,
    'category' => 'lanthanide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p>Europium is a chemical element with the symbol <strong>Eu</strong> and atomic number 63. It belongs to the lanthanide series of rare-earth elements and is known as the softest, least dense, and most chemically reactive of the group—so soft it can be easily cut with a knife! Its most famous superpower is glowing brightly when used in phosphorescent materials. It is what gave older color televisions their vibrant red colors, and it is even used as an anti-counterfeiting measure in euro banknotes. In India, trace amounts of europium are found in the monazite sands along the coasts of Kerala, Tamil Nadu, and Odisha, connecting this fascinating element to local geology and modern Indian scientific research.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/Europium.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 151.9641,
    'density' => 5.264,
    'melt' => 1099,
    'boil' => 1802,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Eugène-Anatole Demarçay',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f7',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.2,
    'electron_affinity' => 11.2,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 547.1,
      1 => 1085,
      2 => 2404,
      3 => 4120,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Physical properties' => '<p>Europium is a soft, silvery-white metal. It is about as soft as lead, meaning it can be dented and cut quite easily. It forms a basic "body-centered cubic" crystal structure. Among its rare-earth neighbors, europium stands out because its atoms take up a relatively large amount of space. This unusual trait happens because its electrons are arranged in a way that makes it act a bit differently, giving it unique magnetic properties.</p>',
      'Chemical properties' => '<p>Europium is the most reactive of all the rare-earth elements. If you leave a shiny piece of europium out in the air, it quickly reacts with oxygen and tarnishes, completely oxidizing in just a few days. It also reacts violently with water—much like calcium does—to produce hydrogen gas. Because it is so eager to react, scientists rarely get to see its pure, shiny metallic form unless it is carefully stored under special mineral oil. If heated to between 150 and 180 °C, europium will literally catch fire in the air! It also dissolves easily in certain acids, forming beautiful pale pink solutions.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>In nature, europium is mostly made of two main types (or isotopes): Europium-151 and Europium-153. They exist in almost equal amounts. While Europium-153 is perfectly stable, Europium-151 is very slightly radioactive. However, its radioactivity is so weak and slow that it takes billions of years to decay, making it practically harmless. Scientists have also created several artificial, highly radioactive isotopes of europium in laboratories, but these break down very quickly, mostly in a matter of days or minutes.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>You will not find europium sitting freely in nature. Instead, it is locked away in various minerals like bastnäsite and monazite. In <strong>India</strong>, monazite is prominently found in the famous coastal beach sands of states like Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, and Odisha. Because these sands contain valuable elements like thorium (crucial for India\'s nuclear energy program) and rare earths like europium, their mining and processing are carefully managed by the Indian government through IREL (India) Limited.</p>
<p>In geology, scientists study the "europium anomaly"—how much europium is in a rock compared to other rare-earth elements—to understand how ancient magmas cooled and formed rocks. Even out in space, astronomers look for the signature of europium in stars to figure out when and how those stars were born!</p>',
      'History' => '<p>Because rare-earth elements naturally mix together in rocks and behave very similarly, early scientists had a remarkably tough time telling them apart. In 1885, William Crookes noticed some strange, unknown glowing lines when studying rare-earth minerals. By 1896, French chemist Eugène-Anatole Demarçay suspected that samples of a recently discovered element called samarium were actually hiding another unknown element. Demarçay finally managed to separate it in 1901 and named it "europium" after the continent of Europe.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Europium has some very specialized, brilliant uses—literally! Its most famous application relies on its ability to glow (phosphoresce). It is the magical ingredient that provided the bright red colors in traditional tube televisions and helps create the crisp, white light in modern fluorescent bulbs.</p>
<p>Today, <strong>Indian scientists and research institutions</strong> are actively researching europium-doped materials to develop better LEDs, advanced lasers, and cutting-edge tools for bio-imaging. In medicine, europium compounds are used to tag antibodies, helping doctors detect specific diseases with laser-excited fluorescence. Because it glows brightly under UV light, europium is even printed on euro banknotes as a hidden anti-counterfeiting security feature. Furthermore, europium is highly valued in nuclear research for its excellent ability to absorb neutrons, making it useful in the control rods of nuclear reactors.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Gd' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Gd',
    'name' => 'Gadolinium',
    'atomic' => 64,
    'category' => 'lanthanide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b>Gadolinium</b> (Gd) is a silvery-white metal that belongs to a group of elements called rare earths. Discovered in the late 19th century and named after the Finnish chemist Johan Gadolin, this unique metal has some fascinating magnetic properties. While you might never see it in your daily life, gadolinium plays a crucial role in modern medicine and technology.</p>
<p>In the context of <b>India</b>, gadolinium holds special significance. While it is not mined directly on its own, it is extracted from the rich <b>monazite sands</b> found abundantly along the Indian coastline, especially in states like Kerala, Tamil Nadu, and Odisha. India\'s ability to process these sands locally through facilities like IREL (India) Limited ensures the nation remains self-reliant in critical rare earth materials.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c2/Gadolinium-2.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 157.253,
    'density' => 7.9,
    'melt' => 1585,
    'boil' => 3273,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Jean Charles Galissard de Marignac',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f7 5d1',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.2,
    'electron_affinity' => 13.22,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 593.4,
      1 => 1170,
      2 => 1990,
      3 => 4250,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p>Gadolinium is a soft, shiny, silvery metal. It is somewhat malleable, meaning it can be shaped without breaking. What makes gadolinium truly special is how it reacts to magnets and temperature. At room temperature or lower, gadolinium becomes strongly magnetic. In fact, if you cool it down, its magnetic pull becomes even stronger!</p>',
      'Physical properties' => '<p>When you hold gadolinium, it feels like a typical metal, but its inner workings are quite unique. It has an unusually high ability to capture neutrons, making it very useful in nuclear reactors. In addition, when it is placed in a magnetic field, gadolinium actually changes temperature—a rare property known as the magnetocaloric effect.</p>',
      'Chemical properties' => '<p>In the air, gadolinium forms a thin layer of rust (oxidation) that eventually flakes off, exposing more metal to the air. Because of this, it needs to be stored carefully to avoid tarnishing. It reacts slowly with cold water and much faster with hot water, and it easily dissolves in acids to form colorful salts.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>Gadolinium in nature is made up of several different versions of the same element, known as isotopes. Some of these are stable and will last forever, while others very slowly break down over billions of years. Scientists can also create artificial isotopes of gadolinium in laboratories for specialized research.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>Gadolinium was discovered in 1880 by a Swiss chemist named Jean Charles Galissard de Marignac. He found it in a mineral called gadolinite. Six years later, another scientist managed to separate it into a purer form. The element and the mineral are both named in honor of Johan Gadolin, a pioneering chemist who studied rare earth elements.</p>
<p>Historically, India\'s involvement with these rare earths began around 1909, when monazite sands were discovered on its beaches. Over the decades, India developed advanced domestic capabilities for separating rare earth elements, allowing the extraction of high-purity gadolinium today.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>You won\'t find gadolinium lying around as a pure chunk of metal in nature. Instead, it is locked inside minerals like monazite and bastnäsite. </p>
<p>In <b>India</b>, vast coastal deposits of monazite sand—particularly in Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, and Odisha—are the primary source of gadolinium. State-run companies carefully process these sands to separate out the valuable rare earth elements. Globally, gadolinium is also mined in countries like China, the United States, and Australia.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Gadolinium\'s most famous use is in hospitals. It is the key ingredient in MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging) contrast agents. When injected into a patient, gadolinium helps create incredibly clear pictures of the body\'s internal organs and blood vessels. In <b>India</b>, extensive medical research is conducted at top institutions like the IITs, focusing on advanced gadolinium-based nanoparticles for improved tumor imaging and targeted therapies.</p>
<p>Beyond medicine, gadolinium is used in nuclear power plants to control nuclear reactions, thanks to its neutron-absorbing powers. It is also used in creating green phosphors for color TV tubes, and researchers are exploring its use in revolutionary magnetic refrigeration systems that could one day replace traditional, less eco-friendly fridges.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Tb' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Tb',
    'name' => 'Terbium',
    'atomic' => 65,
    'category' => 'lanthanide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Tb" class="wiki-link" title="Terbium (Tb)">Terbium</a></b> is a fascinating and rare silvery-white metal that belongs to a group of elements called the lanthanides. Discovered in 1843 in a Swedish quarry, it is so soft you can cut it with a knife! Even though it might not be a household name, terbium is a hidden superstar in modern technology. It gives the brilliant green colors you see in older color TVs and modern smartphone screens, and it plays a vital role in creating super-strong magnets. In India, the quest for rare earth elements like terbium is an active frontier. While the rich coastal sands of Kerala and Odisha are famous for containing light rare earths, heavy rare earths like terbium are much harder to find in large amounts. Today, leading Indian scientific institutions, such as the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) and Indian Rare Earths Limited (IREL), are working tirelessly to develop advanced technologies to extract and purify these precious elements from domestic minerals, pushing India toward self-reliance in high-tech materials.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9a/Terbium-2.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 158.925352,
    'density' => 8.23,
    'melt' => 1629,
    'boil' => 3396,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Carl Gustaf Mosander',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f9',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.1,
    'electron_affinity' => 112.4,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 565.8,
      1 => 1110,
      2 => 2114,
      3 => 3839,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => 'Terbium is a shiny, silvery-white metal that is surprisingly soft—you can actually cut it with a knife! Like its neighbors on the periodic table, it belongs to the rare earth family. It is relatively stable in the air, meaning it doesn\'t rust or tarnish as quickly as some other metals. However, it can slowly react with water and burns brightly when heated.',
      'Physical properties' => 'At room temperature, terbium is a solid metal. One of its most magical physical traits is how it responds to magnets and temperatures. When it gets very cold, it acts like a strong magnet. In everyday conditions, it is weakly magnetic. It also has a special party trick: when mixed into certain materials, it glows with a brilliant, vibrant green light, making it incredibly useful for lighting and displays.',
      'Chemical properties' => 'Chemically, terbium is quite friendly and likes to combine with other elements. It is mostly found in a "+3" state, which simply means it likes to share three of its electrons when bonding. It dissolves beautifully in acids to form colorful solutions that often look faintly pink or colorless.',
      'Isotopes' => 'In nature, terbium is incredibly pure—it exists almost entirely as just one stable version (or "isotope") called Terbium-159. Scientists have created other radioactive versions of terbium in laboratories, but they break down quickly and aren\'t found naturally on Earth.',
      'History' => 'Terbium\'s story began in 1843 when Swedish chemist Carl Gustaf Mosander was studying a strange black rock from a quarry in Ytterby, Sweden. He found that it contained not one, but three new elements! He named them yttrium, erbium, and terbium, all honoring the small town of Ytterby. For a long time, terbium was just a scientific curiosity, but as technology advanced, its glowing and magnetic secrets were unlocked.',
      'Occurrence' => 'You won\'t find terbium lying around on its own in nature. It is always hidden inside complex minerals like monazite and bastnäsite. In India, the beautiful beaches of Kerala (especially the Chavara-Neendakara belt), Tamil Nadu, and Odisha hold massive reserves of monazite sand. However, these sands are mostly rich in "light" rare earths. Heavy rare earths like terbium are present in much smaller amounts. Today, Indian institutions like the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) and Indian Rare Earths Limited (IREL) are at the forefront of researching new ways to efficiently extract these vital heavy rare earths from domestic resources, aiming to boost India\'s technological independence.',
      'Applications' => 'Terbium is a modern-day technological hero. Its most famous job is creating the bright green colors in screens, from vintage color televisions to the crisp displays of modern smartphones and tablets. It is also used in energy-saving fluorescent lamps. Beyond lighting, terbium is combined with iron and its sister element, dysprosium, to create "Terfenol-D," a special alloy that physically changes its shape in a magnetic field! This shape-shifting superpower is used in advanced sonar systems, sensors, and special speakers.',
    ),
  ),
  'Dy' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Dy',
    'name' => 'Dysprosium',
    'atomic' => 66,
    'category' => 'lanthanide',
    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 66 (Dy)',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b>Dysprosium</b> is a shiny, silvery-white rare earth metal that belongs to the lanthanide series on the periodic table. Its name comes from the Greek word <i>dysprositos</i>, meaning "hard to get," which makes perfect sense considering it took scientists over 30 attempts to finally isolate it! While you will never find dysprosium floating around as a free element in nature, it is hidden within minerals like monazite and bastnäsite. It is incredibly magnetic, highly reactive to water and air at room temperature, and easily cuts with a knife. Despite being "hard to get," dysprosium plays a crucial role in the modern world, powering everything from electric vehicles to nuclear reactors.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/55/Dysprosium-2.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 162.5001,
    'density' => 8.54,
    'melt' => 1680,
    'boil' => 2840,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Lecoq de Boisbaudran',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f10',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.22,
    'electron_affinity' => 33.96,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 573,
      1 => 1130,
      2 => 2200,
      3 => 3990,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p>Dysprosium is a soft, highly magnetic metal that glows with a bright, silvery sheen. However, this glow doesn\'t last long if left out in the open—dysprosium slowly tarnishes in the air and burns readily to form dysprosium oxide. When exposed to water or cold acids, it quickly dissolves and releases hydrogen gas. One of its most fascinating features is its behavior at extremely low temperatures. Below -188°C, dysprosium is strongly magnetic (ferromagnetic), but as it warms up, it enters a bizarre state called antiferromagnetism, where its magnetic fields cancel each other out. Finally, at room temperature, it becomes paramagnetic, meaning it is only weakly attracted to magnets.</p>',
      'Physical properties' => '<p>Dysprosium is a dense, heavy metal that remains solid at room temperature. It melts at a scorching 1,407°C (2,565°F) and boils at 2,562°C (4,653°F). What makes dysprosium physically remarkable is its ability to shrink and expand when exposed to a magnetic field—a property known as magnetostriction. In fact, an alloy of dysprosium, iron, and terbium (called Terfenol-D) has the highest known magnetostriction of any material, allowing it to change its shape in response to magnetism.</p>',
      'Chemical properties' => '<p>Chemically, dysprosium is a very reactive rare earth element. It loves to bond with other elements, particularly halogens like fluorine, chlorine, and bromine, creating brightly colored salts that range from pale yellow to vibrant green. When mixed with water, dysprosium reacts to form dysprosium hydroxide. It is generally found in a +3 oxidation state, which simply means it tends to share three of its electrons when making chemical bonds. While dysprosium itself isn\'t highly toxic, its soluble salts can be mildly dangerous, so chemists handle it with care!</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>In nature, dysprosium is a blend of seven different stable isotopes (atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons). The most abundant of these is Dysprosium-164, which makes up about 28% of all naturally occurring dysprosium. Scientists have also created over 29 artificial, radioactive isotopes in laboratories. Most of these radioactive versions are very short-lived, decaying in just a few milliseconds or days, though Dysprosium-154 can last for millions of years!</p>',
      'History' => '<p>The story of dysprosium began in 1886 when a French chemist named Paul Émile Lecoq de Boisbaudran was analyzing an ore called erbia. After painstakingly dissolving the ore in acid and adding ammonia over 30 separate times, he finally succeeded in separating a brand-new element! Because it was so incredibly difficult to isolate, he named it dysprosium, meaning "hard to get." It wasn\'t until the 1950s, when ion-exchange technology was developed, that scientists were finally able to produce pure dysprosium metal in large quantities.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Dysprosium doesn\'t exist on its own in nature; it prefers to hide inside complex rare-earth minerals like xenotime, fergusonite, and monazite. It is relatively rare, making up only about 5.2 parts per million of the Earth\'s crust. For many years, the primary source of dysprosium was the heavy mineral sands of the world. In India, the coastal sands of Kerala—specifically the Chavara-Neendakara-Kayamkulam belt—are incredibly rich in monazite. While Indian monazite is mostly known for lighter rare earths, it also contains trace amounts of dysprosium. To tap into this strategic resource, the state-owned IREL (India) Limited established its Rare Earths Division in Aluva, Kerala, where scientists have developed advanced chemical processes to extract these critical metals from the sands.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Dysprosium is a hidden superhero in today\'s green technology revolution! Because it can withstand high temperatures without losing its magnetic powers, it is a vital ingredient in the ultra-strong neodymium magnets used in electric vehicle (EV) motors and offshore wind turbines. This makes it a highly strategic metal for countries pushing for clean energy, aligning perfectly with India\'s modern "mine-to-magnet" initiatives. Additionally, because dysprosium is excellent at absorbing neutrons, it is heavily used in control rods to keep nuclear reactors safely balanced. You can also find it in high-intensity commercial lighting, specialized lasers, and data storage devices like computer hard drives.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Ho' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Ho',
    'name' => 'Holmium',
    'atomic' => 67,
    'category' => 'lanthanide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><strong>Holmium (Ho)</strong> is a silvery-white, relatively soft, and malleable rare earth metal with the atomic number 67. To the naked eye, it might just look like another shiny piece of metal, but holmium has a secret power: it possesses the highest magnetic strength of any naturally occurring element! This extraordinary magnetism makes it incredibly valuable for specialized high-tech applications, from generating the strongest artificial magnetic fields to powering advanced medical lasers.</p>
<p>In the context of India, holmium plays a fascinating role. While India boasts some of the world\'s largest rare earth reserves—particularly in the monazite sands along the coasts of Kerala, Tamil Nadu, and Odisha—these sands are mostly rich in "light" rare earths. Because holmium is a "heavy" rare earth, extracting it from domestic sands is highly challenging. To secure a steady supply of crucial elements like holmium, India launched the National Critical Mineral Mission in 2025 and established Dedicated Rare Earth Corridors. Meanwhile, premier Indian institutions like the IITs and CSIR are spearheading research into advanced extraction technologies, ensuring that the nation\'s high-tech and medical sectors continue to thrive.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0a/Holmium2.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 164.930332,
    'density' => 8.79,
    'melt' => 1734,
    'boil' => 2873,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Marc Delafontaine',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f11',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.23,
    'electron_affinity' => 32.61,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 581,
      1 => 1140,
      2 => 2204,
      3 => 4100,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Physical properties' => '<p>Holmium is a shiny, silvery-white metal that is relatively soft and easy to shape. What truly sets holmium apart is its magnetic properties. It has the highest magnetic strength of any element found in nature! If you cool holmium down to very low temperatures, it becomes powerfully magnetic. Because of this, it is often combined with other metals, like yttrium, to create specialized, highly magnetic compounds used in advanced technology.</p>',
      'Chemical properties' => '<p>In dry air, holmium remains shiny and relatively stable. However, when exposed to moisture or higher temperatures, it starts to react. It slowly tarnishes in the air, developing a yellowish crust that looks a bit like iron rust. It also dissolves easily in acids, bubbling up as it releases hydrogen gas and forming bright yellow solutions. Like many rare earth elements, it happily combines with elements like oxygen and halogens (such as fluorine and chlorine) to form beautiful, colorful salts.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>In nature, holmium exists almost entirely as a single stable version, or "isotope," called holmium-165. Scientists have also created several artificial, radioactive versions of holmium in laboratories. One of these, holmium-166, has found an important calling in the medical field. It releases a specific type of energy that makes it incredibly useful for targeted cancer therapies and for calibrating highly sensitive radiation-detecting instruments.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>Holmium\'s story begins in 1878 when Swiss chemists Jacques-Louis Soret and Marc Delafontaine noticed a mysterious, previously unseen light pattern while examining rare earth minerals. Around the same time, Swedish chemist Per Teodor Cleve independently discovered the element while studying erbium. Cleve managed to separate out a brown substance he called <em>holmia</em>, after the Latin name for his hometown of Stockholm. It took several more decades before scientists could completely isolate pure holmium metal, but the name stuck, honoring the Swedish capital where part of its discovery took place.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Holmium is a superstar in both the medical and technological worlds. Its incredible magnetic properties make it ideal for creating the strongest artificial magnetic fields, which are used in scientific research and specialized equipment. One of its most life-changing uses is in medical lasers. Holmium lasers (often called Ho:YAG lasers) emit a very precise type of light that is perfect for delicate surgeries. In India, for example, these lasers have revolutionized urology, offering a highly effective, minimally invasive way to treat kidney stones and prostate conditions, dramatically improving patient recovery times.</p>
<p>Beyond medicine, holmium is used to color glass and cubic zirconia, giving them a beautiful yellow or pink tint. Because it can absorb neutrons, it is also used to safely regulate nuclear reactors. Recently, researchers have even stored data on single atoms of holmium, pointing toward a future where this remarkable element could help build ultra-powerful quantum computers!</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Er' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Er',
    'name' => 'Erbium',
    'atomic' => 68,
    'category' => 'lanthanide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b>Erbium</b> is a fascinating, silvery-white metal belonging to the rare-earth group of elements on the periodic table. While it might not be a household name, you likely interact with erbium\'s magic every day! It is the secret ingredient that powers global internet communications, acting as a light amplifier in the long fiber-optic cables that send data across oceans. </p><p>Discovered in a small Swedish village in the 19th century, erbium has grown into a globally critical material. In <b>India</b>, erbium-bearing minerals such as monazite are naturally found in the rich coastal beach sands of states like Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, and Odisha. Today, Indian scientists at premier institutions (like the IITs) are pioneering modern research on erbium-doped nano-materials for next-generation electronics, sensors, and optics. Furthermore, erbium-powered lasers (Er:YAG) are widely utilized in Indian medical research and dermatology, proving highly effective for safely treating acne and post-traumatic scars on diverse Indian skin types.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2a/Erbium-2.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 167.2593,
    'density' => 9.066,
    'melt' => 1802,
    'boil' => 3141,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Carl Gustaf Mosander',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f12',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.24,
    'electron_affinity' => 30.1,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 589.3,
      1 => 1150,
      2 => 2194,
      3 => 4120,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => 'Erbium is a soft, malleable metal with a bright, silvery shine. When exposed to air, it is surprisingly stable compared to some of its rare-earth cousins, meaning it doesn\'t rust or tarnish as quickly. One of erbium\'s most famous traits is its beautiful pink color. When erbium is mixed with other elements, its salts naturally give off a pastel pink hue, which has historically made it a popular coloring agent for glass and porcelain.',
      'Physical properties' => 'As a solid metal, erbium is fairly heavy and dense. It has a high melting point of 1,529 °C (2,784 °F), which allows it to withstand extreme environments. What makes erbium truly special physically is how it interacts with light. Erbium atoms can absorb invisible infrared light and emit it in a way that perfectly matches the wavelengths used in optical fibers. This unique optical property is why erbium is completely irreplaceable in modern telecommunications.',
      'Chemical properties' => 'Chemically, erbium is part of the lanthanide series. It reacts slowly with water and quickly dissolves in acids to form erbium salts. In its most common chemical form, it loses three of its electrons (known as the +3 oxidation state) to bond with other elements. When it burns, it forms erbium oxide, a stunning pink compound that has been prized by glassmakers and artists for over a century.',
      'Isotopes' => 'In nature, erbium is a mix of six different stable isotopes. You can think of isotopes as \'siblings\' of the element—they have the same chemical personality but slightly different weights. Erbium-166 is the most abundant of these siblings. Scientists have also created many radioactive, artificial isotopes of erbium in laboratories to study nuclear physics, though these decay very quickly and aren\'t found in nature.',
      'History' => 'Erbium\'s story begins in 1843 in a tiny Swedish village called Ytterby, a place famous for yielding several new elements. The Swedish chemist Carl Gustaf Mosander discovered erbium while analyzing a strange black rock from a local quarry. For decades, it was difficult to separate erbium from its rare-earth neighbors. It wasn\'t until 1934 that scientists finally managed to produce pure erbium metal. While its origins are Scandinavian, erbium\'s impact is now global. In modern India, the historical mapping of monazite sands along the peninsular coasts has shifted erbium from a geological curiosity to a strategic mineral of immense national interest.',
      'Occurrence' => 'Like most rare-earth elements, erbium is never found pure in nature. Instead, it is locked inside complex minerals like monazite and bastnäsite. While countries like China and the United States hold major deposits, India boasts significant reserves of rare-earth-bearing beach sands. Specifically, the coastal belts of Kerala, Tamil Nadu, and Odisha contain monazite sand rich in these elements. Although extracting heavy rare earths like erbium from these sands requires advanced processing, India\'s domestic reserves are a vital part of its strategic mineral security.',
      'Applications' => 'Erbium\'s most vital job is keeping the internet running! It is used in erbium-doped fiber amplifiers (EDFAs), which boost light signals traveling through fiber-optic cables so data can cross continents without fading. 

Beyond the internet, erbium is used in specialized lasers. The Er:YAG laser is heavily used in dentistry and dermatology because it can cut tissue precisely without generating excess heat. In India, these lasers have been extensively studied and embraced for scar removal and cosmetic skin treatments. Erbium is also added to sunglasses and safety glasses to block harmful laser light, and it is still used today to give glass and cubic zirconia a beautiful, permanent pink tint.',
    ),
  ),
  'Tm' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Tm',
    'name' => 'Thulium',
    'atomic' => 69,
    'category' => 'lanthanide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => 'Thulium is a fascinating, silvery-gray metal and a member of the lanthanide series on the periodic table. With the atomic number 69 and the symbol Tm, it is the second-least abundant rare earth element naturally found on Earth (only promethium is rarer). Though soft enough to be cut with a knife, thulium has powerful modern uses. It is prized in advanced medical lasers and portable X-ray devices. Due to its scarcity and high cost, it is considered a highly specialized, niche element rather than a household name.',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6b/Thulium-2.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 168.934222,
    'density' => 9.32,
    'melt' => 1818,
    'boil' => 2223,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Per Teodor Cleve',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f13',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.25,
    'electron_affinity' => 99,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 596.7,
      1 => 1160,
      2 => 2285,
      3 => 4120,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Physical properties' => 'Despite being a metal, pure thulium is surprisingly soft—you can actually slice it with a knife! It boasts a bright, silvery shine, though it will slowly tarnish when exposed to air. Like many metals, it is both malleable (can be hammered into sheets) and ductile (can be drawn into wires). When it comes to its magnetic personality, thulium changes its behavior based on the temperature. Below a chilling -241°C (32 K), it is ferromagnetic like iron. As it warms up, it becomes antiferromagnetic, and finally, above -217°C (56 K), it settles into being paramagnetic.',
      'Chemical properties' => 'Thulium is fairly well-behaved in the air but will slowly tarnish. If heated to 150°C, it readily burns to form thulium(III) oxide. It reacts quite slowly with cold water but much more quickly with hot water, releasing hydrogen gas. Thulium also reacts enthusiastically with all halogens—like fluorine and chlorine—especially at higher temperatures. When dissolved in dilute sulfuric acid, it creates a pale green solution. Interestingly, in most of its chemical reactions, thulium forms a +3 oxidation state, and these thulium ions can glow with a beautiful, bright blue luminescence under certain conditions.',
      'Isotopes' => 'In nature, thulium exists entirely as a single, stable isotope: thulium-169. However, scientists have created over 30 artificial, radioactive isotopes of thulium in laboratories. The most important of these is thulium-170, which has a half-life of about 128.6 days. When natural thulium-169 is bombarded in a nuclear reactor, it turns into thulium-170, which naturally emits X-rays. This makes it incredibly useful as a portable radiation source. Most other artificial thulium isotopes are short-lived, with half-lives of less than 10 minutes.',
      'History' => 'Thulium was discovered in 1879 by Swedish chemist Per Teodor Cleve. He was studying a rare earth mineral called erbia and realized it contained unknown impurities. By carefully separating them, he found two new materials: holmium and thulium. He named thulium after "Thule", the ancient Greek and Roman name for a mythical, icy region in the far north, often associated with Scandinavia or Iceland. Initially given the symbol "Tu", it was later changed to "Tm". Thulium is so rare that early chemists could not even isolate enough of it to see its green oxide. It wasn\'t until 1911 that an almost pure sample was finally produced, requiring a grueling 15,000 purification steps!',
      'Occurrence' => 'You will never find pure thulium lying around in nature. It is always locked away in minerals alongside other rare earth elements, most notably in monazite, gadolinite, and euxenite. It is incredibly scarce, making up just 0.5 parts per million of the Earth\'s crust. It is the rarest of all the stable lanthanides! Most of the world\'s thulium ore is mined in China, but there are also significant reserves in Australia, Brazil, Greenland, the United States, and India. In the Indian context, thulium is found in coastal mineral sands, primarily within monazite deposits along the coasts of Andhra Pradesh, Odisha, and Kerala. It is increasingly viewed as a critical strategic mineral for India\'s high-tech, green energy, and defense future.',
      'Applications' => 'Because it is rare and expensive, thulium is reserved for specialized, high-tech jobs rather than everyday products. One of its most important uses is in the medical field. Thulium-doped lasers are incredibly precise and are used for minimally invasive surgeries, such as prostate procedures and skin treatments. Furthermore, the radioactive isotope thulium-170 is used in portable X-ray machines. Because these devices do not need a heavy electrical power source, they are life-savers in remote areas, field hospitals, and emergency sites. Thulium is also used as an anti-counterfeiting measure in some high-value banknotes (like the Euro) because it glows a brilliant blue under UV light. Finally, it plays a key role in advanced microwave equipment, high-precision optical lenses, and high-temperature superconductors.',
      'Biological role' => 'Thulium has no known biological role in humans or any other living organisms. We do consume tiny, trace amounts of it every year through vegetables, but plant roots generally do not absorb much of it. While soluble thulium salts are considered mildly toxic and should be handled with care, insoluble thulium is completely non-toxic. Overall, thulium is regarded as having a very low level of toxicity, meaning it poses virtually no threat to human health.',
    ),
  ),
  'Yb' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Yb',
    'name' => 'Ytterbium',
    'atomic' => 70,
    'category' => 'lanthanide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Yb" class="wiki-link" title="Ytterbium (Yb)">Ytterbium</a></b> is a silvery-white, soft metal that belongs to a group of elements called the lanthanides, or rare earth metals. Found in trace amounts in the Earth\'s crust, it is rarely used in its pure form but plays a crucial role in modern technology, from atomic clocks to powerful lasers. Although it\'s named after a small Swedish village where it was first discovered, Ytterbium is found globally. In India, it can be traced to the rich monazite sands along the coastal beaches of Kerala, an important geological source for rare earth minerals.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ce/Ytterbium-3.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 173.0451,
    'density' => 6.9,
    'melt' => 1097,
    'boil' => 1469,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Jean Charles Galissard de Marignac',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.1,
    'electron_affinity' => -1.93,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 603.4,
      1 => 1174.8,
      2 => 2417,
      3 => 4203,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Physical properties' => '<p>Ytterbium is a shiny, soft metal that you could easily shape or bend. It has a silvery-white appearance with a slight pale yellow tint. One of its most unique features is how it reacts to extreme conditions. Under normal room temperature, it conducts electricity like a typical metal. However, if you squeeze it under immense pressure, it changes its behavior and acts more like a semiconductor (similar to the materials used in computer chips). Unlike many of its rare-earth cousins, ytterbium isn\'t strongly magnetic at low temperatures. It also has an unusually low melting point (824 °C or 1515 °F) and boiling point for a metal in its group.</p>',
      'Chemical properties' => '<p>When left in the open air, ytterbium slowly loses its shine, taking on a brownish or golden tarnish as it reacts with oxygen. If you grind it into a fine powder, it becomes highly reactive and can even burn with a brilliant, emerald-green flame! It dissolves slowly in water, but if dropped into acidic solutions, it quickly bubbles away as it releases hydrogen gas. When it forms compounds, they are usually colorless or white.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>In nature, ytterbium is a mix of seven stable "versions," known as isotopes. These natural isotopes are perfectly safe and don\'t break down over time. Scientists have also created over 30 artificial, radioactive isotopes in laboratories. Some of these radioactive versions, especially Ytterbium-169, give off specific types of radiation and are highly useful in medicine, acting like portable X-ray machines to help doctors see inside the human body.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Ytterbium doesn\'t exist as a pure nugget in nature. Instead, it hides inside complex rare-earth minerals. One of the most important sources is monazite sand. Interestingly, the beautiful coastal beaches of Kerala in southern India are famous for their rich deposits of monazite sands, which hold a treasure trove of rare-earth elements like ytterbium, alongside thorium. While China, the US, and Brazil are also major producers, the monazite deposits in Kerala represent an important geological and strategic resource for India. Since ytterbium looks and acts so much like the other rare earths mixed with it, scientists had a very hard time figuring out how to separate it until the mid-20th century.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>The story of ytterbium starts in 1878 with a Swiss chemist named Jean Charles Galissard de Marignac. He found it hidden inside a mineral from a famous quarry in Ytterby, Sweden—a tiny village that has remarkably lent its name to four different elements on the periodic table! For decades, what chemists thought was just "ytterbium" turned out to be a mix of two different elements. In 1907, a French chemist named Georges Urbain finally managed to separate the true ytterbium from a new element, which he called lutetium.</p>',
      'Applications and Modern Research' => '<p>Because it is scarce and hard to separate, ytterbium isn\'t used in everyday items like copper or iron. However, it has some incredible high-tech jobs. It is used to make extremely precise atomic clocks—some of the most accurate timekeepers ever created! It\'s also used to build powerful solid-state lasers and to strengthen stainless steel. In India, leading scientific institutions like the Jawaharlal Nehru Centre for Advanced Scientific Research (JNCASR) conduct cutting-edge research using ytterbium. They study it to develop advanced intermetallic compounds, exploring new quantum materials and their potential for future technologies.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Lu' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Lu',
    'name' => 'Lutetium',
    'atomic' => 71,
    'category' => 'lanthanide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p>Lutetium (symbol Lu, atomic number 71) is a silvery-white metal that marks the end of the lanthanide series in the periodic table. Often grouped with rare earth elements, it is known for being the hardest and densest among them. While it doesn\'t appear in ancient Indian texts like traditional metals (e.g., copper or gold), lutetium has significant modern relevance in India. Today, Indian scientists at institutions like the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) in Mumbai have made critical contributions by indigenously producing Lutetium-177, a vital radioisotope used for advanced cancer treatments. Furthermore, India\'s rich monazite sand deposits along the coasts of Kerala, Tamil Nadu, and Odisha serve as important natural sources for rare earth elements, including lutetium. Though rare and challenging to extract, lutetium is a fascinating element with specialized uses in medical science, radiometric dating, and chemical research.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e8/Lutetium.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 174.96681,
    'density' => 9.841,
    'melt' => 1925,
    'boil' => 3675,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Georges Urbain',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d1',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.27,
    'electron_affinity' => 33.4,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 523.5,
      1 => 1340,
      2 => 2022.3,
      3 => 4370,
      4 => 6445,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p>Lutetium is a chemical element with the symbol Lu and atomic number 71. It is a silvery-white metal that resists corrosion in dry air but can tarnish when exposed to moisture. As the final element in the lanthanide series, it is traditionally counted among the rare earth elements, although some scientists classify it as the first element of the sixth-period transition metals. Lutetium is quite rare, though it is more abundant in the Earth\'s crust than silver. Because of the \'lanthanide contraction\'—a phenomenon where atoms get slightly smaller across the series—lutetium is the smallest, densest, and hardest atom among its rare earth peers. Its physical toughness is notable, boasting the highest Brinell hardness (890–1300 MPa) of any lanthanide.</p>',
      'Physical properties' => '<p>At its core, a lutetium atom contains 71 electrons. In chemical reactions, it typically loses its three outermost electrons, resulting in a +3 oxidation state. What makes lutetium unique among lanthanides is its atomic structure: its 4f electron shell is completely full and highly stabilized, meaning only its outermost electrons are involved in chemical bonding. This behavior makes it act more like a transition metal (such as scandium or yttrium) than a typical rare earth element. Lutetium boasts the highest density and melting point of all the lanthanides, solidifying its reputation as an incredibly dense, tough, and robust metal.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>In nature, lutetium is found as a mix of two isotopes: lutetium-175 and lutetium-176. Lutetium-175 is stable and makes up the vast majority (about 97.4%) of the natural element. The remaining 2.6% is lutetium-176, which is weakly radioactive with an incredibly long half-life of 38 billion years. Because it decays so slowly, scientists use it to determine the age of ancient minerals and meteorites. Beyond these natural forms, scientists have created over 40 synthetic radioisotopes in laboratories. The most important of these in modern times is Lutetium-177. Institutions like India\'s Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) have pioneered the indigenous production of Lutetium-177. This isotope is revolutionary in \'theranostics\' (therapy combined with diagnostics), offering highly targeted and effective treatments for certain types of cancers, such as neuroendocrine tumors.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>The discovery of lutetium in 1907 involved a dramatic race between three scientists: Georges Urbain of France, Baron Carl Auer von Welsbach of Austria, and Charles James of the United States. All three independently found lutetium hidden as an impurity in a mineral called ytterbia. Urbain was the first to publish his findings, which led to a bitter dispute with Welsbach over who truly deserved the credit. In 1909, the naming honors were awarded to Urbain, who named the element \'lutecium\' (later changed to lutetium) after Lutetia, the ancient Roman name for Paris. Although Welsbach\'s proposed name, \'cassiopeium,\' was popular among German scientists for decades, the International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry officially settled on lutetium in 1949. Pure lutetium metal was incredibly difficult to isolate and was not produced until 1953. In a modern context, while lutetium lacks the ancient historical roots of metals like iron or gold in ancient Indian texts, India\'s geographical history plays a part in its story today; the country\'s vast coastal monazite deposits represent significant reserves of rare earth elements, from which lutetium can be extracted.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Because it is so rare and difficult to separate from other elements, natural lutetium has very few commercial applications and is used only in small quantities. It is sometimes used as a catalyst in complex chemical reactions, such as cracking petroleum in refineries, or mixed into metal alloys. However, its most profound application is in the field of nuclear medicine. The synthetic radioisotope Lutetium-177 is used globally—and is now produced domestically in India—for targeted radiotherapy. Bound to special molecules like DOTA-TATE, it seeks out and destroys cancer cells with remarkable precision, making lutetium a tiny but mighty weapon in the fight against neuroendocrine tumors and prostate cancer.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Hf' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Hf',
    'name' => 'Hafnium',
    'atomic' => 72,
    'category' => 'transition metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><strong>Hafnium</strong> (symbol <strong>Hf</strong>, atomic number 72) is a shiny, silvery-gray metal that looks and acts a lot like its periodic table neighbor, zirconium. Because they are so similar, hafnium is almost always found hiding inside zirconium minerals, and separating the two is quite a challenge!</p>
<p>Despite being relatively rare, hafnium plays a massive role in high-tech industries. It is incredibly good at absorbing neutrons and can withstand extreme heat, making it a star player in nuclear reactors and aerospace engineering. In India, hafnium is officially recognized as a critical strategic mineral. It is vital for the nation\'s advanced space missions and nuclear energy programs, with major research driven by institutions like the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) and the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO).</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Hafnium_%2872_Hf%29.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 178.492,
    'density' => 13.31,
    'melt' => 2506,
    'boil' => 4876,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Dirk Coster',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d2',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.3,
    'electron_affinity' => 17.18,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 658.5,
      1 => 1440,
      2 => 2250,
      3 => 3216,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      0 => 
      array (
        'title' => 'Characteristics',
        'content' => '
<p>Hafnium is a tough, heavy transition metal that resists corrosion and handles extreme temperatures with ease. One of its most famous features is its ability to swallow up neutrons, a property that makes it incredibly useful in nuclear science. Curiously, while a solid chunk of hafnium is perfectly safe, hafnium in powder form is pyrophoric—meaning it can spontaneously catch fire in the air!</p>
<p>Chemically, it is like a twin to zirconium. The two elements are so identical in their chemical behaviors that early chemists struggled for years to tell them apart. It wasn\'t until scientists started using X-rays to look at their atomic structures that they could finally separate the two.</p>
',
      ),
      1 => 
      array (
        'title' => 'Isotopes',
        'content' => '
<p>Hafnium has dozens of different versions, known as isotopes, which vary slightly in their weight. Five of these are stable and make up the hafnium we find in nature.</p>
<p>There is also one very famous (and controversial) radioactive form called a "nuclear isomer" (specifically, Hafnium-178m2). For years, scientists debated whether this highly energetic isomer could be triggered to release a massive burst of gamma rays all at once, potentially creating a new kind of weapon. Ultimately, creating this isomer proved far too difficult and expensive, and the idea of a hafnium weapon remains the stuff of science fiction.</p>
',
      ),
      2 => 
      array (
        'title' => 'Occurrence',
        'content' => '
<p>You won\'t find pure hafnium sitting around in nature. It prefers to blend in, naturally occurring inside zirconium-rich minerals like zircon. Extracting it requires processing massive amounts of heavy mineral sands.</p>
<p>In India, these heavy mineral sands are found abundantly along the coastal stretches of states like <strong>Kerala, Odisha, and Tamil Nadu</strong>. Because hafnium is found within these domestic zircon deposits, India\'s ability to mine and process it locally is a tremendous advantage for its strategic industries.</p>
',
      ),
      3 => 
      array (
        'title' => 'History',
        'content' => '
<p>When the famous chemist Dmitri Mendeleev created his early periodic table in 1869, he left a blank space for element 72, predicting that a heavier cousin of titanium and zirconium must exist. For decades, scientists hunted for it. It wasn\'t until 1923 that Dirk Coster and George de Hevesy finally found it hiding inside a piece of Norwegian zircon.</p>
<p>They made the discovery in Copenhagen, Denmark. To honor the city, they named the new element "Hafnium," derived from <em>Hafnia</em>, the Latin name for Copenhagen.</p>
<p>In modern times, India has made significant historical strides in hafnium technology to ensure national self-reliance. Historically dependent on complex foreign supply chains for this critical metal, India established its first indigenous hafnium metal sponge pilot plant at <strong>C-MET (Centre for Materials for Electronics Technology)</strong> in Hyderabad. This milestone, achieved in collaboration with ISRO\'s Vikram Sarabhai Space Centre (VSSC), marked a major step forward in India\'s ability to produce space-grade hafnium domestically.</p>
',
      ),
      4 => 
      array (
        'title' => 'Applications',
        'content' => '
<p>Because hafnium is a master at absorbing neutrons, its most crucial job is inside nuclear reactors. It is used to make control rods that regulate the nuclear fission process—acting like the brakes in a nuclear power plant. In India, the <strong>Nuclear Fuel Complex (NFC)</strong> and the <strong>Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC)</strong> have pioneered the complex chemical processes needed to completely separate hafnium from zirconium, ensuring both elements can be used safely in the country\'s nuclear fuel cycle.</p>
<p>Beyond nuclear energy, hafnium\'s ability to survive blazing temperatures makes it perfect for aerospace alloys, including rocket engines and spacecraft thrusters. It\'s also found in everyday technology: if you have a modern smartphone or computer, there\'s a good chance hafnium oxide is inside its tiny microchips, helping the transistors run faster and more efficiently.</p>
',
      ),
    ),
  ),
  'Ta' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Ta',
    'name' => 'Tantalum',
    'atomic' => 73,
    'category' => 'transition metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b>Tantalum</b> (symbol <b>Ta</b>, atomic number 73) is a rare, hard, blue-gray metal that is highly resistant to corrosion and heat. It is a critical mineral in the modern world, essential for making the electronics and semiconductors used in everyday devices like smartphones and laptops. In late 2023, a team of researchers from IIT Ropar made a groundbreaking discovery: they found traces of tantalum in the sands of the Sutlej River in Punjab, India! This remarkable find highlights India\'s potential to become a significant player in the global supply of this strategic metal. Historically named after Tantalus from Greek mythology, tantalum today sits at the intersection of modern technology and exciting new geological discoveries in India.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/Tantalum.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 180.947882,
    'density' => 16.69,
    'melt' => 3290,
    'boil' => 5731,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Anders Gustaf Ekeberg',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d3',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.5,
    'electron_affinity' => 31,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 761,
      1 => 1500,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'History' => '<p>Tantalum was first discovered in 1802 by the Swedish chemist Anders Gustaf Ekeberg. He named the element after Tantalus, a character from Greek mythology who was punished by having water recede whenever he tried to drink. Ekeberg chose this name because tantalum oxide doesn\'t react or \'drink\' acid when placed in it! For a long time, scientists confused tantalum with another element called niobium because they are so similar and often found together. It wasn\'t until 1846 that Heinrich Rose finally proved they were two distinct elements.</p><p>In modern times, the history of tantalum took an exciting turn in India. In 2023, researchers from the Indian Institute of Technology (IIT) Ropar serendipitously discovered tantalum in the Sutlej River in Punjab. This recent discovery adds a thrilling new chapter to the story of tantalum, showing that this ancient metal is still surprising us today!</p>',
      'Characteristics' => '<p>Tantalum is a superhero among metals! It is incredibly dense, hard, and has a beautiful blue-gray color. One of its most amazing superpowers is its ability to resist corrosion—it is almost completely immune to chemical attacks at temperatures below 150°C. In fact, it is so resistant that it won\'t even dissolve in strong acids that can melt other metals.</p><p>Tantalum is also highly ductile, meaning it can be stretched into very thin wires without breaking. When you combine this with a super high melting point (over 3,000°C), you get a metal that can survive the most extreme environments imaginable. This makes it a perfect fit for high-tech applications, and its discovery in the Sutlej riverbed means India could soon harness these remarkable characteristics locally.</p>',
      'Physical properties' => '<p>Physically, tantalum is heavy and extremely tough. Its melting point is a scorching 3,017 °C (5,463 °F)—one of the highest of all elements, surpassed only by metals like tungsten and rhenium! This means it can withstand temperatures that would turn most other metals into puddles.</p><p>When exposed to air, it forms a protective oxide layer that makes it even more durable. Because it is so biocompatible (meaning the human body doesn\'t reject it or react to it negatively), it can even be used safely inside the human body! The physical resilience of tantalum is exactly why its recent discovery in Indian river sands is so valuable for the nation\'s high-tech manufacturing goals.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>In nature, tantalum comes in two main \'flavors\' or isotopes: Tantalum-181 and Tantalum-180m. Tantalum-181 makes up almost all of the naturally occurring tantalum (99.99%). The other one, Tantalum-180m, is incredibly rare and has a fascinating quirk—it exists in an \'excited\' state but is remarkably stable, with a half-life so incredibly long that it has never actually been observed to decay!</p><p>Scientists have also created several artificial, radioactive isotopes of tantalum in laboratories for research purposes. Understanding these isotopes helps scientists around the world, including those at India\'s top research institutes, trace the origins of geological samples like the ones found in Punjab.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Tantalum is quite rare, making up just a tiny fraction of the Earth\'s crust (about 1 or 2 parts per million). It is typically found in minerals like tantalite and columbite. For many years, countries like Australia, Brazil, and nations in Central Africa (like Rwanda and the Democratic Republic of the Congo) have been the main producers of tantalum.</p><p>However, the geographic map of tantalum occurrence is shifting. In a landmark discovery in 2023, researchers from IIT Ropar identified the presence of tantalum in the Sutlej River basin in Punjab, India. This was a monumental find, as India traditionally relied on imports for this critical mineral. The Sutlej river discovery suggests that there may be untapped mineral wealth within the country, offering a potential boost to India\'s self-reliance in critical minerals.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Tantalum is practically everywhere in modern technology! Its biggest use is in electronics. Because it can hold a large electrical charge in a very small volume, it is used to make tiny capacitors found in smartphones, laptops, gaming consoles, and automotive electronics. Beyond gadgets, tantalum\'s incredibly high melting point makes it ideal for making parts for jet engines, rockets, and chemical manufacturing equipment.</p><p>Furthermore, because the human body completely ignores it (it\'s biocompatible), it is widely used in medicine for surgical instruments, bone implants, and pacemakers. With the burgeoning electronics manufacturing sector in India—such as the \'Make in India\' semiconductor push—the newly discovered tantalum deposits in the Sutlej River could provide a vital homegrown supply chain for creating the next generation of electronic devices!</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'W' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'W',
    'name' => 'Tungsten',
    'atomic' => 74,
    'category' => 'transition metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b>Tungsten</b> (symbol <b>W</b>, atomic number 74), also known as wolfram, is a remarkable transition metal famous for having the highest melting point of any element. Its name comes from the Swedish words "tung sten," meaning "heavy stone." It is a grayish-white, lustrous metal that is incredibly hard and dense. Found in minerals like wolframite and scheelite, tungsten is essential for everything from old-school incandescent lightbulb filaments to cutting-edge aerospace engineering. In India, tungsten is classified as a strategic and critical mineral, vital for defense and high-tech industrial growth. The country has historical deposits in places like Degana in Rajasthan, and ongoing exploration by the Geological Survey of India (GSI) continues to secure this precious resource for the nation\'s future.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c8/Tungsten_rod_with_oxidised_surface.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 183.841,
    'density' => 19.25,
    'melt' => 3695,
    'boil' => 6203,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Carl Wilhelm Scheele',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d4',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 2.36,
    'electron_affinity' => 78.76,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 770,
      1 => 1700,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p>Tungsten is a grayish-white, lustrous metal that shines brightly when polished. Because of its incredible density and hardness, it is not an element you can easily dent or scratch. Its name originally comes from the Swedish words "tung sten," meaning "heavy stone," which perfectly describes its nature. However, it’s most famous symbol, "W", actually comes from its other historical name, "Wolfram".</p>',
      'Physical properties' => '<p>Tungsten is the ultimate heavyweight champion of the periodic table. It holds the record for the highest melting point of all discovered metals—a blistering 3,422 °C (6,192 °F)—and also boasts the highest boiling point. Its density is almost exactly the same as gold, making it impressively heavy and substantial. When pure, it can be stretched and pulled into incredibly thin wires, but usually, it is hard and brittle.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>In nature, tungsten is a mix of five different isotopes (versions of the element with slightly different weights). Four of these are considered completely stable, while one is technically radioactive—but it decays so unimaginably slowly that its half-life is billions of times longer than the age of the universe! Scientists also study synthetic, short-lived tungsten isotopes in advanced physics labs to better understand nuclear structure.</p>',
      'Chemical properties' => '<p>Despite its ruggedness, tungsten is surprisingly well-behaved chemically. It strongly resists corrosion and doesn\'t easily react with oxygen or water at room temperature. It only begins to oxidize when things get really hot! It also gracefully ignores most acids, though it can be dissolved by a specific mix of nitric and hydrofluoric acids. Its ability to remain stable under extreme conditions makes it incredibly valuable for high-stakes scientific applications, including research conducted by Indian space and nuclear scientists.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>Tungsten’s journey is a tale of early chemistry and modern industry. While ancient Indian metallurgists were globally renowned masters of iron and steel (such as the famous Wootz steel), they did not identify tungsten as a distinct element. Instead, tungsten was first hypothesized by Peter Woulfe in 1779 and officially isolated in 1783 by Spanish brothers Juan José and Fausto Elhuyar. They extracted it from wolframite, a mineral often found alongside tin ores. The element’s alternative name, "wolfram", is the source of its chemical symbol, W. Today, the scientific legacy of tungsten continues robustly in India. Premier institutions like the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) pioneer the development of indigenous tungsten heavy alloys (WHA) and powder metallurgy techniques, cementing India\'s capabilities in nuclear and aerospace applications.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>In nature, tungsten is never found as a pure, shiny metal but is rather locked away in chemical compounds inside rocks. It is most commonly extracted from minerals like wolframite and scheelite. In India, key tungsten occurrences are mapped in states like Rajasthan (notably the historic Degana mines), Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh, and Maharashtra. Although domestic extraction has faced challenges due to low-grade ores, Indian scientists and the Indian Bureau of Mines are actively developing advanced beneficiation technologies. These physical and chemical processes aim to efficiently extract high-quality tungsten, boosting India\'s strategic mineral security.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Because of its incredible strength, density, and heat resistance, tungsten is practically everywhere! For over a century, it literally lit up our homes as the glowing wire filament inside incandescent lightbulbs. Today, it is mostly used to manufacture tungsten carbide—an extraordinarily hard material essential for industrial cutting tools, mining drills, and even scratch-resistant jewelry. In the modern Indian context, tungsten is indispensable. It forms the core of specialized heavy alloys used extensively by the defense sector for radiation shielding, missile tips, and armor-piercing ammunition. Its unmatched high-temperature tolerance also makes it a crucial component in India\'s rapidly growing aerospace sector, electronics manufacturing, and high-performance engineering.</p>',
      'Biological role' => '<p>Surprisingly, tungsten holds the record as the heaviest element known to play a functional biological role! While it can be toxic in high concentrations to animals and humans, some ancient species of bacteria and archaea actually rely on tungsten in their enzymes. These microscopic organisms often thrive in extreme environments, like scalding deep-sea hydrothermal vents, where they utilize tungsten much like other lifeforms use molybdenum. While it is not a nutrient for humans, modern environmental science—including research by Indian agricultural and soil scientists—carefully monitors tungsten levels in soil and water. Understanding how tungsten interacts with the ecosystem helps ensure environmental safety and sustainable industrial practices.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Re' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Re',
    'name' => 'Rhenium',
    'atomic' => 75,
    'category' => 'transition metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><strong>Rhenium</strong> is a silvery-white, exceedingly rare transition metal with the atomic number 75. Discovered in 1925, it was the last stable element to be found. With one of the highest melting points (3,186°C) and the highest boiling point among stable elements, rhenium is built for extreme environments. Today, this incredibly dense metal plays a critical role in modern technology, from crafting heat-resistant superalloys for jet engines and spacecraft to serving as a catalyst in producing high-octane fuel. Due to its scarcity and immense strategic value, it is considered a critical mineral globally, including in India\'s rapidly growing aerospace and defense sectors.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/Pure_rhenium_bead%2C_arc_melted%2C_21_grams._Original_size_in_cm_-_1.5_x_1.7.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 186.2071,
    'density' => 21.02,
    'melt' => 3459,
    'boil' => 5869,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Masataka Ogawa',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d5',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.9,
    'electron_affinity' => 5.8273,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 760,
      1 => 1260,
      2 => 2510,
      3 => 3640,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'History' => '<p>In 1908, Japanese chemist Masataka Ogawa claimed to have discovered the 43rd element, naming it <em>nipponium</em> after Japan. However, modern analysis reveals he had actually discovered element 75 (rhenium) but misinterpreted his findings. The element was officially "rediscovered" and named in 1925 by German scientists Walter Noddack, Ida Tacke-Noddack, and Otto Berg. They named it rhenium after the river Rhine (Latin: <em>Rhenus</em>).</p>
<p>To obtain a usable sample, they extracted just 1 gram of rhenium by processing a staggering 660 kilograms of molybdenite ore! Rhenium\'s discovery marked a historical milestone: it was the very last naturally occurring stable element ever discovered on the periodic table.</p>',
      'Characteristics' => '<p>Rhenium is a dense, silvery-white metal known for its extreme resilience. It boasts the second-highest melting point of any element (exceeded only by tungsten) and the highest boiling point of all stable elements. It is also remarkably dense—only platinum, iridium, and osmium pack more mass into the same space.</p>
<p>When prepared in its typical solid form, rhenium is highly ductile, meaning it can be easily bent, coiled, or rolled. In addition to its physical toughness, rhenium resists wear and corrosion from harsh chemicals like alkalis and acids. At ultra-low temperatures, certain rhenium alloys, such as those mixed with molybdenum or tungsten, even become superconducting.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>Naturally occurring rhenium is made up of just two isotopes. Interestingly, the only stable isotope (rhenium-185) makes up a minority (37.4%) of the natural supply. The remaining 62.6% is rhenium-187, which is technically unstable and radioactive, but its half-life is so incredibly long—over 41 billion years—that it is practically stable on human timescales.</p>
<p>Because of this slow decay, scientists use rhenium-187 to date ancient ores and rocks. In the medical field, synthetic radioactive isotopes like rhenium-186 and rhenium-188 are utilized in targeted radiotherapy for treating bone cancer and relieving pain.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Rhenium is exceptionally scarce, ranking among the rarest elements in the Earth\'s crust with an average concentration of just 1 part per billion. It is generally not found as a free metal in nature. Instead, it "hides" in trace amounts within the mineral molybdenite, which is its primary commercial source. Chile is currently the world\'s leading producer, holding the largest reserves within its copper ore deposits.</p>
<p>Interestingly, a natural rhenium mineral called rheniite was only discovered in 1994, condensing from the hot volcanic gases of the Kudriavy volcano in the Kuril Islands. In India, rhenium is recognized as a strategic and critical mineral. With potential traces found in copper belts (like Singhbhum) and coal mines, India is actively exploring domestic sources and recycling technologies to secure its supply for national industries.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Rhenium is a heavy hitter in high-tech industries. The vast majority of the world\'s rhenium (about 70%) is mixed with nickel to create high-temperature superalloys. These tough materials form the critical turbine blades in jet engines and gas turbines, allowing them to run hotter, longer, and more efficiently.</p>
<p>Another major use is in the petrochemical industry, where platinum-rhenium catalysts help refine crude oil into high-octane, lead-free gasoline. Because it can withstand extreme heat and wear, rhenium is also used in electrical contacts, thermocouples, and spacecraft components. In India, as the aerospace and defense sectors rapidly expand, the demand for rhenium superalloys and advanced metal recovery technologies is higher than ever.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Os' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Os',
    'name' => 'Osmium',
    'atomic' => 76,
    'category' => 'transition metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b>Osmium (Os)</b> is a fascinating and incredibly dense metal, known for being the heaviest naturally occurring element on Earth. Imagine a block of osmium the size of a milk jug—it would weigh about as much as an adult human! First discovered in 1803 by English chemist Smithson Tennant, it has a beautiful bluish-silver color and is highly prized for its extreme hardness.</p><p>While ancient Indian texts like the Vedas and traditional Ayurvedic treatises like the <em>Charaka Samhita</em> demonstrate a rich history of metallurgy (focusing on gold, silver, copper, and iron), osmium is notably absent. It requires modern, advanced chemical techniques to extract. Today, osmium plays a role in modern Indian scientific research, such as studying river sediments in the Ganga to understand ancient geological changes, and it is catching the eye of the growing Indian luxury market as a rare, investment-grade precious metal.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3c/Osmium-bead.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 190.233,
    'density' => 22.59,
    'melt' => 3306,
    'boil' => 5285,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Smithson Tennant',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d6',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 2.2,
    'electron_affinity' => 103.99,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 840,
      1 => 1600,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => 'Osmium is part of the platinum group of metals. It is incredibly hard, brittle, and remains shiny even at very high temperatures. Because it\'s so dense and tough, it\'s very difficult to shape or mold. However, these same properties make it an excellent addition to alloys when you need something that won\'t wear down easily.',
      'Physical properties' => 'Osmium holds the title for the densest naturally occurring element—it\'s about twice as heavy as lead! It has a striking bluish-silver appearance and an incredibly high melting point of over 3,000°C (5,400°F). Because it is so hard and brittle, it can easily shatter if struck hard enough, making it challenging to work with in its pure form.',
      'Chemical properties' => 'When osmium is exposed to the air, especially in a powdered form, it slowly reacts with oxygen to form osmium tetroxide. This compound has a very strong, sharp smell—in fact, the name \'osmium\' comes from the Greek word \'osme\', meaning \'odor\'. Osmium tetroxide is highly toxic and can cause damage to the eyes, skin, and lungs, which is why scientists handle this element with extreme care.',
      'Isotopes' => 'Osmium exists in nature as a mix of seven different versions, called isotopes. Six of these are completely stable, while one is very slightly radioactive, though it breaks down so slowly that it takes billions of years. In India, geologists have analyzed osmium isotopes in the sediments of the mighty Ganga river. By studying these tiny traces, Indian scientists can learn about how the Earth\'s continents have weathered and changed over millions of years.',
      'History' => 'Osmium was discovered in 1803 by Smithson Tennant in London. He found it alongside another metal, iridium, in the leftover residue when platinum was dissolved in powerful acids. Although ancient Indian metallurgy (Rasashastra) was highly advanced, extracting osmium required chemical techniques that were not yet invented. Thus, there are no mentions of osmium in ancient Indian texts. Today, however, osmium is gaining recognition in India\'s modern technological and luxury sectors.',
      'Occurrence' => 'Osmium is one of the rarest elements in the Earth\'s crust. It is typically found mixed with other precious metals like platinum and iridium in natural river sands and specific rock formations. While India does not have major primary mines for osmium, the metal is sourced globally and brought into the country for specialized scientific and industrial uses. Most of the world\'s osmium comes from large mining operations in South Africa, Russia, and North America.',
      'Applications' => 'Because pure osmium is so hard and brittle, it is usually mixed with other metals to create super-durable alloys. In the past, it was famous for being used in the tough tips of fountain pens and phonograph needles. Today, osmium is used in high-wear electrical contacts and specialized scientific equipment. In modern Indian research labs, osmium compounds like osmium tetroxide are crucial for preparing biological samples for electron microscopes, allowing scientists to see the tiny structures of cells with incredible contrast.',
    ),
  ),
  'Ir' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Ir',
    'name' => 'Iridium',
    'atomic' => 77,
    'category' => 'transition metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><strong>Iridium</strong> (symbol <strong>Ir</strong>, atomic number 77) is a remarkable transition metal belonging to the platinum group. Known for its extreme durability and silvery-white appearance, it is the most corrosion-resistant metal on Earth. Iridium is so resilient that it remains completely unaffected by air, water, and even highly corrosive acids like aqua regia. Because of its incredible hardness and high melting point, it is challenging to shape but incredibly valuable in high-tech industries.</p><p>Interestingly, iridium is exceptionally rare in the Earth\'s crust but is found in higher concentrations in meteorites. The discovery of an iridium-rich layer of clay across the globe provided key evidence for the asteroid impact that wiped out the dinosaurs 66 million years ago!</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a8/Iridium-2.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 192.2173,
    'density' => 22.56,
    'melt' => 2719,
    'boil' => 4403,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Smithson Tennant',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d7',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 2.2,
    'electron_affinity' => 150.94,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 880,
      1 => 1600,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p>Iridium is a silvery-white, hard, and extremely brittle metal. It belongs to the platinum group and shares a visual resemblance to platinum, though it has a slight yellowish tint. Because it is so stiff and has a very high melting point, working with solid iridium is quite difficult. Instead of traditional metalworking, it is usually processed using powder metallurgy. Amazingly, iridium is the only metal that can maintain excellent mechanical properties in the air at scorching temperatures above 1,600 &deg;C (2,910 &deg;F).</p>',
      'Physical properties' => '<p>Iridium is famous for being incredibly dense. In fact, it is the second-densest naturally occurring element, just slightly behind osmium, with a density of 22.56 g/cm&sup3;. It melts at a staggering 2,446 &deg;C (4,435 &deg;F) and boils at 4,130 &deg;C (7,466 &deg;F). It is also highly resilient to deformation. The stiffness of iridium, measured by its modulus of elasticity, is the second highest of all metals. When cooled to near absolute zero (below 0.14 K), iridium even becomes a superconductor, meaning it can conduct electricity with zero resistance.</p>',
      'Chemical properties' => '<p>When it comes to corrosion, iridium is practically invincible. It is the most corrosion-resistant metal known to science. It does not react with standard acids, including the notoriously strong <em>aqua regia</em> (a mix of nitric and hydrochloric acids). However, under very specific conditions, it can be dissolved in concentrated hydrochloric acid if sodium perchlorate is present. At high temperatures, it will react with oxygen, halogens, or sulfur, forming compounds like iridium disulfide.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>In nature, iridium is found as a mix of two stable isotopes: <sup>191</sup>Ir (about 37.3%) and <sup>193</sup>Ir (about 62.7%). Scientists have also discovered or created dozens of radioactive isotopes. The most important of these is Iridium-192, which has a half-life of roughly 74 days. Iridium-192 is highly valued in the medical field for cancer treatments (brachytherapy) and in industrial settings to X-ray and inspect steel welds in oil and gas pipelines.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>Iridium was discovered in 1803 by the British chemist Smithson Tennant. While studying platinum ores, he found a residue that would not dissolve in acid. He realized this residue contained two new elements: osmium and iridium. Tennant named iridium after <em>Iris</em>, the Greek goddess of the rainbow, because the salts produced by the metal displayed a wide and striking array of colors. A major historical milestone for the metal occurred in 1889, when an alloy of 90% platinum and 10% iridium was used to craft the International Prototype Meter bar, which stood as the global standard for the unit of length for decades.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>On Earth, iridium is one of the rarest elements in the crust&mdash;much rarer than gold or platinum. It is generally found alongside other platinum-group metals in natural alloys or nickel and copper deposits. The largest primary reserves are located in South Africa, Russia, and Canada. However, iridium is surprisingly common in meteorites. During Earth\'s early, molten history, most of its iridium sank into the planet\'s core because of its density and tendency to bond with iron. The thin layer of iridium found in the Earth\'s crust at the Cretaceous-Paleogene boundary is what gave scientists the clue that a massive asteroid impact caused the extinction of the dinosaurs.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Today, iridium is essential in modern technology. Its extreme heat resistance makes it perfect for high-grade spark plugs in airplanes and cars, as well as for making crucibles used to grow laser and LED crystals. It is also used in OLED screens and as a catalyst in chemical production. In the push for green energy, iridium electrodes are increasingly important for systems that produce green hydrogen. In the Indian context, iridium plays a role in advanced aerospace research. Institutions like the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO) and the Council of Scientific and Industrial Research (CSIR-NIIST) have studied iridium coatings for carbon-carbon composites, developing specialized materials designed to withstand the extreme conditions of space travel.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Pt' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Pt',
    'name' => 'Platinum',
    'atomic' => 78,
    'category' => 'transition metal',
    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 78 (Pt)',
    'extract_html' => 'Platinum is a chemical element with the symbol Pt and atomic number 78. Known for its brilliant silvery-white luster, it is one of the most precious and versatile metals on Earth. As a transition metal, platinum is dense, malleable, and highly unreactive, earning it the title of a \'noble metal\'. Because it does not corrode or tarnish, even at incredibly high temperatures, it has transitioned from an ancient decorative material to a cornerstone of modern industrial and medical technology. Today, its scarcity and unique chemical properties make it highly valuable, playing a critical role in everything from clean energy technologies to life-saving medical devices and luxury jewelry.',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/68/Platinum_crystals.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 195.0849,
    'density' => 21.45,
    'melt' => 2041.4,
    'boil' => 4098,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Antonio de Ulloa',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s1 4f14 5d9',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 2.28,
    'electron_affinity' => 205.041,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 870,
      1 => 1791,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => 'Platinum is renowned for its remarkable physical and chemical properties. It is exceptionally dense—almost twice as dense as lead—and highly malleable, meaning it can be easily shaped into intricate forms or drawn into thin wires. With a staggering melting point of 1,768.3 °C (3,214.9 °F), it can withstand extreme heat that would melt most other metals. Chemically, platinum is famous for its resistance to corrosion and tarnishing. It does not react with oxygen in the air, which makes it a \'noble\' metal. Despite its unreactive nature, platinum is an extraordinary catalyst, meaning it can speed up chemical reactions without being consumed itself—a property that makes it invaluable in industrial and environmental applications.',
      'Isotopes' => 'In nature, platinum exists as a mix of six different isotopes: Platinum-190, 192, 194, 195, 196, and 198. The most abundant of these is Platinum-195, which makes up about 33.8% of all natural platinum. Platinum-195 is particularly important to scientists because its unique atomic properties make it highly useful in Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR) spectroscopy, a technique used to study molecular structures. While most platinum isotopes are completely stable, Platinum-190 is technically radioactive, though it is incredibly rare (only 0.012% of natural platinum) and decays so slowly (with a half-life of hundreds of billions of years) that its radioactivity is entirely negligible.',
      'Occurrence' => 'Platinum is incredibly rare, occurring at a concentration of only 0.005 parts per million in the Earth\'s crust. It is often found in its uncombined, native form or alloyed with other similar metals in riverbed (alluvial) deposits. Today, the vast majority of the world\'s platinum comes from the Merensky Reef in South Africa\'s Bushveld Complex, which accounts for about 75% of known reserves. Other major deposits are found in the Ural Mountains of Russia and the Sudbury Basin in Canada, where platinum was famously deposited by an ancient meteor strike!

In the context of India, platinum is primarily a strategic import rather than a domestically mined commodity. However, as demand grows for critical minerals, the Geological Survey of India (GSI) has been actively exploring potential deposits. Significant prospects have been identified in the Baula-Nuasahi complex in Odisha, the Sittampundi complex in Tamil Nadu, and recently, promising mineralized zones have been discovered in the Mahasamund district of Chhattisgarh, offering hope for future domestic production.',
      'History' => 'While platinum may seem like a modern marvel, its history stretches back thousands of years. As early as 700 BCE, Ancient Egyptian artisans used platinum in decorative objects, such as the famous Casket of Thebes. Over 2,000 years ago, indigenous civilizations in South America were already forging the metal into ceremonial jewelry.

However, when Spanish conquistadors encountered the metal in the 16th century while searching for gold, they were less than impressed. Because platinum was so difficult to melt, they viewed it as a nuisance and an \'unripe\' impurity. They dismissively named it \'platina\', meaning \'little silver\', and often threw it back into rivers. It wasn\'t until the mid-18th century, after Spanish scientist Antonio de Ulloa brought samples back to Europe in 1746, that platinum was finally recognized and studied by the scientific community as a distinct and highly valuable element.',
      'Applications' => 'Today, platinum is a \'strategic metal\' found in a surprising number of everyday and high-tech applications. Its most significant use is in the automotive industry, where it serves as the crucial catalyst in catalytic converters, transforming toxic engine exhaust fumes into safer emissions. In the chemical industry, it helps refine crude oil and produce fertilizers.

Because it is completely biocompatible and resists corrosion, platinum is essential in modern medicine. It is used to manufacture pacemakers, artificial heart valves, and dental equipment. Remarkably, platinum-based compounds like cisplatin are among the most effective chemotherapy drugs used to treat certain cancers.

Beyond industry, platinum\'s rarity and durability make it a highly sought-after status symbol in fine jewelry, notably in India where it is marketed as a \'new-age\' luxury metal. Looking to the future, platinum is set to play a major role in the green energy transition, serving as a key component in hydrogen fuel cells that generate clean electricity.',
    ),
  ),
  'Au' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Au',
    'name' => 'Gold',
    'atomic' => 79,
    'category' => 'transition metal',
    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 79 (Au)',
    'extract_html' => 'Gold is a fascinating and beautiful chemical element with the symbol Au (from the Latin word *aurum*) and the atomic number 79. It is one of the densest and most malleable metals on Earth, so soft that a single ounce can be beaten into a sheet covering over 300 square feet! Known for its brilliant, warm yellow color and its remarkable resistance to tarnishing or rusting, gold has captivated human beings for thousands of years. From ancient civilizations who worshipped it as the \'flesh of the gods\' to modern scientists who use it in cutting-edge space technology, gold is much more than just a pretty metal; it is a vital part of our history, culture, and technological advancement.',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8a/Gold_%2879_Au%29.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 196.9665695,
    'density' => 19.3,
    'melt' => 1337.33,
    'boil' => 3243,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Middle East',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s1 4f14 5d10',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 2.54,
    'electron_affinity' => 222.747,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 890.1,
      1 => 1980,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => 'Gold is what scientists call a \'noble metal,\' meaning it is highly unreactive and won\'t rust, tarnish, or corrode over time. This incredible stability is why ancient gold coins and jewelry dug out of the ground still look as shiny as the day they were made! It is an excellent conductor of both heat and electricity. Because it is incredibly malleable (easy to press into thin sheets) and ductile (easy to stretch into wires), gold is highly prized in modern electronics. If you crack open a smartphone, computer, or television, you\'ll find tiny gold components that keep the device running smoothly. Gold also reflects infrared radiation, which makes it perfect for the visors of astronaut space suits and the protective shielding on satellites.',
      'Isotopes' => 'In nature, gold only has one stable isotope: Gold-197. This means that all naturally occurring gold is made of atoms with exactly 118 neutrons. However, scientists have been able to create many other radioactive isotopes of gold in laboratories. Among these artificial versions, Gold-198 is the most famous, as it has been used in medical treatments, particularly for targeting certain types of cancer and in medical imaging.',
      'Occurrence' => 'Most of the gold found on Earth actually arrived from outer space! Billions of years ago, meteorites containing precious metals bombarded our young planet, seeding the Earth\'s crust with gold. Today, gold is found in rock veins and deep underground deposits across the world, often alongside quartz or other minerals. It can also be found as small flakes or nuggets in the beds of rivers and streams, which is what triggered the famous gold rushes of the 19th century. Despite humans mining it for millennia, gold is quite rare; it\'s estimated that all the gold ever mined in human history would fit into just three Olympic-sized swimming pools!',
      'History' => 'Gold has a rich and profound history, widely regarded as the first metal ever used by humans. In ancient Egypt, it was heavily tied to the sun god Ra and used for the pharaohs\' magnificent death masks. But perhaps nowhere is the history of gold as vibrant and culturally deeply rooted as in India.

Ancient Indian texts, including the Rig Veda, celebrate rivers as \'givers of gold.\' Over 5,000 years ago, the Indus Valley Civilization was already crafting intricate gold jewelry, a tradition of adornment and spiritual devotion that continues in India today. During times of economic shifts, gold served as a trusted currency and a symbol of power.

India is also home to the legendary Kolar Gold Fields (KGF) in Karnataka. Mining in the Kolar region dates back over 2,000 years. During the British era, KGF became one of the world\'s deepest and most productive gold mines, reaching depths of up to 3,000 meters. The mining town was so advanced and wealthy that it was the first city in India to get electricity, earning it the nickname \'Little England.\' Although the mines were closed in 2001, the rich legacy of KGF and India\'s ancient love affair with gold continues to shine brightly.',
    ),
  ),
  'Hg' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Hg',
    'name' => 'Mercury',
    'atomic' => 80,
    'category' => 'transition metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b>Mercury</b> (chemical symbol: <b>Hg</b>, atomic number: 80) is a fascinating and unique transition metal. Nicknamed "quicksilver" for its heavy, silvery appearance and liquid state at room temperature, it is the only metal with this remarkable property under standard conditions. Mercury is incredibly dense—so dense that materials like lead or iron can float on it. Throughout history, it has captivated alchemists and scientists alike, though today it is most widely known for its high toxicity. Extracted primarily from the striking red mineral called cinnabar, mercury has a complex legacy spanning ancient medicine, medieval alchemy, and modern industry.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/be/Hydrargyrum_%2880_Hg%29.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 200.5923,
    'density' => 13.534,
    'melt' => 234.321,
    'boil' => 629.88,
    'phase' => 'Liquid',
    'discovered_by' => 'unknown, before 2000 BCE',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 2,
    'electron_affinity' => -48,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 1007.1,
      1 => 1810,
      2 => 3300,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Physical properties' => '<p>Mercury stands out on the periodic table as the only metal that is liquid at room temperature. It has a high density of 13.534 grams per cubic centimeter, meaning a small vial of mercury is surprisingly heavy! It freezes at −38.83 °C and boils at 356.73 °C, giving it one of the narrowest liquid ranges of any metal. Chemically, it does not react with most acids but can form alloys, known as amalgams, with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Au" class="wiki-link" title="Gold (Au)">gold</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ag" class="wiki-link" title="Silver (Ag)">silver</a>, and other metals. Its unique electron configuration prevents its atoms from sharing electrons strongly, causing it to act almost like a noble gas and remain a liquid.</p>',
      'History and Indian context' => '<p>Mercury has a profound and ancient history, especially in India, where it has been studied for centuries under <b>Rasashastra</b>—a specialized branch of Ayurveda often translated as the "Science of Mercury." Ancient Indian alchemists considered mercury (known as <i>Rasa</i> or <i>Parada</i>) to be a powerful, transformative substance. They believed that through elaborate purification processes (<i>Samskaras</i>), mercury\'s toxicity could be removed, turning it into a potent medicine for rejuvenation and longevity. While modern pseudoscientific myths sometimes claim mercury fueled ancient flying machines (<i>Vimanas</i>), historians view these as symbolic alchemical metaphors. Beyond India, mercury was used globally by ancient Chinese, Egyptian, and Greek civilizations, often prized for its mystical properties and used as a brilliant red pigment (vermilion) derived from its ore, cinnabar.</p>',
      'Modern uses and safety' => '<p>Today, the practical uses of mercury have sharply declined due to its severe toxicity. Historically, it was essential in thermometers, barometers, blood pressure monitors, and dental amalgams. In India and worldwide, it also saw heavy industrial use, particularly in the chlor-alkali process to manufacture caustic soda. However, mercury is a potent neurotoxin that can cause serious damage to the nervous and immune systems if inhaled, ingested, or absorbed. Because of this, global agreements like the Minamata Convention aim to phase out its use. Now, electronic devices have largely replaced mercury-based instruments, though it still finds limited application in fluorescent lighting and some specialized scientific equipment.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Tl' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Tl',
    'name' => 'Thallium',
    'atomic' => 81,
    'category' => 'post-transition metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p>Thallium is a chemical element with the symbol <b>Tl</b> and atomic number 81. It is a soft, silvery-white metal that quickly tarnishes to a dull bluish-gray when exposed to air. Although it looks a lot like tin or lead, it hides a deadly secret: thallium is highly toxic and historically infamous as the "poisoner\'s poison." Discovered in 1861 by its brilliant green spectral line, it is now primarily used in high-tech electronics, specialized optics, and nuclear medicine. Because it is not found free in nature, it is typically gathered as a byproduct of refining other metal ores.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/55/Thallium_%2881_Tl%29.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 204.38,
    'density' => 11.85,
    'melt' => 577,
    'boil' => 1746,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'William Crookes',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p1',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.62,
    'electron_affinity' => 36.4,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 589.4,
      1 => 1971,
      2 => 2878,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p>Thallium is a heavy, post-transition metal that is soft enough to be cut with a standard knife at room temperature. Freshly cut thallium shines with a bright, silvery metallic luster, but it rapidly reacts with oxygen in the air, forming a heavy, grayish oxide crust that makes it look like lead. To prevent this, scientists often store it under oil.</p><p>Chemically, it behaves in unusual ways. While it shares a group on the periodic table with elements like aluminum and gallium, its chemical bonds are influenced by the "inert pair effect"—a phenomenon where its outermost electrons are reluctant to participate in reactions. This makes thallium often act more like potassium or silver in its compounds. When ingested, the human body actually mistakes thallium for potassium, which is one of the reasons it is so devastatingly toxic at a cellular level.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>Thallium has 41 known isotopes, but only two of them—Thallium-203 and Thallium-205—are stable and make up all naturally occurring thallium on Earth. The rest are radioactive and generally short-lived.</p><p>The most famous of these radioactive forms is Thallium-201. Far from being a poison, this artificially created isotope is a lifesaver in modern medicine. Because it emits low-energy X-rays and gamma rays, it is widely used in nuclear cardiology. During a cardiac stress test, doctors inject a tiny, safe amount of Thallium-201 into a patient\'s bloodstream to capture highly detailed images of blood flow to the heart muscle, helping diagnose heart disease.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>The story of thallium begins in 1861 with a brilliant flash of green light. British chemist William Crookes and French chemist Claude-Auguste Lamy independently discovered the element using a then-revolutionary technique called flame spectroscopy. When analyzing leftover sludge from a sulfuric acid factory, they noticed an unknown, bright green line in the spectrum. Crookes named the new element "thallium" after the Greek word <i>thallos</i>, meaning a green shoot or twig.</p><p>Though both men played crucial roles—Crookes identified it, and Lamy first isolated the solid metal—a bitter rivalry erupted over who deserved the credit.</p><p>In the 20th century, thallium gained a dark reputation. Because its soluble salts are colorless, odorless, and nearly tasteless, it became a popular ingredient in rat poisons and insecticides. Unfortunately, these same traits made it the weapon of choice for murderers, earning it the grim nickname of "the poisoner\'s poison." Today, its use in commercial pesticides has been largely banned worldwide.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>While its days as a household pesticide are long gone, thallium remains valuable in specialized modern industries. It is a critical component in manufacturing high-tech infrared detectors, photoelectric cells, and specialized optical glasses that require a high refractive index.</p><p>In India, thallium occasionally makes headlines for different reasons. Due to its historical notoriety, it still surfaces in rare but high-profile homicidal poisonings and domestic disputes. These criminal cases highlight a major challenge in public health: diagnosing thallium poisoning is difficult because early symptoms—such as unexplained nerve pain, gastrointestinal distress, and delayed hair loss (alopecia)—mimic other illnesses. Consequently, Indian medical literature frequently emphasizes the need for heightened clinical awareness to catch and treat exposure early.</p><p>Despite its dark side, thallium\'s contributions to advanced electronics and nuclear medicine make it an irreplaceable, if dangerous, element in the modern world.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Pb' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Pb',
    'name' => 'Lead',
    'atomic' => 82,
    'category' => 'post-transition metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pb" class="wiki-link" title="Lead (Pb)">Lead</a></b> is a heavy, soft, and easily shaped metal with a dull gray appearance when exposed to air. Sitting at atomic number 82, it has the highest atomic number of any stable element. Although it is relatively unreactive, lead is highly valued for its high density, low melting point, and resistance to corrosion. Because it is so easy to extract from its ores, humans have used it for thousands of years. In ancient India, lead was known during the Vedic period and later held a special place in Ayurvedic medicine and alchemy (Rasa Shastra). Through a rigorous purification process, raw toxic lead was transformed into <i>Naga Bhasma</i>, an ash-like preparation used in traditional remedies. Today, while we still use lead in batteries and radiation shielding, we are much more aware of its toxicity, as it can be harmful to the human nervous system and organs.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/63/Lead-2.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 207.21,
    'density' => 11.34,
    'melt' => 600.61,
    'boil' => 2022,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Middle East',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p2',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.87,
    'electron_affinity' => 34.4204,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 715.6,
      1 => 1450.5,
      2 => 3081.5,
      3 => 4083,
      4 => 6640,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Physical properties' => '<p>Lead is a heavy, dense metal that feels surprisingly soft—you can even scratch it with your fingernail! When freshly cut, it shines with a bluish-silver gleam, but it quickly turns a dull gray as it reacts with the air. It has a relatively low melting point, which made it incredibly easy for ancient metallurgists to work with and mold into various shapes. It does not conduct electricity as well as metals like copper or silver, but it is highly resistant to corrosion. Because of its density, it acts as an excellent shield against sound and radiation.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>In nature, lead exists as a mix of four stable isotopes (different versions of the same element with varying atomic weights). Interestingly, three of these natural isotopes are the final, stable endpoints of the radioactive decay chains of heavier elements like uranium and thorium. This means that over billions of years, much of the universe’s radioactive elements slowly decay and eventually turn into lead! Because of this unique property, scientists often use the ratio of different lead isotopes in rocks to determine the age of the Earth and even date ancient artifacts.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>Lead’s story spans millennia. Its principal ore, galena, usually contains silver, which sparked widespread mining in ancient times, particularly during the Roman Empire where it was used for plumbing (giving us the word "plumber" from the Latin <i>plumbum</i>). However, India also shares a rich history with lead. Texts from the Vedic period mention the metal, and it was deeply integrated into ancient Indian alchemy and Ayurvedic medicine. Through a complex, fiery purification process known as <i>Rasa Shastra</i>, Indian healers neutralized the metal’s toxicity to create <i>Naga Bhasma</i>, an herbo-metallic powder historically used to treat ailments like diabetes and digestive issues. During the Industrial Revolution, lead production soared globally, eventually leading to its use in paints and gasoline—before its severe health hazards were widely recognized.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Today, the world produces millions of tons of lead each year, heavily relying on recycling. By far, its most common use is in lead-acid batteries, which are essential for starting everyday cars and providing backup power for computer networks. Because it blocks radiation so effectively, lead is also used to line the protective aprons you wear during dental X-rays and to shield nuclear reactors. It has historical uses in construction, bullets, weights, and solders. However, due to its well-documented toxicity—causing neurological and developmental harm—many everyday uses of lead, such as in paint, pipes, and gasoline, have been strictly phased out to protect public health.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Bi' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Bi',
    'name' => 'Bismuth',
    'atomic' => 83,
    'category' => 'post-transition metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => 'Bismuth (symbol Bi, atomic number 83) is a fascinating heavy metal that stands out in the periodic table for its striking appearance and surprisingly friendly nature. Unlike its toxic heavy metal neighbors like lead and polonium, bismuth is incredibly safe for humans. When freshly produced, it has a silvery-white color, but as it reacts with the air, it develops a dazzling, iridescent, stair-step crystal structure that looks like a miniature, rainbow-colored ancient temple. Because of its safety and unique properties, bismuth has become a modern superhero in replacing lead in everything from plumbing to medicine. In fact, you might have even swallowed some—it is the active ingredient in popular pink stomach medicines!',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/Bismuth-2.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 208.980401,
    'density' => 9.78,
    'melt' => 544.7,
    'boil' => 1837,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Claude François Geoffroy',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p3',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 2.02,
    'electron_affinity' => 90.924,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 703,
      1 => 1610,
      2 => 2466,
      3 => 4370,
      4 => 5400,
      5 => 8520,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => 'Bismuth has some of the most unusual physical properties of any metal. It is incredibly brittle and has a remarkably low melting point (271.5 °C or 520.7 °F), making it easy to melt on an ordinary stovetop. But what truly makes bismuth weird and wonderful is how it behaves when it freezes. Like water turning into ice, liquid bismuth actually expands as it cools and turns into a solid—a very rare trait among metals!

It is also highly diamagnetic, meaning it actively repels magnetic fields. If you place a piece of bismuth near a strong magnet, it will actually push away. Furthermore, it is one of the worst conductors of both electricity and heat among all metals. Its natural tendency to form intricate, hopper-shaped (stair-step) crystals, combined with the thin layer of oxidation that creates brilliant hues of pink, yellow, blue, and green, makes bismuth a favorite among rock collectors and science enthusiasts.',
      'Isotopes' => 'For a long time, scientists believed that natural bismuth (specifically its only primordial isotope, bismuth-209) was completely stable—meaning it would never undergo radioactive decay. However, in 2003, researchers in France discovered that bismuth is actually very, very slightly radioactive.

Don\'t worry, though! It undergoes alpha decay with a half-life of over 20 quintillion years (2.01 x 10^19 years). To put that into perspective, that is roughly a billion times longer than the estimated age of the entire universe! Because it decays so unfathomably slowly, it is treated as completely stable and safe for all everyday and medical uses. In the medical world, other short-lived isotopes of bismuth (like bismuth-213) are artificially created and used as targeted radiation to hunt down and treat certain cancers like leukemia.',
      'Applications' => 'Bismuth may not be as famous as gold or iron, but its unique properties make it incredibly useful. Historically, it was used to treat digestive complaints, and today, it is still the star ingredient in stomach remedies (like bismuth subsalicylate) that soothe indigestion and fight stomach ulcers. In the context of Ayurveda, bismuth is sometimes debated by scholars to be "Chapala," a mineral discussed in ancient Indian texts, though its exact traditional use remains controversial. Recognizing its growing importance, the Indian government in 2023 classified bismuth as one of 30 "critical minerals" essential for national security and economic growth.

In modern industry, bismuth is primarily known as the ultimate "green" substitute for lead. Because it is non-toxic, it is replacing lead in drinking water pipes, fishing weights, bullets, and solders. Its low melting point makes it perfect for creating specialized alloys used in fire sprinkler systems and electrical fuses, which are designed to melt and trigger safety mechanisms when they get too hot. It is also highly valued in cosmetics, where bismuth compounds are added to eyeshadows, lipsticks, and nail polishes to give them a luxurious, pearlescent shimmer.',
    ),
  ),
  'Po' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Po',
    'name' => 'Polonium',
    'atomic' => 84,
    'category' => 'post-transition metal',
    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 84 (Po)',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b>Polonium</b> (symbol <b>Po</b>, atomic number <b>84</b>) is a highly radioactive and rare post-transition metal. It was discovered in 1898 by the pioneering scientists Marie and Pierre Curie, who named it after Marie\\\'s homeland, Poland. It has the distinction of being the first element discovered solely because of its radioactivity. A remarkably toxic and dangerous element, polonium is famous for its intense radioactive decay, glowing blue in the dark because it constantly excites the surrounding air. Due to its extreme radioactivity, it has a very short lifespan in the environment and must be handled with extraordinary care.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://images-of-elements.com/polonium.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 209,
    'density' => 9.196,
    'melt' => 527,
    'boil' => 1235,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Pierre Curie',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p4',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 2,
    'electron_affinity' => 136,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 812.1,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p>Polonium is a silvery-grey metal that is incredibly radioactive. One of its most well-known forms, Polonium-210, is 5,000 times more radioactive than radium! In fact, a small piece of polonium emits so many alpha particles that it glows with a bright blue light as it ionizes the surrounding air. This intense radioactivity also generates an enormous amount of heat. A one-gram chunk of Polonium-210 will quickly heat itself to over 500&deg;C (932&deg;F). Because of this intense radiation, polonium is highly toxic—billions of times more dangerous than hydrogen cyanide—and can cause severe damage to living tissues if inhaled or swallowed.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>Polonium has 42 known isotopes, all of which are radioactive—meaning there are no stable forms of this element. Its isotopes range in atomic mass from 186 to 227. In nature, it appears only in tiny amounts as part of the natural decay chains of uranium and thorium. Polonium-210 is the most abundant natural isotope and has a half-life of about 138 days, meaning half of it safely decays away into stable lead every few months. Scientists can also create it artificially in nuclear reactors by bombarding bismuth with neutrons. Two other forms, Polonium-209 and Polonium-208, last a bit longer, with half-lives of 124 years and nearly 3 years, respectively.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>When Marie and Pierre Curie were studying a uranium-rich ore called pitchblende in 1898, they noticed something strange: the ore was significantly more radioactive than the uranium and thorium inside it. This clue led them to discover polonium! Marie Curie named the newly found element after her native country, Poland (Latin: <i>Polonia</i>). At the time, Poland was divided among neighboring empires and didn\\\'t exist as an independent nation. Curie hoped that naming the element would draw global attention to Poland\\\'s struggle for independence, making polonium one of the first elements ever named to make a political statement.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Because polonium is so dangerously radioactive, many of its past uses have been discontinued. However, it still plays some fascinating roles in modern science and industry. Historically, the immense heat generated by Polonium-210\\\'s decay made it an excellent atomic heat source. It was famously used to keep the internal components of Soviet Moon rovers (Lunokhod 1 and 2) warm during freezing lunar nights in the 1970s.</p><p>Today, it is mostly used in scientific research as a powerful source of alpha radiation. In specialized industrial settings, it helps neutralize static electricity in devices that manufacture paper, wire, or sheet metal. When mixed with beryllium, it is also used as a portable tool to generate neutrons. In India, researchers at institutions like the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) study polonium carefully for environmental monitoring, particularly tracking its presence in food chains and areas with naturally high background radiation.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'At' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'At',
    'name' => 'Astatine',
    'atomic' => 85,
    'category' => 'metalloid',
    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 85 (At)',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b>Astatine</b> (symbol <b>At</b>, atomic number 85) is the rarest naturally occurring element in the Earth\'s crust. It is a highly radioactive element that only appears temporarily as heavier elements decay. Because its most stable isotope only lasts for about 8.1 hours before breaking apart, scientists have never seen a solid piece of it! If you gathered enough to see, its intense radiation would generate so much heat that it would instantly vaporize itself.</p><p>Positioned in the halogen group on the periodic table, right below iodine, astatine is predicted to look dark, lustrous, and metallic. Today, it stands at the exciting forefront of cancer research, where its powerful but short-lived radiation is being harnessed for Targeted Alpha Therapy to destroy tumor cells without harming surrounding healthy tissue.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://images-of-elements.com/astatine.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 210,
    'density' => 6.35,
    'melt' => 575,
    'boil' => 610,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Dale R. Corson',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p5',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 2.2,
    'electron_affinity' => 233,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 899.003,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p>Because astatine is so short-lived and intensely radioactive, its physical traits remain a mystery—scientists simply cannot keep a large enough sample around long enough to weigh or test it! If a visible piece were ever assembled, it would instantly vaporize itself due to the heat generated by its own radiation.</p><p>Most of what we know is predicted based on its neighbors in the periodic table. As a heavy halogen, it shares similarities with iodine but also sits on the borderline between metals and nonmetals, showing some metallic behavior. All of its isotopes are incredibly unstable, decaying rapidly into lighter elements like polonium, bismuth, or radon.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>When Dmitri Mendeleev created his famous periodic table, he left a blank space below iodine for an undiscovered element, temporarily dubbing it "eka-iodine." Finding this missing puzzle piece became a global quest, leading to several false alarms.</p><p>For example, in 1937, Indian chemist Dr. Rajendralal De claimed to have discovered the missing element in monazite sand and named it "dakin." However, his claim was dismissed by the broader scientific community because the sample he described would have been impossibly and dangerously radioactive to handle, and its properties did not match the actual element.</p><p>The true breakthrough finally came in 1940 at the University of California, Berkeley. Scientists Dale R. Corson, Kenneth Ross MacKenzie, and Emilio Segrè synthesized the element by bombarding bismuth with alpha particles in a cyclotron. They appropriately named it "astatine," drawn from the Ancient Greek word <i>ástatos</i>, which perfectly translates to "unstable."</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>There are 41 known isotopes of astatine, and every single one of them is fleeting and radioactive. There are absolutely no stable forms of astatine in existence. The most "stable" isotope, astatine-210, boasts a short half-life of just 8.1 hours. On the other end of the spectrum, astatine-213 disappears almost instantaneously, with a half-life of merely 125 nanoseconds!</p><p>The incredible, focused energy released when these isotopes decay is exactly what makes astatine so valuable for modern medicine. <b>Astatine-211</b> (which has a half-life of 7.2 hours) is currently a rising star in experimental Targeted Alpha Therapy (TAT) for cancers like prostate and thyroid cancer. By attaching astatine-211 to specialized molecules that seek out cancer cells, doctors can deliver potent, localized radiation directly to tumors—destroying the cancer from the inside out while sparing the surrounding healthy tissue.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Rn' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Rn',
    'name' => 'Radon',
    'atomic' => 86,
    'category' => 'noble gas',
    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 86 (Rn)',
    'extract_html' => '<p><strong>Radon (Rn)</strong> is a fascinating, invisible, and odorless gas. It belongs to a group known as the "noble gases," meaning it typically prefers to be left alone and doesn\'t easily react with other elements. What makes radon truly unique, however, is that it is radioactive. It\'s naturally created when heavier elements like uranium and radium slowly break down in the earth\'s crust.</p><p>In the context of India, radon holds significant geographic and scientific interest. The coastal areas of Kerala, particularly places like Panmana, are known as High Background Radiation Areas (HBRAs) due to the presence of radioactive minerals in the soil, which naturally release more radon. Modern Indian scientists, particularly at the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) in Mumbai, have dedicated extensive research to monitoring indoor radon levels across the country to ensure public health and safety. Furthermore, researchers in the Himalayan region have studied fluctuations in radon gas emissions from the earth as potential early warning signs for earthquakes.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://images-of-elements.com/radon.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 222,
    'density' => 9.73,
    'melt' => 202,
    'boil' => 211.5,
    'phase' => 'Gas',
    'discovered_by' => 'Friedrich Ernst Dorn',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p6',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 2.2,
    'electron_affinity' => -68,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 1037,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => 'Radon is a heavy, invisible gas that you cannot see, smell, or taste. Because it is much heavier than the air we breathe, it tends to settle in low-lying areas like basements and ground floors of buildings. It is highly radioactive, meaning it is constantly breaking down and releasing energy. As it breaks down, it turns into other solid radioactive elements known as \'radon daughters\' or \'radon progeny\', which can attach to dust particles in the air.',
      'Physical properties' => 'At room temperature, radon is a colorless gas. However, when it is cooled down to a solid state, it glows with a beautiful, bright yellow color! If it is cooled even further, that glow changes to an orange-red. It is one of the heaviest gases known in nature—about nine times heavier than the air we breathe. It also dissolves in water, which is why it can sometimes be found in groundwater or well water.',
      'Chemical properties' => 'As a \'noble gas\', radon is incredibly stubborn and rarely reacts with other chemicals. For a long time, scientists believed it was completely unreactive. However, in modern laboratories, chemists have been able to force it to combine with a few other highly reactive elements, like fluorine, to create new compounds. Still, in everyday nature, you will almost always find radon completely by itself as a single, independent atom.',
      'Isotopes' => 'An \'isotope\' is a version of an element that has a different weight. Radon has many isotopes, but they are all radioactive and unstable. The most common and longest-lasting one is called Radon-222. Even then, its \'half-life\' (the time it takes for half of it to break down) is only about 3.8 days. This means radon doesn\'t stick around for very long; it quickly transforms into other elements like polonium and lead.',
      'Occurrence' => 'Radon is born from the natural decay of uranium and radium, which are found in rocks and soils all over the world. It slowly seeps out of the ground and into the air. In the open outdoors, it disperses quickly and is harmless. However, it can become trapped inside buildings, especially in poorly ventilated basements. In India, the geographic distribution of radon is very diverse. Areas with uranium-rich soils, such as parts of Punjab, Rajasthan, and the coastal monazite sands of Kerala, show naturally higher levels of radon. Institutions like Panjab University and BARC have conducted extensive surveys to map these occurrences and help guide safe building practices.',
      'Applications' => 'Historically, radon was sometimes used in hospitals to treat certain types of cancer through radiation therapy, though safer alternatives are mostly used today. Today, its most fascinating application is in earth sciences. Because radon is released from rocks, scientists use it to trace how groundwater flows. In India, measuring radon gas escaping from the ground along the Himalayan fault lines is an active area of research for predicting earthquakes! When tectonic plates shift and build up pressure, the rocks crack and release trapped radon, providing a potential clue that an earthquake might be coming.',
    ),
  ),
  'Fr' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Fr',
    'name' => 'Francium',
    'atomic' => 87,
    'category' => 'alkali metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><strong>Francium</strong> is an incredibly rare, highly radioactive alkali metal with the symbol <strong>Fr</strong> and atomic number <strong>87</strong>. It holds the fascinating distinction of being the second-rarest naturally occurring element in the Earth\'s crust, right after astatine. In fact, scientists estimate that there are only about 20 to 30 grams of francium present in the entire Earth\'s crust at any given time! Due to its extreme radioactivity and incredibly short half-life—its most stable isotope lasts for just 22 minutes—francium is exceedingly difficult to study. If you could somehow gather enough of it to see, it would likely appear as a shiny, silvery-white metal. However, as an alkali metal, it is highly reactive and would vaporize itself almost instantly while reacting explosively with moisture in the air.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://images-of-elements.com/francium.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 223,
    'density' => 1.87,
    'melt' => 300,
    'boil' => 950,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Marguerite Perey',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p6 7s1',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 0.79,
    'electron_affinity' => 46.89,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 380,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p>Francium is the heaviest known alkali metal. Like other members of its group (such as sodium and potassium), it has a single valence electron, making it extremely reactive. While it has never been collected in a large enough quantity to be seen by the naked eye or weighed on a scale, scientists predict it would be a silvery-white solid at room temperature. Its melting point is estimated to be quite low, around 27 °C (81 °F). Chemically, it reacts violently with water and air, much like its lighter cousins, though observing this in a lab is practically impossible because francium decays into other elements far too quickly. Its extreme instability is due to the sheer size of its atomic nucleus, which simply cannot hold itself together.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>Francium is notoriously unstable. It has 34 known isotopes, ranging in atomic mass from 199 to 232, and every single one of them is highly radioactive. The most stable isotope is <strong>Francium-223</strong>, but even this form has a half-life of just 21.8 minutes. This means that if you had a lump of Francium-223, half of it would decay into radium or astatine in less than 22 minutes! Most other isotopes of francium have half-lives measured in mere seconds or fractions of a second. This rapid decay makes isolating the element practically impossible and presents a major challenge for scientists attempting to study its atomic properties.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Because francium is so incredibly rare and vanishes so quickly due to radioactive decay, it has absolutely no commercial, industrial, or medical applications. You will not find it in any everyday products or technologies. However, it holds significant value in the realm of specialized scientific research. Physicists and chemists synthesize tiny amounts of francium in laboratories using particle accelerators to study atomic structure, quantum mechanics, and the weak nuclear force. For instance, advanced spectroscopy experiments on francium help test theories of quantum electrodynamics (QED) and atomic parity violation. In India, like in the rest of the world, francium is mostly a topic of deep academic interest, taught in chemistry curricula (such as in competitive exams like JEE and NEET) as a prime theoretical example of periodic trends and intense radioactivity.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>The discovery of francium is a story of persistence and national pride. For decades, chemists knew there was a missing alkali metal in the periodic table, right below cesium. Russian chemist Dmitri Mendeleev, the creator of the periodic table, had predicted its existence and temporarily named it \'eka-caesium\'. Many scientists claimed to have discovered it in the early 20th century, but all were proven wrong. It wasn\'t until <strong>1939</strong> that French physicist <strong>Marguerite Perey</strong> at the Curie Institute in Paris finally discovered the elusive element. She found it while purifying a sample of actinium-227, which decays into francium. Perey proudly named the new element \'francium\' in honor of her home country, France. Francium also holds a unique place in history: it was the very last chemical element to be discovered first in nature, rather than being artificially synthesized in a lab.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Finding francium in nature is like searching for a needle in a planetary haystack. It occurs naturally only in trace amounts in uranium and thorium ores, specifically as a brief intermediate step in the radioactive decay of actinium-227. At any given moment, the entire crust of the Earth contains no more than 20 to 30 grams (about an ounce) of francium! Because it decays so rapidly, any naturally occurring francium is constantly disappearing, only to be replaced by newly formed francium from actinium decay. To study it today, scientists don\'t bother mining for it; instead, they artificially create it in laboratories by bombarding radium or gold atoms with other particles in high-energy accelerators.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Ra' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Ra',
    'name' => 'Radium',
    'atomic' => 88,
    'category' => 'alkaline earth metal',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b>Radium</b> is a highly radioactive, silvery-white metal that glows in the dark with a faint blue light. Discovered by Marie and Pierre Curie in 1898, this fascinating element was once hailed as a miracle cure and used in everyday products like toothpaste and glowing watches, before its dangerous radiation was fully understood. In India, the arrival of radium in the 1930s at centers like the Radium Institute in Patna and the Tata Memorial Hospital in Mumbai revolutionized early cancer treatment and laid the foundation for the nation\'s modern radiological and nuclear safety standards. Though rarely used today due to its extreme radioactivity, radium remains a legendary element that changed our understanding of science and medicine.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bb/Radium226.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 226,
    'density' => 5.5,
    'melt' => 1233,
    'boil' => 2010,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Pierre Curie',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p6 7s2',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 0.9,
    'electron_affinity' => 9.6485,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 509.3,
      1 => 979,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Isotopes' => '<p>All versions of radium—called isotopes—are radioactive, meaning they are unstable and break apart by releasing radiation. There are no stable forms of radium. The most common and longest-lived isotope is Radium-226, which has a \'half-life\' of 1,600 years. This means it takes 1,600 years for half of a sample of Radium-226 to decay into another element (specifically, the radioactive gas called radon).</p><p>Because it takes so long to break down, Radium-226 is practically the only form of radium found in nature, making up nearly 100% of it. Other isotopes of radium exist, but they break down so quickly—some in a matter of days or even fractions of a second—that they vanish almost as soon as they are created in the natural decay chain of uranium and thorium. Interestingly, scientific experiments, including some using advanced techniques, have revealed that certain radium isotopes like Radium-224 have unique, asymmetrical \'pear-like\' shapes in their nucleus.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Radium does not exist in nature as a stable element; it is highly radioactive and constantly breaking down into other elements. Because of its short lifespan compared to the age of the Earth, any original radium that existed when the planet was formed is long gone.</p><p>However, radium is constantly being reborn. It is a natural byproduct of the decay of heavier radioactive elements like uranium and thorium. Wherever these heavy elements are found, tiny traces of radium will be there too.</p><h3>Indian Geography and Radium</h3><p>In India, you won\'t find mines dedicated to digging up radium. Instead, its presence is tied to the country\'s rich deposits of thorium and uranium. For instance, the famous monazite sands along the coasts of Kerala and Tamil Nadu are packed with thorium, which slowly decays to produce minuscule amounts of radium over time. Similarly, uranium-rich regions in Jharkhand and Rajasthan also harbor natural traces of this glowing element. Scientists continually monitor these areas to study natural background radiation, ensuring the safety of the local environment.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>Radium was discovered by the famous husband-and-wife team, Marie and Pierre Curie, on December 21, 1898. While studying a radioactive mineral called pitchblende, they managed to separate a tiny amount of this new, highly radioactive element. It was named \'radium\' from the Latin word for \'ray\' because of the intense energy it emitted.</p><p>In the early 20th century, radium was considered a scientific marvel. Because it glowed in the dark and was thought to have health benefits, people added it to everyday items like drinking water, toothpaste, cosmetics, and the dials of clocks and watches. Unfortunately, many of the workers who painted these glowing dials, known as the \'Radium Girls\', suffered severe radiation poisoning, revealing the dark and deadly side of this glowing metal.</p><h3>Radium in India</h3><p>India was remarkably quick to adopt this new wonder-element for medical science. Around 1930, the first stocks of radium were brought to the Radium Institute in Patna. Soon, pioneers of Indian radiotherapy established radium treatment centers across the country. The Tata Memorial Hospital in Mumbai even built a special \'Radon plant\' to use the radioactive gas produced by radium for cancer therapy. These early efforts were crucial; they not only helped countless cancer patients but also forced India to develop strict radiation safety rules under the guidance of institutions like the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC). Over time, safer artificial radioactive materials replaced radium, but its legacy as the starting point of India\'s journey into nuclear medicine and radiation safety remains profoundly important.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Ac' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Ac',
    'name' => 'Actinium',
    'atomic' => 89,
    'category' => 'actinide',
    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 89 (Ac)',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b>Actinium</b> (symbol <b>Ac</b>, atomic number 89) is a highly radioactive and incredibly rare metal that gives its name to the actinide series of the periodic table. Born from the decay of heavier elements like uranium and thorium, actinium is so intensely radioactive that it literally glows with an eerie pale blue light in the dark! Its name is derived from the ancient Greek word <i>aktis</i>, meaning "beam" or "ray," which perfectly captures its radiant nature. It is about 150 times more radioactive than radium. Because it is so scarce in nature, scientists today produce it synthetically in nuclear reactors. While it has no everyday consumer uses, actinium is stepping into the spotlight as a powerful tool in modern medicine, offering promising new ways to target and destroy cancer cells.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/27/Actinium_sample_%2831481701837%29.png',
    'atomic_mass' => 227,
    'density' => 10,
    'melt' => 1500,
    'boil' => 3500,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Friedrich Oskar Giesel',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p6 7s2 6d1',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.1,
    'electron_affinity' => 33.77,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 499,
      1 => 1170,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'History' => '<p>The story of <b>Actinium</b> begins in 1899 with French chemist André-Louis Debierne. While working with pitchblende (a uranium-rich ore) left over after Marie and Pierre Curie had extracted radium, Debierne noticed a new, mysterious radioactive substance. Independent of Debierne, German chemist Friedrich Oskar Giesel discovered the same element in 1902. Giesel initially named it "emanium" because it emanated such strong radioactivity, but Debierne\'s earlier discovery was eventually recognized, and the name "actinium" became official. Historically, actinium was incredibly difficult to study because its chemical behavior is almost identical to the rare-earth element lanthanum, making the two notoriously hard to separate. It wasn\'t until 1955 that completely pure actinium metal was finally isolated.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>Actinium has absolutely no stable isotopes; every natural or synthetic form of it is radioactive. The most common and longest-lived isotope found in nature is <b>Actinium-227</b>, which has a half-life of about 21.77 years. It decays over time, primarily emitting beta particles as it transforms into lighter elements. However, the true star of modern research is <b>Actinium-225</b>. With a much shorter half-life of just 10 days, this isotope decays by rapidly firing off powerful alpha particles. In the scientific world, creating and studying these isotopes requires specialized nuclear reactors and particle accelerators, as natural actinium is far too rare to mine directly.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Because of its extreme rarity, high cost, and dangerous radioactivity, <b>actinium</b> has no commercial or industrial uses. However, it is a superstar in the field of <b>Targeted Alpha Therapy (TAT)</b> for cancer treatment. Scientists attach the isotope Actinium-225 to special targeting molecules (like antibodies) that seek out specific cancer cells. Once attached to the tumor, the actinium acts like a microscopic smart-bomb, blasting the cancer cells with high-energy alpha particles while leaving surrounding healthy tissue largely unharmed! Beyond medicine, Actinium-227 is sometimes combined with beryllium to create reliable neutron sources for scientific experiments and oil well logging. In India, institutions under the Department of Atomic Energy (DAE) are actively researching such cutting-edge radiopharmaceuticals, aligning with global efforts to use advanced nuclear technology for life-saving medical treatments and deep radiochemical research.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Th' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Th',
    'name' => 'Thorium',
    'atomic' => 90,
    'category' => 'actinide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><strong>Thorium (Th)</strong>, atomic number 90, is a weakly radioactive, silvery-white metal that holds incredible promise for the future of clean energy. When exposed to air, it slowly tarnishes to an olive-grey color as it forms thorium dioxide. Despite being a heavy actinide metal, it is surprisingly soft and malleable, and it boasts a very high melting point. If ground into a fine powder, it is reactive enough to ignite spontaneously in the air!</p>

<p>Every single isotope of thorium is technically unstable (radioactive), but its most common form, Thorium-232, decays so incredibly slowly that its half-life is 14 billion years—roughly the age of the universe itself. Because it decays so slowly, thorium is still found abundantly in nature today, making it one of the rare "primordial" radioactive elements alongside uranium. In fact, thorium is about three times more abundant in the Earth\'s crust than uranium.</p>

<p>Historically, thorium was discovered in 1828 by the Swedish chemist Jöns Jacob Berzelius, who named it after Thor, the fierce Norse god of thunder. For a long time, it was used in everyday items like gas lantern mantles, camera lenses, and heat-resistant ceramics. However, as people learned more about radioactivity in the 20th century, many of these everyday uses were phased out. Today, its most exciting application lies in the realm of nuclear power. Thorium is famously abundant in India, particularly in the rich monazite sands of the Kerala coastline, setting the stage for India\'s unique and ambitious thorium-based nuclear energy program.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Thorium-1.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 232.03774,
    'density' => 11.724,
    'melt' => 2023,
    'boil' => 5061,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Jöns Jakob Berzelius',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p6 7s2 6d2',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.3,
    'electron_affinity' => 112.72,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 587,
      1 => 1110,
      2 => 1930,
      3 => 2780,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Isotopes' => '<p>While there are several isotopes of thorium, none of them are perfectly stable. However, <strong>Thorium-232</strong> makes up nearly all naturally occurring thorium, and it is a true survivor. With a half-life of 14 billion years, it has been around since the formation of the Earth! It decays by emitting alpha particles, eventually turning into a stable isotope of lead (Lead-208) after a long chain of transformations.</p>

<p>What makes Thorium-232 incredibly special is that it is "fertile." While it cannot sustain a nuclear chain reaction on its own, absorbing a neutron transforms it into Uranium-233, an excellent "fissile" nuclear fuel. This remarkable property is the foundation of India\'s long-term energy strategy. By designing special reactors—like the Prototype Fast Breeder Reactor (PFBR)—scientists can breed fuel from thorium, unlocking a virtually inexhaustible energy source.</p>

<p>Other trace isotopes, like Thorium-230, are found in deep ocean waters and are used by scientists to study ancient oceans and date geological events.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Thorium is surprisingly plentiful, being three to four times more abundant in the Earth\'s crust than uranium. It is primarily extracted as a by-product from <strong>monazite sands</strong>, a reddish-brown phosphate mineral that is also rich in rare-earth elements.</p>

<p>India is blessed with some of the largest thorium reserves on the planet, accounting for roughly 25% of the global supply! These massive deposits are largely found along the beautiful coastal regions of <strong>Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Odisha, and Andhra Pradesh</strong>. Over millions of years, the weathering of ancient granites and gneisses in the Western Ghats washed these heavy, thorium-rich minerals down rushing rivers, eventually depositing them along the sun-drenched beaches of the Indian peninsula.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>The story of thorium began in 1828 when a Norwegian vicar and amateur mineralogist named Morten Thrane Esmark discovered a strange black mineral on the island of Løvøya. He sent it to the renowned Swedish chemist Jöns Jacob Berzelius, who identified a new element and named it after <strong>Thor</strong>, the Norse god of thunder and war.</p>

<p>For over a century, thorium\'s main claim to fame was its use in the glowing mantles of gas lamps and in specialized glass for high-end optics. But its true destiny was uncovered in the atomic age. Recognizing that India had vast reserves of thorium but very little uranium, the visionary Indian physicist <strong>Dr. Homi J. Bhabha</strong> architected India\'s ambitious three-stage nuclear power program in the 1950s. Bhabha\'s genius was to design a series of reactors that would eventually use the country\'s domestic thorium as their main fuel, bypassing the need for imported uranium and ensuring India\'s long-term energy independence.</p>

<p>Today, Indian scientists at institutions like the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) and the Indira Gandhi Centre for Atomic Research (IGCAR) continue to build on this legacy. With modern advancements in fast breeder reactors and advanced heavy water reactors, Bhabha\'s dream of a thorium-powered nation is steadily becoming a reality.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Pa' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Pa',
    'name' => 'Protactinium',
    'atomic' => 91,
    'category' => 'actinide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><strong>Protactinium</strong> (symbol <strong>Pa</strong>, atomic number <strong>91</strong>) is a dense, silvery-gray actinide metal and one of the rarest and most expensive naturally occurring elements on Earth. Highly radioactive and toxic, it readily reacts with oxygen, water vapor, and inorganic acids. It most commonly forms compounds in a +5 oxidation state, though +4, +3, and +2 states are also possible. It is incredibly scarce, typically found in concentrations of just a few parts per trillion in the Earth\'s crust. Because of its extreme rarity and dangerous radioactivity, protactinium has no commercial uses outside of specialized scientific research. Notably, it is the only highly radioactive element with only one naturally occurring isotope that isn\'t monoisotopic—arising almost exclusively from the decay of Uranium-235.</p>

<p><strong>Key Scientific Facts:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Appearance:</strong> Bright, silvery metallic luster</li>
  <li><strong>Phase at Room Temperature:</strong> Solid (Body-centered tetragonal crystal structure)</li>
  <li><strong>Melting Point:</strong> 1568 °C (2854 °F)</li>
  <li><strong>Boiling Point:</strong> 4027 °C (7280 °F)</li>
  <li><strong>Density:</strong> 15.43 g/cm³</li>
  <li><strong>Standard Atomic Weight:</strong> 231.03588</li>
  <li><strong>Electron Configuration:</strong> [Rn] 5f² 6d¹ 7s²</li>
</ul>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Protactinium-233.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 231.035882,
    'density' => 15.37,
    'melt' => 1841,
    'boil' => 4300,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'William Crookes',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p6 7s2 5f2 6d1',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.5,
    'electron_affinity' => 53.03,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 568,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'History' => '<p>In 1871, the pioneering chemist Dmitri Mendeleev predicted the existence of an element between thorium and uranium, noting a gap in his famous periodic table. However, it wasn\'t until 1913 that Kazimierz Fajans and Oswald Helmuth Göhring first identified a short-lived isotope of the element, initially naming it "brevium" because of its brief half-life of just 1.17 minutes. Later, in 1917 and 1918, two separate groups of scientists—Otto Hahn and Lise Meitner in Germany, and Frederick Soddy and John Cranston in Great Britain—independently discovered a much more stable isotope, Protactinium-231. The name was officially changed to "protactinium", meaning "parent of actinium", because it eventually decays into the element actinium. In 1934, Aristid von Grosse became the first to successfully isolate pure protactinium.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>Scientists have discovered thirty radioisotopes of protactinium, ranging from Pa-210 to Pa-239. The most stable isotope is Protactinium-231 (Pa-231), which has a remarkable half-life of 32,650 years. Another important isotope is Pa-233, which has a half-life of about 27 days, and Pa-230, with a half-life of 17.4 days. All the other known isotopes have half-lives of less than two days, and the vast majority decay in under two minutes. Protactinium also has two nuclear isomers, Pa-217m and Pa-234m, with Pa-234m having a short half-life of just 1.17 minutes.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Protactinium is extremely rare and expensive, making it one of the most elusive natural elements. It naturally occurs in just two isotopes: Pa-231 and Pa-234, though nearly all natural protactinium exists as Pa-231. This isotope is an alpha emitter formed by the radioactive decay of Uranium-235. To put its rarity into perspective, a typical ton of pitchblende (a uranium-rich ore) contains only about 0.1 milligrams of protactinium. Today, rather than mining it from ores, most of the world\'s very limited supply of protactinium is carefully extracted from the spent nuclear fuel of power plants for advanced scientific research.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Due to its extreme scarcity, high radioactivity, and significant toxicity, protactinium has no commercial, industrial, or everyday uses. Its applications are strictly confined to the realm of scientific research.</p><p><strong>Geochemical Tracing:</strong> Scientists use Protactinium-231 alongside Thorium-230 as a powerful "clock" (known as Pa/Th dating) to measure historical sedimentation rates in oceans. This helps researchers understand ancient climate changes and ocean current dynamics.</p><p><strong>Nuclear Energy and India\'s Context:</strong> Protactinium plays a starring, hidden role in advanced nuclear physics—particularly in thorium-based nuclear reactors. This is deeply relevant to India\'s Three-Stage Nuclear Power Programme, which aims to harness the country\'s vast thorium reserves. When Thorium-232 absorbs a neutron, it temporarily becomes Protactinium-233 before decaying into Uranium-233, the actual fissile fuel. Understanding and managing this "bridge" isotope is critical for the safety and efficiency of next-generation thorium reactors, making protactinium a key stepping stone in India\'s quest for long-term energy independence.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'U' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'U',
    'name' => 'Uranium',
    'atomic' => 92,
    'category' => 'actinide',
    'description' => 'Chemical element with atomic number 92 (U)',
    'extract_html' => '<p><strong>Uranium</strong> is a silvery-gray, radioactive metal that has forever changed the course of human history. With an atomic number of 92 (symbol <strong>U</strong>), it is one of the heaviest elements found naturally on Earth. While it might look like just another shiny metal, its atomic structure holds an incredible secret: the power of nuclear fission. By splitting its atoms, we can unlock vast amounts of energy, making uranium the undisputed heavyweight champion of modern electricity generation.</p><p>Beyond its scientific intrigue, uranium plays a unique role in global energy and geopolitics. It doesn\'t just fuel nuclear power plants—it also serves as the cornerstone for strategic defense programs and advanced industrial applications. In India, uranium takes on special significance as the starting point for a highly ambitious, self-reliant nuclear program spearheaded by legendary physicist Dr. Homi J. Bhabha.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b2/Ames_Process_uranium_biscuit.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 238.028913,
    'density' => 19.1,
    'melt' => 1405.3,
    'boil' => 4404,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Martin Heinrich Klaproth',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p6 7s2 5f3 6d1',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.38,
    'electron_affinity' => 50.94,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 597.6,
      1 => 1420,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p>In its pure form, uranium is a dense, silvery-white metal that is approximately 70% denser than lead. It has a high melting point of 1,132 °C (2,070 °F) and a boiling point of 4,131 °C (7,468 °F). If you were to leave a piece of uranium exposed to the air, it would slowly tarnish, developing a dark oxide coating.</p><p>One of the most defining characteristics of uranium is its radioactivity. It is constantly, albeit slowly, decaying and releasing particles. Because of its massive atomic nucleus, uranium is weakly radioactive and primarily emits alpha particles. Interestingly, uranium is also paramagnetic (weakly attracted to magnets) and happens to be a surprisingly good conductor of heat and electricity.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Uranium is most famous for its role in <strong>nuclear energy</strong>. Within the heart of a nuclear reactor, the uranium-235 isotope is bombarded with neutrons, causing it to split (fission) and release an enormous amount of heat. This heat boils water into steam, which spins massive turbines to generate clean, carbon-free electricity.</p><p>In the <strong>military and defense</strong> sectors, enriched uranium is used to power nuclear submarines, allowing them to stay submerged for years without refueling, and forms the core of nuclear weapons.</p><p>Additionally, <strong>depleted uranium</strong>—the leftover material after uranium is enriched—is incredibly dense. This makes it highly useful in civilian applications such as radiation shielding in medical therapy machines, as well as counterweights in aircraft wings and industrial machinery.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>The story of uranium begins in 1789, when German chemist Martin Heinrich Klaproth discovered it in a mineral called pitchblende. He named the new element after the recently discovered planet Uranus. For over a century, uranium was mostly used as a colorant, adding brilliant yellow-green hues to ceramic glazes and glass.</p><p>Everything changed in 1896 when French physicist Henri Becquerel accidentally discovered radioactivity while working with uranium salts. The world suddenly realized that atoms held hidden, immense energy. This led to the dawn of the atomic age in the mid-20th century, culminating in the first nuclear reactors and, tragically, the atomic bombs of World War II.</p><p>In the <strong>Indian context</strong>, the history of uranium is deeply tied to the visionary scientist <strong>Dr. Homi J. Bhabha</strong>. Known as the father of India\'s nuclear program, Dr. Bhabha founded the Atomic Energy Establishment, Trombay in 1954 (later renamed the <strong>Bhabha Atomic Research Centre, or BARC</strong>, in his honor). Bhabha devised a unique three-stage nuclear power program. Since India had limited high-grade uranium but vast thorium reserves, his brilliant strategy was to use natural uranium in the first stage (Pressurized Heavy Water Reactors) to produce power and plutonium. This plutonium would then fuel the second stage, eventually leading to a third stage powered entirely by India\'s abundant thorium—a vision that continues to drive the country\'s energy independence today.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Uranium is surprisingly common. It is found in trace amounts everywhere—in rocks, soil, and even the oceans. In fact, it is about as abundant in the Earth\'s crust as tin or tungsten. However, extracting it requires finding concentrated deposits in specific mineral formations.</p><p>Globally, countries like Kazakhstan, Canada, and Australia are the heavyweights in uranium mining. In India, domestic uranium reserves are relatively modest but strategically vital. The Uranium Corporation of India Limited (UCIL) manages mining operations primarily in the Singhbhum belt of Jharkhand, as well as in regions like Tummalapalli in Andhra Pradesh, which holds one of the largest low-grade uranium reserves in the world.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>Uranium exists in nature primarily as three distinct isotopes: <strong>Uranium-238</strong> (which makes up about 99.27% of natural uranium), <strong>Uranium-235</strong> (about 0.72%), and a tiny trace of <strong>Uranium-234</strong> (0.005%).</p><p>The star of the show is <strong>U-235</strong>. It is the only naturally occurring isotope that is "fissile," meaning it can easily sustain a nuclear chain reaction. Because natural uranium contains so little U-235, it often has to be "enriched"—a complex process that increases the concentration of U-235 so it can be used efficiently in power plants and reactors.</p><p>Meanwhile, <strong>U-238</strong> is considered "fertile." While it doesn\'t split easily on its own, it can absorb a neutron and eventually transform into Plutonium-239, another powerful fissile material. This transformation is the exact science underlying the second stage of India\'s nuclear program, utilizing Fast Breeder Reactors to produce more fuel than they consume.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Np' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Np',
    'name' => 'Neptunium',
    'atomic' => 93,
    'category' => 'actinide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b>Neptunium</b> is a fascinating synthetic, radioactive metal with the symbol <b>Np</b> and atomic number 93. Named after the planet Neptune—following uranium, which was named after Uranus—neptunium was the very first transuranic element to be artificially created. It sits in the actinide series of the periodic table, possessing a silvery metallic appearance when freshly prepared. Although it can be found in vanishingly small trace amounts in natural uranium ores, nearly all of the world\'s neptunium is produced in nuclear reactors as a byproduct of uranium irradiation.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e5/Neptunium2.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 237,
    'density' => 20.45,
    'melt' => 912,
    'boil' => 4447,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Edwin McMillan',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p6 7s2 5f4 6d1',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.36,
    'electron_affinity' => 45.85,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 604.5,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => 'Neptunium is a hard, silvery actinide metal that tarnishes slowly in air. It boasts a notably high density of 20.45 g/cm³ and melts at a relatively low 639 °C (1182 °F), but it boils at a much higher extrapolated temperature of 4174 °C (7545 °F). One of its most intriguing physical properties is its ability to exist in three different crystal structures depending on the temperature (orthorhombic at room temperature, tetragonal, and body-centered cubic at higher temperatures). Chemically, it\'s quite active, capable of exhibiting oxidation states ranging from +2 to +7, with +5 being the most common in solutions. Because it is radioactive, handling it requires specialized safety equipment.',
      'Isotopes' => 'All isotopes of neptunium are radioactive, meaning none are completely stable. The most significant and longest-lived isotope is neptunium-237, which has a staggering half-life of 2.14 million years. It decays mostly by emitting alpha particles. Another important isotope is neptunium-239, which has a much shorter half-life of just 2.4 days. This short-lived isotope was the very first form of neptunium ever synthesized and is an important intermediate step in the creation of plutonium-239 in nuclear reactors.',
      'Occurrence' => 'For a long time, neptunium was considered an entirely artificial element. However, trace amounts of neptunium-237 and neptunium-239 do occur naturally in uranium-bearing ores. These tiny traces are created by natural nuclear reactions when uranium atoms capture stray neutrons. Despite this, the natural occurrence is so microscopic that practically all neptunium used in scientific research today is synthetically produced as a byproduct in nuclear power plants, extracted from spent nuclear fuel.',
      'History' => 'The story of neptunium is a landmark in nuclear science. It was discovered in 1940 by scientists Edwin McMillan and Philip H. Abelson at the Berkeley Radiation Laboratory in California. By bombarding uranium-238 with slow neutrons using a cyclotron, they successfully created neptunium-239, marking the discovery of the first element heavier than uranium (a transuranic element). Following the planetary naming theme started by uranium (Uranus), they named the new element after the next planet in the solar system, Neptune. McMillan was later awarded the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1951 for this groundbreaking discovery.',
      'Applications' => 'While neptunium doesn\'t have many everyday commercial uses, it plays a vital strategic and scientific role. Its primary application is serving as the raw material to produce plutonium-238, an essential power source used in Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generators (RTGs). These RTGs act as long-lasting nuclear batteries for deep-space missions, powering iconic spacecraft like the Voyager probes and the Curiosity rover. In specialized industrial and research settings, neptunium is also used in instruments designed to detect high-energy neutrons.<br><br><b>The Indian Context:</b> In India, neptunium has a special significance within the nation\'s ambitious Three-Stage Nuclear Power Programme, originally envisioned by Dr. Homi J. Bhabha. In the first stage, Indian Pressurized Heavy Water Reactors (PHWRs) use natural uranium as fuel. The neutron irradiation in these reactors naturally produces neptunium and plutonium as byproducts. Managing and processing these transuranic elements is a critical capability developed at institutions like the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC). The mastery over these processes is essential as India transitions toward Fast Breeder Reactors and ultimately a closed nuclear fuel cycle, optimizing energy output and carefully managing nuclear waste.',
    ),
  ),
  'Pu' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Pu',
    'name' => 'Plutonium',
    'atomic' => 94,
    'category' => 'actinide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p>Plutonium (symbol Pu, atomic number 94) is a fascinating, silvery-gray metal that belongs to a group of elements called actinides. When exposed to air, it quickly loses its shine and turns into a dull gray or sometimes greenish-yellow color. It is a radioactive, synthetic (human-made) element, though incredibly tiny trace amounts can be found in nature. Plutonium is famous for being a powerful source of nuclear energy. While it is highly toxic and must be handled with extreme care, it has changed the course of world history and continues to play a vital role in modern science, including space exploration and energy production. In India, plutonium is the cornerstone of the country\'s ambitious three-stage nuclear power program, envisioned by Dr. Homi Bhabha to achieve long-term energy independence.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0f/Plutonium_ring.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 244,
    'density' => 19.816,
    'melt' => 912.5,
    'boil' => 3505,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Glenn T. Seaborg',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p6 7s2 5f6',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.28,
    'electron_affinity' => -48.33,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 584.7,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p>Plutonium is a highly unusual metal. It exists in six different solid forms (called allotropes) under normal pressure, and each form behaves differently. Unlike most metals, it is a poor conductor of electricity and heat. Even more strangely, it actually gets denser when it melts into a liquid! Chemically, it readily reacts with elements like carbon, halogens, nitrogen, silicon, and hydrogen. When it comes into contact with moist air, plutonium forms oxides and hydrides that can expand the sample by up to 70% in volume. This can cause it to flake off into a powder that is \'pyrophoric\'—meaning it can spontaneously catch fire in the air. Furthermore, handling plutonium is extremely dangerous because it is highly toxic and radioactive; if it enters the body, it can accumulate in the bones.</p>',
      'Physical properties' => '<p>Freshly prepared plutonium looks bright and silvery, much like nickel, but it quickly oxidizes in air to a dull gray (though yellow and olive green tarnish have also been reported). At room temperature, its typical form is as hard and brittle as gray cast iron, but when mixed (alloyed) with other metals, it can become soft and ductile. It has a relatively low melting point (640 °C or 1,184 °F) but a very high boiling point (3,228 °C or 5,842 °F), meaning it stays in a liquid state across a huge range of temperatures. The radiation it naturally emits slowly damages its own crystal structure over time, a process known as self-irradiation. A chunk of the isotope plutonium-238 generates so much heat from its own radioactive decay that it can glow red-hot if not cooled.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Plutonium is generally considered a human-made element, but incredibly tiny, trace amounts do exist in nature. It is the heaviest element known to occur naturally. These traces are found in natural uranium ores where uranium atoms occasionally absorb neutrons and transform into plutonium. Another form, plutonium-244, has such a long lifespan (a half-life of 80 million years) that it can survive from the ancient cosmic events that created it. Traces of primordial plutonium-244, created by exploding stars (supernovae) and colliding neutron stars long before Earth was formed, have been found in the deep sea floor. Today, minute traces of plutonium also exist in the environment due to atmospheric nuclear weapons testing carried out in the mid-20th century.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>Plutonium was first synthesized and isolated in late 1940 and early 1941 by scientists Glenn T. Seaborg, Arthur Wahl, Joseph W. Kennedy, and Edwin McMillan at the University of California, Berkeley. They achieved this by bombarding uranium with deuterons in a cyclotron. Following the tradition of naming elements after planets (uranium after Uranus and neptunium after Neptune), it was named after the dwarf planet Pluto. Because of World War II, its discovery was kept a strict secret until 1948. Producing plutonium was a major part of the Manhattan Project, and it formed the core of the first atomic bombs, including the one detonated in the 1945 Trinity test and the \'Fat Man\' bomb dropped on Nagasaki.</p><p>In India, plutonium holds immense strategic importance. Following its independence, India established the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) at Trombay. Guided by the visionary Dr. Homi J. Bhabha, India developed research reactors like CIRUS and Dhruva, which successfully produced weapons-grade plutonium. This indigenous capability enabled India\'s peaceful nuclear explosion in 1974 (Smiling Buddha) and the 1998 Pokhran tests (Shakti). While ancient Indian texts like the Vedas do not mention synthetic elements like plutonium, modern Indian scientific achievements strongly reflect a deep, enduring pursuit of harnessing the fundamental forces of nature.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>The most famous use of plutonium is as a fuel for nuclear reactors and in nuclear weapons. The isotopes plutonium-239 and plutonium-241 are \'fissile\', meaning they can easily sustain a nuclear chain reaction to release massive amounts of energy. The percentage of another isotope, plutonium-240, determines the grade of the material, such as weapons-grade or reactor-grade, because of its high rate of spontaneous fission.</p><p>In India, plutonium is the absolute key—the \'bridge\' fuel—in the nation\'s unique Three-Stage Nuclear Power Programme. Because India has limited uranium but vast reserves of thorium, the strategy involves extracting plutonium from spent uranium fuel (Stage 1). This plutonium is then used in Fast Breeder Reactors (Stage 2) to generate electricity and convert thorium into usable nuclear material for the final stage. A monumental achievement for Indian science occurred recently when the Prototype Fast Breeder Reactor (PFBR) at Kalpakkam achieved first criticality in April 2026, marking a massive advancement in commercial fast breeder technology.</p><p>Another isotope, plutonium-238, generates a steady supply of heat through natural decay. This heat is converted into electricity in devices called Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generators (RTGs), which are used to power spacecraft like the Voyager probes, the Curiosity and Perseverance Mars rovers, and New Horizons, allowing them to explore deep space where solar panels wouldn\'t work. On Earth, the disposal of plutonium waste from dismantled weapons and power plants remains a critical environmental and non-proliferation challenge.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Am' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Am',
    'name' => 'Americium',
    'atomic' => 95,
    'category' => 'actinide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b>Americium (Am)</b> is a fascinating synthetic, radioactive element with the atomic number 95. Part of the actinide series, it sits below europium on the periodic table and was named after the Americas to honor this connection. A silvery-white metal that tarnishes slowly in dry air, americium is unique among synthetic elements because it is widely found in everyday households! Its most famous application is in ionization smoke detectors, where a tiny amount of americium-241 is used to keep us safe from fires. While primarily synthesized in nuclear reactors, americium continues to play a vital role in industry, scientific research, and even deep-space exploration.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/Americium_microscope.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 243,
    'density' => 12,
    'melt' => 1449,
    'boil' => 2880,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Glenn T. Seaborg',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p6 7s2 5f7',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.13,
    'electron_affinity' => 9.93,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 578,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'History' => '<p>The story of americium begins during the intense era of the Manhattan Project. In the late autumn of 1944, a team of pioneering scientists—Glenn T. Seaborg, Leon O. Morgan, Ralph A. James, and Albert Ghiorso—first intentionally synthesized and identified americium at the University of California, Berkeley, and the University of Chicago\'s Metallurgical Laboratory.</p><p>They created it by bombarding plutonium with neutrons in a cyclotron. It was the fourth transuranium element (elements beyond uranium) to be discovered. Because it sits directly below europium in the newly restructured periodic table of the time, Seaborg suggested naming it "americium" after the Americas. The discovery was kept secret until after World War II, finally being announced to the public in November 1945 by Seaborg on a national radio show!</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Americium is practically non-existent in nature. While minuscule trace amounts might exist in uranium ores as a result of rare natural nuclear reactions, essentially all americium on Earth today is synthetic. It is produced as a by-product in nuclear reactors when uranium and plutonium absorb neutrons. Over decades of atmospheric nuclear testing and reactor operations, small amounts of americium have been released into the environment, but commercial quantities are strictly synthesized in specialized facilities under heavy regulation.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>All isotopes of americium are radioactive and none are stable. The most important and widely used isotope is <b>Americium-241</b>, which has a half-life of 432.2 years. It decays by emitting alpha particles and gamma rays, eventually turning into neptunium-237. Another significant isotope is <b>Americium-243</b>, which is the most stable isotope of the element, boasting a half-life of 7,370 years. Scientists use americium-243 for deeper chemical studies and to act as target material for synthesizing even heavier transuranic elements in particle accelerators.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Unlike many synthetic elements that exist only in laboratories, americium has tangible, everyday uses. Its most famous role is in <b>ionization smoke detectors</b>. A tiny fraction of a gram of americium-241 emits alpha particles that ionize the air inside the detector, creating a small electric current. When smoke enters the chamber, it disrupts this current, triggering the alarm.</p><p>In the industrial sector, americium-241 is used in thickness and density gauges to measure materials like glass, paper, and metal during manufacturing. When mixed with beryllium, it creates a portable neutron source used extensively for oil well logging, geological exploration, and highway construction.</p><p><b>Indian Context & Future Horizons:</b> In India, americium is utilized within the nation\'s nuclear research and industrial monitoring sectors. Indian scientific organizations rely on americium-beryllium neutron sources for soil moisture testing, agriculture, and geological surveys. Looking to the future, space agencies worldwide are researching americium-241 as a long-lasting power source (Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generators, or RTGs) for deep-space probes. Because it generates heat through radioactive decay, it could power spacecraft exploring the outer planets where solar energy is insufficient.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Cm' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Cm',
    'name' => 'Curium',
    'atomic' => 96,
    'category' => 'actinide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p>Curium (symbol <strong>Cm</strong>, atomic number <strong>96</strong>) is a fascinating, synthetic, and highly radioactive metal. Named in honor of the pioneering scientists Marie and Pierre Curie, this element is a heavy hitter in the actinide series. It glows with a mesmerizing purple light in the dark because of its intense radioactivity. While you will not find curium naturally on Earth today, scientists have mastered the art of creating it in laboratories and nuclear reactors. It has proven incredibly valuable in space exploration, powering devices that analyze Martian rocks, and remains a proud subject of radiochemistry research around the world, including landmark achievements in India.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://images-of-elements.com/s/curium-glow.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 247,
    'density' => 13.51,
    'melt' => 1613,
    'boil' => 3383,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Glenn T. Seaborg',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p6 7s2 5f7 6d1',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.28,
    'electron_affinity' => 27.17,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 581,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'History' => '<p>Curium\'s story began in secret during the height of World War II. It was first synthesized in 1944 as part of the Manhattan Project by a team of brilliant scientists: Glenn T. Seaborg, Ralph A. James, and Albert Ghiorso at the University of California, Berkeley. By bombarding plutonium with alpha particles in a cyclotron, they successfully created this new element. Its discovery was closely guarded and only revealed to the public after the war in 1945 - interestingly, Seaborg first let the secret slip on a children\'s radio show called the <em>Quiz Kids</em>! The element was proudly named \'Curium\' after Marie and Pierre Curie, matching the naming pattern of gadolinium (named after Johan Gadolin), which sits just above it on the periodic table.</p>',
      'Characteristics' => '<p>Curium is a hard, dense, and silvery-white metal. One of its most striking features is that it literally glows purple in the dark due to the sheer intensity of its radiation. It has a melting point of 1,340 deg C (2,444 deg F) and is chemically reactive, easily forming compounds. Curium is electropositive and typically exists in a +3 oxidation state. Because it is highly toxic and radioactive, it poses significant health risks - if absorbed into the body, it tends to accumulate in the bones, disrupting the production of red blood cells.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>Curium has no stable forms; all 19 of its known isotopes (from <sup>233</sup>Cm to <sup>251</sup>Cm) are radioactive. The longest-lived isotope is Curium-247, boasting a half-life of 15.6 million years! Other notable isotopes include Curium-242 and Curium-244, which are more commonly used in research and have shorter half-lives of 162.8 days and 18.1 years, respectively. Curium-250 is particularly unique because it mostly decays by spontaneously splitting apart (spontaneous fission). Many of its isotopes can sustain nuclear chain reactions, meaning they could theoretically be used as nuclear reactor fuel, though their extreme radioactivity and scarcity make this impractical today.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Because even its longest-lived isotope (Curium-247) has a half-life much shorter than the age of the Earth, any primordial curium that existed when our planet formed has long since decayed away into other elements. Today, curium is almost entirely synthetic, created in small amounts in nuclear reactors or laboratories. It does occasionally appear as a byproduct in spent nuclear fuel. Historically, trace amounts of curium were also detected in the environment following atmospheric nuclear weapons testing, such as the <em>Ivy Mike</em> thermonuclear test in 1952. While natural curium does not exist on Earth, scientists believe traces of it may occasionally be brought here by cosmic rays or produced in distant stellar events.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Despite its rarity and hazards, curium has carved out a niche in some truly out-of-this-world applications. Its most famous role is in space exploration. Curium-244 serves as the alpha particle source for Alpha Particle X-ray Spectrometers (APXS). These incredible instruments have been mounted on Mars rovers - like <em>Pathfinder</em>, <em>Spirit</em>, <em>Opportunity</em>, and <em>Curiosity</em> - to zap Martian rocks and soil, helping scientists understand their chemical composition. Thanks to the intense heat generated by its radioactivity, curium has also been studied as a power source for radioisotope thermoelectric generators (RTGs) in spacecraft. In the past, Curium-242 was even considered for powering long-lasting pacemakers before safer lithium batteries took over. In nuclear research, curium is used as a stepping stone to synthesize even heavier, undiscovered elements.</p>',
      'Indian Context' => '<p>India has made significant strides in the complex field of radiochemistry. A major milestone for the country occurred in December 1975, when scientists at the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) in Trombay successfully isolated and purified Curium-242 for the very first time in India. The team achieved this by irradiating indigenously prepared Americium-241 with neutrons in the Cirus reactor. Working with highly radioactive materials required immense skill and the use of specialized glove boxes, marking a proud achievement in India\'s nuclear research capabilities.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Bk' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Bk',
    'name' => 'Berkelium',
    'atomic' => 97,
    'category' => 'actinide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p>Berkelium is a highly radioactive, silvery-white synthetic metal that doesn\'t exist naturally on Earth. Born in a laboratory in 1949, it was named after Berkeley, California, where it was first created. Because it is so rare and difficult to produce, you won\'t find berkelium in any everyday items. Instead, this extreme element plays a starring role in advanced scientific research, helping scientists understand the complex behavior of heavy metals and serving as a crucial building block to discover even newer, superheavy elements on the periodic table.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fc/Berkelium.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 247,
    'density' => 14.78,
    'melt' => 1259,
    'boil' => 2900,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p6 7s2 5f9',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.3,
    'electron_affinity' => -165.24,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 601,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Characteristics' => '<p>Berkelium is a soft, silvery-white metal, but its most defining trait is its intense radioactivity. Because it is both extremely rare and highly radioactive, scientists must use specialized, heavily shielded equipment just to study it. The element can exist in two different crystal structures depending on the temperature, and it melts at around 986 &deg;C (1,807 &deg;F).</p>
<p>One of the biggest challenges in studying berkelium is that it decays very quickly. The most common form used in labs, berkelium-249, has a half-life of just 330 days. As it decays, it transforms into another element called californium and emits powerful radiation. This means a piece of berkelium will actually generate its own heat from radiation and slowly contaminate itself over time, making it incredibly tricky to analyze.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>Every single form&mdash;or isotope&mdash;of berkelium is radioactive; there are no stable versions. Scientists have discovered over a dozen different isotopes, with atomic weights ranging from 235 to 254.</p>
<p>The longest-lasting isotope is berkelium-247, which takes about 1,380 years for half of it to decay. However, producing it is extremely difficult. Instead, researchers rely on berkelium-249. Even though half of it decays in just 330 days, it emits a "softer" type of radiation (beta particles) that is slightly easier to handle safely. Still, because it constantly decays into a more dangerous element, scientists have to work fast!</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>You won\'t find berkelium anywhere in nature. It is a strictly synthetic (human-made) element. Even if some berkelium had formed when the Earth was born billions of years ago, its short half-life means it would have vanished long before humans existed.</p>
<p>Today, berkelium is born in specialized nuclear reactors, like the ones at Oak Ridge National Laboratory in the US and the Joint Institute for Nuclear Research in Russia. Making berkelium is a painstakingly slow and expensive process&mdash;in fact, since its discovery, only slightly more than a single gram has ever been produced in the United States! It can take years of careful work to create and purify just a few milligrams of this elusive metal.</p>',
      'History' => '<p>The story of berkelium began in December 1949. A team of pioneering scientists&mdash;including Glenn T. Seaborg, Albert Ghiorso, Stanley G. Thompson, and Kenneth Street Jr.&mdash;successfully created the element at the University of California, Berkeley. To do this, they used a cyclotron (a type of particle accelerator) to bombard a smaller element, americium, with helium nuclei.</p>
<p>They named their new discovery "berkelium" in honor of the city of Berkeley. This was a clever nod to the periodic table: the element sitting directly above berkelium is terbium, which was named after the Swedish village of Ytterby where it was found.</p>
<p>Since those early days, exploring these heavy elements has become a worldwide effort. Indian nuclear scientists, for example, are active contributors to the global research on the nuclear fuel cycle and the complex chemistry of actinides (the family of elements berkelium belongs to). Their theoretical models and research help international teams better understand these mysterious, heavy elements.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Because it is so incredibly rare, dangerous, and expensive to produce, berkelium has no practical or commercial uses. You won\'t find it in any gadgets or medical devices.</p>
<p>Instead, berkelium\'s true value lies in pushing the boundaries of science. Its most famous role is acting as a "target" to create even heavier, newer elements. In 2009, a tiny 22-milligram sample of berkelium was created in Tennessee and shipped to Russia. There, scientists bombarded it with calcium atoms for months. This historic international collaboration&mdash;which involved theoretical contributions from scientists across the globe, including Indian physicists&mdash;successfully created the first atoms of a brand new element: tennessine (element 117).</p>
<p>Recently, scientists have also used berkelium to create "berkelocene," a complex molecule that helps researchers finally understand how this mysterious element forms chemical bonds. For now, berkelium remains an invaluable tool for unlocking the deepest secrets of the periodic table.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Cf' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Cf',
    'name' => 'Californium',
    'atomic' => 98,
    'category' => 'actinide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b>Californium (Cf)</b> is a fascinating, human-made chemical element with the atomic number 98. First created in 1950 at the University of California, Berkeley (which inspired its name), it is a highly radioactive metal belonging to the actinide series. Californium is famous for being one of the heaviest elements ever produced in amounts large enough to see with the naked eye.</p>
<p>Unlike most synthetic elements that exist only in laboratories for fleeting moments, Californium actually has practical, real-world applications. Its most widely used isotope, Californium-252, is an incredibly powerful source of neutrons. Because of this, it is used to kick-start nuclear reactors, scan for precious metals, and even treat certain types of cancers. However, due to its intense radioactivity, it requires highly specialized handling and is exceptionally expensive to produce.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/93/Californium.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 251,
    'density' => 15.1,
    'melt' => 1173,
    'boil' => 1743,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p6 7s2 5f10',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.3,
    'electron_affinity' => -97.31,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 608,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Physical properties' => 'Californium is a silvery-white metal that is surprisingly soft—it can easily be sliced with an ordinary knife. In a vacuum, it starts to turn into a gas at temperatures above 300 °C (570 °F), but under normal conditions, it melts at around 900 °C (1,650 °F). 

What\'s especially interesting about Californium is its magnetic personality. When cooled to extremely low temperatures, it can act like a regular magnet, and at slightly warmer temperatures, it responds to external magnetic fields. The metal exists in two distinct crystal shapes depending on the temperature, and it will slowly tarnish if left exposed to room-temperature air.',
      'Isotopes' => 'There are 20 known isotopes of Californium, which are different versions of the element that vary in weight. The most stable one is Californium-251, which takes 898 years for half of it to decay away. 

However, the superstar of the group is Californium-252. Even though its half-life is only about 2.6 years, it is an absolute powerhouse when it comes to emitting neutrons. A single microgram (one-millionth of a gram) of Californium-252 shoots out an astonishing 2.3 million neutrons every single second! Because it throws off so much radiation, it is extremely hazardous but also incredibly useful for specialized scientific and industrial tasks.',
      'History' => 'Californium was born in the lab! It was first synthesized on February 9, 1950, by a legendary team of scientists—Stanley G. Thompson, Kenneth Street Jr., Albert Ghiorso, and Glenn T. Seaborg—at the University of California, Berkeley. To create it, they used a particle accelerator called a cyclotron to smash alpha particles (helium ions) into a tiny target made of curium. 

They named the new element "Californium" in honor of both the university and the state. At the time, they joked that making the element was just as difficult as it was for early pioneers to travel to California a century earlier. By the 1960s and 70s, specialized reactors began producing small, weighable batches of the element. In the early 1970s, it was sold for a mind-boggling $10 per microgram, making it one of the most expensive materials on Earth!',
      'Occurrence' => 'You won\'t find Californium naturally occurring on Earth today. Because all of its isotopes are radioactive and eventually decay, any Californium that might have existed when our planet formed billions of years ago is long gone. 

Today, traces of Californium can only be found in the environment near specialized nuclear facilities or medical centers that use it. Small amounts were also released into the atmosphere during nuclear weapons testing in the mid-20th century. Interestingly, scientists once believed that Californium was created naturally in the explosive deaths of stars (supernovas), but modern astronomy suggests that other elements are responsible for the light we see from those cosmic blasts.',
      'Applications and Modern Uses' => 'Californium is one of the few superheavy, synthetic elements with major practical uses, largely thanks to its ability to unleash a massive flood of neutrons. 

• **Nuclear Power:** It is used as a highly reliable "spark plug" to start up nuclear reactors.
• **Material Scanning:** Industries use it for Prompt Gamma Neutron Activation Analysis (PGNAA), a technique that bombs coal, cement, or minerals with neutrons to instantly analyze their chemical makeup. 
• **Oil and Gas Exploration:** It is lowered into boreholes to detect water, oil, and gas layers deep underground.
• **Medical Treatments:** In specialized radiation therapies, Californium-252 is used to target and shrink certain types of stubborn cancers when traditional treatments fail.
• **Scientific Discovery:** It is often used as a target in particle accelerators to help scientists discover even heavier, brand-new elements like Oganesson (element 118).',
      'Indian Context' => 'In India, Californium has made headlines in a rather unusual and bizarre way—through high-stakes scams. Over the years, Indian law enforcement agencies have arrested several smuggling gangs trying to sell mysterious "stones" or metallic objects for millions of rupees, falsely claiming the material was the ultra-rare Californium. 

When these seized materials were tested by India\'s Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) and the Department of Atomic Energy (DAE), they were consistently found to be completely ordinary, non-radioactive fakes. Real Californium is strictly regulated, intensely radioactive, and requires massive lead-lined casks for transport, making it virtually impossible to find on the black market! On the legitimate side, Indian research institutions like BARC have safely used loaned Californium-252 sources for decades to conduct advanced radiological physics training and research.',
    ),
  ),
  'Es' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Es',
    'name' => 'Einsteinium',
    'atomic' => 99,
    'category' => 'actinide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b>Einsteinium</b> (symbol <b>Es</b>, atomic number <b>99</b>) is a fascinating, highly radioactive synthetic metal. Part of the actinide series, it was first discovered in 1952 in the radioactive debris of the world\'s first hydrogen bomb explosion ("Ivy Mike"). It was named in honor of the renowned physicist Albert Einstein.</p>
<p>Because it is highly radioactive and decays quickly, einsteinium is extremely difficult to study. Its most common isotope, einsteinium-253, glows with a mysterious blue light due to its intense radiation, generating so much heat that it can actually damage its own crystalline structure! This element does not exist naturally on Earth and is produced in tiny quantities—about a milligram per year—in specialized nuclear reactors. While einsteinium currently has no everyday practical applications, it represents a remarkable achievement in nuclear chemistry and pushes the boundaries of our understanding of the periodic table.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/55/Einsteinium.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 252,
    'density' => 8.84,
    'melt' => 1133,
    'boil' => 1269,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p6 7s2 5f11',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.3,
    'electron_affinity' => -28.6,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 619,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'History' => '<p>The story of einsteinium\'s discovery is deeply tied to the dawn of the thermonuclear age. It was first identified in December 1952 by a team of scientists led by Albert Ghiorso at the University of California, Berkeley, alongside researchers from the Argonne and Los Alamos National Laboratories. They discovered the new element while analyzing the radioactive fallout from the "Ivy Mike" hydrogen bomb test in the Pacific Ocean.</p>
<p>The immense explosion created a massive burst of neutrons, allowing uranium atoms to rapidly absorb multiple neutrons and transform into heavier, entirely new elements. Initially, this groundbreaking discovery was kept a strict military secret due to Cold War tensions, and was only declassified and announced to the public in 1955. The element was officially named <b>einsteinium</b> to honor Albert Einstein, while the closely related element 100 was named fermium after Enrico Fermi.</p>',
      'Characteristics' => '<p>Einsteinium is a soft, silvery, paramagnetic metal. Its most striking physical feature is its self-luminescence—it emits a visible blue glow in the dark, a direct result of its intense radiation releasing heat (nearly 1,000 watts per gram). This extreme radioactivity makes studying the metal in bulk nearly impossible because the radiation rapidly destroys its own crystal lattice.</p>
<p>Chemically, it typically exhibits a +3 oxidation state, which is standard for late actinides, though a +2 state is also possible. It has a melting point of 860 °C (1580 °F) and an estimated boiling point of 996 °C (1825 °F). As a synthetic transuranium element, einsteinium is extremely hazardous and toxic. It must be handled with extraordinary caution in specialized, highly controlled facilities.</p>',
      'Isotopes' => '<p>Einsteinium has 18 known isotopes and four nuclear isomers, with mass numbers ranging from 240 to 257. All of them are highly radioactive and unstable. The most stable isotope is einsteinium-252, which has a half-life of 471.7 days, though it is incredibly difficult to produce. The most commonly used isotope for scientific research, einsteinium-253, has a much shorter half-life of just 20.47 days.</p>
<p>Other notable isotopes include einsteinium-254 (with a half-life of 275.7 days) and einsteinium-255 (39.8 days). Because its isotopes decay so quickly—often transforming into lighter elements like berkelium or californium—studying einsteinium requires scientists to work in a continuous race against time before their samples literally disappear.</p>',
      'Applications' => '<p>Due to its scarcity, short half-life, and extreme radioactivity, einsteinium has no everyday commercial or industrial uses. Instead, it is highly valued in basic scientific research, specifically as a stepping stone for synthesizing even heavier, superheavy elements. For example, in 1955, einsteinium was crucially used as a target to discover mendelevium (element 101). Later, researchers used the rare isotope einsteinium-254 to attempt the creation of element 119 (ununennium).</p>
<p>An interesting historical application occurred when einsteinium-254 was used as a calibration marker in the chemical analysis spectrometer aboard the Surveyor 5 lunar probe, helping scientists analyze the surface of the Moon.</p>
<p>In the <b>Indian context</b>, while the element is not produced or used locally in industries, einsteinium holds an important place in advanced academic curricula. Students of nuclear physics and chemistry across India study its properties as a prime example of the f-block actinide series. Furthermore, Indian scientific media closely follow global breakthroughs related to this elusive element to keep the nation\'s robust research community informed about the frontiers of nuclear science.</p>',
    ),
  ),
  'Fm' => 
  array (
    'symbol' => 'Fm',
    'name' => 'Fermium',
    'atomic' => 100,
    'category' => 'actinide',
    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><b>Fermium</b> (symbol <b>Fm</b>, atomic number 100) is a fascinating, highly radioactive synthetic element. It holds a unique place in scientific history as the heaviest element that can be formed by bombarding lighter elements with neutrons. Because of this, it is the last element on the periodic table that can theoretically be prepared in macroscopic (visible) quantities, although pure fermium metal has not yet been produced.</p>

<p>Fermium was first discovered in the radioactive debris of the "Ivy Mike" nuclear test in 1952—the world\'s first successful hydrogen bomb explosion. Due to the secrecy of the Cold War, this discovery was kept classified by the U.S. military until 1955. The element was named in honor of Enrico Fermi, the visionary physicist who developed the first artificial nuclear reactor.</p>

<p>As a member of the actinide series, fermium does not occur naturally on Earth and must be synthesized in specialized high-flux nuclear reactors. It typically exhibits a +3 oxidation state in chemical compounds. Because only microscopic amounts of fermium can be produced and its isotopes decay quickly, it has no commercial, industrial, or everyday applications. Instead, it is used exclusively for advanced scientific research to help physicists understand the limits of nuclear stability and the behavior of the heaviest elements in the universe.</p>',
    'local_image' => 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/58/Ivy_Mike_-_mushroom_cloud.jpg',
    'atomic_mass' => 257,
    'density' => NULL,
    'melt' => 1800,
    'boil' => NULL,
    'phase' => 'Solid',
    'discovered_by' => 'Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory',
    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p6 7s2 5f12',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.3,
    'electron_affinity' => 33.96,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 627,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'Isotopes' => '<p>Fermium has 20 known isotopes, ranging in atomic weight from 241 to 260. All of them are radioactive and relatively short-lived. The most stable isotope is <b>fermium-257</b>, which has a half-life of about 100.5 days. Other notable isotopes include fermium-253 (half-life of 3 days) and fermium-255 (20.1 hours). Most other isotopes decay much faster, with half-lives ranging from a few hours to mere milliseconds.</p>

<p>An interesting scientific roadblock occurs with heavier fermium isotopes. When fermium-257 captures a neutron to become fermium-258, it undergoes spontaneous fission (splitting apart) in just 370 microseconds. Heavier isotopes like fermium-259 and fermium-260 also undergo rapid spontaneous fission. This phenomenon prevents scientists from creating even heavier elements through standard neutron capture in reactors, creating a barrier known as the "fermium gap." Because of this gap, elements heavier than fermium must be painstakingly synthesized by colliding ions in particle accelerators.</p>',
      'Occurrence' => '<p>Fermium does not exist naturally in the Earth\'s crust. It is a strictly synthetic element that must be artificially created in highly specialized laboratories. Today, it is produced in microscopic quantities by bombarding lighter elements, such as plutonium or curium, with intense neutrons in high-flux nuclear reactors.</p>

<p>While India has a robust nuclear research program led by institutions like the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) and the Indira Gandhi Centre for Atomic Research (IGCAR), fermium is not a focus of domestic industrial or energy programs. Indian nuclear research primarily targets energy security, advanced reactor technologies, and the thorium fuel cycle. Since fermium has no commercial or practical applications, it remains a subject of fundamental global physics research and is produced only in a few elite high-flux reactor facilities worldwide.</p>',
    ),
