    'electron_configuration' => '1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6 5s2 4d10 5p6 6s2 4f14 5d10 6p6 7s2 5f13',
    'electronegativity_pauling' => 1.3,
    'electron_affinity' => 93.91,
    'ionization_energies' => 
    array (
      0 => 635,
    ),
    'sections' => 
    array (
      'History and Discovery' => '<p>Mendelevium was first synthesized in 1955 by a legendary team of scientists at the University of California, Berkeley, which included Albert Ghiorso, Glenn T. Seaborg, Bernard Harvey, Gregory Choppin, and Stanley G. Thompson. They created the new element by bombarding atoms of einsteinium-253 with alpha particles (helium nuclei) using a cyclotron. The element was proudly named to honor <strong>Dmitri Mendeleev</strong>, the famous Russian chemist who created the periodic table of elements, serving as a lasting tribute to his foundational contributions to science.</p>',
      'Scientific Facts and Isotopes' => '<p>As a highly radioactive metal, mendelevium typically exhibits a +3 oxidation state, though a +2 state is also accessible in certain aqueous solutions. Mendelevium has seventeen known isotopes, all of which are radioactive and artificially produced. The most stable of these is <strong>mendelevium-258</strong>, which has a half-life of approximately 51.5 days. However, scientists often use the shorter-lived mendelevium-256 (with a half-life of about 77 minutes) in experiments because it is easier to produce in somewhat larger quantities. Mendelevium stands out as the heaviest element that still has an isotope with a half-life longer than a single day.</p>',
      'Modern Uses' => '<p>Because it can only be produced in incredibly minuscule amounts and has a relatively short half-life, mendelevium currently has <strong>no commercial or industrial applications</strong>. Its use is strictly confined to basic scientific research. Today, scientists study mendelevium to better understand the chemical and physical properties of the late actinide elements and to push the boundaries of what we know about atomic structure.</p>',
      'Indian Context' => '<p>While there are no specific facilities in India producing mendelevium or unique industrial applications for it within the country, the element holds a special place in the Indian scientific and educational landscape. In Indian academic literature and university curricula, mendelevium is frequently highlighted as a powerful example of human ingenuity in nuclear science. It serves as a key pedagogical tool to illustrate the fascinating history of the periodic table, the successful synthesis of transuranic elements, and the deeply international, collaborative nature of scientific discovery.</p>',
