    'description' => '',
    'extract_html' => '<p><strong>Livermorium</strong> (symbol <strong>Lv</strong>, atomic number <strong>116</strong>) is a superheavy, highly radioactive synthetic element. It does not exist in nature and must be created entirely in laboratories. In fact, since its discovery, only a few dozen atoms of livermorium have ever been produced.</p><p>Livermorium was first synthesized during experiments conducted between 2000 and 2006 through a joint effort by scientists at the Joint Institute for Nuclear Research (JINR) in Dubna, Russia, and the Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory (LLNL) in California, USA. It was officially recognized by IUPAC and added to the periodic table in 2011, and was named Livermorium in 2012 to honor the LLNL and the city of Livermore.</p><p>Located in the p-block and sitting in group 16 (the chalcogen family), livermorium shares its column with oxygen, sulfur, and selenium. Because its most stable known isotope (livermorium-293) has a fleeting half-life of roughly 53 to 80 milliseconds, experimental study of its chemistry is incredibly challenging. Based on periodic trends, scientists predict it would be a solid metal at room temperature.</p><p>Currently, livermorium has <strong>no commercial, industrial, or biological uses</strong>. Its exclusive application is in fundamental scientific research, helping physicists map the limits of nuclear stability and explore the island of stability in superheavy elements. In an Indian context, while there were no direct contributions from India in its synthesis, Livermorium is a standard topic in advanced chemistry and nuclear physics curricula across the country, particularly for students preparing for competitive exams like JEE and NEET, highlighting global advancements in synthetic particle physics.</p>',
