    'sections' => 
    array (
      'History' => '<p>Tennessine was first synthesized in 2010 through an epic international collaboration. Scientists at the Joint Institute for Nuclear Research (JINR) in Dubna, Russia, teamed up with researchers from the Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory and Oak Ridge National Laboratory in the United States. To create it, they smashed calcium-48 ions into a target of berkelium-249 inside a powerful particle accelerator.</p>
<p>In 2016, the International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC) officially named it <strong>Tennessine</strong>. The name is a tribute to the US state of Tennessee, honoring the vital contributions of Oak Ridge National Laboratory, Vanderbilt University, and the University of Tennessee to the discovery of superheavy elements.</p>',
      'Modern Uses & Scientific Facts' => '<p>Currently, tennessine has absolutely no commercial or practical uses. It is simply too unstable to be collected or used in everyday life. Its only "use" is in the realm of cutting-edge scientific research.</p>
<p>Scientists study tennessine to learn more about the fundamental forces of the universe and to explore the theoretical <strong>"island of stability."</strong> This is a hypothesized region on the periodic table where superheavy elements might have special combinations of protons and neutrons that allow them to survive much longer than their fleeting neighbors.</p>',
      'Indian Context' => '<p>While the initial discovery was a joint effort between the US and Russia, India played a crucial role in verifying the existence of tennessine. In 2014, a massive international team involving 16 institutions—including Indian scientists—conducted independent confirmation experiments. This successful verification was critical for tennessine to be officially recognized and added to the periodic table.</p>',
