    'sections' => 
    array (
      'History' => '<p>Oganesson was first synthesized in 2002 by a joint team of Russian and American scientists at the Joint Institute for Nuclear Research (JINR) in Dubna, Russia, in collaboration with the Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory. It was officially recognized by the scientific community in 2015 and formally named in 2016.</p><p>The element is named in honor of Yuri Oganessian, a pioneering Russian nuclear physicist who played a monumental role in discovering the heaviest elements in the periodic table. Fascinatingly, Oganesson is one of the very few elements ever named after a person who was still living at the time of naming!</p>',
      'Characteristics and Scientific Facts' => '<p>Due to its extremely short half-life—just 0.89 milliseconds for its most stable isotope, Oganesson-294—and the fact that only a handful of atoms have ever been created, scientists haven\'t been able to measure its physical or chemical properties in a traditional laboratory setting. Instead, they rely on complex theoretical models and relativistic quantum chemistry.</p><p>While it sits in the noble gas column alongside helium and neon, calculations predict that oganesson would actually be a solid at room temperature rather than a gas. Its electron configuration ([Rn] 5f<sup>14</sup> 6d<sup>10</sup> 7s<sup>2</sup> 7p<sup>6</sup>) is expected to exhibit unusual behaviors where its electrons defy standard periodic trends. Its predicted melting point is around 325 K (52 &deg;C) and its boiling point is roughly 450 K (177 &deg;C). Its density is predicted to be quite heavy, at about 7.2 g/cm&sup3; when solid.</p>',
      'Modern Uses' => '<p>Because oganesson is incredibly difficult and expensive to produce and decays almost instantly, it currently has no commercial, industrial, or medical applications. Its value is entirely fundamental: it helps scientists test complex theories in quantum mechanics and nuclear physics. Researchers study it to search for the theoretical "Island of Stability"&mdash;a hypothetical region of superheavy elements that might have much longer half-lives.</p>',
      'Indian Context' => '<p>In India, oganesson does not have any specialized industrial production or use, but it holds great academic significance. It is a topic of fascination within the Indian scientific and educational community. It is frequently discussed in higher education curricula and competitive scientific exams, such as the IIT JEE, as a prime example of element synthesis, radioactivity, and the ultimate limits of the modern periodic table. Indian researchers and physics students continue to study its theoretical properties to better understand the cutting-edge boundaries of atomic science.</p>',
